export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"detailcraft","name":"DetailCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Neuerungen in 0.8 und 0.8.1","summary":"Ein Montagefilm aus der Traglasttabelle eines Krans, ein Anschluss-Autopilot, eine Karte der Bewehrungsdichte, Fundamentprüfungen, Rahmen aus Holz und Leichtbauprofilen, Prüfungen zur Bauausführbarkeit und Schweißzugänglichkeit, Lagerbestand von Lieferanten, Was versagt zuerst, eine Live-Neuberechnung, weitere Betonprüfungen und die Korrekturen von 0.8.1.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["Neuerungen","Versionshinweise","Änderungsprotokoll","Änderungen","0.8","0.8.1","Version","neue Funktionen","Montage","Kran","Traglasttabelle","Film","Autopilot","Anschlüsse","Bewehrung","Bewehrungsdichte","Gründung","Fundament","Matte","Setzung","Rahmenbau","Holz","Leichtbau","Zuschnittsliste","Bauausführbarkeit","Schweißzugänglichkeit","Lieferant","Lagerbestand","Lieferzeit","Was versagt zuerst","Live","Beton","seismisch","Decke","Sprachen","Assistent"],"headings":[{"id":"neu","text":"Neu","level":2},{"id":"montage","text":"Montage","level":3},{"id":"anschl-sse","text":"Anschlüsse","level":3},{"id":"gr-ndungen-und-bewehrung","text":"Gründungen und Bewehrung","level":3},{"id":"rahmenbau-und-lieferung","text":"Rahmenbau und Lieferung","level":3},{"id":"berechnung-und-bemessung","text":"Berechnung und Bemessung","level":3},{"id":"in-allen-apps","text":"In allen Apps","level":3},{"id":"in-0-8-1-behoben","text":"In 0.8.1 behoben","level":2},{"id":"noch-nicht-in-dieser-version","text":"Noch nicht in dieser Version","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDies sind die Änderungen in DetailCraft 0.8 (1. November 2026) und 0.8.1 (2. November 2026) gegenüber 0.7. 0.7 prüfte Stahlbauteile; 0.8 plant die Hubvorgänge, schlägt Anschlüsse vor, prüft die Fundamente und die Bewehrung und rahmt Holz- und Leichtbauwände, Decken und Dächer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"neu\"\u003ENeu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"montage\"\u003EMontage\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMontageplan und Film.\u003C/strong\u003E Beschreiben Sie in der Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMontage\u003C/span\u003E einen Kran (wo er steht, seinen höchsten Haken, das Anschlagmittel, das nicht in der Tabelle steht) und tragen Sie die Zeilen seiner Traglasttabelle ein. DetailCraft hebt das Modell eine Baugruppe nach der anderen, in Phasenreihenfolge und Lage für Lage, und prüft jeden Hub gegen die Tabelle bei seinem Ausladungsradius und gegen die Reserve, die Sie festlegen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFilm speichern\u003C/span\u003E schreibt eine HTML-Datei, die offline in jedem Browser abspielt, auch auf einem Telefon. Hübe, die über der Tabelle liegen, darüber hinausgehen oder zu hoch sind, stehen unter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProbleme\u003C/span\u003E. Es ist ein Plan aus Ihren Zahlen, kein Hubplan. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/erection-movie\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/erection-movie\"\u003EMontageplan und Film\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"anschl-sse\"\u003EAnschlüsse\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAnschluss-Autopilot.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutopilot\u003C/span\u003E betrachtet jeden Knoten ohne Anschluss und schlägt anhand der Profile einen vor: Fußplatten, Anschlusslaschen, Kopfplatten für die Träger, die Sie als Momententräger markieren, Stöße und Knotenbleche. Geben Sie eine Gleichlast oder eine Querkraft an einem Ende an, und die Schraubenzahl richtet sich nach der Bemessungsfestigkeit der Schraube. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlle hinzufügen\u003C/span\u003E fügt die Vorschläge als einen Rückgängig-Schritt hinzu. Sie sind Ausgangswerte zur Prüfung, kein Anschlussnachweis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrüfungen zur Bauausführbarkeit.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProbleme\u003C/span\u003E für eine Schraube, die kein Schlüssel erreicht, Schrauben, die für die Nuss zu dicht beieinander liegen, Kehlnähte außerhalb ihrer Größengrenzen und Hohlprofile, die Wasser halten. Neu ist die Schweißzugänglichkeit: Eine rundum laufende Kehlnaht, die der Brenner eines Schweißers nicht erreicht, wird mit dem Teil, das im Weg ist, und dem betroffenen Anteil der Naht aufgelistet. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/constructability\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/constructability\"\u003EPrüfungen zur Bauausführbarkeit\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gr-ndungen-und-bewehrung\"\u003EGründungen und Bewehrung\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFundamentprüfungen.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGründung\u003C/span\u003E prüft Einzelfundamente, kombinierte Fundamente und Plattenfundamente auf dem Baugrund, den Sie beschreiben: Auflagerdruck, Gleiten und Kippen aus Gebrauchslasten; Biegung, Querkraft, Durchstanzen, Verankerung und Mindestbewehrung aus Bemessungslasten (ACI 318-19). Eine Stütze nahe einem Rand oder einer Ecke wird mit dem Querschnitt geprüft, der vom Rand des Fundaments abgeschnitten ist. Die Setzung ergibt sich aus dem Steifemodul des Bodens, ein biegsames Plattenfundament wird auf Federn mit einem Bettungsmodul gelöst, und Pfahlgruppen und Stützwände haben eigene Felder. Stützenlasten können eingegeben oder aus der \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBerechnung\u003C/span\u003E übernommen werden. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/foundation-checks\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/foundation-checks\"\u003EFundamentprüfungen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKarte der Bewehrungsdichte.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBewehrungsdichte\u003C/span\u003E zeichnet für jedes Fundament eine Wärmekarte aus der Draufsicht: wo die Stäbe zu wenig Platz lassen, damit der Beton nach unten gelangt, verglichen mit der Regel für den lichten Stababstand und, wenn Sie sie angeben, dem größten Zuschlagkorn und dem Rüttelkopf. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/rebar-congestion\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/rebar-congestion\"\u003EKarte der Bewehrungsdichte\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rahmenbau-und-lieferung\"\u003ERahmenbau und Lieferung\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERahmen aus Holz und Leichtbauprofilen.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERahmenbau\u003C/span\u003E rahmt Wand-, Decken- und Dachtafeln mit jedem Einzelteil, einer Zuschnittsliste, dem zu kaufenden Material und einer maßstäblichen Werkstattzeichnung. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAls Stäbe platzieren\u003C/span\u003E setzt die Teile ins Modell, und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF-Blätter\u003C/span\u003E speichert ein Blatt für jede Tafel. Querschnitte, Abstände und Stürze legen Sie fest: DetailCraft prüft nicht, ob sie die Last tragen. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/framing-panels\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/framing-panels\"\u003ERahmen aus Holz und Leichtbauprofilen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELagerbestand und Lieferzeiten.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELieferung\u003C/span\u003E liest die Lagerliste eines Lieferanten (CSV) und zeigt für jeden Querschnitt, den die Zuschnittsliste kauft, ob genug auf Lager ist und wie lange die Wartezeit dauert. Fehlmengen und verspätete Querschnitte stehen unter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProbleme\u003C/span\u003E. Es wird nichts bestellt. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/supplier-stock\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/supplier-stock\"\u003ELagerbestand und Lieferzeiten\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"berechnung-und-bemessung\"\u003EBerechnung und Bemessung\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWas versagt zuerst?\u003C/strong\u003E Auf der Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBerechnung\u003C/span\u003E findet die Schaltfläche die maßgebende Lastkombination, das schwächste Bauteil, wie weit die Lasten wachsen können, bevor das erste Bauteil seine Grenze erreicht, und den schwächsten Kopfplatten- oder Querkraftanschluss, sofern die Scherfestigkeit seiner Schrauben bekannt ist. Dazu brauchen Sie zuerst die Kombinationen und eine Bemessungsnorm. Lochleibung, Blockscheren, Schweißnähte und Hebelwirkung werden nicht geprüft. Ihr angebundener Assistent kann dasselbe abfragen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELive.\u003C/strong\u003E Setzen Sie den Haken bei \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive: nach jeder Änderung neu lösen\u003C/span\u003E, dann wird der Rahmen nach jeder Änderung neu berechnet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeton, weitere.\u003C/strong\u003E Eine schlanke Stütze in einem unverschieblichen Rahmen wird für ihr vergrößertes Moment bemessen (ACI 318-19 6.6.4.5). Die seismische Periode kann nach der Methode von Rayleigh aus der Berechnung übernommen werden. Eine Platte aus Blechen erhält den Stahl, den jede Lage aus ihren Momenten braucht, sowie die einachsige Querkraft. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/concrete-and-seismic\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/concrete-and-seismic\"\u003EBetonbauteile prüfen und seismische Lasten erzeugen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZur Prüfung durch einen Ingenieur.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Ergebnisse für Fundament, Beton und „Was versagt zuerst?“ sind Hilfsmittel. Windlasten sind nicht umgesetzt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"in-allen-apps\"\u003EIn allen Apps\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESprachen.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Hilfe liegt in 54 Sprachen vor. Die Sprachen außer Englisch sind maschinelle Entwürfe, bis eine Muttersprachlerin oder ein Muttersprachler sie gelesen hat; zehn sind neu (Litauisch, Lettisch, Bosnisch, Marathi, Telugu, Albanisch, Afrikaans, Filipino, Swahili, Usbekisch).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymbolleistenzeilen.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllgemein\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbolleistenzeilen\u003C/span\u003E wählt Umbruch, 2 Zeilen oder 1 Zeile. Eine Leiste, die bereits passt, bleibt, wie sie ist; die übrigen lassen sich seitlich scrollen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEigenschaftenraster.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Felder des Fundaments verwenden das gemeinsame Raster: einklappbare Kategorien und eine kompakte Dichte. Eingegebene Rechnungen wie 2*450 funktionieren weiterhin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEingabegeräte.\u003C/strong\u003E Trackpad, Stift, 3D-Maus, Controller und MIDI können die Apps steuern. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/navigate-3d\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/navigate-3d\"\u003EIn 3D navigieren\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProbleme-Bereich.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Warnungen der oben genannten neuen Prüfungen erscheinen im selben Bereich \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProbleme\u003C/span\u003E wie bisher.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKI-Assistent anbinden.\u003C/strong\u003E Ihr eigener Assistent, angebunden über CadCraft Studio, arbeitet mit allen acht Apps und sieht nur, was Sie freigeben. Das Anbinden per Klick, der gehostete Connector und Ihr eigener API-Schlüssel in Studio folgen noch. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\"\u003EDetailCraft mit einem KI-Assistenten verwenden\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-0-8-1-behoben\"\u003EIn 0.8.1 behoben\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESprachen in Studio.\u003C/strong\u003E Studio und die gepackten Ausgaben können die Sprache zur Laufzeit wechseln; bisher gab es nur Englisch. Es ist noch nicht auf allen Plattformen getestet: Sagen Sie uns Bescheid, falls sich die Sprache nicht ändert.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAuswahl mit Kreuzen-Fenster.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Installationsprogramme von 0.8.1 enthalten das Auswählen per Fenster bereits in der richtigen Form, wie in der Web-Version von 0.8; ein älteres Installationsprogramm tat dies nicht.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETablets im Hochformat.\u003C/strong\u003E Freie Rasterhöhe geht an die Zeichenfläche, und das Blattlayout beginnt bei 760 px oder weniger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"noch-nicht-in-dieser-version\"\u003ENoch nicht in dieser Version\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENC an eine Maschine senden\u003C/strong\u003E ist nicht freigegeben.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWindlasten\u003C/strong\u003E für Beton sind nicht umgesetzt, und die Beton- und Fundamentprüfungen sind für einen Ingenieur zur Prüfung bestimmt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECodesignierung.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Windows-Installationsprogramme und die macOS-.dmg sind nicht signiert; Windows warnt daher möglicherweise, und macOS verlangt einen Rechtsklick, dann „Öffnen“.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrühere Änderungen stehen unter \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/whats-new-0-7\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/whats-new-0-7\"\u003ENeuerungen in 0.7\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Dies sind die Änderungen in DetailCraft 0.8 (1. November 2026) und 0.8.1 (2. November 2026) gegenüber 0.7. 0.7 prüfte Stahlbauteile; 0.8 plant die Hubvorgänge, schlägt Anschlüsse vor, prüft die Fundamente und die Bewehrung und rahmt Holz- und Leichtbauwände, Decken und Dächer. Neu Montage Montageplan und Film. Beschreiben Sie in der Registerkarte Montage einen Kran (wo er steht, seinen höchsten Haken, das Anschlagmittel, das nicht in der Tabelle steht) und tragen Sie die Zeilen seiner Traglasttabelle ein. DetailCraft hebt das Modell eine Baugruppe nach der anderen, in Phasenreihenfolge und Lage für Lage, und prüft jeden Hub gegen die Tabelle bei seinem Ausladungsradius und gegen die Reserve, die Sie festlegen. Film speichern schreibt eine HTML-Datei, die offline in jedem Browser abspielt, auch auf einem Telefon. Hübe, die über der Tabelle liegen, darüber hinausgehen oder zu hoch sind, stehen unter Probleme. Es ist ein Plan aus Ihren Zahlen, kein Hubplan. Siehe Montageplan und Film. Anschlüsse Anschluss-Autopilot. Die Registerkarte Autopilot betrachtet jeden Knoten ohne Anschluss und schlägt anhand der Profile einen vor: Fußplatten, Anschlusslaschen, Kopfplatten für die Träger, die Sie als Momententräger markieren, Stöße und Knotenbleche. Geben Sie eine Gleichlast oder eine Querkraft an einem Ende an, und die Schraubenzahl richtet sich nach der Bemessungsfestigkeit der Schraube. Alle hinzufügen fügt die Vorschläge als einen Rückgängig-Schritt hinzu. Sie sind Ausgangswerte zur Prüfung, kein Anschlussnachweis. Prüfungen zur Bauausführbarkeit. Probleme für eine Schraube, die kein Schlüssel erreicht, Schrauben, die für die Nuss zu dicht beieinander liegen, Kehlnähte außerhalb ihrer Größengrenzen und Hohlprofile, die Wasser halten. Neu ist die Schweißzugänglichkeit: Eine rundum laufende Kehlnaht, die der Brenner eines Schweißers nicht erreicht, wird mit dem Teil, das im Weg ist, und dem betroffenen Anteil der Naht aufgelistet. Siehe Prüfungen zur Bauausführbarkeit. Gründungen und Bewehrung Fundamentprüfungen. Die Registerkarte Gründung prüft Einzelfundamente, kombinierte Fundamente und Plattenfundamente auf dem Baugrund, den Sie beschreiben: Auflagerdruck, Gleiten und Kippen aus Gebrauchslasten; Biegung, Querkraft, Durchstanzen, Verankerung und Mindestbewehrung aus Bemessungslasten (ACI 318-19). Eine Stütze nahe einem Rand oder einer Ecke wird mit dem Querschnitt geprüft, der vom Rand des Fundaments abgeschnitten ist. Die Setzung ergibt sich aus dem Steifemodul des Bodens, ein biegsames Plattenfundament wird auf Federn mit einem Bettungsmodul gelöst, und Pfahlgruppen und Stützwände haben eigene Felder. Stützenlasten können eingegeben oder aus der Berechnung übernommen werden. Siehe Fundamentprüfungen. Karte der Bewehrungsdichte. Die Registerkarte Bewehrungsdichte zeichnet für jedes Fundament eine Wärmekarte aus der Draufsicht: wo die Stäbe zu wenig Platz lassen, damit der Beton nach unten gelangt, verglichen mit der Regel für den lichten Stababstand und, wenn Sie sie angeben, dem größten Zuschlagkorn und dem Rüttelkopf. Siehe Karte der Bewehrungsdichte. Rahmenbau und Lieferung Rahmen aus Holz und Leichtbauprofilen. Die Registerkarte Rahmenbau rahmt Wand-, Decken- und Dachtafeln mit jedem Einzelteil, einer Zuschnittsliste, dem zu kaufenden Material und einer maßstäblichen Werkstattzeichnung. Als Stäbe platzieren setzt die Teile ins Modell, und PDF-Blätter speichert ein Blatt für jede Tafel. Querschnitte, Abstände und Stürze legen Sie fest: DetailCraft prüft nicht, ob sie die Last tragen. Siehe Rahmen aus Holz und Leichtbauprofilen. Lagerbestand und Lieferzeiten. Die Registerkarte Lieferung liest die Lagerliste eines Lieferanten (CSV) und zeigt für jeden Querschnitt, den die Zuschnittsliste kauft, ob genug auf Lager ist und wie lange die Wartezeit dauert. Fehlmengen und verspätete Querschnitte stehen unter Probleme. Es wird nichts bestellt. Siehe Lagerbestand und Lieferzeiten. Berechnung und Bemessung Was versagt zuerst? Auf der Registerkarte Berechnung findet die Schaltfläche die maßgebende Lastkombination, das schwächste Bauteil, wie weit die Lasten wachsen können, bevor das erste Bauteil seine Grenze erreicht, und den schwächsten Kopfplatten- oder Querkraftanschluss, sofern die Scherfestigkeit seiner Schrauben bekannt ist. Dazu brauchen Sie zuerst die Kombinationen und eine Bemessungsnorm. Lochleibung, Blockscheren, Schweißnähte und Hebelwirkung werden nicht geprüft. Ihr angebundener Assistent kann dasselbe abfragen. Live. Setzen Sie den Haken bei Live: nach jeder Änderung neu lösen, dann wird der Rahmen nach jeder Änderung neu berechnet. Beton, weitere. Eine schlanke Stütze in einem unverschieblichen Rahmen wird für ihr vergrößertes Moment bemessen (ACI 318-19 6.6.4.5). Die seismische Periode kann nach der Methode von Rayleigh aus der Berechnung übernommen werden. Eine Platte aus Blechen erhält den Stahl, den jede Lage aus ihren Momenten braucht, sowie die einachsige Querkraft. Siehe Betonbauteile prüfen und seismische Lasten erzeugen. Zur Prüfung durch einen Ingenieur. Die Ergebnisse für Fundament, Beton und „Was versagt zuerst?“ sind Hilfsmittel. Windlasten sind nicht umgesetzt. In allen Apps Sprachen. Die Hilfe liegt in 54 Sprachen vor. Die Sprachen außer Englisch sind maschinelle Entwürfe, bis eine Muttersprachlerin oder ein Muttersprachler sie gelesen hat; zehn sind neu (Litauisch, Lettisch, Bosnisch, Marathi, Telugu, Albanisch, Afrikaans, Filipino, Swahili, Usbekisch). Symbolleistenzeilen. Einstellungen › Allgemein › Symbolleistenzeilen wählt Umbruch, 2 Zeilen oder 1 Zeile. Eine Leiste, die bereits passt, bleibt, wie sie ist; die übrigen lassen sich seitlich scrollen. Eigenschaftenraster. Die Felder des Fundaments verwenden das gemeinsame Raster: einklappbare Kategorien und eine kompakte Dichte. Eingegebene Rechnungen wie 2*450 funktionieren weiterhin. Eingabegeräte. Trackpad, Stift, 3D-Maus, Controller und MIDI können die Apps steuern. Siehe In 3D navigieren. Probleme-Bereich. Die Warnungen der oben genannten neuen Prüfungen erscheinen im selben Bereich Probleme wie bisher. KI-Assistent anbinden. Ihr eigener Assistent, angebunden über CadCraft Studio, arbeitet mit allen acht Apps und sieht nur, was Sie freigeben. Das Anbinden per Klick, der gehostete Connector und Ihr eigener API-Schlüssel in Studio folgen noch. Siehe DetailCraft mit einem KI-Assistenten verwenden. In 0.8.1 behoben Sprachen in Studio. Studio und die gepackten Ausgaben können die Sprache zur Laufzeit wechseln; bisher gab es nur Englisch. Es ist noch nicht auf allen Plattformen getestet: Sagen Sie uns Bescheid, falls sich die Sprache nicht ändert. Auswahl mit Kreuzen-Fenster. Die Installationsprogramme von 0.8.1 enthalten das Auswählen per Fenster bereits in der richtigen Form, wie in der Web-Version von 0.8; ein älteres Installationsprogramm tat dies nicht. Tablets im Hochformat. Freie Rasterhöhe geht an die Zeichenfläche, und das Blattlayout beginnt bei 760 px oder weniger. Noch nicht in dieser Version NC an eine Maschine senden ist nicht freigegeben. Windlasten für Beton sind nicht umgesetzt, und die Beton- und Fundamentprüfungen sind für einen Ingenieur zur Prüfung bestimmt. Codesignierung. Die Windows-Installationsprogramme und die macOS-.dmg sind nicht signiert; Windows warnt daher möglicherweise, und macOS verlangt einen Rechtsklick, dann „Öffnen“. Frühere Änderungen stehen unter Neuerungen in 0.7.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"whats-new-0-7","scope":"detailcraft","title":"What's new in 0.7 (earlier)","summary":"Design checks of steel I-shapes with load combinations from ASCE 7 or EN 1990 and member checks to AISC 360 or EN 1993-1-1, USD export of the steel model, an assistant tool for USD, a Steel ribbon with large and small buttons, panels that tidy away and dialogs that move.","section":"start","order":6,"keywords":["whats new 0.7","earlier release","release notes","changelog","changes","0.7","version","new features","design checks","steel design","utilisation","utilization","load combinations","ASCE 7","EN 1990","AISC 360","EN 1993-1-1","Eurocode","National Annex","roof live","deflection limit","usd","usda","usdc","usdz","openusd","assistant","mcp","steel ribbon","ribbon","split button","units","compact","panels","dialogs","portuguese"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"mitre-joints-0-7-1","text":"Mitre joints (0.7.1)","level":3},{"id":"design-checks","text":"Design checks","level":3},{"id":"usd","text":"USD","level":3},{"id":"ai-assistants","text":"AI assistants","level":3},{"id":"changed","text":"Changed","level":2},{"id":"in-every-app","text":"In every app","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in DetailCraft 0.7, compared with 0.6. The frame analysis of 0.6 now has a second step: checking the steel members to a design code.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mitre-joints-0-7-1\"\u003EMitre joints (0.7.1)\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo I-shapes meeting at an angle\u003C/strong\u003E, such as a portal knee or a ridge: select both, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMitre\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, and both ends are cut on the plane that halves the angle. End plates bolted together and web stiffeners are tick boxes in the new Mitre dialog, whose numbered fields match a drawing of your joint made as you type. The mitre follows the members when they move, and carries through to drawings, NC files (skew cuts), IFC and the bill of materials. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/mitres\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/mitres\"\u003EMitre joints\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"design-checks\"\u003EDesign checks\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoad combinations from the code.\u003C/strong\u003E In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab, the new \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesign checks\u003C/span\u003E block has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesign code\u003C/span\u003E: ASCE 7 + AISC 360 (US, LRFD) or EN 1990 + EN 1993-1-1 (Eurocode). Give each load case its kind (dead, live, the new roof live, snow, wind, seismic), press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake combinations\u003C/span\u003E, and DetailCraft writes the code&#039;s strength and serviceability combinations of your cases. The combinations you typed yourself stay. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks#make-the-combinations\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\" data-hash=\"make-the-combinations\"\u003EMake the combinations\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENational Annex values.\u003C/strong\u003E For the Eurocode the factors start at the recommended values; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENational Annex values\u003C/span\u003E lets you type your country&#039;s, and they are saved in the model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks#make-the-combinations\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\" data-hash=\"make-the-combinations\"\u003EMake the combinations\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck members.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck members\u003C/span\u003E checks every steel I-shape for tension, compression with buckling, bending with lateral-torsional buckling, shear, bending with axial force and deflection against the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeflection limit\u003C/span\u003E you set. The table shows each member&#039;s utilisation, the check that governs and its combination, and the members are coloured on the model (blue, green, ochre, red, grey for not checked). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks#check-the-members\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\" data-hash=\"check-the-members\"\u003ECheck the members\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn the report.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E add the code editions, the settings, utilisation by member and every clause used. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks#the-report\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\" data-hash=\"the-report\"\u003EThe report\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn aid until an engineer has reviewed it.\u003C/strong\u003E It uses first-order forces only. Tubes, angles, channels, welded shapes and slender or class 4 parts read &quot;not checked&quot;, with the reason; torsion, connections and concrete are not checked. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks#what-is-not-checked\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\" data-hash=\"what-is-not-checked\"\u003EWhat is not checked\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"usd\"\u003EUSD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport the steel model as USD.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group writes the model as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText (.usda)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBinary (.usdc)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPackage (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E for rendering programs and scene assembly: each assembly holds its parts, each part is its section run along its length with copes cut, and each carries its part mark, profile, grade, length and weight. Bolt holes, bolts, welds and footings are not in the USD meshes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/usd\"\u003EExport the steel model as USD\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ai-assistants\"\u003EAI assistants\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUSD for assistants.\u003C/strong\u003E Your own assistant, connected through CadCraft Studio, can export the model as USD (\u003Ccode\u003Eusd_export\u003C/code\u003E), and in Studio write it to a folder you allowed. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/use-with-ai-assistants#what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-hash=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"changed\"\u003EChanged\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe Steel ribbon\u003C/strong\u003E has large and small buttons: a large \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam\u003C/span\u003E beside small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrace\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeling\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMembers\u003C/span\u003E; a large \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd plates\u003C/span\u003E on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailing\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnections\u003C/span\u003E, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplice\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGusset\u003C/span\u003E sharing one split button; a large \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClipboard\u003C/span\u003E, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E behind the group&#039;s arrow. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFabrication\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExchange\u003C/span\u003E has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport USD\u003C/span\u003E beside IFC. The other menu styles look as they did. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse#the-three-styles\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-hash=\"the-three-styles\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/frame-analysis\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/frame-analysis\"\u003EAnalyse the frame\u003C/a\u003E now points to the design checks, and its combinations can be made by the code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-every-app\"\u003EIn every app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E has one list each for length, area, volume, angle and slope, with the exact sizes the units&#039; definitions give (the US survey foot is its own unit). Switch off the units you never use and they leave the unit lists; star one and new work starts in it. A file always keeps its own unit. DetailCraft&#039;s own boxes don&#039;t follow the lists yet: its lengths are still typed in millimetres. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EUnits, typed values and the unit lists\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA denser layout and panels that tidy away.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout density\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompact\u003C/span\u003E fits more on the screen (touch screens and phones keep their large buttons). Each side panel has a slim title bar: drag it to dock or float the panel, drop it on another panel&#039;s bar to share one frame as tabs, pin it to slide away to the edge, or hide it and bring it back from the edge. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#make-the-layout-denser\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"make-the-layout-denser\"\u003EArrange the panels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDialogs move.\u003C/strong\u003E Drag a dialog by its title bar out of the way of the drawing; it comes back where you left it. Double-click the title bar to centre it again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#move-a-dialog\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003EMove a dialog\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecipes for AI assistants.\u003C/strong\u003E Ten worked requests for your own assistant (tidy a drawing, plot a set, check drawings, ask a model questions, total a takeoff, try a part variant…), each with the tools it calls and the permissions it needs, and each replayed by our tests. The apps contain no AI. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELanguages.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Portugal)\u003C/strong\u003E is new in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E. It, \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Brasil)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEspañol (México)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E now have every word of the apps, as drafts not yet reviewed by a native speaker. Help topics are in English.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E says why an update check failed (no file for your system yet, no network, a bad signature) instead of just &quot;Done&quot;, and stops bringing back a What&#039;s new card you closed until there is a newer release.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E0.7 is not code-signed.\u003C/strong\u003E Signing, and the Microsoft Store package, moved to 0.8 because the certificate validation has not come through yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore info\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun anyway\u003C/span\u003E. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E. CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet, and there is no iPad or iPhone build yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe angle and slope lists in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E are saved, but no box reads them yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe design checks have not been reviewed by an engineer yet.\u003C/strong\u003E Our tests compare them with printed table values and formulas worked by hand, not yet with a design guide&#039;s worked examples. Until a review, they are an aid: check members that matter by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe design checks use first-order forces (no second-order effects) and check I-shapes only. Not built: tubes, angles, channels, welded shapes, slender (AISC) or class 4 (Eurocode) parts in bending, torsion, shear buckling together with bending, connections, fatigue, fire, seismic detailing and concrete. A slender part in compression leaves the compression checks out, and the member reads &quot;passes, partly checked&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFrame analysis is still first-order and linear, and it hasn&#039;t been tried by an engineer on a real job yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBolt holes, bolts, welds and footings are not in the USD export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything else is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"This is what changed in DetailCraft 0.7, compared with 0.6. The frame analysis of 0.6 now has a second step: checking the steel members to a design code. New Mitre joints (0.7.1) Two I-shapes meeting at an angle, such as a portal knee or a ridge: select both, choose Mitre in the Connect group's More menu, and both ends are cut on the plane that halves the angle. End plates bolted together and web stiffeners are tick boxes in the new Mitre dialog, whose numbered fields match a drawing of your joint made as you type. The mitre follows the members when they move, and carries through to drawings, NC files (skew cuts), IFC and the bill of materials. See Mitre joints. Design checks Load combinations from the code. In the Analysis tab, the new Design checks block has a Design code: ASCE 7 + AISC 360 (US, LRFD) or EN 1990 + EN 1993-1-1 (Eurocode). Give each load case its kind (dead, live, the new roof live, snow, wind, seismic), press Make combinations, and DetailCraft writes the code's strength and serviceability combinations of your cases. The combinations you typed yourself stay. See Make the combinations. National Annex values. For the Eurocode the factors start at the recommended values; National Annex values lets you type your country's, and they are saved in the model. See Make the combinations. Check members. Check members checks every steel I-shape for tension, compression with buckling, bending with lateral-torsional buckling, shear, bending with axial force and deflection against the Deflection limit you set. The table shows each member's utilisation, the check that governs and its combination, and the members are coloured on the model (blue, green, ochre, red, grey for not checked). See Check the members. In the report. Report (PDF) and Report (CSV) add the code editions, the settings, utilisation by member and every clause used. See The report. An aid until an engineer has reviewed it. It uses first-order forces only. Tubes, angles, channels, welded shapes and slender or class 4 parts read \"not checked\", with the reason; torsion, connections and concrete are not checked. See What is not checked. USD Export the steel model as USD. USD in the Output group writes the model as Text (.usda), Binary (.usdc) or Package (.usdz) for rendering programs and scene assembly: each assembly holds its parts, each part is its section run along its length with copes cut, and each carries its part mark, profile, grade, length and weight. Bolt holes, bolts, welds and footings are not in the USD meshes. See Export the steel model as USD. AI assistants USD for assistants. Your own assistant, connected through CadCraft Studio, can export the model as USD (usd_export), and in Studio write it to a folder you allowed. See What an assistant can ask for. Changed The Steel ribbon has large and small buttons: a large Beam beside small Column, Brace and Plate on Modeling › Members; a large End plates on Detailing › Connections, with Splice and Gusset sharing one split button; a large Paste on Edit › Clipboard, with Paste in place and Duplicate behind the group's arrow. Fabrication › Exchange has Export USD beside IFC. The other menu styles look as they did. See Menus, keys and mouse. Analyse the frame now points to the design checks, and its combinations can be made by the code. In every app Units. Settings › General › Units has one list each for length, area, volume, angle and slope, with the exact sizes the units' definitions give (the US survey foot is its own unit). Switch off the units you never use and they leave the unit lists; star one and new work starts in it. A file always keeps its own unit. DetailCraft's own boxes don't follow the lists yet: its lengths are still typed in millimetres. See Units, typed values and the unit lists. A denser layout and panels that tidy away. Settings › General › Layout density › Compact fits more on the screen (touch screens and phones keep their large buttons). Each side panel has a slim title bar: drag it to dock or float the panel, drop it on another panel's bar to share one frame as tabs, pin it to slide away to the edge, or hide it and bring it back from the edge. See Arrange the panels. Dialogs move. Drag a dialog by its title bar out of the way of the drawing; it comes back where you left it. Double-click the title bar to centre it again. See Move a dialog. Recipes for AI assistants. Ten worked requests for your own assistant (tidy a drawing, plot a set, check drawings, ask a model questions, total a takeoff, try a part variant…), each with the tools it calls and the permissions it needs, and each replayed by our tests. The apps contain no AI. See Recipes for AI assistants. Languages. Português (Portugal) is new in Settings › General › Language. It, Português (Brasil), Español (México) and Bahasa Indonesia now have every word of the apps, as drafts not yet reviewed by a native speaker. Help topics are in English. CadCraft Studio says why an update check failed (no file for your system yet, no network, a bad signature) instead of just \"Done\", and stops bringing back a What's new card you closed until there is a newer release. Not in this version yet 0.7 is not code-signed. Signing, and the Microsoft Store package, moved to 0.8 because the certificate validation has not come through yet. Check the SHA-256 beside each file on getcadcraft.com/download. On Windows, if SmartScreen stops the installer, choose More info, then Run anyway. On a Mac, the first time, right-click CadCraft Studio and choose Open. CadCraft Studio is not in an app store yet, and there is no iPad or iPhone build yet. The angle and slope lists in Units are saved, but no box reads them yet. The design checks have not been reviewed by an engineer yet. Our tests compare them with printed table values and formulas worked by hand, not yet with a design guide's worked examples. Until a review, they are an aid: check members that matter by hand. The design checks use first-order forces (no second-order effects) and check I-shapes only. Not built: tubes, angles, channels, welded shapes, slender (AISC) or class 4 (Eurocode) parts in bending, torsion, shear buckling together with bending, connections, fatigue, fire, seismic detailing and concrete. A slender part in compression leaves the compression checks out, and the member reads \"passes, partly checked\". Frame analysis is still first-order and linear, and it hasn't been tried by an engineer on a real job yet. Bolt holes, bolts, welds and footings are not in the USD export. Everything else is in What is not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Erste Schritte mit DetailCraft","summary":"Was DetailCraft heute kann, wie Sie es öffnen und ein erstes Modell von den Rastern bis zu einer Werkstattzeichnung in zehn Minuten erstellen.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["Erste Schritte","Einführung","erstes Modell","Stahlbau","Bewehrung","Schnellstart","Lernprogramm","Überblick"],"headings":[{"id":"was-es-heute-kann","text":"Was es heute kann","level":2},{"id":"detailcraft-ffnen","text":"DetailCraft öffnen","level":2},{"id":"zuerst-das-beispiel-ansehen","text":"Zuerst das Beispiel ansehen","level":2},{"id":"ihr-erstes-modell","text":"Ihr erstes Modell","level":2},{"id":"wie-es-weitergeht","text":"Wie es weitergeht","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft ist die App zur Detaillierung von Stahl und Bewehrung in der CadCraft-Familie. Sie bauen ein 3D-Modell eines Stahlrahmens auf Rastern und Ebenen, verbinden es und erhalten, was die Werkstatt braucht: Positions- und Montagemarken, eine Stückliste, Werkstattzeichnungen einzelner Teile, DSTV-NC-Dateien, ein IFC-Modell und eine Biegeliste für die Fundamente.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDies ist eine frühe Version. Sie erledigt eine kurze Liste von Aufgaben sorgfältig, statt alles auf einmal anzugehen. Lesen Sie \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWas noch nicht umgesetzt ist\u003C/a\u003E, bevor Sie echte Arbeit darauf planen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-es-heute-kann\"\u003EWas es heute kann\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERaster und Ebenen, die Sie in Millimetern eingeben.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStützen, Träger, Aussteifungen und Bleche aus veröffentlichten Profilkatalogen (AISC W, S, C, L, HSS und Rohr; europäische IPE, HEA, HEB und UPN; britische UB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS und CHS), dazu Ihre eigenen parametrischen Profile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZwei Anschlüsse: eine verschraubte Kopfplatte von einem Träger an einen Stützenflansch und eine Stützenfußplatte mit vier Ankerstangen. Jeder kann auf jede ähnliche Verbindung kopiert werden.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBohrungen und Ausklinkungen, nach Position eingegeben.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERechteckige Einzelfundamente mit unterer Matte und Anschlussbewehrung.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPositions- und Montagemarken, die sich nach jeder Änderung selbst aktualisieren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExporte: IFC4, DSTV-NC-Dateien (I-Profile und Bleche), Stückliste als CSV, Biegeliste als CSV und eine Werkstattzeichnung als PDF für ein Teil.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"detailcraft-ffnen\"\u003EDetailCraft öffnen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÖffnen Sie DetailCraft in einem Desktop-Browser: Chrome, Edge, Firefox oder Safari. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowser und Computer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWarten Sie, bis der Bildschirm sich füllt. Die Statusleiste unten zeigt „Bereit. Beispiel öffnen oder Raster und Ebenen festlegen und Bauteile platzieren.“\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Modellansicht braucht 3D-Grafik (WebGL). Wenn in der Mitte des Bildschirms „Dieser Browser konnte keine 3D-Grafik (WebGL) starten“ steht, lesen Sie \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/troubleshooting#the-3d-view-is-missing\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"the-3d-view-is-missing\"\u003EFehlerbehebung\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIhre Modelle bleiben auf Ihrem Computer. Beim Öffnen eines Modells wird es im Browser gelesen, und beim Speichern wird eine Datei heruntergeladen. Nichts wird hochgeladen. Cloud-Speicherung ist noch nicht verfügbar. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWo Ihre Dateien liegen\u003C/a\u003E. Um DetailCraft später ohne Verbindung zu verwenden, wählen Sie einmal während Sie online sind in der App-Leiste \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFür Offline speichern\u003C/span\u003E; siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EOffline arbeiten\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESie sind offline. DetailCraft funktioniert ohne Verbindung nur, wenn es zuvor für die Offline-Nutzung gespeichert wurde: Die Schaltfläche \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFür Offline speichern\u003C/span\u003E in der App-Leiste zeigt dann \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline bereit\u003C/span\u003E. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EOffline arbeiten\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"zuerst-das-beispiel-ansehen\"\u003EZuerst das Beispiel ansehen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAm schnellsten sehen Sie, was DetailCraft erstellt, am Beispielrahmen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeispiel\u003C/span\u003E in der Gruppe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatei\u003C/span\u003E der Symbolleiste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Ansicht zeigt einen Rahmen mit zwei Feldern: sechs Stützen mit Fußplatten, vier Träger mit Kopfplatten, eine Aussteifung und sieben Fundamente mit Bewehrung.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlicken Sie auf einen Träger. Der Bereich \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenschaften\u003C/span\u003E rechts zeigt seine Positionsnummer, die Schnittlänge, das Gewicht und die Bohrungen, die die Kopfplatten erzeugt haben.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EÖffnen Sie die Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStückliste\u003C/span\u003E unten, um jede Positionsnummer mit Anzahl und Gewicht zu sehen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Anleitung \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/sample-model\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/sample-model\"\u003EDen Beispielrahmen erkunden\u003C/a\u003E geht ihn Stück für Stück durch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ihr-erstes-modell\"\u003EIhr erstes Modell\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEin neues Modell hat bereits die Raster 1 bis 3 und A bis B mit 6000 mm Abstand sowie zwei Ebenen: Ebene 1 bei 0 und Ebene 2 bei 4000.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENeu\u003C/span\u003E. Wenn das aktuelle Modell Änderungen hat, fragt DetailCraft, ob sie zuerst gespeichert werden sollen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrundriss\u003C/span\u003E über der Ansicht. Die Ebenenliste daneben wählt Ebene 1.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStütze\u003C/span\u003E in der Symbolleiste oder drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlicken Sie auf den Rasterschnittpunkt A/1. Eine Stütze W12x50 erscheint von Ebene 1 bis Ebene 2. Die Statusleiste zeigt, wohin sie gesetzt wurde.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlicken Sie auf B/1, A/2 und B/2, um drei weitere zu platzieren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie Ebene 2 in der Ebenenliste und dann \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETräger\u003C/span\u003E oder drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlicken Sie auf A/1 und dann auf A/2. Ein Träger W16x26 verbindet die beiden Stützenköpfe, seine Stahloberkante liegt auf der Ebene.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, um zu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWählen\u003C/span\u003E zurückzukehren, und klicken Sie dann auf den Träger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopfplatten\u003C/span\u003E in der Gruppe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnschlüsse\u003C/span\u003E. Beide Enden erhalten eine verschraubte Kopfplatte, der Träger wird passend gekürzt, und die erste Kopfplatte ist nun ausgewählt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKlicken Sie erneut auf den Träger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWerkstattzeichnung\u003C/span\u003E in der Gruppe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAusgabe\u003C/span\u003E. Eine Vorschau der Werkstattzeichnung des Trägers öffnet sich. Wählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF herunterladen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, um das Modell zu speichern. Der Browser lädt \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E herunter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBenennen Sie das Modell im Feld \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E oben links um, bevor Sie speichern, damit Datei und jeder Export seinen Namen tragen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wie-es-weitergeht\"\u003EWie es weitergeht\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/workspace\"\u003EEin Rundgang durch den Bildschirm\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/grids-and-levels\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/grids-and-levels\"\u003ERaster und Ebenen einrichten\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/navigate-3d\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/navigate-3d\"\u003EIn der 3D-Ansicht navigieren\u003C/a\u003E, mit Maus, Trackpad oder Touchscreen\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/coming-from\"\u003EUmstieg von anderer Detaillierungssoftware\u003C/a\u003E, wenn Sie ein großes Detaillierungspaket kennen\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/help\" data-topic=\"basics/help\"\u003EDiese Hilfe verwenden\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"DetailCraft ist die App zur Detaillierung von Stahl und Bewehrung in der CadCraft-Familie. Sie bauen ein 3D-Modell eines Stahlrahmens auf Rastern und Ebenen, verbinden es und erhalten, was die Werkstatt braucht: Positions- und Montagemarken, eine Stückliste, Werkstattzeichnungen einzelner Teile, DSTV-NC-Dateien, ein IFC-Modell und eine Biegeliste für die Fundamente. Dies ist eine frühe Version. Sie erledigt eine kurze Liste von Aufgaben sorgfältig, statt alles auf einmal anzugehen. Lesen Sie Was noch nicht umgesetzt ist, bevor Sie echte Arbeit darauf planen. Was es heute kann Raster und Ebenen, die Sie in Millimetern eingeben. Stützen, Träger, Aussteifungen und Bleche aus veröffentlichten Profilkatalogen (AISC W, S, C, L, HSS und Rohr; europäische IPE, HEA, HEB und UPN; britische UB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS und CHS), dazu Ihre eigenen parametrischen Profile. Zwei Anschlüsse: eine verschraubte Kopfplatte von einem Träger an einen Stützenflansch und eine Stützenfußplatte mit vier Ankerstangen. Jeder kann auf jede ähnliche Verbindung kopiert werden. Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen, nach Position eingegeben. Rechteckige Einzelfundamente mit unterer Matte und Anschlussbewehrung. Positions- und Montagemarken, die sich nach jeder Änderung selbst aktualisieren. Exporte: IFC4, DSTV-NC-Dateien (I-Profile und Bleche), Stückliste als CSV, Biegeliste als CSV und eine Werkstattzeichnung als PDF für ein Teil. DetailCraft öffnen Öffnen Sie DetailCraft in einem Desktop-Browser: Chrome, Edge, Firefox oder Safari. Siehe Browser und Computer. Warten Sie, bis der Bildschirm sich füllt. Die Statusleiste unten zeigt „Bereit. Beispiel öffnen oder Raster und Ebenen festlegen und Bauteile platzieren.“ Die Modellansicht braucht 3D-Grafik (WebGL). Wenn in der Mitte des Bildschirms „Dieser Browser konnte keine 3D-Grafik (WebGL) starten“ steht, lesen Sie Fehlerbehebung. Ihre Modelle bleiben auf Ihrem Computer. Beim Öffnen eines Modells wird es im Browser gelesen, und beim Speichern wird eine Datei heruntergeladen. Nichts wird hochgeladen. Cloud-Speicherung ist noch nicht verfügbar. Siehe Wo Ihre Dateien liegen. Um DetailCraft später ohne Verbindung zu verwenden, wählen Sie einmal während Sie online sind in der App-Leiste Für Offline speichern; siehe Offline arbeiten. Sie sind offline. DetailCraft funktioniert ohne Verbindung nur, wenn es zuvor für die Offline-Nutzung gespeichert wurde: Die Schaltfläche Für Offline speichern in der App-Leiste zeigt dann Offline bereit. Siehe Offline arbeiten. Zuerst das Beispiel ansehen Am schnellsten sehen Sie, was DetailCraft erstellt, am Beispielrahmen. Wählen Sie Beispiel in der Gruppe Datei der Symbolleiste. Die Ansicht zeigt einen Rahmen mit zwei Feldern: sechs Stützen mit Fußplatten, vier Träger mit Kopfplatten, eine Aussteifung und sieben Fundamente mit Bewehrung. Klicken Sie auf einen Träger. Der Bereich Eigenschaften rechts zeigt seine Positionsnummer, die Schnittlänge, das Gewicht und die Bohrungen, die die Kopfplatten erzeugt haben. Öffnen Sie die Registerkarte Stückliste unten, um jede Positionsnummer mit Anzahl und Gewicht zu sehen. Die Anleitung Den Beispielrahmen erkunden geht ihn Stück für Stück durch. Ihr erstes Modell Ein neues Modell hat bereits die Raster 1 bis 3 und A bis B mit 6000 mm Abstand sowie zwei Ebenen: Ebene 1 bei 0 und Ebene 2 bei 4000. Wählen Sie Neu. Wenn das aktuelle Modell Änderungen hat, fragt DetailCraft, ob sie zuerst gespeichert werden sollen. Wählen Sie Grundriss über der Ansicht. Die Ebenenliste daneben wählt Ebene 1. Wählen Sie Stütze in der Symbolleiste oder drücken Sie C. Klicken Sie auf den Rasterschnittpunkt A/1. Eine Stütze W12x50 erscheint von Ebene 1 bis Ebene 2. Die Statusleiste zeigt, wohin sie gesetzt wurde. Klicken Sie auf B/1, A/2 und B/2, um drei weitere zu platzieren. Wählen Sie Ebene 2 in der Ebenenliste und dann Träger oder drücken Sie B. Klicken Sie auf A/1 und dann auf A/2. Ein Träger W16x26 verbindet die beiden Stützenköpfe, seine Stahloberkante liegt auf der Ebene. Drücken Sie Esc, um zu Wählen zurückzukehren, und klicken Sie dann auf den Träger. Wählen Sie Kopfplatten in der Gruppe Anschlüsse. Beide Enden erhalten eine verschraubte Kopfplatte, der Träger wird passend gekürzt, und die erste Kopfplatte ist nun ausgewählt. Klicken Sie erneut auf den Träger. Wählen Sie Werkstattzeichnung in der Gruppe Ausgabe. Eine Vorschau der Werkstattzeichnung des Trägers öffnet sich. Wählen Sie PDF herunterladen. Drücken Sie Ctrl+S, um das Modell zu speichern. Der Browser lädt Untitled.detailcraft.json herunter. Benennen Sie das Modell im Feld Name oben links um, bevor Sie speichern, damit Datei und jeder Export seinen Namen tragen. Wie es weitergeht Ein Rundgang durch den Bildschirm Raster und Ebenen einrichten In der 3D-Ansicht navigieren, mit Maus, Trackpad oder Touchscreen Umstieg von anderer Detaillierungssoftware, wenn Sie ein großes Detaillierungspaket kennen Diese Hilfe verwenden","lang":"de"},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Ein Rundgang durch den Bildschirm","summary":"Jede Leiste, jeder Bereich und jede Registerkarte in DetailCraft unter ihrem Bildschirmnamen und mit ihrem Zweck.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["Bildschirm","Layout","Symbolleiste","Bereiche","Eigenschaften","Statusleiste","Berichte","Oberfläche","UI","Registerkarten"],"headings":[{"id":"die-app-leiste","text":"Die App-Leiste","level":2},{"id":"die-symbolleiste","text":"Die Symbolleiste","level":2},{"id":"der-bereich-modell-links","text":"Der Bereich Modell (links)","level":2},{"id":"die-ansicht-mitte","text":"Die Ansicht (Mitte)","level":2},{"id":"der-bereich-eigenschaften-rechts","text":"Der Bereich Eigenschaften (rechts)","level":2},{"id":"die-berichtsregisterkarten-unten","text":"Die Berichtsregisterkarten (unten)","level":2},{"id":"die-statusleiste","text":"Die Statusleiste","level":2},{"id":"auf-einem-schmalen-bildschirm","text":"Auf einem schmalen Bildschirm","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft füllt das Browserfenster unterhalb der App-Leiste aus. Von oben nach unten: die Symbolleiste, dann drei Spalten (der Bereich \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E, die Ansicht, der Bereich \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenschaften\u003C/span\u003E), dann die Berichtsregisterkarten und schließlich die Statusleiste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-app-leiste\"\u003EDie App-Leiste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie schmale Leiste ganz oben gehört zur CadCraft-Website, nicht zum Modell. Sie zeigt den App-Namen und die Version, einen Link zu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÜber DetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHilfe\u003C/span\u003E (oder \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) für diese Hilfe, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFür Offline speichern\u003C/span\u003E, die Schaltfläche für den Bildschirmmodus und den Umschalter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapier\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFür Offline speichern\u003C/span\u003E bewahrt DetailCraft, seine 3D-Ansicht und diese Hilfe in Ihrem Browser auf, sodass es ohne Verbindung öffnet. Nach dem Speichern zeigt sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline bereit\u003C/span\u003E; klicken Sie dann darauf, um \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EErneut speichern (neueste Version holen)\u003C/span\u003E oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline-Kopie entfernen\u003C/span\u003E zu wählen. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EOffline arbeiten\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPapier\u003C/span\u003E ergibt einen hellen Bildschirm und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E einen dunklen. Die 3D-Ansicht folgt sofort; an Ihrem Modell ändert sich nichts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Schaltfläche für den Bildschirmmodus und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E wechseln durch normal, Vollbild und Vollbild mit ausgeblendeten Leisten. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E verwendet auf jedem Computer die Taste Ctrl, auch auf dem Mac. Bei ausgeblendeten Leisten zeigen Sie auf den oberen Bildschirmrand (oder tippen darauf), um sie zurückzuholen. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E beendet das Vollbild.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-symbolleiste\"\u003EDie Symbolleiste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Symbolleiste hat fünf Gruppen. Fahren Sie mit der Maus über eine Schaltfläche, um zu sehen, was sie tut und welche Taste dazu gehört.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGruppe\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESchaltflächen\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWas sie tun\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatei\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENeu\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÖffnen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC importieren\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeichern\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeispiel\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEin Modell beginnen, eine gespeicherte \u003Ccode\u003E.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E oder eine IFC-Datei öffnen, eines speichern oder den Beispielrahmen öffnen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlatzieren\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWählen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETräger\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStütze\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAussteifung\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlech\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFundament\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETreppe\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDas Werkzeug, das der nächste Klick in der Ansicht verwendet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnschlüsse\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopfplatten\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFußplatte\u003C/span\u003E und im Menü \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMehr\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnschlusslaschen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoß\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKnotenblech\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÄhnlich\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEinen Anschluss zum gewählten Träger, zur Stütze oder Aussteifung hinzufügen oder den gewählten Anschluss auf jeden gleichartigen Knoten kopieren\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBearbeiten\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERückgängig\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWiederholen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopieren\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinfügen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMehr\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBearbeitungen rückwärts und vorwärts durchgehen; die Auswahl kopieren und einfügen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMehr\u003C/span\u003E öffnet ein Menü mit \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAusschneiden\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopieren\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinfügen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAn gleicher Position einfügen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplizieren\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELöschen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlles auswählen\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuswahl aufheben\u003C/span\u003E, jeweils mit ihrer Taste\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAusgabe\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENC-Dateien\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStückliste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWerkstattzeichnung\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZeichnungen\u003C/span\u003E und im Menü \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMehr\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBiegeliste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGewichtsbericht nach Phase\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKISS-Datei für Fertigungssoftware\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEinen Export herunterladen oder die Zeichnungsblätter öffnen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESchaltflächen und Menüpunkte, die gerade nicht funktionieren können, sind ausgegraut, und wenn Sie mit der Maus darüber fahren, steht dort der Grund. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopfplatten\u003C/span\u003E braucht einen gewählten Träger, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFußplatte\u003C/span\u003E eine gewählte Stütze, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÄhnlich\u003C/span\u003E einen gewählten Anschluss, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWerkstattzeichnung\u003C/span\u003E ein Modell mit Teilen, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopieren\u003C/span\u003E ein gewähltes Bauteil oder Fundament und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinfügen\u003C/span\u003E etwas Kopiertes. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/copy-paste\"\u003EKopieren, Einfügen und Duplizieren\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"der-bereich-modell-links\"\u003EDer Bereich Modell (links)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E: der Modell-\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E. Er benennt jede Datei, die Sie speichern oder exportieren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERaster\u003C/span\u003E: die Rasterlinien und ihre Abstände sowie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERaster übernehmen\u003C/span\u003E. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/grids-and-levels\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/grids-and-levels\"\u003ERaster und Ebenen einrichten\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEbenen\u003C/span\u003E: eine Zeile je Ebene (Name und Höhe in mm), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEbene hinzufügen\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEbenen übernehmen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenes Profil\u003C/span\u003E: Erstellen Sie Ihren eigenen parametrischen Querschnitt. Geschlossen, bis Sie es öffnen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPositionsnummern\u003C/span\u003E: jede Positionsnummer im Modell mit ihrem Profil, ihrer Länge und Anzahl. Klicken Sie auf eine Marke, um das erste Teil mit dieser Marke zu wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERaster\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEbenen\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenes Profil\u003C/span\u003E klappen sich ein: Klicken Sie auf ihre Überschriften.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-ansicht-mitte\"\u003EDie Ansicht (Mitte)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Ansicht zeigt das Modell in 3D. Darüber hat die Ansichtsleiste drei Schaltflächen und eine Liste:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E: das Modell frei drehen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrundriss\u003C/span\u003E: senkrecht auf eine Ebene blicken. Neben den Schaltflächen erscheint eine Liste der Ebenen. Alles, was mehr als 1,5 m über der Ebene liegt, wird ausgeblendet, damit Sie die Ebene deutlich sehen und anklicken können.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnsicht\u003C/span\u003E: senkrecht auf eine beschriftete Rasterlinie blicken (Raster A, Raster B …). Eine Liste der Buchstabenraster erscheint. Es wird nur der Stahl innerhalb von 1 m zu dieser Linie gezeigt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Einpassen-Schaltfläche am rechten Ende der Ansichtsleiste zoomt, damit das ganze Modell zu sehen ist.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKleine Punkte markieren jeden Rasterschnittpunkt auf jeder Ebene. Solange ein Platzierwerkzeug aktiv ist, leuchtet der Punkt, der dem Zeiger am nächsten ist, auf, und die Statusleiste nennt ihn, zum Beispiel „A/2 bei Ebene 1“. Farben unterscheiden die Bauteilarten: Stützen, Träger, Aussteifungen und Bleche haben jeweils eine eigene Farbe, Schrauben und Stäbe eine weitere, Fundamente sind durchsichtig und Bewehrung wird als Röhren gezeichnet. Bohrungen erscheinen als dunkle Stopfen; Ausklinkungen sind ausgeschnitten.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWie Sie drehen, verschieben und zoomen: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/navigate-3d\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/navigate-3d\"\u003EIn der 3D-Ansicht navigieren\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWenn das Modell leer ist, zeigt die Ansicht eine kurze Liste „Ein Modell beginnen“. Sie können auch eine gespeicherte Modelldatei irgendwo auf der Ansicht ablegen, um sie zu öffnen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"der-bereich-eigenschaften-rechts\"\u003EDer Bereich Eigenschaften (rechts)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWas dieser Bereich zeigt, hängt davon ab, was Sie gerade tun, und seine Überschrift sagt es: „Modell“, „Träger platzieren“, „Träger b2“, „Kopfplattenanschluss“, „Fußplatte“, „Fundament F1“, „3 ausgewählt“ oder „Einfügen“.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENichts gewählt:\u003C/strong\u003E eine Zusammenfassung des Modells (Bauteile, Anschlüsse, Fundamente, Teile, Marken, Schrauben, Gewicht von Stahl und Bewehrung, Raster, Ebenen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEin Platzierwerkzeug aktiv:\u003C/strong\u003E die Optionen für das nächste Teil (Profil, Material, Position, Drehung und für Stützen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBis\u003C/span\u003E), eine Zusammenfassung des gewählten Profils und ein Formular \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENach Raster hinzufügen\u003C/span\u003E, um ohne Klick in der Ansicht zu platzieren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEin Bauteil gewählt:\u003C/strong\u003E sein Name, Profil, Material, Position, Drehung, die Koordinaten seiner beiden Enden, seine Marken, Schnittlänge und Gewicht, seine Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen sowie Schaltflächen für Anschlüsse, seine Werkstattzeichnung und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELöschen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEin Anschluss gewählt:\u003C/strong\u003E jede Einstellung für Platten, Schrauben und Schweißnähte, die Schrauben und Schweißnaht, die er bildet, seine Warnungen und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuf ähnliche übernehmen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEin Fundament gewählt:\u003C/strong\u003E seine Position und Größe, die untere Matte, die Anschlussbewehrung und die Stabmarken, die es verwendet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMehrere Dinge gewählt:\u003C/strong\u003E wie viele Bauteile jeder Art, Anschlüsse und Fundamente, ihr Stahlgewicht sowie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKopieren\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplizieren\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELöschen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEinfügen:\u003C/strong\u003E was in der Zwischenablage liegt und Formulare, um mit einem eingegebenen Versatz oder auf einen Rasterschnittpunkt aus Listen einzufügen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EZahlenfelder wirken, wenn Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drücken oder das Feld verlassen. Jede Änderung ist ein Schritt, den Sie rückgängig machen können.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-berichtsregisterkarten-unten\"\u003EDie Berichtsregisterkarten (unten)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProbleme\u003C/span\u003E: alles, was DetailCraft nicht bauen konnte oder was Sie prüfen sollten, mit einer Schaltfläche \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWählen\u003C/span\u003E in jeder Zeile. Die Registerkarte zeigt die Anzahl in Klammern.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStückliste\u003C/span\u003E: Teile, Baugruppen sowie Schrauben und Ankerstangen mit Gewichten und einem Link \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV herunterladen\u003C/span\u003E. Klicken Sie auf eine Positionsnummer, um dieses Teil zu wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBiegeliste\u003C/span\u003E: jede Stabmarke in jedem Fundament mit einem Link \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV herunterladen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWenn eine Registerkarte den Fokus hat, wechseln \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zwischen den Registerkarten.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-statusleiste\"\u003EDie Statusleiste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Leiste unten hat drei Teile: den Rasterschnittpunkt unter dem Zeiger beim Platzieren, die letzte Meldung (was gerade geschehen ist oder warum etwas abgelehnt wurde) und laufende Summen der Teile, Schrauben, des Stahlgewichts und des Bewehrungsgewichts. Wenn eine Aktion scheinbar nichts bewirkt, lesen Sie zuerst die Meldung hier.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDer Titel des Browser-Tabs beginnt mit einem Punkt (•), solange das Modell Änderungen hat, die Sie nicht gespeichert haben.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"auf-einem-schmalen-bildschirm\"\u003EAuf einem schmalen Bildschirm\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnterhalb von etwa 760 Pixeln Breite stapeln sich die Spalten: Symbolleiste, Ansicht, Statusleiste, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenschaften\u003C/span\u003E, Berichtsregisterkarten und zuletzt der Bereich \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E. Scrollen Sie die Seite, um sie zu erreichen. DetailCraft ist für einen Desktop- oder Laptop-Bildschirm gestaltet; ein Telefon eignet sich zum Ansehen, nicht zum Detaillieren.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft füllt das Browserfenster unterhalb der App-Leiste aus. Von oben nach unten: die Symbolleiste, dann drei Spalten (der Bereich Modell, die Ansicht, der Bereich Eigenschaften), dann die Berichtsregisterkarten und schließlich die Statusleiste. Die App-Leiste Die schmale Leiste ganz oben gehört zur CadCraft-Website, nicht zum Modell. Sie zeigt den App-Namen und die Version, einen Link zu Über DetailCraft, Hilfe (oder F1) für diese Hilfe, Für Offline speichern, die Schaltfläche für den Bildschirmmodus und den Umschalter Papier / Modell. Für Offline speichern bewahrt DetailCraft, seine 3D-Ansicht und diese Hilfe in Ihrem Browser auf, sodass es ohne Verbindung öffnet. Nach dem Speichern zeigt sie Offline bereit; klicken Sie dann darauf, um Erneut speichern (neueste Version holen) oder Offline-Kopie entfernen zu wählen. Siehe Offline arbeiten. Papier ergibt einen hellen Bildschirm und Modell einen dunklen. Die 3D-Ansicht folgt sofort; an Ihrem Modell ändert sich nichts. Die Schaltfläche für den Bildschirmmodus und Ctrl+Shift+F wechseln durch normal, Vollbild und Vollbild mit ausgeblendeten Leisten. Ctrl+Shift+F verwendet auf jedem Computer die Taste Ctrl, auch auf dem Mac. Bei ausgeblendeten Leisten zeigen Sie auf den oberen Bildschirmrand (oder tippen darauf), um sie zurückzuholen. Esc beendet das Vollbild. Die Symbolleiste Die Symbolleiste hat fünf Gruppen. Fahren Sie mit der Maus über eine Schaltfläche, um zu sehen, was sie tut und welche Taste dazu gehört. Gruppe Schaltflächen Was sie tun Datei Neu, Öffnen, IFC importieren, Speichern, Beispiel Ein Modell beginnen, eine gespeicherte .detailcraft.json oder eine IFC-Datei öffnen, eines speichern oder den Beispielrahmen öffnen Platzieren Wählen, Träger, Stütze, Aussteifung, Blech, Fundament, Treppe Das Werkzeug, das der nächste Klick in der Ansicht verwendet Anschlüsse Kopfplatten, Fußplatte und im Menü Mehr: Anschlusslaschen, Stoß, Knotenblech, Ähnlich Einen Anschluss zum gewählten Träger, zur Stütze oder Aussteifung hinzufügen oder den gewählten Anschluss auf jeden gleichartigen Knoten kopieren Bearbeiten Rückgängig, Wiederholen, Kopieren, Einfügen, Mehr Bearbeitungen rückwärts und vorwärts durchgehen; die Auswahl kopieren und einfügen. Mehr öffnet ein Menü mit Ausschneiden, Kopieren, Einfügen, An gleicher Position einfügen, Duplizieren, Löschen, Alles auswählen und Auswahl aufheben, jeweils mit ihrer Taste Ausgabe IFC, NC-Dateien, Stückliste, Werkstattzeichnung, Zeichnungen und im Menü Mehr: Biegeliste, Gewichtsbericht nach Phase, KISS-Datei für Fertigungssoftware Einen Export herunterladen oder die Zeichnungsblätter öffnen Schaltflächen und Menüpunkte, die gerade nicht funktionieren können, sind ausgegraut, und wenn Sie mit der Maus darüber fahren, steht dort der Grund. Kopfplatten braucht einen gewählten Träger, Fußplatte eine gewählte Stütze, Ähnlich einen gewählten Anschluss, Werkstattzeichnung ein Modell mit Teilen, Kopieren ein gewähltes Bauteil oder Fundament und Einfügen etwas Kopiertes. Siehe Kopieren, Einfügen und Duplizieren. Der Bereich Modell (links) Modell: der Modell-Name. Er benennt jede Datei, die Sie speichern oder exportieren. Raster: die Rasterlinien und ihre Abstände sowie Raster übernehmen. Siehe Raster und Ebenen einrichten. Ebenen: eine Zeile je Ebene (Name und Höhe in mm), Ebene hinzufügen und Ebenen übernehmen. Eigenes Profil: Erstellen Sie Ihren eigenen parametrischen Querschnitt. Geschlossen, bis Sie es öffnen. Positionsnummern: jede Positionsnummer im Modell mit ihrem Profil, ihrer Länge und Anzahl. Klicken Sie auf eine Marke, um das erste Teil mit dieser Marke zu wählen. Raster, Ebenen und Eigenes Profil klappen sich ein: Klicken Sie auf ihre Überschriften. Die Ansicht (Mitte) Die Ansicht zeigt das Modell in 3D. Darüber hat die Ansichtsleiste drei Schaltflächen und eine Liste: 3D: das Modell frei drehen. Grundriss: senkrecht auf eine Ebene blicken. Neben den Schaltflächen erscheint eine Liste der Ebenen. Alles, was mehr als 1,5 m über der Ebene liegt, wird ausgeblendet, damit Sie die Ebene deutlich sehen und anklicken können. Ansicht: senkrecht auf eine beschriftete Rasterlinie blicken (Raster A, Raster B …). Eine Liste der Buchstabenraster erscheint. Es wird nur der Stahl innerhalb von 1 m zu dieser Linie gezeigt. Die Einpassen-Schaltfläche am rechten Ende der Ansichtsleiste zoomt, damit das ganze Modell zu sehen ist. Kleine Punkte markieren jeden Rasterschnittpunkt auf jeder Ebene. Solange ein Platzierwerkzeug aktiv ist, leuchtet der Punkt, der dem Zeiger am nächsten ist, auf, und die Statusleiste nennt ihn, zum Beispiel „A/2 bei Ebene 1“. Farben unterscheiden die Bauteilarten: Stützen, Träger, Aussteifungen und Bleche haben jeweils eine eigene Farbe, Schrauben und Stäbe eine weitere, Fundamente sind durchsichtig und Bewehrung wird als Röhren gezeichnet. Bohrungen erscheinen als dunkle Stopfen; Ausklinkungen sind ausgeschnitten. Wie Sie drehen, verschieben und zoomen: In der 3D-Ansicht navigieren. Wenn das Modell leer ist, zeigt die Ansicht eine kurze Liste „Ein Modell beginnen“. Sie können auch eine gespeicherte Modelldatei irgendwo auf der Ansicht ablegen, um sie zu öffnen. Der Bereich Eigenschaften (rechts) Was dieser Bereich zeigt, hängt davon ab, was Sie gerade tun, und seine Überschrift sagt es: „Modell“, „Träger platzieren“, „Träger b2“, „Kopfplattenanschluss“, „Fußplatte“, „Fundament F1“, „3 ausgewählt“ oder „Einfügen“. Nichts gewählt: eine Zusammenfassung des Modells (Bauteile, Anschlüsse, Fundamente, Teile, Marken, Schrauben, Gewicht von Stahl und Bewehrung, Raster, Ebenen). Ein Platzierwerkzeug aktiv: die Optionen für das nächste Teil (Profil, Material, Position, Drehung und für Stützen Bis), eine Zusammenfassung des gewählten Profils und ein Formular Nach Raster hinzufügen, um ohne Klick in der Ansicht zu platzieren. Ein Bauteil gewählt: sein Name, Profil, Material, Position, Drehung, die Koordinaten seiner beiden Enden, seine Marken, Schnittlänge und Gewicht, seine Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen sowie Schaltflächen für Anschlüsse, seine Werkstattzeichnung und Löschen. Ein Anschluss gewählt: jede Einstellung für Platten, Schrauben und Schweißnähte, die Schrauben und Schweißnaht, die er bildet, seine Warnungen und Auf ähnliche übernehmen. Ein Fundament gewählt: seine Position und Größe, die untere Matte, die Anschlussbewehrung und die Stabmarken, die es verwendet. Mehrere Dinge gewählt: wie viele Bauteile jeder Art, Anschlüsse und Fundamente, ihr Stahlgewicht sowie Kopieren, Duplizieren und Löschen. Einfügen: was in der Zwischenablage liegt und Formulare, um mit einem eingegebenen Versatz oder auf einen Rasterschnittpunkt aus Listen einzufügen. Zahlenfelder wirken, wenn Sie Enter drücken oder das Feld verlassen. Jede Änderung ist ein Schritt, den Sie rückgängig machen können. Die Berichtsregisterkarten (unten) Probleme: alles, was DetailCraft nicht bauen konnte oder was Sie prüfen sollten, mit einer Schaltfläche Wählen in jeder Zeile. Die Registerkarte zeigt die Anzahl in Klammern. Stückliste: Teile, Baugruppen sowie Schrauben und Ankerstangen mit Gewichten und einem Link CSV herunterladen. Klicken Sie auf eine Positionsnummer, um dieses Teil zu wählen. Biegeliste: jede Stabmarke in jedem Fundament mit einem Link CSV herunterladen. Wenn eine Registerkarte den Fokus hat, wechseln ← und → zwischen den Registerkarten. Die Statusleiste Die Leiste unten hat drei Teile: den Rasterschnittpunkt unter dem Zeiger beim Platzieren, die letzte Meldung (was gerade geschehen ist oder warum etwas abgelehnt wurde) und laufende Summen der Teile, Schrauben, des Stahlgewichts und des Bewehrungsgewichts. Wenn eine Aktion scheinbar nichts bewirkt, lesen Sie zuerst die Meldung hier. Der Titel des Browser-Tabs beginnt mit einem Punkt (•), solange das Modell Änderungen hat, die Sie nicht gespeichert haben. Auf einem schmalen Bildschirm Unterhalb von etwa 760 Pixeln Breite stapeln sich die Spalten: Symbolleiste, Ansicht, Statusleiste, Eigenschaften, Berichtsregisterkarten und zuletzt der Bereich Modell. Scrollen Sie die Seite, um sie zu erreichen. DetailCraft ist für einen Desktop- oder Laptop-Bildschirm gestaltet; ein Telefon eignet sich zum Ansehen, nicht zum Detaillieren.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"files","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Dateien öffnen, speichern und exportieren","summary":"Die DetailCraft-Modelldatei, jeder Export, den sie erzeugt, wohin die Dateien gehen und was sich je nach Browser und Verbindung unterscheidet.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["öffnen","speichern","exportieren","herunterladen","Datei","json","ifc","nc1","dstv","csv","pdf","zip","Formate","importieren"],"headings":[{"id":"die-modelldatei","text":"Die Modelldatei","level":2},{"id":"speichern","text":"Speichern","level":3},{"id":"ffnen","text":"Öffnen","level":3},{"id":"was-sich-ffnen-l-sst","text":"Was sich öffnen lässt","level":3},{"id":"verlorene-arbeit-zuletzt-verwendete-dateien-die-desktop-app","text":"Verlorene Arbeit, zuletzt verwendete Dateien, die Desktop-App","level":2},{"id":"exporte","text":"Exporte","level":2},{"id":"hinweise-zu-den-csv-dateien","text":"Hinweise zu den CSV-Dateien","level":3},{"id":"hinweise-zur-pdf-datei","text":"Hinweise zur PDF-Datei","level":3},{"id":"einheiten","text":"Einheiten","level":2},{"id":"online-und-offline","text":"Online und offline","level":2},{"id":"nicht-gespeicherte-arbeit","text":"Nicht gespeicherte Arbeit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft hat eine eigene Datei, das Modell, und fünf Exporte. Jedes Speichern und jeder Export ist ein Download. Nichts wird hochgeladen, und nichts wird auf einem Server gespeichert.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-modelldatei\"\u003EDie Modelldatei\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEin DetailCraft-Modell wird als \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E gespeichert, wobei \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;\u003C/code\u003E der Modellname im Bereich \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E ist. Sie enthält die Raster, Ebenen, Bauteile (mit ihren Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen), Anschlüsse und deren Einstellungen, Fundamente und Ihre eigenen Profile.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETeile, Marken, Schrauben, Schweißnähte und Bewehrungsstäbe werden nicht gespeichert: DetailCraft berechnet sie bei jedem Öffnen aus dem Modell neu. Deshalb ist eine Modelldatei klein.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"speichern\"\u003ESpeichern\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGeben Sie im Feld \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E oben im Bereich \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModell\u003C/span\u003E einen Namen ein.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeichern\u003C/span\u003E in der Gruppe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatei\u003C/span\u003E oder drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDer Browser lädt \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E herunter. In der Statusleiste steht „Gespeichert … (heruntergeladen)“, und der Punkt am Anfang des Browser-Tab-Titels verschwindet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJedes Speichern ist ein neuer Download. Der Browser überschreibt die geöffnete Datei nicht; je nach Einstellung hängt er eine Nummer an den Namen an, etwa \u003Ccode\u003ERahmen (1).detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E, oder er fragt, wo gespeichert werden soll. Wenn Sie eigene Versionen behalten möchten, benennen Sie die Downloads um.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome und Edge speichern im Ordner Downloads, sofern in den Download-Einstellungen des Browsers nicht \u003Cstrong\u003EVor dem Herunterladen jeder Datei nachfragen, wo sie gespeichert werden soll\u003C/strong\u003E aktiviert ist. Aktivieren Sie es, wenn Sie bei jedem Mal einen Ordner und einen Dateinamen wählen möchten.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirefox speichert im Ordner Downloads oder fragt jedes Mal nach, wenn in den Einstellungen \u003Cstrong\u003EImmer nachfragen, wo Dateien gespeichert werden sollen\u003C/strong\u003E aktiviert ist.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari speichert im Ordner Downloads. Um den Ordner zu ändern, öffnen Sie \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari, Einstellungen, Allgemein, Speicherort für Downloads\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ffnen\"\u003EÖffnen\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÖffnen\u003C/span\u003E in der Gruppe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatei\u003C/span\u003E oder drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie eine Datei \u003Ccode\u003E.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOder ziehen Sie die Datei aus \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EExplorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003EIhrem Dateimanager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E und legen Sie sie auf der Ansicht ab. Während des Ziehens zeigt die Ansicht „Ziehen Sie ein DetailCraft-Modell hierher, um es zu öffnen“.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWenn das geöffnete Modell nicht gespeicherte Änderungen hat, fragt DetailCraft vorher nach: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbbrechen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÄnderungen verwerfen\u003C/span\u003E oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZuerst speichern\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft prüft jedes Feld einer Datei, bevor es sie verwendet. Was nicht verwendbar ist, wird weggelassen, und die Statusleiste nennt es, zum Beispiel „2 Bauteile waren ungültig und wurden weggelassen.“ Ein Modell, das mit einer neueren DetailCraft-Version gespeichert wurde, wird mit einer Meldung abgelehnt, statt halb richtig geöffnet zu werden.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"was-sich-ffnen-l-sst\"\u003EWas sich öffnen lässt\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEigene Modelldateien (\u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E, bis 50 MB) und IFC-Dateien (\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E, IFC2X3 oder IFC4, bis 200 MB). Eine IFC-Datei wird beim Öffnen oder beim Importieren mit \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC importieren\u003C/span\u003E zu einem neuen Modell aus ihren Trägern, Stützen, Bauteilen, Blechen, Rastern und Ebenen, mit den darin geschnittenen Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen und ihren Schrauben als lose Schrauben; ein Bericht listet auf, was weggelassen wurde. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/ifc-export#import\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/ifc-export\" data-hash=\"import\"\u003EIFC-Export\u003C/a\u003E. DWG-, DXF- oder Modelle anderer Detaillierungsprogramme lassen sich noch nicht öffnen; der Versuch zeigt „DetailCraft öffnet eigene Modelle (.detailcraft.json) und IFC-Dateien (.ifc).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"verlorene-arbeit-zuletzt-verwendete-dateien-die-desktop-app\"\u003EVerlorene Arbeit, zuletzt verwendete Dateien, die Desktop-App\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESolange das Modell Änderungen hat, die Sie nicht gespeichert haben, bewahrt DetailCraft eine Kopie in diesem Browser auf. Wenn Tab oder Browser beendet werden, bevor Sie speichern, fragt DetailCraft beim nächsten Öffnen: \u003Cstrong\u003EIhre Arbeit wiederherstellen?\u003C/strong\u003E Stellen Sie die Kopie wieder her oder verwerfen Sie sie. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003EIhre Arbeit wiederherstellen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZuletzt geöffnete Modelle werden auf dem Startbildschirm aufgelistet, sofern der Browser DetailCraft erlaubt, sie erneut zu öffnen. Klicken Sie auf eines, um es erneut zu öffnen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn der Desktop-App schreibt \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeichern\u003C/span\u003E das Modell zurück in die geöffnete Datei (im Browser lädt es eine Kopie herunter), und ein Doppelklick auf ein DetailCraft-Modell oder eine IFC-Datei öffnet sie in DetailCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"exporte\"\u003EExporte\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlle Exporte verwenden den Modellnamen. Wählen Sie sie in der Gruppe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAusgabe\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESchaltfläche\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDatei\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInhalt\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC4: Geschosse, Raster, jedes Stahlteil als Profilextrusion mit seinen Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen, Baugruppen, Schrauben und Ankerstangen, Fundamente und jeder Bewehrungsstab. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/ifc-export\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/ifc-export\"\u003EIFC exportieren\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENC-Dateien\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; NC.zip\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEine DSTV-Datei \u003Ccode\u003E.nc1\u003C/code\u003E je Positionsnummer. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/nc-export\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/nc-export\"\u003ENC-Dateien an die Werkstatt senden\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStückliste\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; BOM.csv\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrei Tabellen in einer Datei: Teile, Baugruppen sowie Schrauben und Ankerstangen. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/marks-and-bom\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/marks-and-bom\"\u003EPositionsnummern und Stückliste\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBiegeliste\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; bending schedule.csv\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJede Stabmarke in den Fundamenten. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/footings-and-rebar\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/footings-and-rebar\"\u003EFundamente und Bewehrung\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWerkstattzeichnung\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; &lt;mark&gt;.pdf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; Shop drawings.pdf\u003C/code\u003E enthält ein Blatt je Positionsnummer; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF herunterladen\u003C/span\u003E liefert das angezeigte Blatt (A3, sofern Sie kein anderes Format gewählt haben). Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/shop-drawings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/shop-drawings\"\u003EWerkstattzeichnungen erstellen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZeichnungen\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; drawings.pdf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.pdf\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E oder \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E, mehrere in \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; drawings DWG.zip\u003C/code\u003E (oder \u003Ccode\u003EDXF.zip\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrundrisse, Ansichten und Montagezeichnungen im von Ihnen gewählten Blattformat; DWG und DXF in voller Größe, 1 Einheit = 1 mm auf dem Papier. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/drawings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/drawings\"\u003EÜbersichts- und Montagezeichnungen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hinweise-zu-den-csv-dateien\"\u003EHinweise zu den CSV-Dateien\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESie beginnen mit einer Byte-Order-Mark, damit Tabellenkalkulationen Umlaute richtig lesen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEine Zelle, die mit \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E oder \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E beginnt (und keine reine Zahl ist), erhält ein vorangestelltes Hochkomma, damit die Tabellenkalkulation sie nie als Formel ausführt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELängen sind in Millimetern und Gewichte in Kilogramm angegeben.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hinweise-zur-pdf-datei\"\u003EHinweise zur PDF-Datei\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDer Text in der PDF-Datei verwendet die Standardschrift Helvetica. Zeichen, die sie nicht enthält (außerhalb des westeuropäischen Zeichensatzes), werden als „?“ gedruckt, und die Statusleiste zeigt an, wie viele es sind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"einheiten\"\u003EEinheiten\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft arbeitet durchgehend in Millimetern: Raster, Ebenen, Koordinaten, Profile, Bohrungen und jeder Export. Profilnamen behalten ihre veröffentlichte Form (W12x50 ist eine US-Bezeichnung, auch wenn die Maße in mm angezeigt werden). Eine Eingabe in Zoll gibt es noch nicht.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-und-offline\"\u003EOnline und offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EÖffnen, Speichern und Exportieren laufen alle im Browser ab und funktionieren daher gleich, mit oder ohne Verbindung, sobald DetailCraft geladen ist.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm DetailCraft später ohne Verbindung zu verwenden, wählen Sie während Sie online sind in der App-Leiste \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFür Offline speichern\u003C/span\u003E. Die Schaltfläche zählt hoch („Speichern 3 von 20“) und zeigt dann \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline bereit\u003C/span\u003E. Die gespeicherte Kopie enthält die 3D-Grafikbibliothek (etwa 2,6 MB) und diese Hilfe; daher funktionieren nach dem Speichern die 3D-Ansicht, jeder Export und die Hilfe offline. Details und was weiterhin eine Verbindung braucht: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EOffline arbeiten\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESie sind offline. Wenn die App-Leiste \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline bereit\u003C/span\u003E anzeigt, funktionieren Öffnen, Speichern und Exportieren wie gewohnt. Wenn nicht, lassen Sie diesen Tab offen, laden Sie ihn nicht neu, bis Sie wieder online sind, und speichern Sie Ihr Modell jetzt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud-Speicherung ist noch nicht verfügbar, und es gibt noch keine Desktop-Versionen. Sichern Sie Ihre \u003Ccode\u003E.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E-Dateien so, wie Sie jede andere Arbeitsdatei sichern. Mehr dazu unter \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWo Ihre Dateien liegen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nicht-gespeicherte-arbeit\"\u003ENicht gespeicherte Arbeit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft bewahrt das Modell nur im geöffneten Tab. Wenn Sie den Tab mit nicht gespeicherten Änderungen schließen oder neu laden, fragt der Browser, ob die Seite verlassen werden soll; der Wortlaut stammt vom Browser. Speichern Sie oft mit \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft hat eine eigene Datei, das Modell, und fünf Exporte. Jedes Speichern und jeder Export ist ein Download. Nichts wird hochgeladen, und nichts wird auf einem Server gespeichert. Die Modelldatei Ein DetailCraft-Modell wird als \u003Cname\u003E.detailcraft.json gespeichert, wobei \u003Cname\u003E der Modellname im Bereich Modell ist. Sie enthält die Raster, Ebenen, Bauteile (mit ihren Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen), Anschlüsse und deren Einstellungen, Fundamente und Ihre eigenen Profile. Teile, Marken, Schrauben, Schweißnähte und Bewehrungsstäbe werden nicht gespeichert: DetailCraft berechnet sie bei jedem Öffnen aus dem Modell neu. Deshalb ist eine Modelldatei klein. Speichern Geben Sie im Feld Name oben im Bereich Modell einen Namen ein. Wählen Sie Speichern in der Gruppe Datei oder drücken Sie Ctrl+S. Der Browser lädt \u003Cname\u003E.detailcraft.json herunter. In der Statusleiste steht „Gespeichert … (heruntergeladen)“, und der Punkt am Anfang des Browser-Tab-Titels verschwindet. Jedes Speichern ist ein neuer Download. Der Browser überschreibt die geöffnete Datei nicht; je nach Einstellung hängt er eine Nummer an den Namen an, etwa Rahmen (1).detailcraft.json, oder er fragt, wo gespeichert werden soll. Wenn Sie eigene Versionen behalten möchten, benennen Sie die Downloads um. Chrome und Edge speichern im Ordner Downloads, sofern in den Download-Einstellungen des Browsers nicht Vor dem Herunterladen jeder Datei nachfragen, wo sie gespeichert werden soll aktiviert ist. Aktivieren Sie es, wenn Sie bei jedem Mal einen Ordner und einen Dateinamen wählen möchten. Firefox speichert im Ordner Downloads oder fragt jedes Mal nach, wenn in den Einstellungen Immer nachfragen, wo Dateien gespeichert werden sollen aktiviert ist. Safari speichert im Ordner Downloads. Um den Ordner zu ändern, öffnen Sie Safari, Einstellungen, Allgemein, Speicherort für Downloads. Öffnen Wählen Sie Öffnen in der Gruppe Datei oder drücken Sie Ctrl+O. Wählen Sie eine Datei .detailcraft.json. Oder ziehen Sie die Datei aus Finder / Explorer / Ihrem Dateimanager und legen Sie sie auf der Ansicht ab. Während des Ziehens zeigt die Ansicht „Ziehen Sie ein DetailCraft-Modell hierher, um es zu öffnen“. Wenn das geöffnete Modell nicht gespeicherte Änderungen hat, fragt DetailCraft vorher nach: Abbrechen, Änderungen verwerfen oder Zuerst speichern. DetailCraft prüft jedes Feld einer Datei, bevor es sie verwendet. Was nicht verwendbar ist, wird weggelassen, und die Statusleiste nennt es, zum Beispiel „2 Bauteile waren ungültig und wurden weggelassen.“ Ein Modell, das mit einer neueren DetailCraft-Version gespeichert wurde, wird mit einer Meldung abgelehnt, statt halb richtig geöffnet zu werden. Was sich öffnen lässt Eigene Modelldateien (.json, bis 50 MB) und IFC-Dateien (.ifc, IFC2X3 oder IFC4, bis 200 MB). Eine IFC-Datei wird beim Öffnen oder beim Importieren mit IFC importieren zu einem neuen Modell aus ihren Trägern, Stützen, Bauteilen, Blechen, Rastern und Ebenen, mit den darin geschnittenen Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen und ihren Schrauben als lose Schrauben; ein Bericht listet auf, was weggelassen wurde. Siehe IFC-Export. DWG-, DXF- oder Modelle anderer Detaillierungsprogramme lassen sich noch nicht öffnen; der Versuch zeigt „DetailCraft öffnet eigene Modelle (.detailcraft.json) und IFC-Dateien (.ifc).\" Verlorene Arbeit, zuletzt verwendete Dateien, die Desktop-App Solange das Modell Änderungen hat, die Sie nicht gespeichert haben, bewahrt DetailCraft eine Kopie in diesem Browser auf. Wenn Tab oder Browser beendet werden, bevor Sie speichern, fragt DetailCraft beim nächsten Öffnen: Ihre Arbeit wiederherstellen? Stellen Sie die Kopie wieder her oder verwerfen Sie sie. Siehe Ihre Arbeit wiederherstellen. Zuletzt geöffnete Modelle werden auf dem Startbildschirm aufgelistet, sofern der Browser DetailCraft erlaubt, sie erneut zu öffnen. Klicken Sie auf eines, um es erneut zu öffnen. In der Desktop-App schreibt Speichern das Modell zurück in die geöffnete Datei (im Browser lädt es eine Kopie herunter), und ein Doppelklick auf ein DetailCraft-Modell oder eine IFC-Datei öffnet sie in DetailCraft. Exporte Alle Exporte verwenden den Modellnamen. Wählen Sie sie in der Gruppe Ausgabe. Schaltfläche Datei Inhalt IFC \u003Cname\u003E.ifc IFC4: Geschosse, Raster, jedes Stahlteil als Profilextrusion mit seinen Bohrungen und Ausklinkungen, Baugruppen, Schrauben und Ankerstangen, Fundamente und jeder Bewehrungsstab. Siehe IFC exportieren. NC-Dateien \u003Cname\u003E NC.zip Eine DSTV-Datei .nc1 je Positionsnummer. Siehe NC-Dateien an die Werkstatt senden. Stückliste \u003Cname\u003E BOM.csv Drei Tabellen in einer Datei: Teile, Baugruppen sowie Schrauben und Ankerstangen. Siehe Positionsnummern und Stückliste. Biegeliste \u003Cname\u003E bending schedule.csv Jede Stabmarke in den Fundamenten. Siehe Fundamente und Bewehrung. Werkstattzeichnung \u003Cname\u003E \u003Cmark\u003E.pdf \u003Cname\u003E Shop drawings.pdf enthält ein Blatt je Positionsnummer; PDF herunterladen liefert das angezeigte Blatt (A3, sofern Sie kein anderes Format gewählt haben). Siehe Werkstattzeichnungen erstellen. Zeichnungen \u003Cname\u003E drawings.pdf, \u003Cname\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.pdf; \u003Cname\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.dwg oder .dxf, mehrere in \u003Cname\u003E drawings DWG.zip (oder DXF.zip) Grundrisse, Ansichten und Montagezeichnungen im von Ihnen gewählten Blattformat; DWG und DXF in voller Größe, 1 Einheit = 1 mm auf dem Papier. Siehe Übersichts- und Montagezeichnungen. Hinweise zu den CSV-Dateien Sie beginnen mit einer Byte-Order-Mark, damit Tabellenkalkulationen Umlaute richtig lesen. Eine Zelle, die mit =, +, - oder @ beginnt (und keine reine Zahl ist), erhält ein vorangestelltes Hochkomma, damit die Tabellenkalkulation sie nie als Formel ausführt. Längen sind in Millimetern und Gewichte in Kilogramm angegeben. Hinweise zur PDF-Datei Der Text in der PDF-Datei verwendet die Standardschrift Helvetica. Zeichen, die sie nicht enthält (außerhalb des westeuropäischen Zeichensatzes), werden als „?“ gedruckt, und die Statusleiste zeigt an, wie viele es sind. Einheiten DetailCraft arbeitet durchgehend in Millimetern: Raster, Ebenen, Koordinaten, Profile, Bohrungen und jeder Export. Profilnamen behalten ihre veröffentlichte Form (W12x50 ist eine US-Bezeichnung, auch wenn die Maße in mm angezeigt werden). Eine Eingabe in Zoll gibt es noch nicht. Online und offline Öffnen, Speichern und Exportieren laufen alle im Browser ab und funktionieren daher gleich, mit oder ohne Verbindung, sobald DetailCraft geladen ist. Um DetailCraft später ohne Verbindung zu verwenden, wählen Sie während Sie online sind in der App-Leiste Für Offline speichern. Die Schaltfläche zählt hoch („Speichern 3 von 20“) und zeigt dann Offline bereit. Die gespeicherte Kopie enthält die 3D-Grafikbibliothek (etwa 2,6 MB) und diese Hilfe; daher funktionieren nach dem Speichern die 3D-Ansicht, jeder Export und die Hilfe offline. Details und was weiterhin eine Verbindung braucht: Offline arbeiten. Sie sind offline. Wenn die App-Leiste Offline bereit anzeigt, funktionieren Öffnen, Speichern und Exportieren wie gewohnt. Wenn nicht, lassen Sie diesen Tab offen, laden Sie ihn nicht neu, bis Sie wieder online sind, und speichern Sie Ihr Modell jetzt. Cloud-Speicherung ist noch nicht verfügbar, und es gibt noch keine Desktop-Versionen. Sichern Sie Ihre .detailcraft.json-Dateien so, wie Sie jede andere Arbeitsdatei sichern. Mehr dazu unter Wo Ihre Dateien liegen. Nicht gespeicherte Arbeit DetailCraft bewahrt das Modell nur im geöffneten Tab. Wenn Sie den Tab mit nicht gespeicherten Änderungen schließen oder neu laden, fragt der Browser, ob die Seite verlassen werden soll; der Wortlaut stammt vom Browser. Speichern Sie oft mit Ctrl+S.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"navigate-3d","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Move around the 3D view","summary":"Turn, pan and zoom with a mouse, a trackpad or a touch screen, and when to use Plan and Elevation instead.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["orbit","rotate","pan","zoom","navigate","view","camera","trackpad","touch","plan","elevation","fit","mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"in-3d","text":"In 3D","level":2},{"id":"in-plan-and-elevation","text":"In Plan and Elevation","level":2},{"id":"on-a-mac-trackpad","text":"On a Mac trackpad","level":2},{"id":"plan-and-elevation-make-placing-easy","text":"Plan and Elevation make placing easy","level":2},{"id":"selecting","text":"Selecting","level":2},{"id":"if-the-view-looks-wrong","text":"If the view looks wrong","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe view has three modes, chosen in the view bar above it: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E. The mouse buttons do different things in 3D than in Plan and Elevation, so read both tables.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-3d\"\u003EIn 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn around the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the wheel, or drag up and down with the middle button (wheel pressed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick it (a click that moves less than a few pixels)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee the whole model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe fit button at the right end of the view bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn around the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with two fingers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch with two fingers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee the whole model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe fit button at the right end of the view bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube at the top right (under the view bar) turns with the model: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to turn, or click its house button to go back to the start. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model turns around the middle of the view, and the wheel zooms toward the middle of the view, not toward the pointer. To look closely at one corner, pan it to the middle first, then zoom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-plan-and-elevation\"\u003EIn Plan and Elevation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese views look straight at the model and cannot be turned.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left or the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the wheel, or drag up and down with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch with two fingers (dragging two fingers also pans)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-mac-trackpad\"\u003EOn a Mac trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E swipe up or down with two fingers, or pinch. Both reach the view as zooming.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETurn (3D):\u003C/strong\u003E press the trackpad and drag with one finger. If you use tap to click, double-tap and drag works too when dragging is turned on in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings, Accessibility, Pointer Control, Trackpad Options\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan (3D):\u003C/strong\u003E hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging also pans. A two-finger click is a right-click, but dragging with it is awkward on a trackpad.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan (Plan and Elevation):\u003C/strong\u003E just drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA Magic Mouse has no middle button: use the \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drag to pan and a swipe on its surface to zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMore about right-clicking and panning without a mouse: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plan-and-elevation-make-placing-easy\"\u003EPlan and Elevation make placing easy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, intersections at different levels can sit on top of each other on screen, and a click goes to the nearest dot. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E only the dots of the level or grid line you are looking at can be picked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E, then pick a level in the list beside the buttons. The view looks down at that level; anything more than 1.5 m above it is cut away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E, then pick a lettered grid line (&quot;Grid A&quot;, &quot;Grid B&quot; …). The view looks straight at that line; only steel within 1 m of it is shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EElevations are only on lettered grid lines. To work on a line of numbered grids (a frame running from A to B), use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, or place by typing in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E form. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/place-members#place-by-grid-without-clicking\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/place-members\" data-hash=\"place-by-grid-without-clicking\"\u003EPlace columns, beams and braces\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"selecting\"\u003ESelecting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a part to select it. It turns the highlight color and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel shows it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick empty space, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to clear the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESteel wins over concrete: a click on a column inside a see-through footing selects the column. Click the footing away from the column, or one of its bars, to select the footing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClicking a bolt or an end plate selects its connection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn Plan and Elevation, parts that are cut away cannot be clicked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click to add a part to the selection or take it out; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selects everything. There is no window (drag a box) selection yet. What you can do with several: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also select from lists: click a mark in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart marks\u003C/span\u003E on the left, a part mark in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/span\u003E tab, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E beside a line in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-the-view-looks-wrong\"\u003EIf the view looks wrong\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELost the model? Choose the fit button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe view is blank or gray with a message about WebGL: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/troubleshooting#the-3d-view-is-missing\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"the-3d-view-is-missing\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything still works without the 3D view except clicking in it: the panels, reports and exports all run, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E places members by typing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The view has three modes, chosen in the view bar above it: 3D, Plan and Elevation. The mouse buttons do different things in 3D than in Plan and Elevation, so read both tables. In 3D To Do this Turn around the model Drag with the left button Pan Drag with the right button, or hold Shift and drag with the left button Zoom Turn the wheel, or drag up and down with the middle button (wheel pressed) Select a part Click it (a click that moves less than a few pixels) See the whole model The fit button at the right end of the view bar To Do this Turn around the model Drag with one finger Pan Drag with two fingers Zoom Pinch with two fingers Select a part Tap it See the whole model The fit button at the right end of the view bar The view cube at the top right (under the view bar) turns with the model: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to turn, or click its house button to go back to the start. See The view cube. The model turns around the middle of the view, and the wheel zooms toward the middle of the view, not toward the pointer. To look closely at one corner, pan it to the middle first, then zoom. In Plan and Elevation These views look straight at the model and cannot be turned. To Do this Pan Drag with the left or the right button Zoom Turn the wheel, or drag up and down with the middle button Select a part Click it To Do this Pan Drag with one finger Zoom Pinch with two fingers (dragging two fingers also pans) Select a part Tap it On a Mac trackpad Zoom: swipe up or down with two fingers, or pinch. Both reach the view as zooming. Turn (3D): press the trackpad and drag with one finger. If you use tap to click, double-tap and drag works too when dragging is turned on in System Settings, Accessibility, Pointer Control, Trackpad Options. Pan (3D): hold Shift and drag. Holding Ctrl or Ctrl while dragging also pans. A two-finger click is a right-click, but dragging with it is awkward on a trackpad. Pan (Plan and Elevation): just drag. A Magic Mouse has no middle button: use the Shift drag to pan and a swipe on its surface to zoom. More about right-clicking and panning without a mouse: Keys, mice and trackpads. Plan and Elevation make placing easy In 3D, intersections at different levels can sit on top of each other on screen, and a click goes to the nearest dot. In Plan and Elevation only the dots of the level or grid line you are looking at can be picked. Choose Plan, then pick a level in the list beside the buttons. The view looks down at that level; anything more than 1.5 m above it is cut away. Or choose Elevation, then pick a lettered grid line (\"Grid A\", \"Grid B\" …). The view looks straight at that line; only steel within 1 m of it is shown. Elevations are only on lettered grid lines. To work on a line of numbered grids (a frame running from A to B), use 3D, or place by typing in the Add by grid form. See Place columns, beams and braces. Selecting Click a part to select it. It turns the highlight color and the Properties panel shows it. Click empty space, or press Esc, to clear the selection. Steel wins over concrete: a click on a column inside a see-through footing selects the column. Click the footing away from the column, or one of its bars, to select the footing. Clicking a bolt or an end plate selects its connection. In Plan and Elevation, parts that are cut away cannot be clicked. Hold Shift (or Ctrl, or Ctrl) and click to add a part to the selection or take it out; Ctrl+A selects everything. There is no window (drag a box) selection yet. What you can do with several: Copy, paste and duplicate. You can also select from lists: click a mark in Part marks on the left, a part mark in the Bill of materials tab, or Select beside a line in Problems. If the view looks wrong Lost the model? Choose the fit button. The view is blank or gray with a message about WebGL: see Troubleshooting. Everything still works without the 3D view except clicking in it: the panels, reports and exports all run, and Add by grid places members by typing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"view-cube","scope":"detailcraft","title":"The view cube","summary":"Click a face, edge or corner of the cube in the corner of the view to look from there, drag it to orbit, turn the compass ring, step around a face-on view, and go home.","section":"tasks","order":11,"keywords":["view cube","viewcube","cube","compass","north","home view","standard views","top","front","right","iso","corner","edge","orbit","rotate view","axis icon","corner indicator","navigation"],"headings":[{"id":"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner","text":"Look from a side, an edge or a corner","level":2},{"id":"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube","text":"Orbit by dragging the cube","level":2},{"id":"the-compass-ring","text":"The compass ring","level":2},{"id":"face-on-views-arrows","text":"Face-on views: arrows","level":2},{"id":"home","text":"Home","level":2},{"id":"with-the-keyboard","text":"With the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead","text":"Axis icon or nothing instead","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view, under the view bar (only in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E already look straight at the model). It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E is up, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E faces south (the side the lettered grids are seen from in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E faces east, along the numbered grids. It stays see-through until you point at it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner\"\u003ELook from a side, an edge or a corner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are 26 places to click: 6 faces (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face view of the cube is a 3D view, not \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E: nothing is cut away, and the model can still be turned.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube\"\u003EOrbit by dragging the cube\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-compass-ring\"\u003EThe compass ring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag the ring\u003C/strong\u003E to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick a letter\u003C/strong\u003E to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"face-on-views-arrows\"\u003EFace-on views: arrows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe four \u003Cstrong\u003Etriangles\u003C/strong\u003E bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe two \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurved arrows\u003C/strong\u003E at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"home\"\u003EHome\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the starting three-quarter view and fits the whole model in, the same as the fit button. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-the-keyboard\"\u003EWith the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to the cube, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a higher or lower one, up to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or down to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elook from the one chosen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift ←\"\u003E⇧←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift →\"\u003E⇧→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe home view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eleave the cube\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start DetailCraft tools.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead\"\u003EAxis icon or nothing instead\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, and set \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. This browser remembers the choice. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings#corner-indicator\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"corner-indicator\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view, under the view bar (only in 3D: Plan and Elevation already look straight at the model). It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: Top is up, Front faces south (the side the lettered grids are seen from in Elevation), Right faces east, along the numbered grids. It stays see-through until you point at it. Look from a side, an edge or a corner Point at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces). Click it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom. There are 26 places to click: 6 faces (Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces. A face view of the cube is a 3D view, not Plan or Elevation: nothing is cut away, and the model can still be turned. Orbit by dragging the cube Drag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere. The compass ring The ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W. Drag the ring to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer. Click a letter to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen. Face-on views: arrows When you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube: The four triangles bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on. The two curved arrows at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen. Home The house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the starting three-quarter view and fits the whole model in, the same as the fit button. Home does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus. With the keyboard Tab to the cube, then: Key Does ← / → choose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which) ↑ / ↓ choose a higher or lower one, up to Top or down to Bottom Enter or Space look from the one chosen Shift+← / Shift+→ turn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis Home the home view Esc leave the cube Keys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start DetailCraft tools. Axis icon or nothing instead Open Settings (Ctrl+,), the DetailCraft tab, and set Corner indicator to View cube (the start), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. This browser remembers the choice. See DetailCraft settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"sample-model","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Explore the sample frame","summary":"Open the built-in two-bay frame and use it to learn what DetailCraft makes, from marks to NC files.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["sample","example","demo","frame","learn","tour","try"],"headings":[{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-it","text":"What is in it","level":2},{"id":"things-to-try","text":"Things to try","level":2},{"id":"use-it-as-a-starting-point","text":"Use it as a starting point","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe sample frame is a small, complete model: every kind of thing DetailCraft builds appears at least once. It is the quickest way to learn the app and to test an export before you rely on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESample\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf your current model has unsaved changes, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave first\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Sample frame: 6 columns with base plates, 4 beams with end plates, a brace, 7 footings with rebar.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sample opens as a new model called &quot;Sample frame&quot;. It does not change anything else, and it has nothing to undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-it\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGrids\u003C/strong\u003E 1, 2, 3 at 6000 mm and A, B at 7000 mm. \u003Cstrong\u003ELevels\u003C/strong\u003E Level 1 at 0 and Level 2 at 4000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESix columns\u003C/strong\u003E, W12x50, from Level 1 to Level 2 at every intersection, each on a 25 mm base plate with four anchor rods.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFour beams\u003C/strong\u003E, W16x26, at Level 2 along grid lines A and B, top of steel on the level, with a flush bolted end plate at both ends (eight in all).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne brace\u003C/strong\u003E, HSS4x4x1/4, from A/1 at Level 1 up to A/2 at Level 2.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESix pad footings\u003C/strong\u003E, 2000 x 2000 x 600, one under each column, with a hooked bottom mat both ways.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne more footing\u003C/strong\u003E beside the frame, 2400 x 1800 x 600, with six hooked starter bars standing up for a concrete pier.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-try\"\u003EThings to try\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a beam. Read its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart mark\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut length\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut back\u003C/span\u003E: the cut length is shorter than the 6000 mm grid spacing because each end plate cuts the beam back by half the column depth plus the plate thickness.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a column. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHoles\u003C/span\u003E it lists the holes the end plates made in its flange (&quot;8 holes from connections&quot;; the middle columns have 16, one set in each flange).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart marks\u003C/span\u003E list on the left, see that all four beams share one mark: they are the same piece of steel. The corner columns share a mark too, even though their holes face opposite ways, because a column turned end for end is the same piece. The middle columns, with holes in both flanges, get another mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an end plate (or one of its bolts). The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel shows the plate, bolt and weld settings, the bolts it makes (with their length worked out from the grip) and any edge-distance warnings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/span\u003E tab. Parts, assemblies (a beam with its two end plates is one assembly), and bolts and anchor rods are listed with weights. You should see six part marks: c1 (the two middle columns) and c2 (the four corner columns), both W12x50 at 3975 mm; b1, four W16x26 beams at 5650 mm; br1, the brace; p1, eight PL20x180 end plates; and p2, six PL25x400 base plates. The fasteners are 64 bolts 20 x 70 A325 and 24 anchor rods 20 x 575.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending schedule\u003C/span\u003E tab. The six identical footings share bar marks; the pier footing adds its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the brace and look at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/span\u003E is grayed out, because shop drawings cover I-shapes and plates only so far.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENC files\u003C/span\u003E. The zip holds a file for every I-shape and plate mark; the status bar lists the brace mark as not written.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC\u003C/span\u003E and open the file in any IFC viewer to check how your other software reads it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-it-as-a-starting-point\"\u003EUse it as a starting point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sample is an ordinary model. Change it, then rename it in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E field and save it under its new name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The sample frame is a small, complete model: every kind of thing DetailCraft builds appears at least once. It is the quickest way to learn the app and to test an export before you rely on it. Open it Choose Sample in the File group. If your current model has unsaved changes, choose Save first or Discard changes. The status bar says \"Sample frame: 6 columns with base plates, 4 beams with end plates, a brace, 7 footings with rebar.\" The sample opens as a new model called \"Sample frame\". It does not change anything else, and it has nothing to undo. What is in it Grids 1, 2, 3 at 6000 mm and A, B at 7000 mm. Levels Level 1 at 0 and Level 2 at 4000. Six columns, W12x50, from Level 1 to Level 2 at every intersection, each on a 25 mm base plate with four anchor rods. Four beams, W16x26, at Level 2 along grid lines A and B, top of steel on the level, with a flush bolted end plate at both ends (eight in all). One brace, HSS4x4x1/4, from A/1 at Level 1 up to A/2 at Level 2. Six pad footings, 2000 x 2000 x 600, one under each column, with a hooked bottom mat both ways. One more footing beside the frame, 2400 x 1800 x 600, with six hooked starter bars standing up for a concrete pier. Things to try Click a beam. Read its Part mark, Assembly, Cut length and Cut back: the cut length is shorter than the 6000 mm grid spacing because each end plate cuts the beam back by half the column depth plus the plate thickness. Click a column. Under Holes it lists the holes the end plates made in its flange (\"8 holes from connections\"; the middle columns have 16, one set in each flange). In the Part marks list on the left, see that all four beams share one mark: they are the same piece of steel. The corner columns share a mark too, even though their holes face opposite ways, because a column turned end for end is the same piece. The middle columns, with holes in both flanges, get another mark. Click an end plate (or one of its bolts). The Properties panel shows the plate, bolt and weld settings, the bolts it makes (with their length worked out from the grip) and any edge-distance warnings. Open the Bill of materials tab. Parts, assemblies (a beam with its two end plates is one assembly), and bolts and anchor rods are listed with weights. You should see six part marks: c1 (the two middle columns) and c2 (the four corner columns), both W12x50 at 3975 mm; b1, four W16x26 beams at 5650 mm; br1, the brace; p1, eight PL20x180 end plates; and p2, six PL25x400 base plates. The fasteners are 64 bolts 20 x 70 A325 and 24 anchor rods 20 x 575. Open the Bending schedule tab. The six identical footings share bar marks; the pier footing adds its own. Click the brace and look at the Output group: Shop drawing is grayed out, because shop drawings cover I-shapes and plates only so far. Choose NC files. The zip holds a file for every I-shape and plate mark; the status bar lists the brace mark as not written. Choose IFC and open the file in any IFC viewer to check how your other software reads it. Use it as a starting point The sample is an ordinary model. Change it, then rename it in the Name field and save it under its new name with Ctrl+S.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"grids-and-levels","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Set up grids and levels","summary":"Type the grid lines, their spacings and the levels that every member is placed on.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["grid","grids","axes","gridlines","levels","elevations","storeys","floors","spacing","setup"],"headings":[{"id":"how-grids-work-here","text":"How grids work here","level":2},{"id":"set-the-grids","text":"Set the grids","level":2},{"id":"set-the-levels","text":"Set the levels","level":2},{"id":"how-levels-are-used","text":"How levels are used","level":2},{"id":"undo","text":"Undo","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery member in DetailCraft is placed on a grid intersection at a level. Set the grids and levels first; you can change them later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-grids-work-here\"\u003EHow grids work here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENumbered lines\u003C/strong\u003E run one way, \u003Cstrong\u003Elettered lines\u003C/strong\u003E the other. The names are only habit: a label can be any text up to 8 characters (1, 2, 3 or A1, A2, or 10, 20).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYou type the labels and the spacings between them, not coordinates. The first line of each set is at 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbered lines are lines of constant x; lettered lines are lines of constant y. So a beam from grid 1 to grid 2 runs along x, and a beam from A to B runs along y.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAll values are millimeters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new model starts with numbered lines 1 2 3 at 6000 6000, lettered lines A B at 6000, and two levels.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-grids\"\u003ESet the grids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrids\u003C/span\u003E if it is folded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENumbered lines\u003C/span\u003E, type the labels separated by spaces, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1 2 3 4 5\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpacings\u003C/span\u003E field below it, type one spacing for each gap, for example \u003Ccode\u003E6000 6000 7500 6000\u003C/code\u003E. Four gaps for five lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDo the same in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELettered lines\u003C/span\u003E and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpacings\u003C/span\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003EA B C\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E8000 8000\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply grids\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Grids updated.&quot; and the view zooms to the new grid. Commas work as separators too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf something does not add up, nothing changes and the status bar says why, for example &quot;Numbered grids: give one spacing between each pair of lines (4).&quot; Other checks: spacings must be positive millimeters, labels must differ, and there can be at most 60 lines each way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMembers keep their coordinates when grids move. If you change a spacing after placing steel, the steel stays where it was and is no longer on the grid. Change the grid first, or move members by editing their end coordinates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-levels\"\u003ESet the levels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach row has a level name and its elevation in millimeters. Edit them as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd level\u003C/span\u003E for another row. It is named &quot;Level 3&quot; (and so on) and placed 4000 mm above the highest level; change both if you need to.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo remove a level, choose the × button at the end of its row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply levels\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply levels\u003C/span\u003E. Levels are sorted by elevation when applied. Every level needs a name, two levels cannot share a name, and you must keep at least one level.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELevels can be below zero (a basement at \u003Ccode\u003E-3500\u003C/code\u003E) and do not need to be evenly spaced.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-levels-are-used\"\u003EHow levels are used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA column placed at a level runs up to the next level, or to the level you pick in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp to\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBeams and plates are placed between two intersections at the level you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFootings are placed with their top at the level you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E looks down at one level at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe IFC export makes one building storey per level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo\"\u003EUndo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGrid and level changes are edits like any other: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the previous grid back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/place-members\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/place-members\"\u003EPlace columns, beams and braces\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every member in DetailCraft is placed on a grid intersection at a level. Set the grids and levels first; you can change them later. How grids work here Numbered lines run one way, lettered lines the other. The names are only habit: a label can be any text up to 8 characters (1, 2, 3 or A1, A2, or 10, 20). You type the labels and the spacings between them, not coordinates. The first line of each set is at 0. Numbered lines are lines of constant x; lettered lines are lines of constant y. So a beam from grid 1 to grid 2 runs along x, and a beam from A to B runs along y. All values are millimeters. A new model starts with numbered lines 1 2 3 at 6000 6000, lettered lines A B at 6000, and two levels. Set the grids In the Model panel on the left, open Grids if it is folded. In Numbered lines, type the labels separated by spaces, for example 1 2 3 4 5. In the Spacings field below it, type one spacing for each gap, for example 6000 6000 7500 6000. Four gaps for five lines. Do the same in Lettered lines and its Spacings, for example A B C and 8000 8000. Choose Apply grids. The status bar says \"Grids updated.\" and the view zooms to the new grid. Commas work as separators too. If something does not add up, nothing changes and the status bar says why, for example \"Numbered grids: give one spacing between each pair of lines (4).\" Other checks: spacings must be positive millimeters, labels must differ, and there can be at most 60 lines each way. Members keep their coordinates when grids move. If you change a spacing after placing steel, the steel stays where it was and is no longer on the grid. Change the grid first, or move members by editing their end coordinates. Set the levels Open Levels in the Model panel. Each row has a level name and its elevation in millimeters. Edit them as you like. Choose Add level for another row. It is named \"Level 3\" (and so on) and placed 4000 mm above the highest level; change both if you need to. To remove a level, choose the × button at the end of its row. Choose Apply levels. Nothing changes until you choose Apply levels. Levels are sorted by elevation when applied. Every level needs a name, two levels cannot share a name, and you must keep at least one level. Levels can be below zero (a basement at -3500) and do not need to be evenly spaced. How levels are used A column placed at a level runs up to the next level, or to the level you pick in Up to. Beams and plates are placed between two intersections at the level you click. Footings are placed with their top at the level you click. Plan looks down at one level at a time. The IFC export makes one building storey per level. Undo Grid and level changes are edits like any other: Ctrl+Z puts the previous grid back. Next Place columns, beams and braces","lang":"en"},{"slug":"shear-tabs","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Shear tabs","summary":"Bolt a beam's web to a plate shop-welded to a column flange or web, also where the beam meets the column at an angle in plan or slopes.","section":"tasks","order":31,"keywords":["shear tab","single plate","fin plate","simple connection","web connection","skewed","skew","sloped","raked","angled beam","bolted","connect"],"headings":[{"id":"where-it-works","text":"Where it works","level":2},{"id":"add-shear-tabs","text":"Add shear tabs","level":2},{"id":"settings-mm","text":"Settings (mm)","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA shear tab (single plate, fin plate) is a plate welded to the column in the shop; on site the beam&#039;s web is bolted to it with one vertical row of bolts. DetailCraft places the tab, cuts the beam back clear of the column, drills the holes in the tab and the beam web, adds the bolts and the weld, and warns about edge distances and fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-works\"\u003EWhere it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA beam (I-shape or channel) whose end lies on the line of a \u003Cstrong\u003Evertical\u003C/strong\u003E I-shape column, meeting its \u003Cstrong\u003Eflange\u003C/strong\u003E or its \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESquare, skewed in plan, or sloped\u003C/strong\u003E: the tab follows the beam&#039;s web and the beam is cut back so its nearest corner keeps the gap to the column. A beam rising or falling at more than 60° is refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-shear-tabs\"\u003EAdd shear tabs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the beam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShear tabs\u003C/span\u003E (or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd shear tabs\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery free end of the beam that meets a column gets a tab. Each beam end takes one connection: a shear tab or an end plate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a tab (click it, or a bolt) to see and change its settings. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to similar\u003C/span\u003E copies it, as a shear tab, to every joint like it that has no connection yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings-mm\"\u003ESettings (mm)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStarts at\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe tab&#039;s thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E115\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far the tab reaches out from the column face, along the beam\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt diameter, Hole diameter, Bolt grade\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20, 22, A325\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs for end plates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt rows, Row pitch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3, 75\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe bolts in the vertical row\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdge distance (top and bottom bolts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E38\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom the top and bottom bolts to the tab&#039;s ends; the tab is rows × pitch + 2 × this deep\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt group raised by\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0 centers the bolts on the beam depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGap to the column\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E13\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear gap between the column and the beam&#039;s nearest corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt line from the column face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E65\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong the beam, from the column face to the bolts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt length allowance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdded to the grip (tab + beam web), rounded up to 5 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet weld size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth sides of the tab to the column\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe tab is part of the column&#039;s assembly (welded in the shop); the beam&#039;s assembly has its holes only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA skewed or sloped beam is cut further back than a square one so its nearest corner keeps the gap. DetailCraft cuts beams square: the fabricator cuts the end to suit. The tab&#039;s edge at the column is drawn square too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDetailCraft does not check strength. Warnings use EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3 minimums for edge distances and pitch, and say when the tab is deeper than the beam web between its fillets, or the bolts sit too close to the beam&#039;s cut end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/clash-check\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/clash-check\"\u003Eclash check\u003C/a\u003E leaves a tab and the members it joins out: they are meant to meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A shear tab (single plate, fin plate) is a plate welded to the column in the shop; on site the beam's web is bolted to it with one vertical row of bolts. DetailCraft places the tab, cuts the beam back clear of the column, drills the holes in the tab and the beam web, adds the bolts and the weld, and warns about edge distances and fit. Where it works A beam (I-shape or channel) whose end lies on the line of a vertical I-shape column, meeting its flange or its web. Square, skewed in plan, or sloped: the tab follows the beam's web and the beam is cut back so its nearest corner keeps the gap to the column. A beam rising or falling at more than 60° is refused. Add shear tabs Select the beam. In the Connect group, open More and choose Shear tabs (or choose Add shear tabs in the Properties panel). Every free end of the beam that meets a column gets a tab. Each beam end takes one connection: a shear tab or an end plate. Select a tab (click it, or a bolt) to see and change its settings. Apply to similar copies it, as a shear tab, to every joint like it that has no connection yet. Settings (mm) Setting Starts at Meaning Plate thickness 10 The tab's thickness Plate width 115 How far the tab reaches out from the column face, along the beam Bolt diameter, Hole diameter, Bolt grade 20, 22, A325 As for end plates Bolt rows, Row pitch 3, 75 The bolts in the vertical row Edge distance (top and bottom bolts) 38 From the top and bottom bolts to the tab's ends; the tab is rows × pitch + 2 × this deep Bolt group raised by 0 0 centers the bolts on the beam depth Gap to the column 13 Clear gap between the column and the beam's nearest corner Bolt line from the column face 65 Along the beam, from the column face to the bolts Bolt length allowance 30 Added to the grip (tab + beam web), rounded up to 5 mm Fillet weld size 6 Both sides of the tab to the column Good to know The tab is part of the column's assembly (welded in the shop); the beam's assembly has its holes only. A skewed or sloped beam is cut further back than a square one so its nearest corner keeps the gap. DetailCraft cuts beams square: the fabricator cuts the end to suit. The tab's edge at the column is drawn square too. DetailCraft does not check strength. Warnings use EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3 minimums for edge distances and pitch, and say when the tab is deeper than the beam web between its fillets, or the bolts sit too close to the beam's cut end. The clash check leaves a tab and the members it joins out: they are meant to meet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"splices","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Splices","summary":"Join two pieces of a beam or column end to end with bolted flange and web cover plates.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["splice","beam splice","column splice","cover plate","splice plate","flange plate","web plate","bolted splice","joint","lift","connect"],"headings":[{"id":"where-it-works","text":"Where it works","level":2},{"id":"add-splices","text":"Add splices","level":2},{"id":"settings-mm","text":"Settings (mm)","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA bolted splice joins two pieces of one I-shape end to end: a cover plate outside each flange and a plate on each side of the web, bolted through both pieces. DetailCraft cuts the pieces back to the gap, places the four plates, drills the holes in the plates and in both pieces, adds the bolts and warns about edge distances, spacing and fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-works\"\u003EWhere it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo beams, or two column lifts, of the \u003Cstrong\u003Esame I-shape profile\u003C/strong\u003E, meeting \u003Cstrong\u003Eend to end in line\u003C/strong\u003E: the ends no more than 50 mm apart, the pieces parallel and on one line (within 1 mm). Either piece may be drawn in either direction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELevel, sloped or vertical pieces all work.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo pieces of different depth or flange width are refused, as are channels, angles and tubes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-splices\"\u003EAdd splices\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the two pieces so they meet end to end. To split a member that is already drawn, draw it again as two pieces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one (or both) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplice\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery free joint of the selected pieces with another piece of the same profile gets a splice. The status bar says how many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the splice (click one of its plates) to change its settings in the right panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to similar (n)\u003C/span\u003E copies it to every other free joint between pieces of the same profile.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings-mm\"\u003ESettings (mm)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGap between the ends\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach piece is cut back by half of it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt diameter, hole, grade, length allowance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20, 22, A325, 30\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs for end plates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlange plate thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe plate is as wide as the flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlange bolt rows (each side)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERows across the flange on each side of the joint\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlange bolt pitch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E75\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong the member\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlange bolt gauge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBetween the two bolt lines; 0 = flange width less 80\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlange end distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E40\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMember end to first bolt, and last bolt to plate end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb plate thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne on each side of the web\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb bolt rows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp the web, centered\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb bolt columns (each side)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong the member on each side of the joint\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb bolt pitch, up the web / along the member\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E75, 75\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb end distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E40\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs for the flanges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb plate height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0 = the clear web between the fillets less 10 at top and bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA36\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlate lengths follow from the bolts: flange plate = gap + 2 × (2 × end distance + (rows − 1) × pitch); the web plates likewise with their columns. With the defaults the flange plates are 470 long.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plates belong to the \u003Cstrong\u003Efirst piece drawn\u003C/strong\u003E of the two (its properties call it the first piece): they show on its assembly drawing and ship with it, bolted, not welded. The second piece&#039;s assembly drawing shows only its holes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlange bolts grip the flange and its plate; web bolts grip the web and both web plates. The bolt lengths are listed separately in the properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWarnings use the same EN 1993-1-8 minimums as the other connections (end and edge distance 1.2 × hole, pitch 2.2 × hole along and 2.4 × hole across), plus a web plate deeper than the clear web and flange bolts too close to the web for the fillet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDetailCraft does not design the splice (no strength check), and does not make fill plates for pieces of different thickness.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopying both pieces copies their splice; deleting either piece deletes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A bolted splice joins two pieces of one I-shape end to end: a cover plate outside each flange and a plate on each side of the web, bolted through both pieces. DetailCraft cuts the pieces back to the gap, places the four plates, drills the holes in the plates and in both pieces, adds the bolts and warns about edge distances, spacing and fit. Where it works Two beams, or two column lifts, of the same I-shape profile, meeting end to end in line: the ends no more than 50 mm apart, the pieces parallel and on one line (within 1 mm). Either piece may be drawn in either direction. Level, sloped or vertical pieces all work. Two pieces of different depth or flange width are refused, as are channels, angles and tubes. Add splices Draw the two pieces so they meet end to end. To split a member that is already drawn, draw it again as two pieces. Select one (or both) and choose Splice in the Connect group's More menu. Every free joint of the selected pieces with another piece of the same profile gets a splice. The status bar says how many. Select the splice (click one of its plates) to change its settings in the right panel. Apply to similar (n) copies it to every other free joint between pieces of the same profile. Settings (mm) Setting Default What it does Gap between the ends 10 Each piece is cut back by half of it Bolt diameter, hole, grade, length allowance 20, 22, A325, 30 As for end plates Flange plate thickness 12 The plate is as wide as the flange Flange bolt rows (each side) 3 Rows across the flange on each side of the joint Flange bolt pitch 75 Along the member Flange bolt gauge 0 Between the two bolt lines; 0 = flange width less 80 Flange end distance 40 Member end to first bolt, and last bolt to plate end Web plate thickness 8 One on each side of the web Web bolt rows 3 Up the web, centered Web bolt columns (each side) 2 Along the member on each side of the joint Web bolt pitch, up the web / along the member 75, 75 Web end distance 40 As for the flanges Web plate height 0 0 = the clear web between the fillets less 10 at top and bottom Plate material A36 Text Plate lengths follow from the bolts: flange plate = gap + 2 × (2 × end distance + (rows − 1) × pitch); the web plates likewise with their columns. With the defaults the flange plates are 470 long. Good to know The plates belong to the first piece drawn of the two (its properties call it the first piece): they show on its assembly drawing and ship with it, bolted, not welded. The second piece's assembly drawing shows only its holes. Flange bolts grip the flange and its plate; web bolts grip the web and both web plates. The bolt lengths are listed separately in the properties. Warnings use the same EN 1993-1-8 minimums as the other connections (end and edge distance 1.2 × hole, pitch 2.2 × hole along and 2.4 × hole across), plus a web plate deeper than the clear web and flange bolts too close to the web for the fillet. DetailCraft does not design the splice (no strength check), and does not make fill plates for pieces of different thickness. Copying both pieces copies their splice; deleting either piece deletes it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"brace-gussets","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Brace gussets","summary":"Connect a brace end to a column or beam with a gusset plate: angles and flat bars bolted, tubes slotted and welded.","section":"tasks","order":34,"keywords":["gusset","gusset plate","brace","bracing","vertical bracing","angle brace","flat bar","strap","hss brace","knife plate","slotted tube","connect"],"headings":[{"id":"where-it-works","text":"Where it works","level":2},{"id":"add-gussets","text":"Add gussets","level":2},{"id":"what-detailcraft-works-out","text":"What DetailCraft works out","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA brace gusset is a plate shop-welded to the column (or beam) the brace lands on, in the plane of the brace. DetailCraft cuts the brace back clear of everything at the joint, places and sizes the gusset, and joins the brace to it: an \u003Cstrong\u003Eangle\u003C/strong\u003E or a \u003Cstrong\u003Eflat bar\u003C/strong\u003E with one line of bolts, a \u003Cstrong\u003Etube\u003C/strong\u003E (square, rectangular or round HSS) slotted over the gusset and welded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-works\"\u003EWhere it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe brace&#039;s end lies on the line of a column or a beam (within 1 mm), and the brace does not run along that line. A column is the support where there is one; otherwise the beam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe gusset lies in the plane of the brace and the support&#039;s line. An \u003Cstrong\u003Eangle\u003C/strong\u003E must have one leg in that plane, and a \u003Cstrong\u003Eflat bar\u003C/strong\u003E its face: turn the brace (its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation\u003C/span\u003E) until it does. With the angle&#039;s legs at 45° to the plane, DetailCraft says so and builds nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI-shapes and channels as braces are not connected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-gussets\"\u003EAdd gussets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one or more braces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGusset\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery end of the selected braces that lands on a column or beam and has no gusset gets one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a gusset (click the plate) to change its settings. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to similar (n)\u003C/span\u003E copies it to every free brace end with the same brace and support profiles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-detailcraft-works-out\"\u003EWhat DetailCraft works out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut back.\u003C/strong\u003E The brace is shortened at that end until its corners clear, by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrace clearance\u003C/span\u003E (25), every member at the joint (beside it, or past its end when the member ends there) and a base plate under the column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBolts (angles, flats).\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBolts\u003C/span\u003E (3) in one line along the brace, the first \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBolt end distance\u003C/span\u003E (40) from the cut end, at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBolt pitch\u003C/span\u003E (75). On an angle they go through the leg in the gusset&#039;s plane, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBolt gauge\u003C/span\u003E from the heel (0 = the middle of the leg); on a flat bar on its center line (or off it by the gauge). Bolt grip = the leg or flat + the gusset.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe gusset.\u003C/strong\u003E A rectangle: from the support&#039;s face out past the last bolt by the end distance, and along the support as far as the bolts need. It sits against the back of the angle&#039;s leg (or the flat&#039;s face). For a tube it runs through the tube&#039;s middle, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETube lap on the gusset\u003C/span\u003E (200) of the tube, with room for the welds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EJoints.\u003C/strong\u003E At a column base the gusset is carried down onto the base plate. Where a beam crosses the joint (a brace up into a beam-column corner), the gusset stays under or over the beam. If that leaves the bolts or the lap without their end distance, DetailCraft warns.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWelds.\u003C/strong\u003E The gusset to its support, both sides. A tube to the gusset, four lines along the lap.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe gusset belongs to the support&#039;s assembly (shop-welded). The brace ships with its holes drilled, and goes to its own NC file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA tube&#039;s slot is not cut in the model; the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/clash-check\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/clash-check\"\u003Eclash check\u003C/a\u003E leaves the brace and its gusset alone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe gusset is not welded to a beam it touches, and corner gussets welded to both beam and column, chamfered corners and the work-point layout of a Uniform Force Method connection are not made yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDetailCraft does not design the gusset or the bolts (no strength checks); the warnings are spacing and fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A brace gusset is a plate shop-welded to the column (or beam) the brace lands on, in the plane of the brace. DetailCraft cuts the brace back clear of everything at the joint, places and sizes the gusset, and joins the brace to it: an angle or a flat bar with one line of bolts, a tube (square, rectangular or round HSS) slotted over the gusset and welded. Where it works The brace's end lies on the line of a column or a beam (within 1 mm), and the brace does not run along that line. A column is the support where there is one; otherwise the beam. The gusset lies in the plane of the brace and the support's line. An angle must have one leg in that plane, and a flat bar its face: turn the brace (its Rotation) until it does. With the angle's legs at 45° to the plane, DetailCraft says so and builds nothing. I-shapes and channels as braces are not connected. Add gussets Select one or more braces. Choose Gusset in the Connect group's More menu. Every end of the selected braces that lands on a column or beam and has no gusset gets one. Select a gusset (click the plate) to change its settings. Apply to similar (n) copies it to every free brace end with the same brace and support profiles. What DetailCraft works out Cut back. The brace is shortened at that end until its corners clear, by Brace clearance (25), every member at the joint (beside it, or past its end when the member ends there) and a base plate under the column. Bolts (angles, flats). Bolts (3) in one line along the brace, the first Bolt end distance (40) from the cut end, at Bolt pitch (75). On an angle they go through the leg in the gusset's plane, Bolt gauge from the heel (0 = the middle of the leg); on a flat bar on its center line (or off it by the gauge). Bolt grip = the leg or flat + the gusset. The gusset. A rectangle: from the support's face out past the last bolt by the end distance, and along the support as far as the bolts need. It sits against the back of the angle's leg (or the flat's face). For a tube it runs through the tube's middle, under Tube lap on the gusset (200) of the tube, with room for the welds. Joints. At a column base the gusset is carried down onto the base plate. Where a beam crosses the joint (a brace up into a beam-column corner), the gusset stays under or over the beam. If that leaves the bolts or the lap without their end distance, DetailCraft warns. Welds. The gusset to its support, both sides. A tube to the gusset, four lines along the lap. Good to know The gusset belongs to the support's assembly (shop-welded). The brace ships with its holes drilled, and goes to its own NC file. A tube's slot is not cut in the model; the clash check leaves the brace and its gusset alone. The gusset is not welded to a beam it touches, and corner gussets welded to both beam and column, chamfered corners and the work-point layout of a Uniform Force Method connection are not made yet. DetailCraft does not design the gusset or the bolts (no strength checks); the warnings are spacing and fit.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"mitres","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Mitre joints","summary":"Join two I-shape members that meet at an angle: both ends cut on the plane that halves the angle, with end plates, bolts and stiffeners if you want them.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["mitre","miter","mitre joint","knee","haunch","portal frame","ridge","corner","angle cut","skew cut","end plate","bolted end plate","stiffener","connect"],"headings":[{"id":"where-it-works","text":"Where it works","level":2},{"id":"add-a-mitre","text":"Add a mitre","level":2},{"id":"the-numbered-fields-mm","text":"The numbered fields (mm)","level":2},{"id":"what-you-get","text":"What you get","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA mitre joins two I-shape members whose ends meet at an angle, such as the knee of a portal frame, a ridge, or two beams turning a corner in plan. DetailCraft cuts both ends on the plane that halves the angle between them, so the two cut faces match. If you want, it adds an end plate to each cut end, bolts the two plates together, and puts a pair of web stiffeners in each member.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-it-works\"\u003EWhere it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo \u003Cstrong\u003EI-shapes\u003C/strong\u003E (IPE, HEA, HEB, W and the like) whose \u003Cstrong\u003Eends meet on the same node\u003C/strong\u003E. Either member can be drawn in either direction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe angle between the members must be at least 15°. Members in line are refused: use a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/splices\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/splices\"\u003Esplice\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plain cut (no plates) works however the members meet. \u003Cstrong\u003EEnd plates\u003C/strong\u003E need the two members to meet in the plane of their webs, as at a portal knee or a ridge. Two beams turning a corner in plan meet in the plane of their flanges, so they get the plain cut only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMembers of different sizes are joined, but with a warning: their cut faces do not match.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-mitre\"\u003EAdd a mitre\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the two members: click one, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMitre\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. The Mitre dialog opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the numbered fields. The drawing beside them is made from your numbers as you type, and its numbered circles show what each field sets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd the mitre\u003C/span\u003E. If you selected more than two members, every free joint among them gets the same mitre.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change a mitre later, select it (click one of its plates) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMitre dialog with preview\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel, or change its settings there directly. A plain cut has no plates to click: select its two members and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMitre\u003C/span\u003E again, and the dialog opens on that mitre. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to similar (n)\u003C/span\u003E copies it to every free joint between members of the same two profiles at the same angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-numbered-fields-mm\"\u003EThe numbered fields (mm)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENo.\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGap between the plates (or the cut ends)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear gap at the joint. Each side keeps half of it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd plates, plate thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eon, 20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn end plate welded all around each cut end. Untick for a plain cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt rows, row pitch, gauge, outside row\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4, 0, 0, off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPitch 0 spreads the rows evenly between the flanges. Gauge 0 = half the plate width, at least 2.4 × the hole. The outside row goes in the plate&#039;s extension past the outside flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate past the outside flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far the plates reach past the outside flange, along the cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate past the inside flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same on the inside\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb stiffeners, distance past the cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eoff, 50\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA pair in each member, both sides of the web, square to the member, this far past the far end of its cut\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStiffener thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBolts\u003C/span\u003E: bolt diameter, hole, grade and length allowance (20, 22, A325, 30), as for end plates. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlates and welds\u003C/span\u003E: plate width (0 = the flange width), fillet weld size (8) and plate material (A36).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-get\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe cuts.\u003C/strong\u003E Each member&#039;s end is cut on its plane. Its length in lists, drawings and NC files is the \u003Cstrong\u003Eoverall length\u003C/strong\u003E, from its square end to the farthest point of the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe plates\u003C/strong\u003E belong to their own member: each member&#039;s assembly is the member, its end plate and its stiffeners. The bolts are site bolts of the joint: the bill of materials counts them once, and each member&#039;s assembly drawing lists them as loose bolts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrawings\u003C/strong\u003E show the slanted ends and the plates; the shop drawing of a mitred member notes each cut&#039;s angle from square.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENC files\u003C/strong\u003E (DSTV) carry the skew cut angles in the header, as the standard asks, and contours that follow the cut. Check them in your shop&#039;s own NC viewer before cutting, as for every NC file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC\u003C/strong\u003E clips each member&#039;s extrusion by its cut plane, so other programs show the true cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarks:\u003C/strong\u003E a mitred member is a different piece from a square-cut one of the same length. Two members with the same cuts share a mark, as the two rafters of a ridge do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe mitre stays live. Move or resize either member and the cut, plates and bolts follow. Delete either member and the mitre goes, and the other member&#039;s end is square again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWarnings use the same EN 1993-1-8 minimums as the other connections (end and edge distance 1.2 × hole, pitch 2.2 × hole, gauge 2.4 × hole), plus bolt rows too close to a flange, plates narrower than the flanges and stiffeners with no room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe values are starting values, not a design. DetailCraft does not check the connection&#039;s strength, and it makes no haunch, no welded mitre with a butt weld, and no stiffeners in line with the other member&#039;s flanges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A mitre joins two I-shape members whose ends meet at an angle, such as the knee of a portal frame, a ridge, or two beams turning a corner in plan. DetailCraft cuts both ends on the plane that halves the angle between them, so the two cut faces match. If you want, it adds an end plate to each cut end, bolts the two plates together, and puts a pair of web stiffeners in each member. Where it works Two I-shapes (IPE, HEA, HEB, W and the like) whose ends meet on the same node. Either member can be drawn in either direction. The angle between the members must be at least 15°. Members in line are refused: use a splice. The plain cut (no plates) works however the members meet. End plates need the two members to meet in the plane of their webs, as at a portal knee or a ridge. Two beams turning a corner in plan meet in the plane of their flanges, so they get the plain cut only. Members of different sizes are joined, but with a warning: their cut faces do not match. Add a mitre Select the two members: click one, then Shift+click the other. Choose Mitre in the Connect group's More menu. The Mitre dialog opens. Set the numbered fields. The drawing beside them is made from your numbers as you type, and its numbered circles show what each field sets. Choose Add the mitre. If you selected more than two members, every free joint among them gets the same mitre. To change a mitre later, select it (click one of its plates) and choose Mitre dialog with preview in the right panel, or change its settings there directly. A plain cut has no plates to click: select its two members and choose Mitre again, and the dialog opens on that mitre. Apply to similar (n) copies it to every free joint between members of the same two profiles at the same angle. The numbered fields (mm) No. Setting Default What it does 1 Gap between the plates (or the cut ends) 0 Clear gap at the joint. Each side keeps half of it 2 End plates, plate thickness on, 20 An end plate welded all around each cut end. Untick for a plain cut 3 Bolt rows, row pitch, gauge, outside row 4, 0, 0, off Pitch 0 spreads the rows evenly between the flanges. Gauge 0 = half the plate width, at least 2.4 × the hole. The outside row goes in the plate's extension past the outside flange 4 Plate past the outside flange 0 How far the plates reach past the outside flange, along the cut 5 Plate past the inside flange 0 The same on the inside 6 Web stiffeners, distance past the cut off, 50 A pair in each member, both sides of the web, square to the member, this far past the far end of its cut 7 Stiffener thickness 10 Under Bolts: bolt diameter, hole, grade and length allowance (20, 22, A325, 30), as for end plates. Under Plates and welds: plate width (0 = the flange width), fillet weld size (8) and plate material (A36). What you get The cuts. Each member's end is cut on its plane. Its length in lists, drawings and NC files is the overall length, from its square end to the farthest point of the cut. The plates belong to their own member: each member's assembly is the member, its end plate and its stiffeners. The bolts are site bolts of the joint: the bill of materials counts them once, and each member's assembly drawing lists them as loose bolts. Drawings show the slanted ends and the plates; the shop drawing of a mitred member notes each cut's angle from square. NC files (DSTV) carry the skew cut angles in the header, as the standard asks, and contours that follow the cut. Check them in your shop's own NC viewer before cutting, as for every NC file. IFC clips each member's extrusion by its cut plane, so other programs show the true cut. Marks: a mitred member is a different piece from a square-cut one of the same length. Two members with the same cuts share a mark, as the two rafters of a ridge do. Good to know The mitre stays live. Move or resize either member and the cut, plates and bolts follow. Delete either member and the mitre goes, and the other member's end is square again. Warnings use the same EN 1993-1-8 minimums as the other connections (end and edge distance 1.2 × hole, pitch 2.2 × hole, gauge 2.4 × hole), plus bolt rows too close to a flange, plates narrower than the flanges and stiffeners with no room. The values are starting values, not a design. DetailCraft does not check the connection's strength, and it makes no haunch, no welded mitre with a butt weld, and no stiffeners in line with the other member's flanges.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"stairs","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Stairs","summary":"Place a straight steel stair from its rise, going and width: two stringers and tread plates, one welded assembly.","section":"tasks","order":36,"keywords":["stair","stairs","staircase","stringer","tread","riser","going","nosing","flight","steel stair","ibc 1011","blondel"],"headings":[{"id":"place-a-stair","text":"Place a stair","level":2},{"id":"what-detailcraft-works-out","text":"What DetailCraft works out","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA straight steel stair: two stringers (channels with their toes in, or flat bars on edge) and a plate tread at every step, welded between them. DetailCraft works out the number of risers from the rise, checks them against the building code&#039;s limits, and makes the stair one shop assembly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-stair\"\u003EPlace a stair\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStair\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the stair on the right: the \u003Cstrong\u003Edirection of climb\u003C/strong\u003E (0° climbs along +x, 90° along +y), the \u003Cstrong\u003Erise\u003C/strong\u003E (0 = up to the next level), the \u003Cstrong\u003Egoing\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003Eclear width\u003C/strong\u003E between the stringers, the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehighest riser allowed\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003Estringer\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003Etread plate thickness\u003C/strong\u003E, the \u003Cstrong\u003Enosing\u003C/strong\u003E and the material.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a grid intersection: that is the foot of the first riser, on the stair&#039;s center line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says how many risers it made. Select the stair (click a stringer or a tread) to change any of its settings, its position or its phase; the properties also show the risers, treads, run, pitch and 2 × riser + going.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-detailcraft-works-out\"\u003EWhat DetailCraft works out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERisers.\u003C/strong\u003E As few as keep each riser at or under the highest allowed: a 3000 mm rise at 178 mm or less gives 17 risers of 176.5 mm. There is one tread fewer than risers (the top floor is the last step).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETreads.\u003C/strong\u003E A plate going + nosing deep (280 + 25 = PL6x305 by default) and as long as the clear width, its top at each riser height, its front edge (the nosing) in front of the riser line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStringers.\u003C/strong\u003E At the stair&#039;s pitch, from where they meet the lower floor to where they meet the upper floor, just outside the clear width, their top edge 50 mm above the line of the nosings. A channel&#039;s toes face into the stair; a flat bar such as PL12x250 stands on edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWelds.\u003C/strong\u003E Each tread to both stringers. The treads and the right stringer are welded to the left stringer, so the stair gets one assembly mark (ST1) and its own assembly drawing; the stringers get part marks st…, the treads p….\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecks.\u003C/strong\u003E Warnings when a riser is outside 4 to 7 in. (102 to 178 mm) or the going is under 11 in. (279 mm), the limits of IBC 2021 section 1011.5.2, when 2 × riser + going is outside 600 to 650 mm (a common comfort rule), and when the stringer is too shallow to carry the treads. An unknown stringer profile stops the stair from being built.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove, rotate, mirror, copy, paste, delete and phases work on a stair as on a member. A rotation turns the climb; a mirror sends it the mirrored way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStringers export to IFC as IfcMember and treads as IfcPlate; both go to NC files and the bill of materials. The clash check leaves a stair&#039;s own parts alone (they are welded together).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe stringers are cut square: their end cuts against the floors, landings, handrails, a toe plate and grating treads are not made yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDetailCraft does not design the stair: the stringers and treads are not checked for strength or deflection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A straight steel stair: two stringers (channels with their toes in, or flat bars on edge) and a plate tread at every step, welded between them. DetailCraft works out the number of risers from the rise, checks them against the building code's limits, and makes the stair one shop assembly. Place a stair Choose Stair in the Place group, or press S. Set the stair on the right: the direction of climb (0° climbs along +x, 90° along +y), the rise (0 = up to the next level), the going, the clear width between the stringers, the highest riser allowed, the stringer, the tread plate thickness, the nosing and the material. Click a grid intersection: that is the foot of the first riser, on the stair's center line. The status bar says how many risers it made. Select the stair (click a stringer or a tread) to change any of its settings, its position or its phase; the properties also show the risers, treads, run, pitch and 2 × riser + going. What DetailCraft works out Risers. As few as keep each riser at or under the highest allowed: a 3000 mm rise at 178 mm or less gives 17 risers of 176.5 mm. There is one tread fewer than risers (the top floor is the last step). Treads. A plate going + nosing deep (280 + 25 = PL6x305 by default) and as long as the clear width, its top at each riser height, its front edge (the nosing) in front of the riser line. Stringers. At the stair's pitch, from where they meet the lower floor to where they meet the upper floor, just outside the clear width, their top edge 50 mm above the line of the nosings. A channel's toes face into the stair; a flat bar such as PL12x250 stands on edge. Welds. Each tread to both stringers. The treads and the right stringer are welded to the left stringer, so the stair gets one assembly mark (ST1) and its own assembly drawing; the stringers get part marks st…, the treads p…. Checks. Warnings when a riser is outside 4 to 7 in. (102 to 178 mm) or the going is under 11 in. (279 mm), the limits of IBC 2021 section 1011.5.2, when 2 × riser + going is outside 600 to 650 mm (a common comfort rule), and when the stringer is too shallow to carry the treads. An unknown stringer profile stops the stair from being built. Good to know Move, rotate, mirror, copy, paste, delete and phases work on a stair as on a member. A rotation turns the climb; a mirror sends it the mirrored way. Stringers export to IFC as IfcMember and treads as IfcPlate; both go to NC files and the bill of materials. The clash check leaves a stair's own parts alone (they are welded together). The stringers are cut square: their end cuts against the floors, landings, handrails, a toe plate and grating treads are not made yet. DetailCraft does not design the stair: the stringers and treads are not checked for strength or deflection.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"frame-analysis","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Analyse the frame","summary":"Forces, reactions and deflections of the beams, columns and braces as drawn, under load cases and combinations you set. An analysis aid, not a code check; the design checks are a separate step.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["analysis","structural analysis","frame analysis","stiffness method","loads","load case","combination","dead load","live load","self weight","own weight","reactions","bending moment","shear","axial force","deflection","deflected shape","supports","pinned","fixed","release","report","pdf","csv","craftscript","frame3d"],"headings":[{"id":"run-it","text":"Run it","level":2},{"id":"load-cases-and-combinations","text":"Load cases and combinations","level":2},{"id":"what-the-frame-is-made-of","text":"What the frame is made of","level":2},{"id":"signs","text":"Signs","level":2},{"id":"the-report","text":"The report","level":2},{"id":"method","text":"Method","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab at the bottom finds the forces in every beam, column and brace of the model, the reactions at the column bases and how far the members bend, for the loads you give it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an \u003Cstrong\u003Eanalysis aid, not a code check\u003C/strong\u003E. The analysis applies no design code: it does not size members, check capacities or connections, or pick load factors. An engineer checks the model, its loads and supports, and decides what the numbers mean. For member checks to ASCE 7 / AISC 360 or EN 1990 / EN 1993-1-1 see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\"\u003ECheck steel members to a design code\u003C/a\u003E, a separate step that is an aid too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-it\"\u003ERun it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one or more members in the view, pick a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECase\u003C/span\u003E, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad\u003C/span\u003E and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDirection\u003C/span\u003E, type the value and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd load\u003C/span\u003E. A load spread along a member is in kN/m, a point load in kN. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDown (gravity)\u003C/span\u003E is the usual one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun analysis\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table lists each member&#039;s largest axial force N, shears Vz and Vy, bending moments My and Mz, torsion T and its deflection, largest moment first; click a member&#039;s name to select it. Below are the reactions at each support. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOn the model\u003C/span\u003E draws the deflected shape, or one action as a diagram beside each member with its largest value written at its place. The results go out of date as soon as the model changes: run it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery load, case and setting is one undo step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and is saved in the model file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-cases-and-combinations\"\u003ELoad cases and combinations\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe analysis starts with three: \u003Cstrong\u003EDead\u003C/strong\u003E (the members&#039; own weight, from their drawn sections and 7850 kg/m³), \u003Cstrong\u003ELive\u003C/strong\u003E (empty) and \u003Cstrong\u003EDead + live\u003C/strong\u003E. Add cases of your own (wind, snow …) and combinations: a combination adds the cases up with the factors you type. The analysis is linear, so each case is solved once and combinations are sums. Type the factors yourself, or choose a design code and let \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake combinations\u003C/span\u003E write the code&#039;s (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\"\u003ECheck steel members to a design code\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-frame-is-made-of\"\u003EWhat the frame is made of\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBeams, columns and braces, each on its work line (the line you drew), with the section&#039;s real properties worked out from its outline. Plates, stairs, footings and bolts are not part of the frame.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA member is split where another member&#039;s end lands on it, so a beam framing into a column at mid-height joins the column there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColumn bases\u003C/strong\u003E: fixed (the start) or pinned (held in place and against twisting, free to turn). A base plate always makes a support.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeam ends\u003C/strong\u003E: an end plate is rigid, a shear tab is pinned. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam ends with no connection\u003C/span\u003E sets the rest. \u003Cstrong\u003EBraces\u003C/strong\u003E are pinned at both ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one member to give its ends, or a column&#039;s base, a rule of its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESteel everywhere: E = 200 GPa, G = 77 GPa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the frame can move without bending anything — a mechanism, such as pinned bases with no bracing in one direction — the run stops and says where and which way; hold it there with a support, a rigid joint or a brace.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"signs\"\u003ESigns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EN &gt; 0 is tension. My &gt; 0 puts the member&#039;s local −z side in tension: a beam sagging under gravity has My &gt; 0. Mz &gt; 0 puts the local −y side in tension. Deflection is measured from the straight line between the member&#039;s ends. A member&#039;s local z points up (for a column: along X); its local axes turn with the member&#039;s rotation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-report\"\u003EThe report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E hold everything: the assumptions the frame was built on, sections, cases and combinations, then for each case and combination the reactions (with the sums of loads and reactions, which balance), the member forces and the displacements, and an envelope of every case. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport as CraftScript\u003C/span\u003E writes the same frame as a script for PartCraft&#039;s Script workspace, where the library function \u003Ccode\u003Eframe3d\u003C/code\u003E solves it step by step in readable code.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"method\"\u003EMethod\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe direct stiffness method, linear and first order unless you tick Second order (P-Delta): two-node beam-columns with axial, torsion and two bending stiffnesses (no shear deformation, no warping), end releases by static condensation, loads along members through the members&#039; own shape functions. Its answers are checked against the classic textbook cases (cantilevers, simple, fixed and continuous beams, a portal frame, a grid in torsion, a truss).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESecond order (P-Delta).\u003C/strong\u003E Tick \u003Cem\u003ESecond order (P-Delta)\u003C/em\u003E beside Run: each combination is solved again with the members&#039; axial forces acting on the deflected frame (each member cut into 4 pieces, so the bending of a member between its ends counts too), until the axial forces settle. The result line gives the number of passes and how much the sway grew; the moments, deflections, design checks and report then use these results. Above the frame&#039;s elastic critical load the run stops and says so. It is elastic with small rotations, flexural only, with no stiffness reduction or notional loads (not the AISC direct analysis method by itself); slabs, walls and rigid floors stay first order.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENatural frequencies (finite elements).\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFinite elements: natural frequencies\u003C/span\u003E block under the results finds the lowest modes of the same model with the finite element engine: beams with their end releases, slabs and walls as flat four-node shells, rigid floors, supports. Choose how many modes (3, 6, 10 or 20) and the slab element (the four-node shell for thick or thin slabs, or the thin-slab one) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind the frequencies\u003C/span\u003E. The table gives each mode&#039;s frequency in Hz, its period in seconds and its effective modal mass along X, Y and Z as a share of the mass that can move; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow on the model\u003C/span\u003E draws any mode the way the deflected shape is drawn. Mass is the material density times the volume: loads do not add mass, and a material with no density has none. The report (PDF, CSV) carries the table. It is elastic and small-movement only, and no seismic or wind load is made from a code here. Not a code check; your engineer reviews the results.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResponse spectrum, time history and pushover.\u003C/strong\u003E Three more blocks sit below the frequencies, using the same finite element model. Mass for all of them is the material density plus the downward loads of the cases you tick, each times a factor you type (for example Dead at 1 and Live at the share you count as mass).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResponse spectrum\u003C/span\u003E reads each mode off a spectrum you give (a table of period and acceleration, or the ASCE 7 shape from SDS, SD1 and TL; the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse the Seismic block values\u003C/span\u003E button copies those from the Seismic block) and combines the modes by CQC, SRSS, the ten percent rule or an absolute sum, then puts the X, Y and Z directions together (100 % + 30 % or SRSS). It gives the base shear, storey drifts, member end forces and a mode table that warns when the modes used carry less than 90 % of the mass. You can scale the results by a factor or up to a base shear you type; the block never computes that base shear itself. Results have no sign.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime history\u003C/span\u003E runs a ground acceleration by modal superposition (exact for the record&#039;s straight lines between points) or by direct integration (Newmark average acceleration with Rayleigh damping). The input is a made-up sine or pulse (always labelled synthetic), a record you paste, or a text file you open (time and acceleration, one acceleration per line with a step, or a PEER-style file with a DT= header; in g or m/s²). It gives the roof displacement and base shear against time, the drift of each level, and the envelope of the member end forces. Models with a very long record keep it for the session only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPushover\u003C/span\u003E pushes the roof sideways in small steps (pattern like the first mode, or uniform), with plastic hinges at the ends of members that have an I shape. A hinge yields at the plastic moment, which falls as the axial force grows (worked out from the drawn shape, fillets ignored); hardening is optional, P-delta is optional, and a gravity combination can be applied first. It gives the capacity curve (base shear against roof displacement), the first hinge, and the state and plastic rotation of each hinge. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun the nonlinear time history\u003C/span\u003E runs the same hinges under the Time history block&#039;s record on small models. Hinges form at member ends only (put a node where one should form inside a span); a member released at both ends gets none, and yielding and buckling of braces are not modelled; slabs and walls stay elastic; no acceptance criteria are given. Not a code check; your engineer reviews the results. The settings are saved with the model, and the report carries the tables.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet: buckling analysis, a missing-mass correction for the response spectrum, record scaling, acceptance criteria for hinges (dynamic response is there, above), area and wind loads made for you (earthquake forces on floors are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/concrete-and-seismic\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/concrete-and-seismic\"\u003ECheck concrete members and make seismic loads\u003C/a\u003E), member offsets, tension-only braces. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Analysis tab at the bottom finds the forces in every beam, column and brace of the model, the reactions at the column bases and how far the members bend, for the loads you give it. It is an analysis aid, not a code check. The analysis applies no design code: it does not size members, check capacities or connections, or pick load factors. An engineer checks the model, its loads and supports, and decides what the numbers mean. For member checks to ASCE 7 / AISC 360 or EN 1990 / EN 1993-1-1 see Check steel members to a design code, a separate step that is an aid too. Run it Open the Analysis tab. Select one or more members in the view, pick a Case, a Load and a Direction, type the value and press Add load. A load spread along a member is in kN/m, a point load in kN. Down (gravity) is the usual one. Press Run analysis. The table lists each member's largest axial force N, shears Vz and Vy, bending moments My and Mz, torsion T and its deflection, largest moment first; click a member's name to select it. Below are the reactions at each support. On the model draws the deflected shape, or one action as a diagram beside each member with its largest value written at its place. The results go out of date as soon as the model changes: run it again. Every load, case and setting is one undo step (Ctrl+Z) and is saved in the model file. Load cases and combinations The analysis starts with three: Dead (the members' own weight, from their drawn sections and 7850 kg/m³), Live (empty) and Dead + live. Add cases of your own (wind, snow …) and combinations: a combination adds the cases up with the factors you type. The analysis is linear, so each case is solved once and combinations are sums. Type the factors yourself, or choose a design code and let Make combinations write the code's (Check steel members to a design code). What the frame is made of Beams, columns and braces, each on its work line (the line you drew), with the section's real properties worked out from its outline. Plates, stairs, footings and bolts are not part of the frame. A member is split where another member's end lands on it, so a beam framing into a column at mid-height joins the column there. Column bases: fixed (the start) or pinned (held in place and against twisting, free to turn). A base plate always makes a support. Beam ends: an end plate is rigid, a shear tab is pinned. Beam ends with no connection sets the rest. Braces are pinned at both ends. Select one member to give its ends, or a column's base, a rule of its own. Steel everywhere: E = 200 GPa, G = 77 GPa. If the frame can move without bending anything — a mechanism, such as pinned bases with no bracing in one direction — the run stops and says where and which way; hold it there with a support, a rigid joint or a brace. Signs N \u003E 0 is tension. My \u003E 0 puts the member's local −z side in tension: a beam sagging under gravity has My \u003E 0. Mz \u003E 0 puts the local −y side in tension. Deflection is measured from the straight line between the member's ends. A member's local z points up (for a column: along X); its local axes turn with the member's rotation. The report Report (PDF) and Report (CSV) hold everything: the assumptions the frame was built on, sections, cases and combinations, then for each case and combination the reactions (with the sums of loads and reactions, which balance), the member forces and the displacements, and an envelope of every case. Export as CraftScript writes the same frame as a script for PartCraft's Script workspace, where the library function frame3d solves it step by step in readable code. Method The direct stiffness method, linear and first order unless you tick Second order (P-Delta): two-node beam-columns with axial, torsion and two bending stiffnesses (no shear deformation, no warping), end releases by static condensation, loads along members through the members' own shape functions. Its answers are checked against the classic textbook cases (cantilevers, simple, fixed and continuous beams, a portal frame, a grid in torsion, a truss). Second order (P-Delta). Tick Second order (P-Delta) beside Run: each combination is solved again with the members' axial forces acting on the deflected frame (each member cut into 4 pieces, so the bending of a member between its ends counts too), until the axial forces settle. The result line gives the number of passes and how much the sway grew; the moments, deflections, design checks and report then use these results. Above the frame's elastic critical load the run stops and says so. It is elastic with small rotations, flexural only, with no stiffness reduction or notional loads (not the AISC direct analysis method by itself); slabs, walls and rigid floors stay first order. Natural frequencies (finite elements). The Finite elements: natural frequencies block under the results finds the lowest modes of the same model with the finite element engine: beams with their end releases, slabs and walls as flat four-node shells, rigid floors, supports. Choose how many modes (3, 6, 10 or 20) and the slab element (the four-node shell for thick or thin slabs, or the thin-slab one) and press Find the frequencies. The table gives each mode's frequency in Hz, its period in seconds and its effective modal mass along X, Y and Z as a share of the mass that can move; Show on the model draws any mode the way the deflected shape is drawn. Mass is the material density times the volume: loads do not add mass, and a material with no density has none. The report (PDF, CSV) carries the table. It is elastic and small-movement only, and no seismic or wind load is made from a code here. Not a code check; your engineer reviews the results. Response spectrum, time history and pushover. Three more blocks sit below the frequencies, using the same finite element model. Mass for all of them is the material density plus the downward loads of the cases you tick, each times a factor you type (for example Dead at 1 and Live at the share you count as mass). Response spectrum reads each mode off a spectrum you give (a table of period and acceleration, or the ASCE 7 shape from SDS, SD1 and TL; the Use the Seismic block values button copies those from the Seismic block) and combines the modes by CQC, SRSS, the ten percent rule or an absolute sum, then puts the X, Y and Z directions together (100 % + 30 % or SRSS). It gives the base shear, storey drifts, member end forces and a mode table that warns when the modes used carry less than 90 % of the mass. You can scale the results by a factor or up to a base shear you type; the block never computes that base shear itself. Results have no sign. Time history runs a ground acceleration by modal superposition (exact for the record's straight lines between points) or by direct integration (Newmark average acceleration with Rayleigh damping). The input is a made-up sine or pulse (always labelled synthetic), a record you paste, or a text file you open (time and acceleration, one acceleration per line with a step, or a PEER-style file with a DT= header; in g or m/s²). It gives the roof displacement and base shear against time, the drift of each level, and the envelope of the member end forces. Models with a very long record keep it for the session only. Pushover pushes the roof sideways in small steps (pattern like the first mode, or uniform), with plastic hinges at the ends of members that have an I shape. A hinge yields at the plastic moment, which falls as the axial force grows (worked out from the drawn shape, fillets ignored); hardening is optional, P-delta is optional, and a gravity combination can be applied first. It gives the capacity curve (base shear against roof displacement), the first hinge, and the state and plastic rotation of each hinge. Run the nonlinear time history runs the same hinges under the Time history block's record on small models. Hinges form at member ends only (put a node where one should form inside a span); a member released at both ends gets none, and yielding and buckling of braces are not modelled; slabs and walls stay elastic; no acceptance criteria are given. Not a code check; your engineer reviews the results. The settings are saved with the model, and the report carries the tables. Not built yet: buckling analysis, a missing-mass correction for the response spectrum, record scaling, acceptance criteria for hinges (dynamic response is there, above), area and wind loads made for you (earthquake forces on floors are in Check concrete members and make seismic loads), member offsets, tension-only braces. See Not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"place-members","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Place columns, beams and braces","summary":"Pick a profile, material and position, then click grid intersections or type them in to place steel members.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["column","beam","brace","member","place","profile","material","rotation","position","offset","insert","add by grid"],"headings":[{"id":"choose-a-tool-and-its-options","text":"Choose a tool and its options","level":2},{"id":"place-by-clicking","text":"Place by clicking","level":2},{"id":"a-column","text":"A column","level":3},{"id":"a-beam","text":"A beam","level":3},{"id":"a-brace","text":"A brace","level":3},{"id":"place-by-grid-without-clicking","text":"Place by grid, without clicking","level":2},{"id":"change-a-member-after-placing-it","text":"Change a member after placing it","level":2},{"id":"delete-a-member","text":"Delete a member","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMembers are placed on grid intersections: a column on one, a beam or a brace between two. Set up \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/grids-and-levels\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/grids-and-levels\"\u003Egrids and levels\u003C/a\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-a-tool-and-its-options\"\u003EChoose a tool and its options\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrace\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right becomes &quot;Place a column&quot; (or beam, or brace) and shows the options for the next member:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E: pick from the list. Sizes are grouped by catalog (W shapes, Channels, Angles, HSS, IPE, HEA, HEB, UB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS, CHS, and your Custom profiles). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/profile-catalog\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/profile-catalog\"\u003EProfile catalog\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E: A992, A36, A572 Gr 50, A500 Gr C, S235, S275 or S355.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosition\u003C/span\u003E: where the section sits on the line between the two points: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop of steel on the line\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECentered on the line\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom on the line\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E: turns the section about the member&#039;s own axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp to\u003C/span\u003E (columns only): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe next level up\u003C/span\u003E, or a level name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBelow the options, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E box lists the chosen section&#039;s dimensions, area, mass per meter and the catalog it comes from.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe defaults are W12x50 in A992 for columns (centered), W16x26 in A992 for beams (top of steel on the line) and HSS6x6x1/4 in A500 Gr C for braces (centered). The options stay set until you change them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-by-clicking\"\u003EPlace by clicking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"a-column\"\u003EA column\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and pick the level the column starts at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer near an intersection. The nearest dot lights up and the status bar names it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. The column runs from that level to the next level up (or to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp to\u003C/span\u003E level). The status bar says, for example, &quot;Column W12x50 at A/1, Level 1 to Level 2.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep clicking to place more. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when you are done.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf there is no level above the one you clicked, the status bar says &quot;There is no level above Level 2 for the column to reach. Add a level first.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"a-beam\"\u003EA beam\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and pick the level of the beam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first intersection. The status bar says &quot;From A/1 at Level 2. Now click the other end (Esc cancels).&quot; A dashed line follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the second intersection. The beam appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick again to start the next beam, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after the first click cancels just that point; a second \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the tool away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"a-brace\"\u003EA brace\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA brace runs between two intersections at different levels, so it is easiest in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E and pick the lettered grid line the brace is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrace\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the bottom end, then the top end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EElevations exist only for lettered grid lines. For a brace in a frame along a numbered line (from A to B), use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E form below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen, tap close to the intersection: a tap picks the nearest dot within a short distance and does nothing if no dot is near. There is no highlight before you tap, so check the status bar message after each tap. One-finger drags turn or pan the view instead of placing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-by-grid-without-clicking\"\u003EPlace by grid, without clicking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery placing tool has an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E form at the bottom of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel. It works with the keyboard alone and does not need the 3D view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the tool, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E, pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom grid (letter)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom grid (number)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom level\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETo grid (letter)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETo grid (number)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETo level\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a column or a footing the form has one point only: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid (letter)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid (number)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-member-after-placing-it\"\u003EChange a member after placing it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the member.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosition\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo move it, edit the x, y and z of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnds (x, y, z mm)\u003C/span\u003E. The grid name of each end shows underneath, or its coordinates when it is off the grid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is one undo step. Below the fields you see the member&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart mark\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut length\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWeight\u003C/span\u003E (gross: holes and copes are not taken off) and which catalog the profile comes from.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMembers cannot be dragged or arrayed yet. To make another member like it, copy and paste it, or duplicate it (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-a-member\"\u003EDelete a member\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E. Connections on it go too. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings everything back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhich way a column faces.\u003C/strong\u003E At \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E 0, a column&#039;s flanges face along x, toward the neighboring numbered grid lines. A beam from grid 1 to grid 2 frames into a flange; a beam from A to B frames into the web. End plates are built only into flanges, so if your beams run from A to B, give those columns a rotation of 90.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeams at a level hang below it\u003C/strong\u003E with the default \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop of steel on the line\u003C/span\u003E. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECentered on the line\u003C/span\u003E if your levels are member centerlines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColumns run level to level.\u003C/strong\u003E For a column through two storeys, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUp to\u003C/span\u003E to the higher level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESloped beams\u003C/strong\u003E are allowed (pick ends at different levels), but end plates are not built on them yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENext: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/end-plates\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/end-plates\"\u003EConnect beams to columns with end plates\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Members are placed on grid intersections: a column on one, a beam or a brace between two. Set up grids and levels first. Choose a tool and its options Choose Column (C), Beam (B) or Brace (R) in the Place group. The Properties panel on the right becomes \"Place a column\" (or beam, or brace) and shows the options for the next member: Profile: pick from the list. Sizes are grouped by catalog (W shapes, Channels, Angles, HSS, IPE, HEA, HEB, UB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS, CHS, and your Custom profiles). See Profile catalog. Material: A992, A36, A572 Gr 50, A500 Gr C, S235, S275 or S355. Position: where the section sits on the line between the two points: Top of steel on the line, Centered on the line or Bottom on the line. Rotation (°): turns the section about the member's own axis. Up to (columns only): The next level up, or a level name. Below the options, a Profile box lists the chosen section's dimensions, area, mass per meter and the catalog it comes from. The defaults are W12x50 in A992 for columns (centered), W16x26 in A992 for beams (top of steel on the line) and HSS6x6x1/4 in A500 Gr C for braces (centered). The options stay set until you change them. Place by clicking A column Choose Plan and pick the level the column starts at. Choose Column. Move the pointer near an intersection. The nearest dot lights up and the status bar names it. Click. The column runs from that level to the next level up (or to the Up to level). The status bar says, for example, \"Column W12x50 at A/1, Level 1 to Level 2.\" Keep clicking to place more. Press Esc when you are done. If there is no level above the one you clicked, the status bar says \"There is no level above Level 2 for the column to reach. Add a level first.\" A beam Choose Plan and pick the level of the beam. Choose Beam. Click the first intersection. The status bar says \"From A/1 at Level 2. Now click the other end (Esc cancels).\" A dashed line follows the pointer. Click the second intersection. The beam appears. Click again to start the next beam, or press Esc. Esc after the first click cancels just that point; a second Esc puts the tool away. A brace A brace runs between two intersections at different levels, so it is easiest in Elevation. Choose Elevation and pick the lettered grid line the brace is on. Choose Brace. Click the bottom end, then the top end. Elevations exist only for lettered grid lines. For a brace in a frame along a numbered line (from A to B), use 3D or the Add by grid form below. On a touch screen, tap close to the intersection: a tap picks the nearest dot within a short distance and does nothing if no dot is near. There is no highlight before you tap, so check the status bar message after each tap. One-finger drags turn or pan the view instead of placing. Place by grid, without clicking Every placing tool has an Add by grid form at the bottom of the Properties panel. It works with the keyboard alone and does not need the 3D view. Choose the tool, for example Beam. In Add by grid, pick From grid (letter), From grid (number) and From level. Pick To grid (letter), To grid (number) and To level. Choose Add. For a column or a footing the form has one point only: Grid (letter), Grid (number) and Level. Change a member after placing it Press Esc (or choose Select, V) and click the member. In the Properties panel, change Name, Profile, Material, Position or Rotation (°). To move it, edit the x, y and z of Start or End under Ends (x, y, z mm). The grid name of each end shows underneath, or its coordinates when it is off the grid. Each change is one undo step. Below the fields you see the member's Part mark, Assembly, Cut length, Weight (gross: holes and copes are not taken off) and which catalog the profile comes from. Members cannot be dragged or arrayed yet. To make another member like it, copy and paste it, or duplicate it (Ctrl+D): see Copy, paste and duplicate. Delete a member Select it and press Delete or Backspace, or choose Delete. Connections on it go too. Ctrl+Z brings everything back. Things to know Which way a column faces. At Rotation (°) 0, a column's flanges face along x, toward the neighboring numbered grid lines. A beam from grid 1 to grid 2 frames into a flange; a beam from A to B frames into the web. End plates are built only into flanges, so if your beams run from A to B, give those columns a rotation of 90. Beams at a level hang below it with the default Top of steel on the line. Choose Centered on the line if your levels are member centerlines. Columns run level to level. For a column through two storeys, set Up to to the higher level. Sloped beams are allowed (pick ends at different levels), but end plates are not built on them yet. Next: Connect beams to columns with end plates.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"design-checks","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Check steel members to a design code","summary":"Make load combinations from ASCE 7 or EN 1990, check each steel I-shape against AISC 360 or EN 1993-1-1, and see how much of its capacity every member uses. An aid until an engineer has reviewed it.","section":"tasks","order":41,"keywords":["design","design check","steel design","utilisation","utilization","capacity","AISC 360","ASCE 7","EN 1990","EN 1993-1-1","Eurocode","load combinations","LRFD","National Annex","buckling","lateral-torsional buckling","shear","bending","interaction","structural","students","engineer"],"headings":[{"id":"choose-a-code","text":"Choose a code","level":2},{"id":"make-the-combinations","text":"Make the combinations","level":2},{"id":"check-the-members","text":"Check the members","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-checked","text":"What is not checked","level":2},{"id":"the-report","text":"The report","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAfter you run the analysis, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesign checks\u003C/span\u003E block in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab turns its forces into a verdict for every steel member: how much of its capacity the worst combination uses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an \u003Cstrong\u003Eaid until an engineer has reviewed it\u003C/strong\u003E. It uses first-order forces only (no second-order effects), checks no torsion, no slender (class 4) parts, no welded shapes and no connections, and it leaves out what it cannot check and says so. It does not replace an engineer&#039;s check, and its results are not a stamp.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-a-code\"\u003EChoose a code\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDesign code\u003C/span\u003E is saved with the model; pick one of two families:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EASCE 7 + AISC 360\u003C/strong\u003E (US, LRFD): the load combinations of ASCE 7-16 §2.3.1 and the member checks of ANSI/AISC 360-16.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEN 1990 + EN 1993-1-1\u003C/strong\u003E (Eurocode): the combinations of EN 1990 (Eq. 6.10, or 6.10a and 6.10b) and the member checks of EN 1993-1-1.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThen pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESteel grade\u003C/span\u003E (A992, A36 and others; or S235 to S450, with the lower yield strength above 40 mm).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-combinations\"\u003EMake the combinations\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive each load case its \u003Cstrong\u003Ekind\u003C/strong\u003E (dead, live, roof live, snow, wind, seismic) in the case list, then press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake combinations\u003C/span\u003E. DetailCraft writes the code&#039;s combinations as combinations of your cases: every strength combination the code lists for the kinds you have, and serviceability combinations for deflection. The ones you typed yourself stay; they are not checked against the code. A case of kind \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOther\u003C/span\u003E is not combined.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor the Eurocode the factors are the \u003Cstrong\u003Erecommended values\u003C/strong\u003E of EN 1990 Annex A1 (γG 1.35, γQ 1.5, the ψ values of imposed, snow and wind loads) and EN 1993-1-1 (γM0 = γM1 = 1.0). Your country&#039;s National Annex changes some of them: open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENational Annex values\u003C/span\u003E, type yours, and make the combinations again. They are saved in the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-members\"\u003ECheck the members\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck members\u003C/span\u003E. For each steel I-shape the table shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Eutilisation\u003C/strong\u003E (demand over capacity: 100 % is the limit), whether it passes, which check governs, and which combination. Members are coloured on the model: blue little used, green about half, ochre near the limit, red over it, grey not checked. Click a member&#039;s name to select it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe checks, with the clause each one follows (they are listed in the report):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etension, compression with flexural and torsional buckling, bending about both axes with lateral-torsional buckling, shear, and bending with axial force;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Edeflection of the serviceability combinations against the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeflection limit\u003C/span\u003E you set (L/360 to start; a project value, not a code value).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELengths.\u003C/strong\u003E The braced length is a share of each analysed piece (1 = unbraced); braces are taken equally spaced from the start. K factors multiply the length for buckling. A joint where another member lands splits a member, and each piece is checked on its own length, as braced there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-checked\"\u003EWhat is not checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA member shows \u003Cstrong\u003Enot checked\u003C/strong\u003E, with the reason, instead of a number when its section is not an I-shape the checks know (tubes, angles, channels), when it is a custom (welded) shape, or when a part is slender (AISC) or class 4 (Eurocode) in bending. A slender part in compression leaves the compression checks out and the member reads \u003Cem\u003Epasses, partly checked\u003C/em\u003E. Also not built: second-order effects, torsion, shear buckling together with bending, connections, fatigue, fire and seismic detailing. Concrete members and seismic loads have \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/concrete-and-seismic\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/concrete-and-seismic\"\u003Etheir own checks\u003C/a\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-report\"\u003EThe report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E gain the design tables once you have checked: the code editions, the settings and National Annex values, utilisation by member, every clause used, and for each member the demand, capacity and utilisation of each check in its governing combination.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"After you run the analysis, the Design checks block in the Analysis tab turns its forces into a verdict for every steel member: how much of its capacity the worst combination uses. It is an aid until an engineer has reviewed it. It uses first-order forces only (no second-order effects), checks no torsion, no slender (class 4) parts, no welded shapes and no connections, and it leaves out what it cannot check and says so. It does not replace an engineer's check, and its results are not a stamp. Choose a code Design code is saved with the model; pick one of two families: ASCE 7 + AISC 360 (US, LRFD): the load combinations of ASCE 7-16 §2.3.1 and the member checks of ANSI/AISC 360-16. EN 1990 + EN 1993-1-1 (Eurocode): the combinations of EN 1990 (Eq. 6.10, or 6.10a and 6.10b) and the member checks of EN 1993-1-1. Then pick the Steel grade (A992, A36 and others; or S235 to S450, with the lower yield strength above 40 mm). Make the combinations Give each load case its kind (dead, live, roof live, snow, wind, seismic) in the case list, then press Make combinations. DetailCraft writes the code's combinations as combinations of your cases: every strength combination the code lists for the kinds you have, and serviceability combinations for deflection. The ones you typed yourself stay; they are not checked against the code. A case of kind Other is not combined. For the Eurocode the factors are the recommended values of EN 1990 Annex A1 (γG 1.35, γQ 1.5, the ψ values of imposed, snow and wind loads) and EN 1993-1-1 (γM0 = γM1 = 1.0). Your country's National Annex changes some of them: open National Annex values, type yours, and make the combinations again. They are saved in the model. Check the members Press Check members. For each steel I-shape the table shows its utilisation (demand over capacity: 100 % is the limit), whether it passes, which check governs, and which combination. Members are coloured on the model: blue little used, green about half, ochre near the limit, red over it, grey not checked. Click a member's name to select it. The checks, with the clause each one follows (they are listed in the report): tension, compression with flexural and torsional buckling, bending about both axes with lateral-torsional buckling, shear, and bending with axial force; deflection of the serviceability combinations against the Deflection limit you set (L/360 to start; a project value, not a code value). Lengths. The braced length is a share of each analysed piece (1 = unbraced); braces are taken equally spaced from the start. K factors multiply the length for buckling. A joint where another member lands splits a member, and each piece is checked on its own length, as braced there. What is not checked A member shows not checked, with the reason, instead of a number when its section is not an I-shape the checks know (tubes, angles, channels), when it is a custom (welded) shape, or when a part is slender (AISC) or class 4 (Eurocode) in bending. A slender part in compression leaves the compression checks out and the member reads passes, partly checked. Also not built: second-order effects, torsion, shear buckling together with bending, connections, fatigue, fire and seismic detailing. Concrete members and seismic loads have their own checks. See Not built yet. The report Report (PDF) and Report (CSV) gain the design tables once you have checked: the code editions, the settings and National Annex values, utilisation by member, every clause used, and for each member the demand, capacity and utilisation of each check in its governing combination.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"concrete-and-seismic","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Check concrete members and make seismic loads","summary":"Set up the bars of concrete beams and columns, check them to ACI 318-19, make equivalent lateral forces to ASCE 7-22 on rigid floors, and see storey drift. An aid for an engineer to check.","section":"tasks","order":42,"keywords":["concrete","reinforced concrete","ACI 318","ACI 318-19","rebar","beam","column","shear","ties","seismic","earthquake","equivalent lateral force","ELF","ASCE 7-22","drift","storey drift","floors","rigid diaphragm","story weight","utilisation","utilization","structural","engineer"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-the-members","text":"Draw the members","level":2},{"id":"set-up-the-sections","text":"Set up the sections","level":2},{"id":"make-combinations-and-check","text":"Make combinations and check","level":2},{"id":"floors-and-seismic-loads","text":"Floors and seismic loads","level":2},{"id":"the-report","text":"The report","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete members\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeismic loads\u003C/span\u003E blocks in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab check reinforced concrete beams and columns, and make the sideways forces of an earthquake on the floors of a frame. Both follow the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab&#039;s run and are saved with the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe results are \u003Cstrong\u003Efor checking by an engineer\u003C/strong\u003E: an aid until one has reviewed it. Concrete checks use first-order forces, except that a column in a frame you call non-sway is designed for its magnified moment, with no sway magnification, no torsion design, no seismic detailing (ACI chapter 18) and no tie detailing. The seismic loads leave out the redundancy factor, the vertical seismic effect, torsional amplification and P-delta effects. Wind loads are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-members\"\u003EDraw the members\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw concrete members as rectangles: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E profile such as \u003Ccode\u003EPL400x400\u003C/code\u003E for a column and \u003Ccode\u003EPL300x600\u003C/code\u003E for a beam gives the section. Columns and beams are placed like any other member.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-up-the-sections\"\u003ESet up the sections\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete members\u003C/span\u003E block lists every section the members use. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet up as concrete…\u003C/span\u003E on the ones that are concrete. DetailCraft reads the width \u003Cstrong\u003Eb\u003C/strong\u003E (along the member&#039;s own y axis) and depth \u003Cstrong\u003Eh\u003C/strong\u003E (along z) from the drawn profile, then asks for:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Ecover\u003C/strong\u003E to the centre of the bars, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Etop\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Ebottom\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Eside\u003C/strong\u003E bars (how many and which diameter) for a beam, or a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecolumn cage\u003C/strong\u003E (bars along b and along h, which diameter, spiral ties) for a column;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Eties\u003C/strong\u003E: legs, diameter and spacing;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ewhat the member \u003Cstrong\u003Eacts as\u003C/strong\u003E: a beam, a column, or decided from its forces (a vertical member, or one carrying more than 0.10 f′c Ag, is a column).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet f′c, the bar yield fy and the tie yield fyt (420 MPa at most counts for shear), K and whether the frame is sway or non-sway above them. A section set up as concrete is analysed as concrete: E = 4700 √f′c, 2400 kg/m³, and by default with \u003Cstrong\u003Ecracked sections\u003C/strong\u003E (columns 0.70 Ig, beams 0.35 Ig; ACI 318-19 Table 6.6.3.1.1(a)). Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGross sections\u003C/span\u003E to turn that off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-combinations-and-check\"\u003EMake combinations and check\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive the load cases their kind, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake combinations\u003C/span\u003E: ACI 318-19 takes the strength combinations of ASCE 7 (§5.3.1). Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck concrete\u003C/span\u003E and every member gets a utilisation, coloured on the model like the steel checks: flexure and minimum steel (22.2, 9.6.1.2), tension-controlled strain (9.3.3.1), shear with the ties (22.5, 9.6.3), tie spacing, and axial load with moment for columns (22.4, with Bresler&#039;s method for two-way bending). A member where the drawn section differs from the b × h you typed reads \u003Cem\u003Epartly checked\u003C/em\u003E, as does a slender column or torsion above the threshold: those are not designed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMembers over their capacity are listed in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E; accept one with a reason, or fix the section.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"floors-and-seismic-loads\"\u003EFloors and seismic loads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floor is a level where the frame is held together by a rigid slab (a \u003Cstrong\u003Erigid diaphragm\u003C/strong\u003E). In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeismic loads\u003C/span\u003E block press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind floors\u003C/span\u003E: it takes the levels where two or more beams meet. Then give each floor its \u003Cstrong\u003Eseismic weight\u003C/strong\u003E, typed or read: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake weights from cases\u003C/span\u003E sums the vertical loads of the cases you give a factor (the dead case is 1; add 0.25 on a storage live case or snow on a flat roof as ASCE 7-22 12.7.2 asks). Each node&#039;s vertical load goes to the floor at or above it, so a column between two floors gives half its weight to each.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFill in the design values: SDS, SD1, S1, R, Ie, Cd, TL, the structural system (for the period) and the risk category. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake seismic cases\u003C/span\u003E then works out the period from the building height (12.8.2.1), the base shear V = Cs W (12.8.1), spreads it up the floors (12.8.3) and writes four load cases, in X and Y with the 5 % accidental torsion each way (12.8.4.2). The storey forces and the storey drift check (12.8.6, Table 12.12-1, the drift Cd δxe / Ie against 0.020 hsx) appear after a run; a drift over its limit is a problem too. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake combinations\u003C/span\u003E again to bring the seismic cases into the combinations.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe period from the analysis.\u003C/strong\u003E The approximate period Ta comes from the building&#039;s height. When you have run the analysis with the seismic cases, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake the period from the analysis\u003C/span\u003E reads how far each floor moved under the forces and works out the period by Rayleigh&#039;s method, T = 2π √(Σ w δ² / (g Σ f δ)), once for X and once for Y; the seismic cases are made again with it. The period used is never more than Cu·Ta (ASCE 7-22 12.8.2), so a soft model cannot lower the base shear below that. Take the period again after the second run: it settles in a step or two. Un-tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPeriod from the analysis\u003C/span\u003E to go back to Ta.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESlender columns (ACI 318-19 6.6.4.5).\u003C/strong\u003E For a frame you call non-sway, a column whose k·ℓu/r is over 34 + 12·M1/M2 (at most 40) is designed for the magnified moment Mc = δns · M2: δns = Cm / (1 − Pu / 0.75 Pc), Pc = π² (EI)eff / (k ℓu)² with (EI)eff = 0.4 Ec Ig / (1 + βdns), and the minimum moment Pu (15 + 0.03 h). βdns (0.6 to start) is the share of the factored axial load that is sustained: type your own. A column whose Pu reaches 0.75 Pc fails with its own check. The report lists each slender column&#039;s numbers. In a \u003Cstrong\u003Esway\u003C/strong\u003E frame a slender column is still &quot;partly checked&quot;: its magnification needs a second-order analysis, which is not built.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESlabs.\u003C/strong\u003E When the model has plates, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlab reinforcement\u003C/span\u003E block takes the plate moments of every strength combination and, by the Wood–Armer rule (bending and twisting together), works out the steel each layer needs per metre: bottom and top, in x and in y. You give the thickness, the cover and the bars; the table shows needed against given and the one-way shear against φVc, and a layer that falls short is a problem with its clause. Deflection, punching at columns and trimmer bars at openings are not covered, and a moment over a support converges slowly with four-node plates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery floor you list becomes a rigid diaphragm in the analysis: its beams carry no axial force from the floor&#039;s movement. The CraftScript export is not made for a model with floors.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-report\"\u003EThe report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (PDF)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReport (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E gain the concrete tables (assumptions, utilisation by member, every clause), the seismic parameters and base shear, the forces on the floors and the drift table.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Concrete members and Seismic loads blocks in the Analysis tab check reinforced concrete beams and columns, and make the sideways forces of an earthquake on the floors of a frame. Both follow the Analysis tab's run and are saved with the model. The results are for checking by an engineer: an aid until one has reviewed it. Concrete checks use first-order forces, except that a column in a frame you call non-sway is designed for its magnified moment, with no sway magnification, no torsion design, no seismic detailing (ACI chapter 18) and no tie detailing. The seismic loads leave out the redundancy factor, the vertical seismic effect, torsional amplification and P-delta effects. Wind loads are not built yet. Draw the members Draw concrete members as rectangles: a Plate profile such as PL400x400 for a column and PL300x600 for a beam gives the section. Columns and beams are placed like any other member. Set up the sections The Concrete members block lists every section the members use. Press Set up as concrete… on the ones that are concrete. DetailCraft reads the width b (along the member's own y axis) and depth h (along z) from the drawn profile, then asks for: the cover to the centre of the bars, and the top, bottom and side bars (how many and which diameter) for a beam, or a column cage (bars along b and along h, which diameter, spiral ties) for a column; the ties: legs, diameter and spacing; what the member acts as: a beam, a column, or decided from its forces (a vertical member, or one carrying more than 0.10 f′c Ag, is a column). Set f′c, the bar yield fy and the tie yield fyt (420 MPa at most counts for shear), K and whether the frame is sway or non-sway above them. A section set up as concrete is analysed as concrete: E = 4700 √f′c, 2400 kg/m³, and by default with cracked sections (columns 0.70 Ig, beams 0.35 Ig; ACI 318-19 Table 6.6.3.1.1(a)). Choose Gross sections to turn that off. Make combinations and check Give the load cases their kind, then Make combinations: ACI 318-19 takes the strength combinations of ASCE 7 (§5.3.1). Press Check concrete and every member gets a utilisation, coloured on the model like the steel checks: flexure and minimum steel (22.2, 9.6.1.2), tension-controlled strain (9.3.3.1), shear with the ties (22.5, 9.6.3), tie spacing, and axial load with moment for columns (22.4, with Bresler's method for two-way bending). A member where the drawn section differs from the b × h you typed reads partly checked, as does a slender column or torsion above the threshold: those are not designed. Members over their capacity are listed in Problems; accept one with a reason, or fix the section. Floors and seismic loads A floor is a level where the frame is held together by a rigid slab (a rigid diaphragm). In the Seismic loads block press Find floors: it takes the levels where two or more beams meet. Then give each floor its seismic weight, typed or read: Take weights from cases sums the vertical loads of the cases you give a factor (the dead case is 1; add 0.25 on a storage live case or snow on a flat roof as ASCE 7-22 12.7.2 asks). Each node's vertical load goes to the floor at or above it, so a column between two floors gives half its weight to each. Fill in the design values: SDS, SD1, S1, R, Ie, Cd, TL, the structural system (for the period) and the risk category. Make seismic cases then works out the period from the building height (12.8.2.1), the base shear V = Cs W (12.8.1), spreads it up the floors (12.8.3) and writes four load cases, in X and Y with the 5 % accidental torsion each way (12.8.4.2). The storey forces and the storey drift check (12.8.6, Table 12.12-1, the drift Cd δxe / Ie against 0.020 hsx) appear after a run; a drift over its limit is a problem too. Press Make combinations again to bring the seismic cases into the combinations. The period from the analysis. The approximate period Ta comes from the building's height. When you have run the analysis with the seismic cases, Take the period from the analysis reads how far each floor moved under the forces and works out the period by Rayleigh's method, T = 2π √(Σ w δ² / (g Σ f δ)), once for X and once for Y; the seismic cases are made again with it. The period used is never more than Cu·Ta (ASCE 7-22 12.8.2), so a soft model cannot lower the base shear below that. Take the period again after the second run: it settles in a step or two. Un-tick Period from the analysis to go back to Ta. Slender columns (ACI 318-19 6.6.4.5). For a frame you call non-sway, a column whose k·ℓu/r is over 34 + 12·M1/M2 (at most 40) is designed for the magnified moment Mc = δns · M2: δns = Cm / (1 − Pu / 0.75 Pc), Pc = π² (EI)eff / (k ℓu)² with (EI)eff = 0.4 Ec Ig / (1 + βdns), and the minimum moment Pu (15 + 0.03 h). βdns (0.6 to start) is the share of the factored axial load that is sustained: type your own. A column whose Pu reaches 0.75 Pc fails with its own check. The report lists each slender column's numbers. In a sway frame a slender column is still \"partly checked\": its magnification needs a second-order analysis, which is not built. Slabs. When the model has plates, a Slab reinforcement block takes the plate moments of every strength combination and, by the Wood–Armer rule (bending and twisting together), works out the steel each layer needs per metre: bottom and top, in x and in y. You give the thickness, the cover and the bars; the table shows needed against given and the one-way shear against φVc, and a layer that falls short is a problem with its clause. Deflection, punching at columns and trimmer bars at openings are not covered, and a moment over a support converges slowly with four-node plates. Every floor you list becomes a rigid diaphragm in the analysis: its beams carry no axial force from the floor's movement. The CraftScript export is not made for a model with floors. The report Report (PDF) and Report (CSV) gain the concrete tables (assumptions, utilisation by member, every clause), the seismic parameters and base shear, the forces on the floors and the drift table.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"select-and-change","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Select by window, move, rotate, mirror, change many","summary":"Drag a window to select, drag selected parts to move them, move, rotate or mirror the selection (or a copy of it), or change the profile, material, position or rotation of many members at once.","section":"tasks","order":44,"keywords":["lasso","fence","freehand selection","drag","drag and drop","move by dragging","snap","window","crossing","box select","drag select","select many","move","rotate","mirror","flip","copy","change many","several members","profile","material","position","rotation","varies","grid line"],"headings":[{"id":"select-with-a-window","text":"Select with a window","level":2},{"id":"lasso","text":"Lasso","level":3},{"id":"drag-parts-to-move-them","text":"Drag parts to move them","level":2},{"id":"change-a-property-on-many-members","text":"Change a property on many members","level":2},{"id":"move-rotate-or-mirror","text":"Move, rotate or mirror","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEach of these is one undo step: one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it all back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-with-a-window\"\u003ESelect with a window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E, drag with the left button from an empty spot. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag (a plain drag turns the model).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag \u003Cstrong\u003Eto the right\u003C/strong\u003E for a window: it takes what is wholly inside, drawn with a solid edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag \u003Cstrong\u003Eto the left\u003C/strong\u003E for a crossing window: it takes everything it touches, drawn dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you start the drag to add to the selection instead of replacing it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plan or elevation only offers what it shows: parts cut away above the plan, or more than 1 m from the elevation&#039;s grid line, are not picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging drops the window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"lasso\"\u003ELasso\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ELasso\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDragging to select\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E and the same drag draws a freehand line instead of a rectangle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIts first move sideways decides how it selects: \u003Cstrong\u003Eto the right\u003C/strong\u003E, what is wholly inside (solid line); \u003Cstrong\u003Eto the left\u003C/strong\u003E, what it touches (dashed).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging to switch between inside, touching and \u003Cstrong\u003Efence\u003C/strong\u003E: an open line that takes whatever it crosses (dotted, orange).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to add and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to drop it work as for the window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen, one finger pans the plan and elevation as before; select parts by tapping them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E, pan with the right or middle button while the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool is on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drag-parts-to-move-them\"\u003EDrag parts to move them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the parts first (click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or a window).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress on one of the selected parts and drag. The whole selection moves; a dashed outline shows where it goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELet go to place it. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before you let go puts everything back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E the parts move across the level. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you start the drag in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E to move them up and down instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E they move within the elevation: along the grid line and up and down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe part you hold snaps onto a grid intersection when its end comes within a few pixels of one (the intersection lights up and the status bar names it). Elsewhere the move goes in 10 mm steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPressing on a part that is not selected still turns or pans the view, so a drag never moves something by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drag is one undo step, like \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E. Connections follow their members.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-property-on-many-members\"\u003EChange a property on many members\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect several members. Under \u003Cstrong\u003EChange on all of them\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosition\u003C/span\u003E: for the beams, columns and braces among them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate thickness (mm)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate width (mm)\u003C/span\u003E: for the plates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E: for all of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA field shows \u003Cstrong\u003EVaries\u003C/strong\u003E when the members do not agree. Pick or type a value and every member it applies to takes it; the status bar says how many changed and how many kept their own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumber fields in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel also take a calculation: \u003Ccode\u003E2*450\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E4500/3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E(1200-2*25)/2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E. A calculation that cannot be read is refused, and the status bar says where it went wrong.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-rotate-or-mirror\"\u003EMove, rotate or mirror\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect members and footings (a single one works too), then use \u003Cstrong\u003EMove, rotate, mirror\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel. Connections are not moved on their own: they follow their members. If you move a beam but not its column, the status bar says how many connections now join a moved member to one that stayed, and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/troubleshooting\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/troubleshooting\"\u003EProblems\u003C/a\u003E says if they no longer fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove:\u003C/strong\u003E type x, y and z in millimeters and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E. Or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick two points\u003C/span\u003E (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click the grid intersection to move from, then the one to move to. A dashed outline follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERotate in plan:\u003C/strong\u003E type the angle (counter-clockwise, seen from above) and choose what to turn about: the selection&#039;s middle, or a grid intersection. Columns turn with the frame.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMirror:\u003C/strong\u003E choose a grid line. The mirror image is the same profile turned the other way: an I-shape keeps its flanges, a channel faces the other way (its top and bottom swap), and holes and copes go with the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cstrong\u003EKeep the original (make a copy)\u003C/strong\u003E to leave the selection where it is and move, rotate or mirror a copy. Its connections come along when \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy end plates and base plates with their members\u003C/span\u003E is on in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERotating turns about a vertical line and mirroring is in a vertical plane, so levels stay level and footings stay upright.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Each of these is one undo step: one Ctrl+Z takes it all back. Select with a window In Plan and Elevation, drag with the left button from an empty spot. In 3D, hold Shift and drag (a plain drag turns the model). Drag to the right for a window: it takes what is wholly inside, drawn with a solid edge. Drag to the left for a crossing window: it takes everything it touches, drawn dashed. Hold Shift while you start the drag to add to the selection instead of replacing it. A plan or elevation only offers what it shows: parts cut away above the plan, or more than 1 m from the elevation's grid line, are not picked. Esc while dragging drops the window. Lasso Choose Lasso under Dragging to select in DetailCraft settings and the same drag draws a freehand line instead of a rectangle. Its first move sideways decides how it selects: to the right, what is wholly inside (solid line); to the left, what it touches (dashed). Press Space while dragging to switch between inside, touching and fence: an open line that takes whatever it crosses (dotted, orange). Shift to add and Esc to drop it work as for the window. On a touch screen, one finger pans the plan and elevation as before; select parts by tapping them. In Plan and Elevation, pan with the right or middle button while the Select tool is on. Drag parts to move them Select the parts first (click, Shift+click or a window). Press on one of the selected parts and drag. The whole selection moves; a dashed outline shows where it goes. Let go to place it. Esc before you let go puts everything back. In 3D and Plan the parts move across the level. Hold Ctrl while you start the drag in 3D to move them up and down instead. In Elevation they move within the elevation: along the grid line and up and down. The part you hold snaps onto a grid intersection when its end comes within a few pixels of one (the intersection lights up and the status bar names it). Elsewhere the move goes in 10 mm steps. Pressing on a part that is not selected still turns or pans the view, so a drag never moves something by accident. The drag is one undo step, like Move. Connections follow their members. Change a property on many members Select several members. Under Change on all of them in the Properties panel: Profile, Position: for the beams, columns and braces among them. Plate thickness (mm) and Plate width (mm): for the plates. Material and Rotation (°): for all of them. A field shows Varies when the members do not agree. Pick or type a value and every member it applies to takes it; the status bar says how many changed and how many kept their own. Number fields in the Properties panel also take a calculation: 2*450, 4500/3, (1200-2*25)/2, 6-1/2. A calculation that cannot be read is refused, and the status bar says where it went wrong. Move, rotate or mirror Select members and footings (a single one works too), then use Move, rotate, mirror in the Properties panel. Connections are not moved on their own: they follow their members. If you move a beam but not its column, the status bar says how many connections now join a moved member to one that stayed, and Problems says if they no longer fit. Move: type x, y and z in millimeters and choose Move. Or choose Pick two points (or press M): click the grid intersection to move from, then the one to move to. A dashed outline follows the pointer. Rotate in plan: type the angle (counter-clockwise, seen from above) and choose what to turn about: the selection's middle, or a grid intersection. Columns turn with the frame. Mirror: choose a grid line. The mirror image is the same profile turned the other way: an I-shape keeps its flanges, a channel faces the other way (its top and bottom swap), and holes and copes go with the part. Tick Keep the original (make a copy) to leave the selection where it is and move, rotate or mirror a copy. Its connections come along when Copy end plates and base plates with their members is on in DetailCraft settings. Rotating turns about a vertical line and mirroring is in a vertical plane, so levels stay level and footings stay upright.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"check-against-ids","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Check the IFC export against a client's IDS","summary":"Load the .ids file you were given and see every exported part that does not meet it in Problems, before you send the IFC.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["ids","information delivery specification","buildingsmart","requirements","delivery","check","validation","qa","ifc export","property","material","procurement"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn IDS (Information Delivery Specification, a buildingSMART standard) is the list of what an IFC must contain. DetailCraft checks the IFC it would export (the same file \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC\u003C/span\u003E writes) against the IDS you were given, and lists every part that falls short.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen or draw your model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck against IDS…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group of the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad an .ids file…\u003C/span\u003E and pick the file. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the result: each specification with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPass\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFail\u003C/span\u003E and how many elements it applies to.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen Problems (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift M\"\u003E⌃⇧M\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+M\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click a failure: the member, connection, stair or footing it came from is selected and the view zooms to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne warning per exported element and requirement it misses, with the specification&#039;s name, what the IDS asks for and what the export has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe check report (in Problems) starts with the IDS: its title, version, author and each specification&#039;s counts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe IDS is saved in the model file, so it is checked again when you open the model. Loading or forgetting it is one step you can undo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach change is checked again a moment later; on a large model the check waits a little longer between runs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe export&#039;s GlobalIds come from the model name and the part numbers inside DetailCraft, so they stay the same from one check to the next. Renaming the model gives every element new GlobalIds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbers are compared in SI units (the export is in millimetres; lengths are converted to metres first).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPatterns in the IDS are read as the whole value. A \u003Ccode\u003E^\u003C/code\u003E at the start and a \u003Ccode\u003E$\u003C/code\u003E at the end are taken as &quot;the whole value&quot; too, as most IDS files mean them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/ifc-export\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/ifc-export\"\u003EExport IFC\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do An IDS (Information Delivery Specification, a buildingSMART standard) is the list of what an IFC must contain. DetailCraft checks the IFC it would export (the same file IFC writes) against the IDS you were given, and lists every part that falls short. Steps Open or draw your model. Click Check against IDS… in the Output group of the toolbar. Click Load an .ids file… and pick the file. Nothing is uploaded. Read the result: each specification with Pass or Fail and how many elements it applies to. Open Problems (Ctrl+Shift+M) and click a failure: the member, connection, stair or footing it came from is selected and the view zooms to it. What you should see One warning per exported element and requirement it misses, with the specification's name, what the IDS asks for and what the export has. The check report (in Problems) starts with the IDS: its title, version, author and each specification's counts. Good to know The IDS is saved in the model file, so it is checked again when you open the model. Loading or forgetting it is one step you can undo. Each change is checked again a moment later; on a large model the check waits a little longer between runs. The export's GlobalIds come from the model name and the part numbers inside DetailCraft, so they stay the same from one check to the next. Renaming the model gives every element new GlobalIds. Numbers are compared in SI units (the export is in millimetres; lengths are converted to metres first). Patterns in the IDS are read as the whole value. A ^ at the start and a $ at the end are taken as \"the whole value\" too, as most IDS files mean them. Next Export IFC","lang":"en"},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate","summary":"Select several parts, copy them with their holes, copes and connections, and paste them on a grid intersection, at an offset, in place, or in another tab. Cut, duplicate, delete and select all.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["copy","paste","cut","duplicate","clipboard","paste in place","select all","select none","multi-select","shift click","several","repeat a bay","copy a frame","offset","delete several","edit menu","more menu"],"headings":[{"id":"select-several-parts","text":"Select several parts","level":2},{"id":"copy","text":"Copy","level":2},{"id":"paste-on-a-grid-intersection","text":"Paste on a grid intersection","level":2},{"id":"paste-from-the-keyboard","text":"Paste from the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"paste-in-place","text":"Paste in place","level":2},{"id":"duplicate","text":"Duplicate","level":2},{"id":"cut-and-delete","text":"Cut and delete","level":2},{"id":"marks-after-a-paste","text":"Marks after a paste","level":2},{"id":"if-the-clipboard-is-refused","text":"If the clipboard is refused","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group on the toolbar has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. Its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu holds the rest: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E, each with its key. A button or menu item that cannot act right now is grayed out; hover over it to read why (for example &quot;nothing copied yet&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery paste, cut, duplicate and delete is one step: one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it all back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-several-parts\"\u003ESelect several parts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a part to select it. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click more parts to add them; click a selected one the same way to take it out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E selects every member, connection and footing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E clears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several things selected, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel is headed &quot;4 selected&quot; (or however many) and lists the members by type, the connections, the footings and the gross steel weight, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E buttons.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy\"\u003ECopy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the members and footings you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat comes along:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach member whole: profile, material, ends, position, rotation, name, holes and copes. A custom profile it uses is copied too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach footing whole: size, concrete, bottom mat and starter bars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEnd plates and base plates, but only when every member a connection joins is copied: a beam&#039;s end plate needs the beam and its column. The status bar says how many connections were left out. To leave connections out on purpose, clear \u003Cstrong\u003ECopy end plates and base plates with their members\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA connection on its own cannot be copied; select its members instead. To give other joints the same connection, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESimilar\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/end-plates#copy-it-to-every-similar-joint\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/end-plates\" data-hash=\"copy-it-to-every-similar-joint\"\u003EEnd plates\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser until you copy something else. Another DetailCraft tab or window in the same browser can paste it, so you can copy a frame from one model and paste it into another.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-on-a-grid-intersection\"\u003EPaste on a grid intersection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the view. A dashed outline of the copy follows the grid intersection nearest the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an intersection. The copy&#039;s base point (its lowest corner: the smallest x, y and z of everything copied) lands there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pasted parts are selected afterwards, so you can paste again somewhere else, or change them. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels a paste; the copy stays on the clipboard. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E make the right intersection easier to hit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo repeat a bay, select the bay&#039;s members (or everything with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), copy, paste, and click the intersection one bay along from where the copy&#039;s lowest corner was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-from-the-keyboard\"\u003EPaste from the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile pasting, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel shows what is on the clipboard and two forms:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste by offset (mm, from the original)\u003C/span\u003E: type x, y and z and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste at this offset\u003C/span\u003E. It starts with the offset from \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste onto a grid intersection\u003C/span\u003E: pick a lettered grid, a numbered grid and a level, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste here\u003C/span\u003E. The base point goes there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-in-place\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) puts the copy at exactly the coordinates it was copied from. Use it after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, or to bring parts from another model at the same place. If the originals are still there, the copy sits on top of them and the status bar warns you: move the copies by their \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, or undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) copies the selection and pastes it at once, moved by the offset in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E (6000 mm along x to start with). It does not change what is on the clipboard. The copies are selected afterwards, so pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again makes the next one from them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-and-delete\"\u003ECut and delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) copies the selection, then deletes it. Paste it where it belongs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) deletes the selection. Deleting a member deletes its connections too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-after-a-paste\"\u003EMarks after a paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPart and assembly marks are never stored; DetailCraft numbers them again from the model after every change (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/mark-rules\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/mark-rules\"\u003EHow marks are given\u003C/a\u003E). So:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA copy that is the same piece as its original (same profile, grade, cut length, holes and copes) shares the original&#039;s mark, and the count in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart marks\u003C/span\u003E and the bill of materials goes up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA copy that ends up different gets a mark of its own. A beam pasted without its end plates, for example, is not cut back, so it is a different piece. Marks are numbered again across the whole model, so other parts&#039; numbers can change too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery pasted member, hole, cope, connection and footing gets a new id, so nothing in the model is ever shared between a copy and its original.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-the-clipboard-is-refused\"\u003EIf the clipboard is refused\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat is on the clipboard is checked as carefully as a model file you open: anything that is not a valid DetailCraft part is dropped, and the status bar says what. A clipboard filled by a newer DetailCraft, or by something else, is refused with a message and nothing is pasted. If a private window cannot keep the copy in browser storage, the copy still works in that tab.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Edit group on the toolbar has Undo, Redo, Copy and Paste. Its More menu holds the rest: Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all and Select none, each with its key. A button or menu item that cannot act right now is grayed out; hover over it to read why (for example \"nothing copied yet\"). Every paste, cut, duplicate and delete is one step: one Ctrl+Z takes it all back. Select several parts Click a part to select it. Hold Shift (or Ctrl, or Ctrl) and click more parts to add them; click a selected one the same way to take it out. Ctrl+A or Select all selects every member, connection and footing. Esc or Select none clears the selection. With several things selected, the Properties panel is headed \"4 selected\" (or however many) and lists the members by type, the connections, the footings and the gross steel weight, with Copy, Duplicate and Delete buttons. Copy Select the members and footings you want. Choose Copy or press Ctrl+C. What comes along: Each member whole: profile, material, ends, position, rotation, name, holes and copes. A custom profile it uses is copied too. Each footing whole: size, concrete, bottom mat and starter bars. End plates and base plates, but only when every member a connection joins is copied: a beam's end plate needs the beam and its column. The status bar says how many connections were left out. To leave connections out on purpose, clear Copy end plates and base plates with their members in DetailCraft settings. A connection on its own cannot be copied; select its members instead. To give other joints the same connection, use Similar (see End plates). The copy stays in this browser until you copy something else. Another DetailCraft tab or window in the same browser can paste it, so you can copy a frame from one model and paste it into another. Paste on a grid intersection Choose Paste or press Ctrl+V. Move the pointer over the view. A dashed outline of the copy follows the grid intersection nearest the pointer. Click an intersection. The copy's base point (its lowest corner: the smallest x, y and z of everything copied) lands there. The pasted parts are selected afterwards, so you can paste again somewhere else, or change them. Esc cancels a paste; the copy stays on the clipboard. Plan and Elevation make the right intersection easier to hit. To repeat a bay, select the bay's members (or everything with Ctrl+A), copy, paste, and click the intersection one bay along from where the copy's lowest corner was. Paste from the keyboard While pasting, the Properties panel shows what is on the clipboard and two forms: Paste by offset (mm, from the original): type x, y and z and choose Paste at this offset. It starts with the offset from DetailCraft settings. Paste onto a grid intersection: pick a lettered grid, a numbered grid and a level, and choose Paste here. The base point goes there. Paste in place Paste in place (Ctrl+Shift+V) puts the copy at exactly the coordinates it was copied from. Use it after Cut, or to bring parts from another model at the same place. If the originals are still there, the copy sits on top of them and the status bar warns you: move the copies by their Start and End in Properties, or undo. Duplicate Duplicate (Ctrl+D) copies the selection and pastes it at once, moved by the offset in DetailCraft settings (6000 mm along x to start with). It does not change what is on the clipboard. The copies are selected afterwards, so pressing Ctrl+D again makes the next one from them. Cut and delete Cut (Ctrl+X) copies the selection, then deletes it. Paste it where it belongs. Delete (Delete or Backspace) deletes the selection. Deleting a member deletes its connections too. Marks after a paste Part and assembly marks are never stored; DetailCraft numbers them again from the model after every change (How marks are given). So: A copy that is the same piece as its original (same profile, grade, cut length, holes and copes) shares the original's mark, and the count in Part marks and the bill of materials goes up. A copy that ends up different gets a mark of its own. A beam pasted without its end plates, for example, is not cut back, so it is a different piece. Marks are numbered again across the whole model, so other parts' numbers can change too. Every pasted member, hole, cope, connection and footing gets a new id, so nothing in the model is ever shared between a copy and its original. If the clipboard is refused What is on the clipboard is checked as carefully as a model file you open: anything that is not a valid DetailCraft part is dropped, and the status bar says what. A clipboard filled by a newer DetailCraft, or by something else, is refused with a message and nothing is pasted. If a private window cannot keep the copy in browser storage, the copy still works in that tab.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"cut-list","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Cut list","summary":"Nest the beams, columns, braces and stringers into stock lengths with saw kerf, and see how many bars to buy and the waste.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["cut list","nesting","stock length","bar","kerf","saw","drop","offcut","waste","combining","purchase","multiple"],"headings":[{"id":"set-the-stock","text":"Set the stock","level":2},{"id":"how-it-nests","text":"How it nests","level":2},{"id":"read-it","text":"Read it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut list\u003C/span\u003E tab, under the view, works out how to cut the model&#039;s linear parts from stock bars: how many bars of each length to buy for each profile and material, what goes on each bar, and the waste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-stock\"\u003ESet the stock\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top of the tab:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStock lengths (mm):\u003C/strong\u003E the lengths you can buy, separated by spaces, for example \u003Ccode\u003E6000 12000\u003C/code\u003E, or 20 ft and 40 ft bars as \u003Ccode\u003E6096 12192\u003C/code\u003E. Up to eight.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaw kerf (mm):\u003C/strong\u003E what each cut takes (3 to start).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnd trim (mm):\u003C/strong\u003E cut off the end of every bar before the first piece (0 to start).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep drops from (mm):\u003C/strong\u003E a drop at least this long goes back to stock; shorter is scrap (500 to start).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are kept in this browser, like the other \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003Esettings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-it-nests\"\u003EHow it nests\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery beam, column, brace and stair stringer, by part mark and count; plates are cut from sheet and are not nested here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach profile and material on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELongest pieces first, each into the first bar it fits; a new bar starts as the longest stock length. Then every bar is bought as the shortest stock length that still takes its cuts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach cut takes the kerf (none after a cut that ends exactly at the bar&#039;s end); each bar loses the end trim.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA piece longer than every stock length is listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ELonger than any stock\u003C/strong\u003E, not nested: splice it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/splices\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/splices\"\u003ESplices\u003C/a\u003E) or add a longer stock length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-it\"\u003ERead it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESummary:\u003C/strong\u003E per profile and material, the pieces, the bars to buy (for example &quot;2 × 12000, 1 × 6000&quot;), the metres bought and used, and the waste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBars and their cuts:\u003C/strong\u003E every bar, its stock length, its cuts as part mark and length, and the drop, kept or scrap.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves both tables as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; cut list.csv\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft does not nest plates into sheets, and does not mix profiles or materials on a bar.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Cut list tab, under the view, works out how to cut the model's linear parts from stock bars: how many bars of each length to buy for each profile and material, what goes on each bar, and the waste. Set the stock At the top of the tab: Stock lengths (mm): the lengths you can buy, separated by spaces, for example 6000 12000, or 20 ft and 40 ft bars as 6096 12192. Up to eight. Saw kerf (mm): what each cut takes (3 to start). End trim (mm): cut off the end of every bar before the first piece (0 to start). Keep drops from (mm): a drop at least this long goes back to stock; shorter is scrap (500 to start). These are kept in this browser, like the other settings. How it nests Every beam, column, brace and stair stringer, by part mark and count; plates are cut from sheet and are not nested here. Each profile and material on its own. Longest pieces first, each into the first bar it fits; a new bar starts as the longest stock length. Then every bar is bought as the shortest stock length that still takes its cuts. Each cut takes the kerf (none after a cut that ends exactly at the bar's end); each bar loses the end trim. A piece longer than every stock length is listed under Longer than any stock, not nested: splice it (see Splices) or add a longer stock length. Read it Summary: per profile and material, the pieces, the bars to buy (for example \"2 × 12000, 1 × 6000\"), the metres bought and used, and the waste. Bars and their cuts: every bar, its stock length, its cuts as part mark and length, and the drop, kept or scrap. Download CSV saves both tables as \u003Cname\u003E cut list.csv. DetailCraft does not nest plates into sheets, and does not mix profiles or materials on a bar.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"reference-pictures","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Paste a copied area as a reference picture","summary":"Copy part of a PDF sheet in MarkupCraft and paste it into DetailCraft at its true size, lying on a level under the steel, to model over it.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["picture","reference picture","underlay","background","copy area","markupcraft","pdf","trace","true size","scale","opacity","overlay"],"headings":[{"id":"paste-it","text":"Paste it","level":2},{"id":"move-change-and-delete-it","text":"Move, change and delete it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft&#039;s Copy area tool copies part of a sheet as a picture that knows how big it is. Paste it into DetailCraft and it lies flat on a level, at its true size, under the grid and the steel, so you can place members over a plan from the drawings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-it\"\u003EPaste it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn MarkupCraft, copy the area with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy an area of the page…\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy as overlay\u003C/span\u003E, which makes the paper see-through).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn DetailCraft (same browser), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture lands in the middle of the grid on the level shown in Plan (in 3D or an elevation, on the lowest level) and is selected. The status bar says how big it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrue size:\u003C/strong\u003E when the sheet had a drawing scale, one pixel is drawn as the length it shows on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaper size:\u003C/strong\u003E when the sheet had no scale, the picture is as big as the area was on paper, and the status bar says so. Type a new \u003Cstrong\u003Emm per pixel\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E to scale it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn area copied as an overlay starts at the opacity it was copied with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes whichever you copied last: the area from MarkupCraft, or members and footings copied in DetailCraft. If a different picture was copied to the clipboard after the area (from another program), nothing is pasted; copy the area again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-change-and-delete-it\"\u003EMove, change and delete it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the picture to select it (anything in front of it is picked first). A blue outline shows it is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag it to move it across the level. Its middle and corners snap onto grid intersections.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, type where its middle goes (x, y) and the level it lies at (z), its \u003Cstrong\u003Emm per pixel\u003C/strong\u003E and its \u003Cstrong\u003Eopacity\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach paste, move, change and delete is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture is saved in the model file, so it opens with the model. It is a reference only: it is not in drawings, exports, the bill of materials or a copy.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft's Copy area tool copies part of a sheet as a picture that knows how big it is. Paste it into DetailCraft and it lies flat on a level, at its true size, under the grid and the steel, so you can place members over a plan from the drawings. Paste it In MarkupCraft, copy the area with More › Copy an area of the page…, then Copy (or Copy as overlay, which makes the paper see-through). In DetailCraft (same browser), press Ctrl+V or choose Paste. The picture lands in the middle of the grid on the level shown in Plan (in 3D or an elevation, on the lowest level) and is selected. The status bar says how big it is. True size: when the sheet had a drawing scale, one pixel is drawn as the length it shows on the drawing. Paper size: when the sheet had no scale, the picture is as big as the area was on paper, and the status bar says so. Type a new mm per pixel in Properties to scale it. An area copied as an overlay starts at the opacity it was copied with. Ctrl+V pastes whichever you copied last: the area from MarkupCraft, or members and footings copied in DetailCraft. If a different picture was copied to the clipboard after the area (from another program), nothing is pasted; copy the area again. Move, change and delete it Click the picture to select it (anything in front of it is picked first). A blue outline shows it is selected. Drag it to move it across the level. Its middle and corners snap onto grid intersections. In Properties, type where its middle goes (x, y) and the level it lies at (z), its mm per pixel and its opacity. Delete or Delete removes it. Each paste, move, change and delete is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. The picture is saved in the model file, so it opens with the model. It is a reference only: it is not in drawings, exports, the bill of materials or a copy.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"phases","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Phases","summary":"Split the model into phases (erection sequences or deliveries), show or hide each phase, put parts in a phase, and number marks per phase.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["phase","phases","phase manager","erection sequence","delivery","lot","sequence","hide phase","show phase","filter","numbering","mark prefix","per phase"],"headings":[{"id":"the-phases-panel","text":"The Phases panel","level":2},{"id":"put-parts-in-a-phase","text":"Put parts in a phase","level":2},{"id":"show-and-hide-phases","text":"Show and hide phases","level":2},{"id":"marks-per-phase","text":"Marks per phase","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA phase is a group of parts that are made, delivered or erected together: an erection sequence, a delivery, a lot. Every member and footing is in one phase. A connection is in the phase of its main member: the beam of an end plate, the column of a base plate. A new model has one phase.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-phases-panel\"\u003EThe Phases panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPhases\u003C/span\u003E section of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E panel lists every phase:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe checkbox shows or hides the phase in the view;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe number, and a name you can type (empty = &quot;Phase 2&quot; and so on);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehow many parts are in it;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E×\u003C/span\u003E button removes a phase. Only an empty phase can go: put its parts in another phase first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd phase\u003C/span\u003E adds the next number and makes it the phase new parts go in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-parts-in-a-phase\"\u003EPut parts in a phase\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew parts:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew parts go in\u003C/span\u003E chooses the phase every member and footing you place from now on goes in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne part:\u003C/strong\u003E select it and change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPhase\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMany parts:\u003C/strong\u003E select them (a window, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPhase\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel. It shows \u003Cstrong\u003EVaries\u003C/strong\u003E when they are in different phases. One change is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopies keep the phase of the part they were copied from, also when pasted into another model (the phase is added there).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-and-hide-phases\"\u003EShow and hide phases\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA hidden phase is not drawn in the view, cannot be clicked or caught by a selection window, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves it out. It is still in the bill of materials, the reports, the drawings and every export. Hiding is only for the view: it is not saved and has no undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-per-phase\"\u003EMarks per phase\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003ENumber marks per phase\u003C/strong\u003E on (it starts on) and parts in more than one phase, each phase is numbered on its own and its marks start with the phase number: part marks 1-b1, 2-b1, assembly marks 2-B1, footings 2-F1. Two identical beams in different phases then have different marks, because they are made and shipped at different times. With one phase in use, marks have no prefix. Turn it off to number the whole model as one, so identical pieces share a mark whatever their phase.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe phase is written into the IFC export (property set DetailCraft, Phase).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModels with phases are saved in DetailCraft&#039;s file format 2. The 0.3 release of DetailCraft cannot open them; this one opens older files, with every part in phase 1.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"A phase is a group of parts that are made, delivered or erected together: an erection sequence, a delivery, a lot. Every member and footing is in one phase. A connection is in the phase of its main member: the beam of an end plate, the column of a base plate. A new model has one phase. The Phases panel The Phases section of the Model panel lists every phase: the checkbox shows or hides the phase in the view; the number, and a name you can type (empty = \"Phase 2\" and so on); how many parts are in it; the × button removes a phase. Only an empty phase can go: put its parts in another phase first. Add phase adds the next number and makes it the phase new parts go in. Put parts in a phase New parts: New parts go in chooses the phase every member and footing you place from now on goes in. One part: select it and change Phase in the Properties panel. Many parts: select them (a window, Shift+click, Ctrl+A) and change Phase at the top of the Properties panel. It shows Varies when they are in different phases. One change is one undo step. Copies keep the phase of the part they were copied from, also when pasted into another model (the phase is added there). Show and hide phases A hidden phase is not drawn in the view, cannot be clicked or caught by a selection window, and Ctrl+A leaves it out. It is still in the bill of materials, the reports, the drawings and every export. Hiding is only for the view: it is not saved and has no undo. Marks per phase With Number marks per phase on (it starts on) and parts in more than one phase, each phase is numbered on its own and its marks start with the phase number: part marks 1-b1, 2-b1, assembly marks 2-B1, footings 2-F1. Two identical beams in different phases then have different marks, because they are made and shipped at different times. With one phase in use, marks have no prefix. Turn it off to number the whole model as one, so identical pieces share a mark whatever their phase. The phase is written into the IFC export (property set DetailCraft, Phase). Models with phases are saved in DetailCraft's file format 2. The 0.3 release of DetailCraft cannot open them; this one opens older files, with every part in phase 1.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"erection-movie","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Erection plan and movie","summary":"Enter a crane and its load chart, see every lift checked against the chart in phase order, and save an erection movie that plays offline on any phone.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["erection","crane","lift","load chart","radius","capacity","rigging","sequence","movie","animation","phone","offline","lift plan"],"headings":[{"id":"describe-the-crane","text":"Describe the crane","level":2},{"id":"what-the-plan-does","text":"What the plan does","level":2},{"id":"problems","text":"Problems","level":2},{"id":"save-the-movie","text":"Save the movie","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EErection\u003C/span\u003E tab turns the model into an erection order and checks each lift against a crane you describe. It plans from numbers you type. It is not a lift plan: the rigger and the crane operator make that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"describe-the-crane\"\u003EDescribe the crane\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a crane\u003C/span\u003E, then fill in:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECrane centre X and Y:\u003C/strong\u003E where the crane stands, in millimetres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHighest hook:\u003C/strong\u003E the most the hook can rise, in millimetres. 0 means no limit is checked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHook block and rigging not in the chart:\u003C/strong\u003E kilograms taken off the chart before a load is added, when your chart does not already do it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoad chart:\u003C/strong\u003E one row to a line, radius in metres and then the capacity in kilograms. Copy the rows from the crane&#039;s own chart.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chart is read the way a load chart is read: the capacity listed for a radius holds out to that radius. A load at a radius between two rows gets the capacity of the \u003Cstrong\u003Efarther\u003C/strong\u003E row, never a value between them. Inside the first row, the first row&#039;s capacity holds. Beyond the last row the crane cannot lift at all. The radius that counts for a lift is the larger of the pick-up radius and the set radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA second crane is added the same way. Each lift goes to the first crane that can take it within your margin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-plan-does\"\u003EWhat the plan does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne lift per assembly: a beam with its end plates, a column with its base plate, a stair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe order follows the phases first, then tier by tier: columns, the beams they carry, braces, plates, stairs. A part is never lifted before what carries it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach lift shows its weight (the model&#039;s gross weight, plus \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERigging added to each weight\u003C/span\u003E), its radius, the share of the chart it uses and the minute it starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMost of the chart a lift may use\u003C/span\u003E is your safety margin. A lift above it is marked \u003Cstrong\u003EOver your margin\u003C/strong\u003E; above the chart, \u003Cstrong\u003EOver the chart\u003C/strong\u003E; beyond the chart&#039;s last radius, \u003Cstrong\u003EPast the chart\u003C/strong\u003E; and a part higher than the hook, \u003Cstrong\u003EHook too low\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHoles and copes are not deducted from the weights, and bolts are covered by the rigging percentage.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"problems\"\u003EProblems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery lift that is over, past, too high or set before what carries it appears in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E panel. A beam with no connection to carry it is listed as needing temporary support.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-movie\"\u003ESave the movie\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave movie\u003C/span\u003E writes one HTML file. Open it in any browser, on a phone too, with no network and no account: play and pause, drag the bar to any minute, turn the model with a finger, pinch to zoom. The caption names each lift, its weight, radius and share of the chart. The model is drawn as plain boxes: it shows the order and the lifts, not the true profiles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same table saves as a CSV with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E button. The crane and chart are saved in the model file, and every change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Erection tab turns the model into an erection order and checks each lift against a crane you describe. It plans from numbers you type. It is not a lift plan: the rigger and the crane operator make that. Describe the crane Choose Add a crane, then fill in: Crane centre X and Y: where the crane stands, in millimetres. Highest hook: the most the hook can rise, in millimetres. 0 means no limit is checked. Hook block and rigging not in the chart: kilograms taken off the chart before a load is added, when your chart does not already do it. Load chart: one row to a line, radius in metres and then the capacity in kilograms. Copy the rows from the crane's own chart. The chart is read the way a load chart is read: the capacity listed for a radius holds out to that radius. A load at a radius between two rows gets the capacity of the farther row, never a value between them. Inside the first row, the first row's capacity holds. Beyond the last row the crane cannot lift at all. The radius that counts for a lift is the larger of the pick-up radius and the set radius. A second crane is added the same way. Each lift goes to the first crane that can take it within your margin. What the plan does One lift per assembly: a beam with its end plates, a column with its base plate, a stair. The order follows the phases first, then tier by tier: columns, the beams they carry, braces, plates, stairs. A part is never lifted before what carries it. Each lift shows its weight (the model's gross weight, plus Rigging added to each weight), its radius, the share of the chart it uses and the minute it starts. Most of the chart a lift may use is your safety margin. A lift above it is marked Over your margin; above the chart, Over the chart; beyond the chart's last radius, Past the chart; and a part higher than the hook, Hook too low. Holes and copes are not deducted from the weights, and bolts are covered by the rigging percentage. Problems Every lift that is over, past, too high or set before what carries it appears in the Problems panel. A beam with no connection to carry it is listed as needing temporary support. Save the movie Save movie writes one HTML file. Open it in any browser, on a phone too, with no network and no account: play and pause, drag the bar to any minute, turn the model with a finger, pinch to zoom. The caption names each lift, its weight, radius and share of the chart. The model is drawn as plain boxes: it shows the order and the lifts, not the true profiles. The same table saves as a CSV with the CSV button. The crane and chart are saved in the model file, and every change is one undo step.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"clash-check","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Check for clashes","summary":"Find members, plates and footings that reach into each other, leaving out the parts a connection means to meet, and go to each clash.","section":"tasks","order":48,"keywords":["clash","clash check","collision","interference","overlap","intersect","hard clash","coordination","check model"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-not-a-clash","text":"What is not a clash","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClashes\u003C/span\u003E tab under the view and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck now\u003C/span\u003E. DetailCraft looks at every pair of parts in the phases shown: steel into steel, and steel into a footing. The tab lists each clash, the deepest first, with:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe two parts, by part mark (or footing mark) and profile;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOverlap (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E: how far a point of one part is inside the other, up to the nearest face;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENear\u003C/strong\u003E: the grid intersection and level it is at, or its coordinates;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E: selects both parts and brings the place to the middle of the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tab&#039;s title shows how many clashes the last check found. The check does not run by itself: after you change the model the list says so, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck now\u003C/span\u003E runs it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-a-clash\"\u003EWhat is not a clash\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EParts that only touch: a plate against a flange, a base plate on its footing, two members end to end. \u003Cstrong\u003ETolerance (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E sets how far two parts may run into each other before it counts (1 mm to start; up to 50).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EParts a connection joins: an end plate with its beam and with the column flange it is bolted to, the beam and the column of that joint, a base plate with its column, a splice&#039;s plates with both pieces, a brace with its gusset (a tube&#039;s slot is not cut in the model). The connection means them to meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA base plate&#039;s shear lug in the footing under it (it sits in a grout pocket).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBolts and anchor rods (they pass through holes) and rebar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EParts in hidden phases. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/phases\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/phases\"\u003EPhases\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA brace or beam that runs into a column with no connection between them \u003Cstrong\u003Eis\u003C/strong\u003E a clash: that joint still needs a connection. The sample frame&#039;s brace shows this: it has no gusset. Select it and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGusset\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/brace-gussets\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/brace-gussets\"\u003EBrace gussets\u003C/a\u003E) and its clashes go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOverlap measures depth into material, so a member run into a column&#039;s thin web or flange shows only a few millimeters even when it runs far in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EParts are checked as they are cut: copes and the cut-back of a beam at its end plates are taken into account. Holes are not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Open the Clashes tab under the view and choose Check now. DetailCraft looks at every pair of parts in the phases shown: steel into steel, and steel into a footing. The tab lists each clash, the deepest first, with: the two parts, by part mark (or footing mark) and profile; Overlap (mm): how far a point of one part is inside the other, up to the nearest face; Near: the grid intersection and level it is at, or its coordinates; Show: selects both parts and brings the place to the middle of the view. The tab's title shows how many clashes the last check found. The check does not run by itself: after you change the model the list says so, and Check now runs it again. What is not a clash Parts that only touch: a plate against a flange, a base plate on its footing, two members end to end. Tolerance (mm) sets how far two parts may run into each other before it counts (1 mm to start; up to 50). Parts a connection joins: an end plate with its beam and with the column flange it is bolted to, the beam and the column of that joint, a base plate with its column, a splice's plates with both pieces, a brace with its gusset (a tube's slot is not cut in the model). The connection means them to meet. A base plate's shear lug in the footing under it (it sits in a grout pocket). Bolts and anchor rods (they pass through holes) and rebar. Parts in hidden phases. See Phases. A brace or beam that runs into a column with no connection between them is a clash: that joint still needs a connection. The sample frame's brace shows this: it has no gusset. Select it and choose Gusset (Brace gussets) and its clashes go. Good to know Overlap measures depth into material, so a member run into a column's thin web or flange shows only a few millimeters even when it runs far in. Parts are checked as they are cut: copes and the cut-back of a beam at its end plates are taken into account. Holes are not.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"rebar-congestion","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Rebar congestion map","summary":"See how tight the rebar leaves each footing for concrete to get down, with the aggregate and vibrator you give.","section":"tasks","order":48,"keywords":["rebar","congestion","spacing","clear spacing","concrete placement","aggregate","vibrator","starters","hooks","footing","mat"],"headings":[{"id":"what-the-map-measures","text":"What the map measures","level":2},{"id":"what-counts-as-tight","text":"What counts as tight","level":2},{"id":"problems","text":"Problems","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECongestion\u003C/span\u003E tab draws a heat map of each footing as seen from above. Pale blue is open; yellow and orange are getting tight; red is tighter than the bars ask for. Dark cells are the bars themselves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-map-measures\"\u003EWhat the map measures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConcrete goes down through the steel one layer after another and can move sideways between the layers. So at each point the map takes the narrowest free gap in any one layer: between the bottom-mat bars, between the next layer of bars, and between the starter bars&#039; hook legs on top. The starters stand through all of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-tight\"\u003EWhat counts as tight\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe need is the greater of 25 mm and the bar diameter (ACI 318-19 25.2.1), or, for BS 4449 bars, the greater of the bar diameter and 20 mm (EN 1992-1-1 8.2). Two boxes raise it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELargest aggregate\u003C/span\u003E: the need becomes 4/3 of the aggregate size (ACI) or the aggregate plus 5 mm (EN).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVibrator head\u003C/span\u003E: the need is at least the head&#039;s diameter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth start at 0, meaning not known: DetailCraft does not know your mix or your pump.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"problems\"\u003EProblems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA footing with a place tighter than the need is listed in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E panel with the opening found and the opening needed. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E next to a map selects the footing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe map does not see ties, couplers, ducts or the column&#039;s own bars.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Congestion tab draws a heat map of each footing as seen from above. Pale blue is open; yellow and orange are getting tight; red is tighter than the bars ask for. Dark cells are the bars themselves. What the map measures Concrete goes down through the steel one layer after another and can move sideways between the layers. So at each point the map takes the narrowest free gap in any one layer: between the bottom-mat bars, between the next layer of bars, and between the starter bars' hook legs on top. The starters stand through all of them. What counts as tight The need is the greater of 25 mm and the bar diameter (ACI 318-19 25.2.1), or, for BS 4449 bars, the greater of the bar diameter and 20 mm (EN 1992-1-1 8.2). Two boxes raise it: Largest aggregate: the need becomes 4/3 of the aggregate size (ACI) or the aggregate plus 5 mm (EN). Vibrator head: the need is at least the head's diameter. Both start at 0, meaning not known: DetailCraft does not know your mix or your pump. Problems A footing with a place tighter than the need is listed in the Problems panel with the opening found and the opening needed. Select next to a map selects the footing. The map does not see ties, couplers, ducts or the column's own bars.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"foundation-checks","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Foundation checks","summary":"Check footings, combined footings and mats on the soil you describe, settlement, a flexible mat on springs, pile groups and retaining walls. An aid for an engineer to check.","section":"tasks","order":49,"keywords":["foundation","footing","combined footing","mat","settlement","subgrade modulus","springs","edge column","corner column","bearing","sliding","overturning","punching","one-way shear","flexure","development","minimum steel","pile group","retaining wall","ACI 318-19"],"headings":[{"id":"describe-the-soil","text":"Describe the soil","level":2},{"id":"give-the-loads","text":"Give the loads","level":2},{"id":"what-is-checked","text":"What is checked","level":2},{"id":"a-flexible-mat-on-springs","text":"A flexible mat on springs","level":2},{"id":"pile-groups-and-retaining-walls","text":"Pile groups and retaining walls","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are first-order checks for an engineer to review, not a design sign-off. The allowable bearing pressure and the friction coefficient are yours: DetailCraft does not know your soil.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFoundation\u003C/span\u003E tab checks the footings in the model against the column loads you give them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"describe-the-soil\"\u003EDescribe the soil\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllowable bearing pressure\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase friction coefficient\u003C/span\u003E. Both start at 0, meaning not entered: the check then shows the pressure as a note and does not say it passes. For settlement, type the soil&#039;s modulus \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEs\u003C/span\u003E (MPa), its Poisson&#039;s ratio and the settlement you allow (25 mm to start). For a flexible mat, type a subgrade modulus \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eks\u003C/span\u003E. The fill over the footing, the soil&#039;s unit weight, the factors of safety for sliding and overturning (1.5 to start) and the bar yield strength are there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"give-the-loads\"\u003EGive the loads\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach footing has a list of column loads. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a column load\u003C/span\u003E and fill in the service row (for bearing, sliding and overturning) and the factored row (for the strength checks): the vertical load P down, the horizontal forces Vx and Vy on the footing&#039;s top, the moments Mx and My, the column&#039;s place on the footing and its size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr run the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake column loads from the Analysis\u003C/span\u003E: every support that stands on a footing becomes a column load, from the service and the factored sets you choose. A positive My loads the +x side of the footing more; a positive Mx loads the −y side more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-checked\"\u003EWhat is checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom the service loads, with the footing&#039;s own weight and the fill added:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBearing:\u003C/strong\u003E the pressure under a rigid footing. Inside the middle third the whole base is in contact; outside it, part of the base lifts off and the peak is higher. A resultant outside the base fails.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESliding:\u003C/strong\u003E friction times the vertical load against the horizontal force.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOverturning:\u003C/strong\u003E the resultant&#039;s distance from the edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESettlement:\u003C/strong\u003E the elastic settlement of the average pressure on a half-space with the modulus you gave, at the middle and at a corner (Timoshenko and Goodier&#039;s solution for a flexible rectangle), against the limit. It is an upper bound for a stiff footing and leaves out a soil layer of limited depth, relief by the excavated soil and consolidation. With no modulus it shows the pressure as a note.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom the factored column loads alone (ACI 318-19):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlexure\u003C/strong\u003E at the column face, with the minimum steel (0.0018 for 420 MPa bars) and the bars you drew.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne-way shear\u003C/strong\u003E at d from the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPunching shear\u003C/strong\u003E at d/2 around each column, with the column&#039;s moment. For a column near an edge the critical section is cut off by the footing&#039;s edge (three sides at an edge, two at a corner, ACI 318-19 22.6.4.1 and the smaller αs in Table 22.6.5.2), and the moment about the section&#039;s own centre includes the shift of the load.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDevelopment\u003C/strong\u003E of the bars past the column face, straight or with their hooks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDepth\u003C/strong\u003E above the bottom bars (150 mm).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA combined footing or a mat is taken as rigid, and shear and moment come from statics on cuts across the full width.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-flexible-mat-on-springs\"\u003EA flexible mat on springs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalyse this footing as a flexible mat on springs\u003C/span\u003E when the footing carries many columns and the rigid footing is too coarse. The footing becomes a plate of four-node elements on a bed of springs (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eks\u003C/span\u003E you typed, lumped on the nodes), every column&#039;s load spread over the nodes of its own footprint. A second table gives the largest soil pressure and settlement (and the difference between the most and the least), the bending moment each way with the steel it needs, tension on top against the plain concrete&#039;s strength (a footing has no top bars), one-way shear at d from the columns, and punching on the springs&#039; own pressure. The mesh is refined until a column is three elements wide; a big mat takes a few seconds. The mat&#039;s own weight and the soil above it cancel and are left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery failed check is in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E panel with its clause.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pile-groups-and-retaining-walls\"\u003EPile groups and retaining walls\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a pile group\u003C/span\u003E puts a grid of piles under a rigid cap: type the load and moments and what one pile may carry in compression and in tension (the pile design&#039;s numbers, not this tab&#039;s). Each pile&#039;s reaction is P/n plus its share of the moments (the elastic method), and the largest compression and any tension are checked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a retaining wall\u003C/span\u003E checks a cantilever wall on level backfill, per metre: Rankine active pressure, overturning about the toe, sliding and the base pressure. Passive pressure in front of the toe is left out. Your assistant can run the same checks with \u003Ccode\u003Epile_group_check\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eretaining_wall_check\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPile groups, walls and the mat switch are saved in the model file, and a failed check is in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"These are first-order checks for an engineer to review, not a design sign-off. The allowable bearing pressure and the friction coefficient are yours: DetailCraft does not know your soil. The Foundation tab checks the footings in the model against the column loads you give them. Describe the soil Type the Allowable bearing pressure and the Base friction coefficient. Both start at 0, meaning not entered: the check then shows the pressure as a note and does not say it passes. For settlement, type the soil's modulus Es (MPa), its Poisson's ratio and the settlement you allow (25 mm to start). For a flexible mat, type a subgrade modulus ks. The fill over the footing, the soil's unit weight, the factors of safety for sliding and overturning (1.5 to start) and the bar yield strength are there too. Give the loads Each footing has a list of column loads. Use Add a column load and fill in the service row (for bearing, sliding and overturning) and the factored row (for the strength checks): the vertical load P down, the horizontal forces Vx and Vy on the footing's top, the moments Mx and My, the column's place on the footing and its size. Or run the Analysis tab and press Take column loads from the Analysis: every support that stands on a footing becomes a column load, from the service and the factored sets you choose. A positive My loads the +x side of the footing more; a positive Mx loads the −y side more. What is checked From the service loads, with the footing's own weight and the fill added: Bearing: the pressure under a rigid footing. Inside the middle third the whole base is in contact; outside it, part of the base lifts off and the peak is higher. A resultant outside the base fails. Sliding: friction times the vertical load against the horizontal force. Overturning: the resultant's distance from the edge. Settlement: the elastic settlement of the average pressure on a half-space with the modulus you gave, at the middle and at a corner (Timoshenko and Goodier's solution for a flexible rectangle), against the limit. It is an upper bound for a stiff footing and leaves out a soil layer of limited depth, relief by the excavated soil and consolidation. With no modulus it shows the pressure as a note. From the factored column loads alone (ACI 318-19): Flexure at the column face, with the minimum steel (0.0018 for 420 MPa bars) and the bars you drew. One-way shear at d from the face. Punching shear at d/2 around each column, with the column's moment. For a column near an edge the critical section is cut off by the footing's edge (three sides at an edge, two at a corner, ACI 318-19 22.6.4.1 and the smaller αs in Table 22.6.5.2), and the moment about the section's own centre includes the shift of the load. Development of the bars past the column face, straight or with their hooks. Depth above the bottom bars (150 mm). A combined footing or a mat is taken as rigid, and shear and moment come from statics on cuts across the full width. A flexible mat on springs Tick Analyse this footing as a flexible mat on springs when the footing carries many columns and the rigid footing is too coarse. The footing becomes a plate of four-node elements on a bed of springs (the ks you typed, lumped on the nodes), every column's load spread over the nodes of its own footprint. A second table gives the largest soil pressure and settlement (and the difference between the most and the least), the bending moment each way with the steel it needs, tension on top against the plain concrete's strength (a footing has no top bars), one-way shear at d from the columns, and punching on the springs' own pressure. The mesh is refined until a column is three elements wide; a big mat takes a few seconds. The mat's own weight and the soil above it cancel and are left out. Every failed check is in the Problems panel with its clause. Pile groups and retaining walls Add a pile group puts a grid of piles under a rigid cap: type the load and moments and what one pile may carry in compression and in tension (the pile design's numbers, not this tab's). Each pile's reaction is P/n plus its share of the moments (the elastic method), and the largest compression and any tension are checked. Add a retaining wall checks a cantilever wall on level backfill, per metre: Rankine active pressure, overturning about the toe, sliding and the base pressure. Passive pressure in front of the toe is left out. Your assistant can run the same checks with pile_group_check and retaining_wall_check. Pile groups, walls and the mat switch are saved in the model file, and a failed check is in Problems.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"plates-and-profiles","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Place plates and add custom profiles","summary":"Place loose plates by thickness and width, and define your own I, channel, angle, tube or flat bar sections.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["plate","flat","custom profile","user profile","parametric","section","shape","cross section","pl"],"headings":[{"id":"place-a-plate","text":"Place a plate","level":2},{"id":"add-a-custom-profile","text":"Add a custom profile","level":2},{"id":"what-custom-profiles-get","text":"What custom profiles get","level":3},{"id":"keep-a-profile-for-new-models","text":"Keep a profile for new models","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-plate\"\u003EPlace a plate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plate is placed like a beam: it runs between two intersections, with its width across and its thickness up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThickness (mm)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth (mm)\u003C/span\u003E. The default is 20 x 200 in A36.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E if needed. A rotation of 90 stands the plate on edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick two intersections, or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E form.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe plate&#039;s profile name is written \u003Ccode\u003EPL&lt;thickness&gt;x&lt;width&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, for example PL20x200. Its length is the distance between the two points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo resize a placed plate, select it and change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThickness (mm)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth (mm)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlates you place are parts of their own (mark p, assembly P). End plates and base plates made by connections are plates too, but you change them through their connection; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/end-plates\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/end-plates\"\u003EEnd plates\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-custom-profile\"\u003EAdd a custom profile\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the section you need is not in the catalog, make it from its dimensions. A custom profile belongs to the model and is saved with it. To use it in every new model, keep it in My profiles, described under &quot;Keep a profile for new models&quot; below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom profile\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003EW8x18-custom\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ERHS 120x80x5\u003C/code\u003E. It cannot be a catalog name or look like a plate name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShape\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EI-shape\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChannel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEqual angle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangular tube\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERound tube\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlat bar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the dimensions that appear, all in millimeters:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EI-shape\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth d, Flange width bf, Web tw, Flange tf, Fillet radius r (may be 0)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChannel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth d, Flange width bf, Web tw, Flange tf (square corners)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEqual angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth b (each leg), Thickness t\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERectangular tube\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHeight h, Width b, Thickness t (outer corner radius 2t)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound tube\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter D, Thickness t\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlat bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth b, Thickness t\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep in My profiles\u003C/span\u003E ticked to have it in every new model (see below), or untick it to keep it in this model only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd profile\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Profile &quot;…&quot; added; it is in every profile list under Custom.&quot; It appears in the list under the form and at the end of every \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft checks that the shape can exist. You will see messages such as &quot;The web and flanges do not fit inside the depth and width.&quot;, &quot;The fillet radius is too big for the flange.&quot; or &quot;The wall is too thick.&quot; Fix the numbers and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd profile\u003C/span\u003E again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-custom-profiles-get\"\u003EWhat custom profiles get\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMass per meter from the area × 7850 kg/m³ (catalog sizes use the published weight instead).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECustom I-shapes take holes, copes, end plates, NC files and shop drawings like catalog I-shapes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECustom flat bars behave as plates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECustom channels and tubes take holes; angles and round tubes do not yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot edit or remove a custom profile from a model once it is added (use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E straight away if you made a mistake). Give a corrected one a new name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keep-a-profile-for-new-models\"\u003EKeep a profile for new models\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMy profiles\u003C/span\u003E, under the form, is your own list of custom profiles. It is kept in this browser, on this computer, not in a model file and not in your account.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA profile added with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep in My profiles\u003C/span\u003E ticked goes into the list. Every new model, including the one DetailCraft opens when you start it, begins with all of them in its profile lists.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA profile already in a model but not in the list has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep\u003C/span\u003E button beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a model that does not have one of your profiles (an older file, say), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse here\u003C/span\u003E to add it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E takes a profile out of the list. Models that already use it keep their own copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA model you open keeps the profiles saved in its file. Opening a file never changes your list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA name that is already in the list with different dimensions is refused: give the new one another name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing the browser&#039;s site data, or using another browser or computer, starts with an empty list. A private window cannot keep it; the app says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Place a plate A plate is placed like a beam: it runs between two intersections, with its width across and its thickness up. Choose Plate in the Place group, or press P. In the Properties panel, set Thickness (mm) and Width (mm). The default is 20 x 200 in A36. Set Material and Rotation (°) if needed. A rotation of 90 stands the plate on edge. Click two intersections, or use the Add by grid form. The plate's profile name is written PL\u003Cthickness\u003Ex\u003Cwidth\u003E, for example PL20x200. Its length is the distance between the two points. To resize a placed plate, select it and change Thickness (mm) or Width (mm). Plates you place are parts of their own (mark p, assembly P). End plates and base plates made by connections are plates too, but you change them through their connection; see End plates. Add a custom profile When the section you need is not in the catalog, make it from its dimensions. A custom profile belongs to the model and is saved with it. To use it in every new model, keep it in My profiles, described under \"Keep a profile for new models\" below. In the Model panel on the left, open Custom profile. Type a Name, for example W8x18-custom or RHS 120x80x5. It cannot be a catalog name or look like a plate name. Pick a Shape: I-shape, Channel, Equal angle, Rectangular tube, Round tube or Flat bar. Fill in the dimensions that appear, all in millimeters: Shape Dimensions I-shape Depth d, Flange width bf, Web tw, Flange tf, Fillet radius r (may be 0) Channel Depth d, Flange width bf, Web tw, Flange tf (square corners) Equal angle Width b (each leg), Thickness t Rectangular tube Height h, Width b, Thickness t (outer corner radius 2t) Round tube Diameter D, Thickness t Flat bar Width b, Thickness t Leave Keep in My profiles ticked to have it in every new model (see below), or untick it to keep it in this model only. Choose Add profile. The status bar says \"Profile \"…\" added; it is in every profile list under Custom.\" It appears in the list under the form and at the end of every Profile list. DetailCraft checks that the shape can exist. You will see messages such as \"The web and flanges do not fit inside the depth and width.\", \"The fillet radius is too big for the flange.\" or \"The wall is too thick.\" Fix the numbers and choose Add profile again. What custom profiles get Mass per meter from the area × 7850 kg/m³ (catalog sizes use the published weight instead). Custom I-shapes take holes, copes, end plates, NC files and shop drawings like catalog I-shapes. Custom flat bars behave as plates. Custom channels and tubes take holes; angles and round tubes do not yet. You cannot edit or remove a custom profile from a model once it is added (use Ctrl+Z straight away if you made a mistake). Give a corrected one a new name. Keep a profile for new models My profiles, under the form, is your own list of custom profiles. It is kept in this browser, on this computer, not in a model file and not in your account. A profile added with Keep in My profiles ticked goes into the list. Every new model, including the one DetailCraft opens when you start it, begins with all of them in its profile lists. A profile already in a model but not in the list has a Keep button beside it. In a model that does not have one of your profiles (an older file, say), choose Use here to add it. Remove takes a profile out of the list. Models that already use it keep their own copy. A model you open keeps the profiles saved in its file. Opening a file never changes your list. A name that is already in the list with different dimensions is refused: give the new one another name. Clearing the browser's site data, or using another browser or computer, starts with an empty list. A private window cannot keep it; the app says so.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"framing-panels","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Timber and light-gauge framing panels","summary":"Frame wall, floor and roof panels in timber or light-gauge steel, with every piece, a cut list, a shop drawing and the stock to buy.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["framing","timber","lumber","wood","light-gauge steel","cold-formed","stud","joist","rafter","header","king stud","jack stud","cripple","cut list","shop drawing","panel"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-panel","text":"Add a panel","level":2},{"id":"walls","text":"Walls","level":2},{"id":"floors-and-roofs","text":"Floors and roofs","level":2},{"id":"what-you-get","text":"What you get","level":2},{"id":"place-a-panel-in-the-model","text":"Place a panel in the model","level":2},{"id":"pdf-sheets","text":"PDF sheets","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFraming\u003C/span\u003E tab frames panels you describe and gives you what a framing crew and a lumber yard ask for. The panels are saved in the model file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sizes, the spacing and the headers are yours. DetailCraft does not check that a stud at that spacing carries that wall or that a header spans that opening: that is for your code&#039;s tables and your engineer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-panel\"\u003EAdd a panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a wall\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a floor\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a roof\u003C/span\u003E, then fill in the dimensions in millimetres, the material (timber or light-gauge steel) and the member size. Timber sizes are the nominal names with their dressed sizes (2×4 is 38 × 89 mm). Steel studs are named by depth and thickness, for example \u003Ccode\u003EC92-1.2\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walls\"\u003EWalls\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wall has a bottom plate, one or more top plates (one track for steel), end studs, studs on the spacing you give, and for each opening: two king studs, two jack studs, a header of the size and plies you choose, and cripples over the header (and under the sill of a window). Give each opening its distance from the left end, its width, and its rough-opening top and bottom measured from the subfloor; a door has a bottom of 0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"floors-and-roofs\"\u003EFloors and roofs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floor has joists across its width along its span, rim joists at both ends and, if you tick it, blocking at mid-span. A roof panel is a gable: a pair of rafters on the spacing you give (their length includes the eaves overhang along the slope) and a ridge board.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-get\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel shows its pieces on a drawing to scale with every piece marked, a cut list by mark, and a total. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStock to buy\u003C/span\u003E table nests all the panels&#039; cuts into stock lengths, the shortest that takes each bar&#039;s cuts. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the shop drawing (SVG)\u003C/span\u003E writes the drawing; the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E button writes every cut list and the stock list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-panel-in-the-model\"\u003EPlace a panel in the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder each panel, give where it goes (x, y, z in millimetres and the angle) and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace as members\u003C/span\u003E: every piece becomes a member of the model, with the dressed section of its size and the weight of the material (about 500 kg/m³ for the timber, steel for the studs). A wall runs along the angle from its lower left end with its bottom at z; a floor&#039;s joists lie with their underside at z; a roof&#039;s gable elevation runs along the angle with z at the top of the wall plates. Each header ply is a member side by side. Pressing it again replaces the members placed before, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the placed members\u003C/span\u003E takes them out; each is one undo step. The Analysis leaves these members out: they are not steel frame members.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pdf-sheets\"\u003EPDF sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF sheets\u003C/span\u003E saves one A3 page for each panel: the panel to scale with every piece marked, the overall dimensions, the cut list and a title strip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProblems in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E panel: an opening too near the end of a wall or to another, a header that does not fit under the top plates, a piece longer than the longest stock.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Framing tab frames panels you describe and gives you what a framing crew and a lumber yard ask for. The panels are saved in the model file. The sizes, the spacing and the headers are yours. DetailCraft does not check that a stud at that spacing carries that wall or that a header spans that opening: that is for your code's tables and your engineer. Add a panel Choose Add a wall, Add a floor or Add a roof, then fill in the dimensions in millimetres, the material (timber or light-gauge steel) and the member size. Timber sizes are the nominal names with their dressed sizes (2×4 is 38 × 89 mm). Steel studs are named by depth and thickness, for example C92-1.2. Walls A wall has a bottom plate, one or more top plates (one track for steel), end studs, studs on the spacing you give, and for each opening: two king studs, two jack studs, a header of the size and plies you choose, and cripples over the header (and under the sill of a window). Give each opening its distance from the left end, its width, and its rough-opening top and bottom measured from the subfloor; a door has a bottom of 0. Floors and roofs A floor has joists across its width along its span, rim joists at both ends and, if you tick it, blocking at mid-span. A roof panel is a gable: a pair of rafters on the spacing you give (their length includes the eaves overhang along the slope) and a ridge board. What you get Each panel shows its pieces on a drawing to scale with every piece marked, a cut list by mark, and a total. The Stock to buy table nests all the panels' cuts into stock lengths, the shortest that takes each bar's cuts. Save the shop drawing (SVG) writes the drawing; the CSV button writes every cut list and the stock list. Place a panel in the model Under each panel, give where it goes (x, y, z in millimetres and the angle) and press Place as members: every piece becomes a member of the model, with the dressed section of its size and the weight of the material (about 500 kg/m³ for the timber, steel for the studs). A wall runs along the angle from its lower left end with its bottom at z; a floor's joists lie with their underside at z; a roof's gable elevation runs along the angle with z at the top of the wall plates. Each header ply is a member side by side. Pressing it again replaces the members placed before, and Remove the placed members takes them out; each is one undo step. The Analysis leaves these members out: they are not steel frame members. PDF sheets PDF sheets saves one A3 page for each panel: the panel to scale with every piece marked, the overall dimensions, the cut list and a title strip. Problems in the Problems panel: an opening too near the end of a wall or to another, a header that does not fit under the top plates, a piece longer than the longest stock.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"supplier-stock","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Supplier stock and lead times","summary":"Check the stock bars your cut list buys against a supplier's stock list: is there enough, and how long is the wait?","section":"tasks","order":51,"keywords":["supplier","stock","lead time","steel yard","mill","availability","backorder","bars","procurement","supply"],"headings":[{"id":"add-the-supplier-s-file","text":"Add the supplier's file","level":2},{"id":"what-you-see","text":"What you see","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESupply\u003C/span\u003E tab looks up each section the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut list\u003C/span\u003E buys in a supplier&#039;s stock list. The file stays in the model; nothing is sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-the-supplier-s-file\"\u003EAdd the supplier&#039;s file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a supplier file\u003C/span\u003E and choose a CSV from your supplier (comma, semicolon or tab separated). It needs a column for the item or section (Item, Name, Description, Section, SKU), and one for stock (In stock, Quantity, On hand) or for the lead time. The unit (each, metres, kilograms, tonnes), the price, the supplier and a restock date are read when there is a column for them. Words are understood too: &quot;Out of stock&quot;, &quot;In stock&quot;, &quot;2-3 weeks&quot;, &quot;backorder&quot;. Up to five files can be kept; adding a file with the same name replaces it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-see\"\u003EWhat you see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor each section the cut list buys, the table shows what the model needs, what is in stock, the lead time, the supplier and a result:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECovered:\u003C/strong\u003E enough in stock.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShort by:\u003C/strong\u003E the stock is below the need. If the stock is below the need and you set a longest wait, the lead time tells you how long the rest takes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EToo late:\u003C/strong\u003E there is no stock figure and the lead time is longer than the wait you accept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo stock figure:\u003C/strong\u003E the file does not say.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther unit:\u003C/strong\u003E the supplier lists the section in a unit the model cannot give.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat the model needs is given in bars (from the nested cut list, with your stock lengths), in metres of bar bought and in kilograms (the section&#039;s weight per metre), and the file&#039;s own unit is used. A section is matched by its name (&quot;W16 X 26&quot; and &quot;w16x26&quot; are the same). Sections that are not in the file are listed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShorts and late sections are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E panel. Nothing here orders anything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stock figures and lead times are the file&#039;s, as of the day it was made. Plates are not nested, so they are not looked up. The file is only read: any instruction in it is just text.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Supply tab looks up each section the Cut list buys in a supplier's stock list. The file stays in the model; nothing is sent anywhere. Add the supplier's file Press Add a supplier file and choose a CSV from your supplier (comma, semicolon or tab separated). It needs a column for the item or section (Item, Name, Description, Section, SKU), and one for stock (In stock, Quantity, On hand) or for the lead time. The unit (each, metres, kilograms, tonnes), the price, the supplier and a restock date are read when there is a column for them. Words are understood too: \"Out of stock\", \"In stock\", \"2-3 weeks\", \"backorder\". Up to five files can be kept; adding a file with the same name replaces it. What you see For each section the cut list buys, the table shows what the model needs, what is in stock, the lead time, the supplier and a result: Covered: enough in stock. Short by: the stock is below the need. If the stock is below the need and you set a longest wait, the lead time tells you how long the rest takes. Too late: there is no stock figure and the lead time is longer than the wait you accept. No stock figure: the file does not say. Other unit: the supplier lists the section in a unit the model cannot give. What the model needs is given in bars (from the nested cut list, with your stock lengths), in metres of bar bought and in kilograms (the section's weight per metre), and the file's own unit is used. A section is matched by its name (\"W16 X 26\" and \"w16x26\" are the same). Sections that are not in the file are listed. Shorts and late sections are in the Problems panel. Nothing here orders anything. Limits The stock figures and lead times are the file's, as of the day it was made. Plates are not nested, so they are not looked up. The file is only read: any instruction in it is just text.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"weight-report","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Material and weight report by phase","summary":"Cut lengths, counts, weights and paint areas for each phase, with its bolts and rebar, as a table, CSV or PDF.","section":"tasks","order":52,"keywords":["report","weight","tonnage","kg","paint","paint area","surface area","coating","cut length","material list","phase","bill of materials","csv","pdf"],"headings":[{"id":"how-the-figures-are-worked-out","text":"How the figures are worked out","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWeight report by phase\u003C/span\u003E, or open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy phase\u003C/span\u003E tab under the view. It shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESummary by phase\u003C/strong\u003E: for each phase, the number of parts, the steel weight, the paint area, the bolts and anchor rods, and the rebar; then the totals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne table per phase\u003C/strong\u003E: each part mark in it with its type, profile, material, cut length, count, weight each, weight, and paint area; then its bolts and anchor rods.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves the same tables in one CSV file (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; weight report.csv\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E makes a report on the sheet size from \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings#drawings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"drawings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E, with the summary first, each phase after, long tables carried over to the next page, and every page numbered.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-figures-are-worked-out\"\u003EHow the figures are worked out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWeights\u003C/strong\u003E are gross, as in the bill of materials: the profile&#039;s weight per meter times the cut length. Holes and copes are not taken off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaint area\u003C/strong\u003E is the outside of the section along the cut length: for an I-shape every face of the flanges and web, for a plate both faces and the edges, for a tube the outside only. The ends and the holes are not counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERebar\u003C/strong\u003E is the weight of the bars in the phase&#039;s footings, from the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/footings-and-rebar\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/footings-and-rebar\"\u003Ebending schedule\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA connection&#039;s plates and bolts count in the phase of its main member. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/phases\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/phases\"\u003EPhases\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/phases#marks-per-phase\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/phases\" data-hash=\"marks-per-phase\"\u003Emarks per phase\u003C/a\u003E on, each phase has its own marks (1-b1, 2-b1). With it off, a mark can appear in more than one phase: each phase lists the pieces it has.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"In the Output group, open More and choose Weight report by phase, or open the By phase tab under the view. It shows: Summary by phase: for each phase, the number of parts, the steel weight, the paint area, the bolts and anchor rods, and the rebar; then the totals. One table per phase: each part mark in it with its type, profile, material, cut length, count, weight each, weight, and paint area; then its bolts and anchor rods. Download CSV saves the same tables in one CSV file (\u003Cname\u003E weight report.csv). Download PDF makes a report on the sheet size from DetailCraft settings, with the summary first, each phase after, long tables carried over to the next page, and every page numbered. How the figures are worked out Weights are gross, as in the bill of materials: the profile's weight per meter times the cut length. Holes and copes are not taken off. Paint area is the outside of the section along the cut length: for an I-shape every face of the flanges and web, for a plate both faces and the edges, for a tube the outside only. The ends and the holes are not counted. Rebar is the weight of the bars in the phase's footings, from the bending schedule. A connection's plates and bolts count in the phase of its main member. See Phases. With marks per phase on, each phase has its own marks (1-b1, 2-b1). With it off, a mark can appear in more than one phase: each phase lists the pieces it has.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"kiss-export","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Export a KISS file for fabrication software","summary":"Send the bill of materials to a fabrication management (MIS) program as a KISS 1.1 file: assemblies, parts, phases as sequences, holes, copes and welds.","section":"tasks","order":58,"keywords":["kiss","kss","mis","fabrication management","production control","estimating","bill of materials","bom","export","fabtrol","sequence"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-in-it","text":"What is in it","level":2},{"id":"imperial-or-metric","text":"Imperial or metric","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKISS file for fabrication software\u003C/span\u003E. DetailCraft downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.kss\u003C/code\u003E, a KISS file (&quot;Keep It Simple, Steel&quot;, version 1.1), the plain text format most fabrication management programs import a bill of materials from.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-it\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe header: a job number (the first eight letters and digits of the model name), the model name, the date and time, and whether sizes are metric.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor each assembly mark: its main part (quantity = how many of the assembly), a drawing line (A-B1 and so on, the assembly drawing&#039;s number), an assembly line (name, type, weight, overall length, depth and width), one sequence line per \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/phases\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/phases\"\u003Ephase\u003C/a\u003E the assembly is in, and labor lines: holes (how many, diameter, thickness through), copes and welds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThen each part attached to it, with the \u003Cstrong\u003Etotal\u003C/strong\u003E quantity (per assembly × assemblies), as the standard asks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELoose bolts and anchor rods on drawing BOLTS, assembly FIELD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery line gives the type (W, C, L, HSS, PL, or MI for a custom profile), size, grade, cut length in millimeters, part name and weight.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"imperial-or-metric\"\u003EImperial or metric\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EKISS file sizes\u003C/strong\u003E chooses:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAutomatic\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): imperial when every member is an AISC shape, metric otherwise.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImperial\u003C/strong\u003E: shapes by their AISC designation (W 16X26, HSS 4X4X.250); plates and bolts, which DetailCraft keeps in millimeters, to the nearest 1/16 in; weights in pounds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMetric\u003C/strong\u003E: European shapes by their name (IPE300), plates as thickness X width in millimeters; weights in kilograms.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths are always in millimeters with two decimals, whatever the setting: the KISS standard asks for that and the receiving program converts them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGrades are cut to the standard&#039;s eight characters: A572 Gr 50 becomes A572-50. Commas in names become spaces (the format has no quoting). Check one import in your program before relying on it: DetailCraft&#039;s KISS files are checked against the standard here, not in a fabrication program yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"In the Output group, open More and choose KISS file for fabrication software. DetailCraft downloads \u003Cname\u003E.kss, a KISS file (\"Keep It Simple, Steel\", version 1.1), the plain text format most fabrication management programs import a bill of materials from. What is in it The header: a job number (the first eight letters and digits of the model name), the model name, the date and time, and whether sizes are metric. For each assembly mark: its main part (quantity = how many of the assembly), a drawing line (A-B1 and so on, the assembly drawing's number), an assembly line (name, type, weight, overall length, depth and width), one sequence line per phase the assembly is in, and labor lines: holes (how many, diameter, thickness through), copes and welds. Then each part attached to it, with the total quantity (per assembly × assemblies), as the standard asks. Loose bolts and anchor rods on drawing BOLTS, assembly FIELD. Every line gives the type (W, C, L, HSS, PL, or MI for a custom profile), size, grade, cut length in millimeters, part name and weight. Imperial or metric In DetailCraft settings, KISS file sizes chooses: Automatic (the start): imperial when every member is an AISC shape, metric otherwise. Imperial: shapes by their AISC designation (W 16X26, HSS 4X4X.250); plates and bolts, which DetailCraft keeps in millimeters, to the nearest 1/16 in; weights in pounds. Metric: European shapes by their name (IPE300), plates as thickness X width in millimeters; weights in kilograms. Lengths are always in millimeters with two decimals, whatever the setting: the KISS standard asks for that and the receiving program converts them. Grades are cut to the standard's eight characters: A572 Gr 50 becomes A572-50. Commas in names become spaces (the format has no quoting). Check one import in your program before relying on it: DetailCraft's KISS files are checked against the standard here, not in a fabrication program yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"end-plates","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Connect beams to columns with end plates","summary":"Add bolted end plates where a beam meets a column flange or web, set the plate, bolts and weld, and copy the connection to every similar joint.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["end plate","endplate","moment connection","bolted connection","joint","bolts","weld","extended end plate","flush","similar","copy connection","component"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-can-connect","text":"What it can connect","level":2},{"id":"add-end-plates-to-a-beam","text":"Add end plates to a beam","level":2},{"id":"change-the-plate-bolts-and-weld","text":"Change the plate, bolts and weld","level":2},{"id":"how-the-numbers-are-worked-out","text":"How the numbers are worked out","level":3},{"id":"warnings","text":"Warnings","level":3},{"id":"copy-it-to-every-similar-joint","text":"Copy it to every similar joint","level":2},{"id":"moving-and-deleting","text":"Moving and deleting","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn end plate connection welds a plate to the beam end and bolts it to the column flange or web. DetailCraft places the plate, cuts the beam back, drills matching holes in the plate and the column flange, adds the bolts and the weld, and checks edge distances and spacing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft does not design connections. The settings are starting values for you to change; nothing checks strength. The edge and spacing warnings use the minimums of EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3, which may differ from the rules you work to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-can-connect\"\u003EWhat it can connect\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Elevel\u003C/strong\u003E beam (both ends at the same height), upright (rotation 0), with an I-shape profile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EInto the \u003Cstrong\u003Eflange\u003C/strong\u003E or the \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E of a \u003Cstrong\u003Evertical\u003C/strong\u003E I-shape column, square to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe beam end must sit on the column&#039;s line, between the column&#039;s bottom and top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESkewed or sloped beams and non-I sections are refused with a reason: use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/shear-tabs\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/shear-tabs\"\u003Eshear tabs\u003C/a\u003E for those. On a web the plate and the beam flanges must fit between the column flanges; DetailCraft warns when they do not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt rotation 0 a column&#039;s flanges face along the numbered grid direction (x). A beam running from A to B meets the web, and gets an end plate on the web.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-end-plates-to-a-beam\"\u003EAdd end plates to a beam\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the beam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd plates\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd end plates\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery free end of the beam that meets a column flange or web square gets a plate. The status bar says &quot;2 end plates added. Select one to change its plate, bolts and weld; Similar copies it to joints like it.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first new end plate is selected and its settings are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing happens, read the status bar:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This beam does not end on a column line. End plates go where a beam end meets a vertical column.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This beam meets a column at an angle or slopes: end plates are built for square, level joints. Use Shear tabs.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Both ends already have connections.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-plate-bolts-and-weld\"\u003EChange the plate, bolts and weld\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the end plate (or one of its bolts) to select the connection. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate, bolts and weld (mm)\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe plate&#039;s thickness; also how far the beam is cut back past the flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E180\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAcross the joint\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtension above beam\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbove the top flange; above 0 makes an extended end plate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtension below beam\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBelow the bottom flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENominal bolt size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHole diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E22\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour clearance hole in the plate and the column flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt grade\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA325\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText; printed in the bill of materials\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt rows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERows of two bolts, centered on the plate height\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERow pitch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E75\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVertical spacing between rows\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGauge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E100\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHorizontal spacing between the two bolts of a row\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt length allowance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E30\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdded to the grip for the nut, washer and thread; see below\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet weld size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll around the beam end\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA36\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType a new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The plate, holes, bolts and the beam&#039;s cut length update at once. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow the settings you see what the connection makes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBolts\u003C/span\u003E: for example &quot;8 × 20 × 70 A325 (grip 36.2)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWeld\u003C/span\u003E: size, where, and its length.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E: its size and its part mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"how-the-numbers-are-worked-out\"\u003EHow the numbers are worked out\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe beam is cut back by half the column depth (on a web: half the web thickness) plus the plate thickness, so the plate sits on the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plate height is the beam depth plus the two extensions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBolt length = grip (plate thickness + column flange or web thickness) + allowance, rounded up to the next 5 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe weld length shown is the perimeter of the beam section.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"warnings\"\u003EWarnings\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe connection still builds, but these appear under it and in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBolt rows or bolts closer to the plate edge than 1.2 × the hole diameter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERow pitch under 2.2 × the hole diameter, or gauge under 2.4 × the hole diameter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHoles that run into the column web or its fillet, or off the column flange.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA plate wider than the column flange, or (on a web) wider than the space between the flanges; beam flanges that do not fit between them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA bolt row too close to a beam flange, or a gauge too tight for the beam web and its weld.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-it-to-every-similar-joint\"\u003ECopy it to every similar joint\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the connection you have set up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to similar (n)\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESimilar\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. The number in brackets is how many joints will get it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery joint with the same beam profile and column profile, beam into the same kind of face, that has no connection yet gets a copy with the same settings. The status bar says how many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopies are independent: changing one later does not change the others. Joints that already have a connection are left alone; delete those first if you want them replaced.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-and-deleting\"\u003EMoving and deleting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConnections are remade from their settings every time the model changes, so moving or resizing a beam or column carries its end plates along. If a change means the joint no longer fits, the connection stays in the model and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab says why, for example &quot;The beam end is not on the column line any more.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo delete a connection, select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Deleting the beam or the column deletes its connections too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENext: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/base-plates\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/base-plates\"\u003EAdd base plates and anchor rods\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An end plate connection welds a plate to the beam end and bolts it to the column flange or web. DetailCraft places the plate, cuts the beam back, drills matching holes in the plate and the column flange, adds the bolts and the weld, and checks edge distances and spacing. DetailCraft does not design connections. The settings are starting values for you to change; nothing checks strength. The edge and spacing warnings use the minimums of EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3, which may differ from the rules you work to. What it can connect A level beam (both ends at the same height), upright (rotation 0), with an I-shape profile. Into the flange or the web of a vertical I-shape column, square to it. The beam end must sit on the column's line, between the column's bottom and top. Skewed or sloped beams and non-I sections are refused with a reason: use shear tabs for those. On a web the plate and the beam flanges must fit between the column flanges; DetailCraft warns when they do not. At rotation 0 a column's flanges face along the numbered grid direction (x). A beam running from A to B meets the web, and gets an end plate on the web. Add end plates to a beam Choose Select (V) and click the beam. Choose End plates in the Connect group, or Add end plates in the Properties panel. Every free end of the beam that meets a column flange or web square gets a plate. The status bar says \"2 end plates added. Select one to change its plate, bolts and weld; Similar copies it to joints like it.\" The first new end plate is selected and its settings are in the Properties panel. If nothing happens, read the status bar: \"This beam does not end on a column line. End plates go where a beam end meets a vertical column.\" \"This beam meets a column at an angle or slopes: end plates are built for square, level joints. Use Shear tabs.\" \"Both ends already have connections.\" Change the plate, bolts and weld Click the end plate (or one of its bolts) to select the connection. Under Plate, bolts and weld (mm): Setting Default What it does Plate thickness 20 The plate's thickness; also how far the beam is cut back past the flange Plate width 180 Across the joint Extension above beam 0 Above the top flange; above 0 makes an extended end plate Extension below beam 0 Below the bottom flange Bolt diameter 20 Nominal bolt size Hole diameter 22 Your clearance hole in the plate and the column flange Bolt grade A325 Text; printed in the bill of materials Bolt rows 4 Rows of two bolts, centered on the plate height Row pitch 75 Vertical spacing between rows Gauge 100 Horizontal spacing between the two bolts of a row Bolt length allowance 30 Added to the grip for the nut, washer and thread; see below Fillet weld size 8 All around the beam end Plate material A36 Text Type a new value and press Enter or Tab. The plate, holes, bolts and the beam's cut length update at once. Each change is one undo step. Below the settings you see what the connection makes: Bolts: for example \"8 × 20 × 70 A325 (grip 36.2)\". Weld: size, where, and its length. Plate: its size and its part mark. How the numbers are worked out The beam is cut back by half the column depth (on a web: half the web thickness) plus the plate thickness, so the plate sits on the face. The plate height is the beam depth plus the two extensions. Bolt length = grip (plate thickness + column flange or web thickness) + allowance, rounded up to the next 5 mm. The weld length shown is the perimeter of the beam section. Warnings The connection still builds, but these appear under it and in the Problems tab: Bolt rows or bolts closer to the plate edge than 1.2 × the hole diameter. Row pitch under 2.2 × the hole diameter, or gauge under 2.4 × the hole diameter. Holes that run into the column web or its fillet, or off the column flange. A plate wider than the column flange, or (on a web) wider than the space between the flanges; beam flanges that do not fit between them. A bolt row too close to a beam flange, or a gauge too tight for the beam web and its weld. Copy it to every similar joint Select the connection you have set up. Choose Apply to similar (n) in the Properties panel, or Similar in the Connect group's More menu. The number in brackets is how many joints will get it. Every joint with the same beam profile and column profile, beam into the same kind of face, that has no connection yet gets a copy with the same settings. The status bar says how many. Copies are independent: changing one later does not change the others. Joints that already have a connection are left alone; delete those first if you want them replaced. Moving and deleting Connections are remade from their settings every time the model changes, so moving or resizing a beam or column carries its end plates along. If a change means the joint no longer fits, the connection stays in the model and the Problems tab says why, for example \"The beam end is not on the column line any more.\" To delete a connection, select it and press Delete. Deleting the beam or the column deletes its connections too. Next: Add base plates and anchor rods.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"drawings","scope":"detailcraft","title":"General-arrangement and assembly drawings","summary":"Make plans, elevations and one assembly drawing per assembly mark from the model, on A4 to A1 or ANSI B to D sheets, issue them with revision letters, and download them as PDF, DWG or DXF.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["hidden lines","hidden line removal","dashed","revision cloud","clouds","revision triangle","anchor bolt plan","anchor rods","rod schedule","base plate schedule","setting out","issue","revision","rev","revision table","re-issue","issue log","transmittal","dwg","dxf","cad file","export drawings","drawings","general arrangement","ga","plan","elevation","assembly drawing","sheet size","paper size","a4","a3","a2","a1","ansi b","ansi c","ansi d","tabloid","11 x 17","22 x 34","pdf","print","scale","title block","model ref"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-in-the-set","text":"What is in the set","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-sheet-size","text":"Choose the sheet size","level":2},{"id":"issue-drawings-and-revisions","text":"Issue drawings and revisions","level":2},{"id":"revision-clouds","text":"Revision clouds","level":3},{"id":"download","text":"Download","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group. DetailCraft makes the whole set from the model as it is now. Nothing is stored: change the model, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E again, and the set is made again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-the-set\"\u003EWhat is in the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlans\u003C/strong\u003E, one per level that has something on it, looking down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevations\u003C/strong\u003E, one per grid line with members within 1 m of it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe anchor bolt plan\u003C/strong\u003E, when the model has base plates: every base plate seen from above with its column and anchor rods, the grids with their dimensions, a typical detail of each rod pattern (BP1, BP2 …) with the rod spacing and edge distances, and two schedules: the anchor rods by mark (R1, R2 …: quantity, diameter, length, projection above the plate, embedment, grade) and the base plates by grid (column, pattern, plate, rods, top of plate). A column off the grid is located by its coordinates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssembly drawings\u003C/strong\u003E, one per assembly mark: front, top and end views, chained dimensions to holes and plate edges, the part list, loose bolts and rods, and weld and hole notes. Each welded plate has a fillet weld symbol in the front view (AWS A2.4): the arrow points at the joint, the triangle below the reference line means the arrow side, the figure to its left is the weld size in mm, and a circle where the arrow meets the line means all around.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHidden lines.\u003C/strong\u003E Plans and elevations leave out the lines of a part where a part wholly in front of it covers it (a beam seen end-on behind a column). Assembly drawings draw a plate dashed where the main part covers it: behind the web in the front view (seen from the part&#039;s −u side), under the top flange in the top view. Parts that sit at the same depth, or overlap in depth, are both drawn in full.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach sheet has a title block with the drawing number, the scale, the model name, the date, the sheet number with the sheet size, and the model ref: a code made from the saved model, so a print can be matched to the model it came from.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-sheet-size\"\u003EChoose the sheet size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick the size in the list at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E window: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA4\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA3\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA2\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA1\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EANSI B\u003C/span\u003E (11 × 17 in), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EANSI C\u003C/span\u003E (17 × 22 in) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EANSI D\u003C/span\u003E (22 × 34 in). Every sheet is landscape. The set is made again on the new size at once, and the same size is used for shop drawings. It is also in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings#drawings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"drawings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA bigger sheet draws at a larger scale. Scales are always standard ones (1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100 and so on).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EText stays the same size on every sheet, so it stays readable when printed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a small sheet a long, thin assembly can get a larger scale across its depth than along its length. The view title then says both scales, and the dimensions govern.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"issue-drawings-and-revisions\"\u003EIssue drawings and revisions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line above the preview says where the sheet shown stands: \u003Cstrong\u003ENot issued yet\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ERev B, issued … ; unchanged since\u003C/strong\u003E, or, in red, \u003Cstrong\u003EChanged since rev B: needs re-issue\u003C/strong\u003E. The sheet list marks issued sheets with their revision and the ones that need re-issue.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType what the issue is for (it starts as &quot;Issued for fabrication&quot;) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssue this sheet\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssue changed sheets\u003C/span\u003E for every sheet that is new or has changed since its last issue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first issue of a sheet is revision A, then B, C and so on (I and O are skipped).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe title block shows the drawing number with its revision (&quot;A-B1  rev B&quot;), and a revision table beside it lists the latest issues: revision, date and description. On a small sheet it shows as many as fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA sheet &quot;changes&quot; when what is drawn on it changes: a moved member, a new hole, a renumbered mark. The date, the sheet count and the revision table itself do not count. Changing the sheet size changes every sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe issue log is saved in the model file, and issuing is one undo step. The \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/kiss-export\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/kiss-export\"\u003EKISS file\u003C/a\u003E carries each drawing&#039;s latest revision.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"revision-clouds\"\u003ERevision clouds\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom the second issue of a sheet on, every part or footing that changed is drawn inside a revision cloud with a revision triangle carrying the letter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile a sheet has changed since its last issue, the clouds show what changed since then, with the coming revision in the triangle (rev B issued and changed: the triangles say C).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnce you issue it, the clouds stay on that revision&#039;s sheet, as on a printed set, until the sheet changes again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach sheet clouds only changes it shows. Plans and elevations draw no holes or copes, so they cloud a member that moved, was turned or resized, or whose mark changed. Assembly drawings and the anchor bolt plan also cloud new or moved holes and copes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA renumbered mark counts: when a change makes a beam a new assembly, the beams whose mark moved up (B1 to B2) are clouded on plans and elevations.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA part that was deleted leaves nothing to cloud.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESheets issued before revision clouds came in (DetailCraft 0.4) start clouding from their next issue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClouds are in the PDF, DXF and DWG downloads, and never count as a change themselves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"download\"\u003EDownload\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the format in the list beside the buttons, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis sheet\u003C/span\u003E for the sheet shown or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets\u003C/span\u003E for the whole set.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPDF\u003C/strong\u003E: to print or send. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets\u003C/span\u003E makes one PDF, one page per sheet, each page the chosen size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E, in the 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000 format: to open the sheets in a CAD program. Pick an older format for an older program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF (R12)\u003C/strong\u003E: the version almost every CAD program opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG or DXF sheet is drawn at full size: one drawing unit is one millimeter on the paper, so it prints at 1:1 and every view keeps the scale its title says. Each pen is its own layer: DC-OUTLINE, DC-THICK, DC-THIN, DC-HIDDEN, DC-CENTER, DC-GRID and so on, with its line weight (DWG) and its dashes. Words are text in the DetailCraft text style (Arial). Dimensions are the lines and figures you see, not dimension objects. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets\u003C/span\u003E puts the DWG or DXF files in one zip, one file per sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDXF R12 has no line weights and no fills: the layers keep their names, so set the pens by layer when you plot, and filled shapes are left out (the status bar says how many).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPrint at 100% (no &quot;fit to page&quot;) on paper of the same size if you want the stated scales to hold.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Choose Drawings in the Output group. DetailCraft makes the whole set from the model as it is now. Nothing is stored: change the model, open Drawings again, and the set is made again. What is in the set Plans, one per level that has something on it, looking down. Elevations, one per grid line with members within 1 m of it. The anchor bolt plan, when the model has base plates: every base plate seen from above with its column and anchor rods, the grids with their dimensions, a typical detail of each rod pattern (BP1, BP2 …) with the rod spacing and edge distances, and two schedules: the anchor rods by mark (R1, R2 …: quantity, diameter, length, projection above the plate, embedment, grade) and the base plates by grid (column, pattern, plate, rods, top of plate). A column off the grid is located by its coordinates. Assembly drawings, one per assembly mark: front, top and end views, chained dimensions to holes and plate edges, the part list, loose bolts and rods, and weld and hole notes. Each welded plate has a fillet weld symbol in the front view (AWS A2.4): the arrow points at the joint, the triangle below the reference line means the arrow side, the figure to its left is the weld size in mm, and a circle where the arrow meets the line means all around. Hidden lines. Plans and elevations leave out the lines of a part where a part wholly in front of it covers it (a beam seen end-on behind a column). Assembly drawings draw a plate dashed where the main part covers it: behind the web in the front view (seen from the part's −u side), under the top flange in the top view. Parts that sit at the same depth, or overlap in depth, are both drawn in full. Each sheet has a title block with the drawing number, the scale, the model name, the date, the sheet number with the sheet size, and the model ref: a code made from the saved model, so a print can be matched to the model it came from. Choose the sheet size Pick the size in the list at the top of the Drawings window: A4, A3, A2, A1, ANSI B (11 × 17 in), ANSI C (17 × 22 in) or ANSI D (22 × 34 in). Every sheet is landscape. The set is made again on the new size at once, and the same size is used for shop drawings. It is also in DetailCraft settings, and this browser remembers it. A bigger sheet draws at a larger scale. Scales are always standard ones (1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100 and so on). Text stays the same size on every sheet, so it stays readable when printed. On a small sheet a long, thin assembly can get a larger scale across its depth than along its length. The view title then says both scales, and the dimensions govern. Issue drawings and revisions The line above the preview says where the sheet shown stands: Not issued yet, Rev B, issued … ; unchanged since, or, in red, Changed since rev B: needs re-issue. The sheet list marks issued sheets with their revision and the ones that need re-issue. Type what the issue is for (it starts as \"Issued for fabrication\") and choose Issue this sheet, or Issue changed sheets for every sheet that is new or has changed since its last issue. The first issue of a sheet is revision A, then B, C and so on (I and O are skipped). The title block shows the drawing number with its revision (\"A-B1 rev B\"), and a revision table beside it lists the latest issues: revision, date and description. On a small sheet it shows as many as fit. A sheet \"changes\" when what is drawn on it changes: a moved member, a new hole, a renumbered mark. The date, the sheet count and the revision table itself do not count. Changing the sheet size changes every sheet. The issue log is saved in the model file, and issuing is one undo step. The KISS file carries each drawing's latest revision. Revision clouds From the second issue of a sheet on, every part or footing that changed is drawn inside a revision cloud with a revision triangle carrying the letter. While a sheet has changed since its last issue, the clouds show what changed since then, with the coming revision in the triangle (rev B issued and changed: the triangles say C). Once you issue it, the clouds stay on that revision's sheet, as on a printed set, until the sheet changes again. Each sheet clouds only changes it shows. Plans and elevations draw no holes or copes, so they cloud a member that moved, was turned or resized, or whose mark changed. Assembly drawings and the anchor bolt plan also cloud new or moved holes and copes. A renumbered mark counts: when a change makes a beam a new assembly, the beams whose mark moved up (B1 to B2) are clouded on plans and elevations. A part that was deleted leaves nothing to cloud. Sheets issued before revision clouds came in (DetailCraft 0.4) start clouding from their next issue. Clouds are in the PDF, DXF and DWG downloads, and never count as a change themselves. Download Choose the format in the list beside the buttons, then This sheet for the sheet shown or All sheets for the whole set. PDF: to print or send. All sheets makes one PDF, one page per sheet, each page the chosen size. DWG, in the 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000 format: to open the sheets in a CAD program. Pick an older format for an older program. DXF (R12): the version almost every CAD program opens. A DWG or DXF sheet is drawn at full size: one drawing unit is one millimeter on the paper, so it prints at 1:1 and every view keeps the scale its title says. Each pen is its own layer: DC-OUTLINE, DC-THICK, DC-THIN, DC-HIDDEN, DC-CENTER, DC-GRID and so on, with its line weight (DWG) and its dashes. Words are text in the DetailCraft text style (Arial). Dimensions are the lines and figures you see, not dimension objects. All sheets puts the DWG or DXF files in one zip, one file per sheet. DXF R12 has no line weights and no fills: the layers keep their names, so set the pens by layer when you plot, and filled shapes are left out (the status bar says how many). Print at 100% (no \"fit to page\") on paper of the same size if you want the stated scales to hold.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"base-plates","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Add base plates and anchor rods","summary":"Put a welded base plate with four anchor rods under a column, set its sizes, and copy it to every similar column.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["base plate","baseplate","anchor rods","anchor bolts","holding down bolts","column base","foundation bolts","similar"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-base-plate","text":"Add a base plate","level":2},{"id":"change-it","text":"Change it","level":2},{"id":"base-details-grout-stiffeners-shear-lug-washers","text":"Base details: grout, stiffeners, shear lug, washers","level":2},{"id":"copy-it-to-similar-columns","text":"Copy it to similar columns","level":2},{"id":"base-plates-and-footings","text":"Base plates and footings","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA base plate connection puts a plate under the column, cuts the column short by the plate thickness, welds the two together, and adds four anchor rods through holes in the plate.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sizes are starting values, not a design. DetailCraft does not check bearing, rod strength or embedment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-base-plate\"\u003EAdd a base plate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click a column. It must be vertical.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBase plate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd base plate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Base plate added with four anchor rods.&quot; The new connection is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA column can have one base plate. If it already has one, the status bar says &quot;This column already has a base plate.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-it\"\u003EChange it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the base plate (click the plate or a rod). Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate, bolts and weld (mm)\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E25\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe column is shortened by this much\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate length\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E450\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong the column depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E400\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAcross the column\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERod diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnchor rod size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHole diameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E33\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHole in the plate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt grade\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF1554 Gr 36\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe rod grade, as text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERod spacing (along depth)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E350\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBetween rod centers, along the plate length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERod spacing (across)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E300\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBetween rod centers, across\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERod projection above plate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E100\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom the plate top to the rod top\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERod embedment below plate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E450\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom the plate underside down into the concrete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet weld size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll around the column base\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate material\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA36\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrout gap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpace between the top of concrete and the plate underside\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStiffeners\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone, 2 or 4; see below\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStiffener thickness, height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12, 150\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShear lug depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far the lug reaches below the plate; 0 = no lug\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShear lug thickness, width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E25, 150\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate washers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn every rod, or none\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWasher size, thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0, 0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0 = the minimum from AISC Design Guide 1 for the rod\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERod length = embedment + grout gap + plate thickness + projection. With the defaults that is 575 mm.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"base-details-grout-stiffeners-shear-lug-washers\"\u003EBase details: grout, stiffeners, shear lug, washers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll four are off on a new base plate, and on base plates from models made before DetailCraft 0.4.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGrout gap.\u003C/strong\u003E The plate sits this far above the top of concrete, and the rods get longer by it. AISC Design Guide 1 (2.10) suggests 1 in. on a finished floor and 1½ to 2 in. on a footing or pier; more for large plates and plates with a shear lug.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStiffeners.\u003C/strong\u003E Vertical plates welded to the column and the plate, from the column face out to the plate edge, as rectangles (no chamfer or taper yet). \u003Cstrong\u003E2\u003C/strong\u003E stand in line with the web, beyond each flange; \u003Cstrong\u003E4\u003C/strong\u003E add two square to the web, between the flanges, out to the plate sides. On a tube column they stand at the middle of each face. Each is welded to the column and the plate on both sides.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShear lug.\u003C/strong\u003E A plate welded all round under the base plate, in line with the column depth, centered. DetailCraft warns if it is no deeper than the grout gap. It does not cut the pocket in the footing; the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/clash-check\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/clash-check\"\u003Eclash check\u003C/a\u003E leaves the lug and the footing out for that reason.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlate washers.\u003C/strong\u003E A square plate washer on each rod, sitting on the base plate, listed in the column&#039;s assembly as loose (shipped with the column, not welded: Design Guide 1, 2.6). With size and thickness 0, DetailCraft takes the minimum washer from AISC Design Guide 1, 2nd ed., Table 2.3 for the rod; a metric rod takes the next inch size up (an M20 rod gets the 7/8 in. row: 2½ in. × 5/16 in.). The properties panel shows the size used. The washer&#039;s hole is the rod diameter + 1/16 in.: that is DetailCraft&#039;s choice, the table does not give one. DetailCraft warns when the rods may not stick out far enough for the washer and a nut (projection less than washer thickness + rod diameter).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDesign Guide 1, Table 2.3, also recommends the rod hole in the plate: 1-5/16 in. for a ¾ in. rod, 1-9/16 in. for ⅞ in., 1-13/16 in. for 1 in. DetailCraft does not change your hole size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWarnings also appear when the rod holes are closer than 1.2 × the hole diameter to the plate edge, when the rods fall inside the column outline, or when the plate is smaller than the column.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-it-to-similar-columns\"\u003ECopy it to similar columns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the base plate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to similar (n)\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESimilar\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery vertical column with the same profile that has no base plate and does not stand on another column gets a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"base-plates-and-footings\"\u003EBase plates and footings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA base plate does not need a footing, and a footing does not know about the base plate above it: the anchor rods are not tied into the footing&#039;s bars. Place footings with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFooting\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/footings-and-rebar\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/footings-and-rebar\"\u003EPlace footings with rebar\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A base plate connection puts a plate under the column, cuts the column short by the plate thickness, welds the two together, and adds four anchor rods through holes in the plate. The sizes are starting values, not a design. DetailCraft does not check bearing, rod strength or embedment. Add a base plate Choose Select (V) and click a column. It must be vertical. Choose Base plate in the Connect group, or Add base plate in the Properties panel. The status bar says \"Base plate added with four anchor rods.\" The new connection is selected. A column can have one base plate. If it already has one, the status bar says \"This column already has a base plate.\" Change it Select the base plate (click the plate or a rod). Under Plate, bolts and weld (mm): Setting Default What it does Plate thickness 25 The column is shortened by this much Plate length 450 Along the column depth Plate width 400 Across the column Rod diameter 20 Anchor rod size Hole diameter 33 Hole in the plate Bolt grade F1554 Gr 36 The rod grade, as text Rod spacing (along depth) 350 Between rod centers, along the plate length Rod spacing (across) 300 Between rod centers, across Rod projection above plate 100 From the plate top to the rod top Rod embedment below plate 450 From the plate underside down into the concrete Fillet weld size 8 All around the column base Plate material A36 Text Grout gap 0 Space between the top of concrete and the plate underside Stiffeners None None, 2 or 4; see below Stiffener thickness, height 12, 150 Shear lug depth 0 How far the lug reaches below the plate; 0 = no lug Shear lug thickness, width 25, 150 Plate washers None On every rod, or none Washer size, thickness 0, 0 0 = the minimum from AISC Design Guide 1 for the rod Rod length = embedment + grout gap + plate thickness + projection. With the defaults that is 575 mm. Base details: grout, stiffeners, shear lug, washers All four are off on a new base plate, and on base plates from models made before DetailCraft 0.4. Grout gap. The plate sits this far above the top of concrete, and the rods get longer by it. AISC Design Guide 1 (2.10) suggests 1 in. on a finished floor and 1½ to 2 in. on a footing or pier; more for large plates and plates with a shear lug. Stiffeners. Vertical plates welded to the column and the plate, from the column face out to the plate edge, as rectangles (no chamfer or taper yet). 2 stand in line with the web, beyond each flange; 4 add two square to the web, between the flanges, out to the plate sides. On a tube column they stand at the middle of each face. Each is welded to the column and the plate on both sides. Shear lug. A plate welded all round under the base plate, in line with the column depth, centered. DetailCraft warns if it is no deeper than the grout gap. It does not cut the pocket in the footing; the clash check leaves the lug and the footing out for that reason. Plate washers. A square plate washer on each rod, sitting on the base plate, listed in the column's assembly as loose (shipped with the column, not welded: Design Guide 1, 2.6). With size and thickness 0, DetailCraft takes the minimum washer from AISC Design Guide 1, 2nd ed., Table 2.3 for the rod; a metric rod takes the next inch size up (an M20 rod gets the 7/8 in. row: 2½ in. × 5/16 in.). The properties panel shows the size used. The washer's hole is the rod diameter + 1/16 in.: that is DetailCraft's choice, the table does not give one. DetailCraft warns when the rods may not stick out far enough for the washer and a nut (projection less than washer thickness + rod diameter). Design Guide 1, Table 2.3, also recommends the rod hole in the plate: 1-5/16 in. for a ¾ in. rod, 1-9/16 in. for ⅞ in., 1-13/16 in. for 1 in. DetailCraft does not change your hole size. Warnings also appear when the rod holes are closer than 1.2 × the hole diameter to the plate edge, when the rods fall inside the column outline, or when the plate is smaller than the column. Copy it to similar columns Select the base plate. Choose Apply to similar (n), or Similar in the Connect group's More menu. Every vertical column with the same profile that has no base plate and does not stand on another column gets a copy. Base plates and footings A base plate does not need a footing, and a footing does not know about the base plate above it: the anchor rods are not tied into the footing's bars. Place footings with Footing; see Place footings with rebar.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"holes-and-copes","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Add holes and copes","summary":"Type holes into webs, flanges and plates, and cope the flanges at a beam end, measured from the part's cut end.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["hole","holes","bolt hole","cope","notch","cut","coping","drill","punch","web hole","flange hole"],"headings":[{"id":"where-positions-are-measured-from","text":"Where positions are measured from","level":2},{"id":"add-a-hole","text":"Add a hole","level":2},{"id":"add-a-cope","text":"Add a cope","level":2},{"id":"how-holes-and-copes-affect-marks","text":"How holes and copes affect marks","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EHoles and copes are typed in by position. They belong to the member and move with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHoles made by connections (end plates, base plates) are added for you; you do not type those. They are listed as &quot;… holes from connections (edit the connection)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-positions-are-measured-from\"\u003EWhere positions are measured from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ex\u003C/strong\u003E runs along the part from its \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut start\u003C/strong\u003E: the start end after any connection has cut it back. So x = 0 is the real end of the steel, not the grid point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ey\u003C/strong\u003E is measured across the face from the section&#039;s center:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon the \u003Cstrong\u003Eweb\u003C/strong\u003E: up (positive) or down from mid-depth;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon a \u003Cstrong\u003Eflange\u003C/strong\u003E or a \u003Cstrong\u003Eplate\u003C/strong\u003E: across the width, from the center line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe member&#039;s start is the first point you clicked when you placed it. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnds (x, y, z mm)\u003C/span\u003E rows show which end is which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-hole\"\u003EAdd a hole\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the member.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHoles\u003C/span\u003E box of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFace\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWeb\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop flange\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom flange\u003C/span\u003E (for an I-shape, channel or rectangular tube), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate face\u003C/span\u003E (for a plate).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ex along (mm)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ey across (mm)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/span\u003E. The form starts at x 100, y 0, diameter 22.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd hole\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Hole added. x is from the cut start; y from the section center (web: up, flange/plate: across).&quot; The hole appears in the list, and in the view as a dark plug through the face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo remove a hole, choose the × beside it in the list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAngles and round tubes do not take holes yet: their \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHoles\u003C/span\u003E box says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the 3D view holes are drawn as dark plugs, not cut through the steel. They are real holes in the NC files, the shop drawings and the IFC export.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA hole whose x is beyond either end of the part shows in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab as &quot;a hole is off the end of the part&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-cope\"\u003EAdd a cope\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopes are for I-shapes: they cut away the top or bottom flange (and that depth of web) at one end, so the beam clears another member.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect an I-shape member.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopes\u003C/span\u003E box, pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength (mm)\u003C/span\u003E (along the beam from that end) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth (mm)\u003C/span\u003E (down from the top, or up from the bottom). The form starts at 100 x 40.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd cope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view shows the cope cut out of the steel. The list reads, for example, &quot;Top at the start: 100 x 40&quot;. Remove it with its ×.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopes are square: there is no radius at the inside corner yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-holes-and-copes-affect-marks\"\u003EHow holes and copes affect marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo parts get the same part mark only if their holes and copes match within 1 mm, allowing for the part being turned over. Adding a hole to one beam of a group gives it a new mark. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/mark-rules\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/mark-rules\"\u003EMark rules\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Holes and copes are typed in by position. They belong to the member and move with it. Holes made by connections (end plates, base plates) are added for you; you do not type those. They are listed as \"… holes from connections (edit the connection)\". Where positions are measured from x runs along the part from its cut start: the start end after any connection has cut it back. So x = 0 is the real end of the steel, not the grid point. y is measured across the face from the section's center: on the web: up (positive) or down from mid-depth; on a flange or a plate: across the width, from the center line. The member's start is the first point you clicked when you placed it. The Ends (x, y, z mm) rows show which end is which. Add a hole Select the member. In the Holes box of the Properties panel, pick the Face: Web, Top flange or Bottom flange (for an I-shape, channel or rectangular tube), or Plate face (for a plate). Type x along (mm), y across (mm) and Diameter (mm). The form starts at x 100, y 0, diameter 22. Choose Add hole. The status bar says \"Hole added. x is from the cut start; y from the section center (web: up, flange/plate: across).\" The hole appears in the list, and in the view as a dark plug through the face. To remove a hole, choose the × beside it in the list. Angles and round tubes do not take holes yet: their Holes box says so. In the 3D view holes are drawn as dark plugs, not cut through the steel. They are real holes in the NC files, the shop drawings and the IFC export. A hole whose x is beyond either end of the part shows in the Problems tab as \"a hole is off the end of the part\". Add a cope Copes are for I-shapes: they cut away the top or bottom flange (and that depth of web) at one end, so the beam clears another member. Select an I-shape member. In the Copes box, pick End (Start or End) and Side (Top or Bottom). Type Length (mm) (along the beam from that end) and Depth (mm) (down from the top, or up from the bottom). The form starts at 100 x 40. Choose Add cope. The view shows the cope cut out of the steel. The list reads, for example, \"Top at the start: 100 x 40\". Remove it with its ×. Copes are square: there is no radius at the inside corner yet. How holes and copes affect marks Two parts get the same part mark only if their holes and copes match within 1 mm, allowing for the part being turned over. Adding a hole to one beam of a group gives it a new mark. See Mark rules.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"footings-and-rebar","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Place footings with rebar","summary":"Place rectangular pad footings with a bottom mat and starter bars, check them, and download the bending schedule.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["footing","pad footing","foundation","rebar","reinforcement","bars","mat","starters","dowels","bending schedule","bar list","concrete","hooks","cover"],"headings":[{"id":"place-a-footing","text":"Place a footing","level":2},{"id":"footing-settings","text":"Footing settings","level":2},{"id":"change-a-placed-footing","text":"Change a placed footing","level":2},{"id":"bar-marks-and-the-bending-schedule","text":"Bar marks and the bending schedule","level":2},{"id":"what-detailcraft-does-not-check","text":"What DetailCraft does not check","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis topic is about rectangular pad footings. Beams, columns, slabs and walls are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/reinforced-concrete\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/reinforced-concrete\"\u003EDetail reinforced concrete\u003C/a\u003E. Each footing gets a bottom mat both ways and, if you want them, starter bars standing up out of it. Bars are US sizes #3 to #11.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-footing\"\u003EPlace a footing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and pick the level of the footing top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFooting\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the footing in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel (see below), or keep the defaults.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a grid intersection. The footing is centered on it with its top at the level. The status bar says, for example, &quot;Footing 2000 x 2000 x 600 under A/1.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E form at the bottom of the panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe footing is drawn see-through so you can see its bars, which are drawn as solid tubes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"footing-settings\"\u003EFooting settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength L (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong x; 300 to 50000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth B (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlong y; 300 to 50000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth H (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E600\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E150 to 10000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConcrete\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4000 psi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText, printed in the IFC export\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBottom mat\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELong bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBars running along L, the lower layer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpacing (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E250\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe largest spacing allowed; bars are spread evenly at this or closer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShort bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBars running along B, laid on the long bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpacing (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E250\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs above\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECover (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E75\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear cover on every face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd hooks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns both ends of each mat bar up in a standard 90° hook (shape U); off gives straight bars (shape S)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStarter bars\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarter bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn them off for a footing without starters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#6\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBars along L / Bars along B\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 / 2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA grid of bars, centered on the footing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpacing along L / Spacing along B\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E250 / 250\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBetween bar centers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProjection above top\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E600\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far the bars stand above the footing top\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHook at the bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA 90° hook pointing outward, sitting on the mat (shape L)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the fields, DetailCraft shows the hook leg for the long bars, for example &quot;Hook leg for #5: 254 mm out to out.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-placed-footing\"\u003EChange a placed footing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the footing (click its concrete away from any column, or one of its bars). You get the same fields plus \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenter x\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECenter y\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop z\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E. Below them a table lists the bars of this footing with their marks, counts and lengths, and the concrete volume in cubic meters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is checked before it is kept. A change that cannot be built is refused and the status bar says why, for example &quot;The cover leaves no room for bars in this footing.&quot;, &quot;The footing is too shallow for two layers of bars inside the cover.&quot; or &quot;The starter bars do not fit inside the footing with 75 mm cover.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanges that can be built but need a look are listed under the footing and in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;#11 bars at 59.6 mm leave less than 35.8 mm clear (ACI 318-19 25.2.1).&quot; The clear gap between bars must be at least the bar diameter and at least 25.4 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;#5 hooks need 254 mm of depth; only 150 mm fits inside the cover.&quot; The hook legs are then cut short to fit, so they are no longer standard hooks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;The starter hooks reach into the cover.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Starter bars are closer than the minimum clear spacing.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bar-marks-and-the-bending-schedule\"\u003EBar marks and the bending schedule\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIdentical bars (same size, shape and dimensions to the millimeter) share a bar mark across all footings. Marks are written \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;bar number&gt;F&lt;n&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: 5F1, 5F2 for #5 bars, 6F1 for the first #6 bar, and so on. Footings with the same size, concrete, mat and starters share a footing mark: F1, F2 …\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending schedule\u003C/span\u003E tab at the bottom. Each row is one bar mark: size, shape, dimensions A, B and C, count, cut length, total length, mass per meter, weight each, total weight, and what the bar is used for.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E in the tab, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, for \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; bending schedule.csv\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShapes are DetailCraft&#039;s own codes, not a standard&#039;s: S straight, L one bend, U two bends. Dimensions are out to out; the cut length is the exact centerline length through the bends. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/rebar-reference\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/rebar-reference\"\u003ERebar reference\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-detailcraft-does-not-check\"\u003EWhat DetailCraft does not check\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDevelopment and lap lengths, and whether the starter bars are long enough for the column above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe aggregate-size part of the clear spacing rule (it does not know the mix).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnything about the column or base plate above: footings and steel are separate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"This topic is about rectangular pad footings. Beams, columns, slabs and walls are in Detail reinforced concrete. Each footing gets a bottom mat both ways and, if you want them, starter bars standing up out of it. Bars are US sizes #3 to #11. Place a footing Choose Plan and pick the level of the footing top. Choose Footing in the Place group, or press F. Set the footing in the Properties panel (see below), or keep the defaults. Click a grid intersection. The footing is centered on it with its top at the level. The status bar says, for example, \"Footing 2000 x 2000 x 600 under A/1.\" You can also use the Add by grid form at the bottom of the panel. The footing is drawn see-through so you can see its bars, which are drawn as solid tubes. Footing settings Field Default Notes Length L (mm) 2000 Along x; 300 to 50000 Width B (mm) 2000 Along y; 300 to 50000 Depth H (mm) 600 150 to 10000 Concrete 4000 psi Text, printed in the IFC export Bottom mat Field Default Notes Long bars #5 Bars running along L, the lower layer Spacing (mm) 250 The largest spacing allowed; bars are spread evenly at this or closer Short bars #5 Bars running along B, laid on the long bars Spacing (mm) 250 As above Cover (mm) 75 Clear cover on every face End hooks On Turns both ends of each mat bar up in a standard 90° hook (shape U); off gives straight bars (shape S) Starter bars Field Default Notes Starter bars On Turn them off for a footing without starters Bar #6 Bars along L / Bars along B 2 / 2 A grid of bars, centered on the footing Spacing along L / Spacing along B 250 / 250 Between bar centers Projection above top 600 How far the bars stand above the footing top Hook at the bottom On A 90° hook pointing outward, sitting on the mat (shape L) Under the fields, DetailCraft shows the hook leg for the long bars, for example \"Hook leg for #5: 254 mm out to out.\" Change a placed footing Select the footing (click its concrete away from any column, or one of its bars). You get the same fields plus Center x, Center y, Top z and Rotation (°). Below them a table lists the bars of this footing with their marks, counts and lengths, and the concrete volume in cubic meters. Each change is checked before it is kept. A change that cannot be built is refused and the status bar says why, for example \"The cover leaves no room for bars in this footing.\", \"The footing is too shallow for two layers of bars inside the cover.\" or \"The starter bars do not fit inside the footing with 75 mm cover.\" Changes that can be built but need a look are listed under the footing and in the Problems tab: \"#11 bars at 59.6 mm leave less than 35.8 mm clear (ACI 318-19 25.2.1).\" The clear gap between bars must be at least the bar diameter and at least 25.4 mm. \"#5 hooks need 254 mm of depth; only 150 mm fits inside the cover.\" The hook legs are then cut short to fit, so they are no longer standard hooks. \"The starter hooks reach into the cover.\" \"Starter bars are closer than the minimum clear spacing.\" Bar marks and the bending schedule Identical bars (same size, shape and dimensions to the millimeter) share a bar mark across all footings. Marks are written \u003Cbar number\u003EF\u003Cn\u003E: 5F1, 5F2 for #5 bars, 6F1 for the first #6 bar, and so on. Footings with the same size, concrete, mat and starters share a footing mark: F1, F2 … Open the Bending schedule tab at the bottom. Each row is one bar mark: size, shape, dimensions A, B and C, count, cut length, total length, mass per meter, weight each, total weight, and what the bar is used for. Choose Download CSV in the tab, or Bending in the Output group's More menu, for \u003Cname\u003E bending schedule.csv. Shapes are DetailCraft's own codes, not a standard's: S straight, L one bend, U two bends. Dimensions are out to out; the cut length is the exact centerline length through the bends. See Rebar reference. What DetailCraft does not check Development and lap lengths, and whether the starter bars are long enough for the column above. The aggregate-size part of the clear spacing rule (it does not know the mix). Anything about the column or base plate above: footings and steel are separate. See also What is not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"reinforced-concrete","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Detail reinforced concrete beams, columns, slabs and walls","summary":"Add concrete beams, columns, slabs and walls by grid, give them bars, stirrups and ties, check laps and anchorage against ACI 318-19 or EN 1992-1-1, and make shop drawings and bar-bending sheets.","section":"tasks","order":91,"keywords":["reinforced concrete","rebar","reinforcement","beam","column","slab","wall","stirrups","ties","links","hooks","laps","lap splice","anchorage","development length","ld","ldh","lbd","cover","spacing","trimmers","openings","mats","bar marks","bending schedule","bar bending schedule","bbs","shop drawing","ACI 318","EN 1992","Eurocode 2","IfcReinforcingBar","IFC import"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-concrete-tab","text":"Open the Concrete tab","level":2},{"id":"add-an-element-by-grid","text":"Add an element by grid","level":2},{"id":"change-the-bars","text":"Change the bars","level":2},{"id":"laps-and-anchorage","text":"Laps and anchorage","level":2},{"id":"the-checks","text":"The checks","level":2},{"id":"openings-trimmers-and-edge-bars","text":"Openings, trimmers and edge bars","level":2},{"id":"bar-marks-and-the-bending-schedule","text":"Bar marks and the bending schedule","level":2},{"id":"shop-drawings","text":"Shop drawings","level":2},{"id":"bars-from-an-ifc-file","text":"Bars from an IFC file","level":2},{"id":"the-ifc-export","text":"The IFC export","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete\u003C/span\u003E tab below the view details the bars of concrete beams, columns, slabs and walls. You give the sizes and the bars; DetailCraft places every bar, numbers it, checks the laps, the anchorage, the cover and the spacing against the clause they come from, and draws it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft details the bars you give it. It does not design them. Every result is for checking by an engineer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-concrete-tab\"\u003EOpen the Concrete tab\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete\u003C/span\u003E tab below the view. In a ribbon layout, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete\u003C/span\u003E is in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeling\u003C/span\u003E tab beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFooting\u003C/span\u003E. With the drafting style&#039;s command line, type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECONCRETE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-an-element-by-grid\"\u003EAdd an element by grid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConcrete elements are placed on the model&#039;s grid lines and levels, so set those up first (left panel).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E, choose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElement\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete beam\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete column\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlab\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the grid lines and levels:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea beam runs from one grid intersection to another, its top at the level;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea column stands at an intersection, from one level to another;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea slab fills the rectangle between two numbered and two lettered grid lines, its top at the level;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea wall runs along its centre line from one intersection to another, from one level to another.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the size and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. The element is selected and its fields open in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach element starts with bars that are starting values, not design: change them to the engineer&#039;s.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-bars\"\u003EChange the bars\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the element (in the view, or its name in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete\u003C/span\u003E tab). The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EElement\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you set\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBeam\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth, depth, clear cover; top and bottom bars (size, number, straight or 90° hook at the ends); side bars; stirrups (size, 135° or 90° hooks, the first one&#039;s distance from the support face, the end zone length and spacing, the spacing between); support lengths; lap lengths\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColumn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth, depth across, rotation, cover; longitudinal bars on each face; ties as for stirrups; what the bars do at the top (lap with the column above, hook into the floor, or stop)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThickness, size, top, covers; bottom mat and top mat each way (size and spacing); U-bars round the edges; trimmer bars; openings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThickness, cover, one or two layers, horizontal and vertical bars, U-bars at the ends, vertical bars lapped above or not, trimmer bars, openings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is one undo step. A change that cannot be built is refused and the status bar says why, for example &quot;The opening does not fit inside the element with its cover.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStirrups and ties are closed, with both hooks at one corner. A beam&#039;s bars run into a concrete column at its ends to the column&#039;s far face less cover; where no column is there, give the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESupport length at the start (mm)\u003C/span\u003E and at the end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"laps-and-anchorage\"\u003ELaps and anchorage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBars longer than the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStock bar length (mm)\u003C/span\u003E (12000 to start with) are cut into pieces of equal length, lapped. The table \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELaps and anchorage\u003C/span\u003E lists every lap and every anchorage: what is needed, with the clause and its terms, and what the detail has.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA lap length of 0 means &quot;as needed&quot;: the length the code gives, rounded up to 25 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your own lap length to use it instead. If it is shorter than needed, it is a problem (see below), and it is marked * in the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnchorage is measured from the support face to the bar&#039;s end inside the support. A beam with no known support says so instead of checking it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA column standing on a footing laps with the footing&#039;s starter bars, and on another column with that column&#039;s bars: their lap is checked too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EACI 318-19\u003C/strong\u003E (SI): straight bars in tension 25.4.2.3 (the table, with ψt, ψe, ψg, λ), standard hooks 25.4.3.1 (ψr 1.6 and ψo 1.25 taken unless known otherwise), bars in compression 25.4.9.2, tension laps 25.5.2.1 (Class B, or Class A in the settings), compression laps 25.5.5.1.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEN 1992-1-1\u003C/strong\u003E: anchorage 8.4.4 with fbd from 8.4.2 (good or poor bond), laps 8.7.3 with α6 for every bar lapped at one section. γc, γs and αct start at the recommended values; set your national annex&#039;s.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-checks\"\u003EThe checks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery check that fails is in the shell&#039;s Problems panel (source \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReinforcement checks\u003C/span\u003E), and in the element&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E list:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECheck\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EACI 318-19\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEN 1992-1-1\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECover\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETable 20.5.1.3.1, by exposure\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBond minimum, Table 4.2 (durability cover is yours to give)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear spacing of bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E25.2.1 (beams, slabs, walls), 25.2.3 (columns)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8.2 (2)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStirrup spacing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.7.6.2.2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.2.2 (6)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBeam minimum steel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.6.1.2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.2.1.1 (1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBeam side bars over 900 mm deep\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.7.2.3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enot checked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColumn steel ratio, number of bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10.6.1.1, 10.7.3.1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.5.2\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETies: spacing, size, cross-ties\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E25.7.2.1, 25.7.2.2, 25.7.2.3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.5.3 (1), (3), (4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlab spacing, minimum steel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7.7.2.3, 24.4.3.2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.3.1.1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall spacing, minimum steel, two layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E11.7.2.1, 11.7.3.1, 11.6.1, 11.7.2.3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.6.2, 9.6.3\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELaps, anchorage\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eas above\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eas above\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe aggregate size is not known, so the clear spacing is checked without it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"openings-trimmers-and-edge-bars\"\u003EOpenings, trimmers and edge bars\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd an opening\u003C/span\u003E under a selected slab or wall and set where it is and its size. Bars it crosses stop at cover from its edge. Trimmer bars are added each side, in the same layer, carrying half the area of the bars cut, one anchorage length past the opening. That is DetailCraft&#039;s starting rule, not a code clause: change the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrimmer bars\u003C/span\u003E to the engineer&#039;s. Corner bars are not added.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EU-bars round a slab&#039;s free edges and at a wall&#039;s ends have legs one lap length long.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bar-marks-and-the-bending-schedule\"\u003EBar marks and the bending schedule\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIdentical bars share one mark: footings keep F marks (5F1), beams get B, columns C, slabs S and walls W (7B1, 8C1, 4S2). The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending schedule\u003C/span\u003E tab and its CSV now list every bar of the structure, footings first. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending\u003C/span\u003E downloads it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shop-drawings\"\u003EShop drawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReinforcement drawings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReinforcement drawings\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete\u003C/span\u003E tab, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/span\u003E beside one element. Each element gets a sheet with an elevation (a plan for a slab) showing every bar and its mark, the stirrup or tie zones and the laps dimensioned, and a section at the middle; then bar-bending sheets list every mark with a sketch of its shape, its dimensions, the number, the cut length and the weight.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheets are made from the model each time, and again after every change while the window is open: the title block carries the model ref. Save \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis sheet as PDF\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets as PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bars-from-an-ifc-file\"\u003EBars from an IFC file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport IFC\u003C/span\u003E now reads IfcReinforcingBar: the swept disk along its centre line comes back as straight legs and bends, with its diameter, its mark and the element that holds it. The bars are kept as they were read (they are not turned into a beam&#039;s or a slab&#039;s rules), shown in the view, listed in the bending schedule and written out again with the IFC export. The import report says how many bars were read, of which sizes, in how many elements, and what was left out and why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA beam, column, slab or wall of that file that holds bars or whose material is concrete is not taken for steel; the report says so. DetailCraft&#039;s own IFC export carries each concrete element&#039;s definition, so it comes back as itself, and its bars are made again from it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReading a structural design program&#039;s own files (not IFC) is not built: export IFC from it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-ifc-export\"\u003EThe IFC export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConcrete beams, columns, slabs (with openings) and walls go out as IfcBeam, IfcColumn, IfcSlab and IfcWall, each with its bars as IfcReinforcingBar (main bars, ligatures for stirrups and ties, edge bars) aggregated to it, each bar a swept disk along its centre line with true arcs at the bends, its mark and its cut length.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Concrete tab below the view details the bars of concrete beams, columns, slabs and walls. You give the sizes and the bars; DetailCraft places every bar, numbers it, checks the laps, the anchorage, the cover and the spacing against the clause they come from, and draws it. DetailCraft details the bars you give it. It does not design them. Every result is for checking by an engineer. Open the Concrete tab Choose the Concrete tab below the view. In a ribbon layout, Concrete is in the Modeling tab beside Footing. With the drafting style's command line, type CONCRETE. Add an element by grid Concrete elements are placed on the model's grid lines and levels, so set those up first (left panel). In Add by grid, choose the Element: Concrete beam, Concrete column, Slab or Wall. Pick the grid lines and levels: a beam runs from one grid intersection to another, its top at the level; a column stands at an intersection, from one level to another; a slab fills the rectangle between two numbered and two lettered grid lines, its top at the level; a wall runs along its centre line from one intersection to another, from one level to another. Give the size and choose Add. The element is selected and its fields open in the Properties panel. Each element starts with bars that are starting values, not design: change them to the engineer's. Change the bars Select the element (in the view, or its name in the Concrete tab). The Properties panel shows: Element What you set Beam Width, depth, clear cover; top and bottom bars (size, number, straight or 90° hook at the ends); side bars; stirrups (size, 135° or 90° hooks, the first one's distance from the support face, the end zone length and spacing, the spacing between); support lengths; lap lengths Column Width, depth across, rotation, cover; longitudinal bars on each face; ties as for stirrups; what the bars do at the top (lap with the column above, hook into the floor, or stop) Slab Thickness, size, top, covers; bottom mat and top mat each way (size and spacing); U-bars round the edges; trimmer bars; openings Wall Thickness, cover, one or two layers, horizontal and vertical bars, U-bars at the ends, vertical bars lapped above or not, trimmer bars, openings Each change is one undo step. A change that cannot be built is refused and the status bar says why, for example \"The opening does not fit inside the element with its cover.\" Stirrups and ties are closed, with both hooks at one corner. A beam's bars run into a concrete column at its ends to the column's far face less cover; where no column is there, give the Support length at the start (mm) and at the end. Laps and anchorage Bars longer than the Stock bar length (mm) (12000 to start with) are cut into pieces of equal length, lapped. The table Laps and anchorage lists every lap and every anchorage: what is needed, with the clause and its terms, and what the detail has. A lap length of 0 means \"as needed\": the length the code gives, rounded up to 25 mm. Type your own lap length to use it instead. If it is shorter than needed, it is a problem (see below), and it is marked * in the table. Anchorage is measured from the support face to the bar's end inside the support. A beam with no known support says so instead of checking it. A column standing on a footing laps with the footing's starter bars, and on another column with that column's bars: their lap is checked too. ACI 318-19 (SI): straight bars in tension 25.4.2.3 (the table, with ψt, ψe, ψg, λ), standard hooks 25.4.3.1 (ψr 1.6 and ψo 1.25 taken unless known otherwise), bars in compression 25.4.9.2, tension laps 25.5.2.1 (Class B, or Class A in the settings), compression laps 25.5.5.1. EN 1992-1-1: anchorage 8.4.4 with fbd from 8.4.2 (good or poor bond), laps 8.7.3 with α6 for every bar lapped at one section. γc, γs and αct start at the recommended values; set your national annex's. The checks Every check that fails is in the shell's Problems panel (source Reinforcement checks), and in the element's Checks list: Check ACI 318-19 EN 1992-1-1 Cover Table 20.5.1.3.1, by exposure Bond minimum, Table 4.2 (durability cover is yours to give) Clear spacing of bars 25.2.1 (beams, slabs, walls), 25.2.3 (columns) 8.2 (2) Stirrup spacing 9.7.6.2.2 9.2.2 (6) Beam minimum steel 9.6.1.2 9.2.1.1 (1) Beam side bars over 900 mm deep 9.7.2.3 not checked Column steel ratio, number of bars 10.6.1.1, 10.7.3.1 9.5.2 Ties: spacing, size, cross-ties 25.7.2.1, 25.7.2.2, 25.7.2.3 9.5.3 (1), (3), (4) Slab spacing, minimum steel 7.7.2.3, 24.4.3.2 9.3.1.1 Wall spacing, minimum steel, two layers 11.7.2.1, 11.7.3.1, 11.6.1, 11.7.2.3 9.6.2, 9.6.3 Laps, anchorage as above as above The aggregate size is not known, so the clear spacing is checked without it. Openings, trimmers and edge bars Choose Add an opening under a selected slab or wall and set where it is and its size. Bars it crosses stop at cover from its edge. Trimmer bars are added each side, in the same layer, carrying half the area of the bars cut, one anchorage length past the opening. That is DetailCraft's starting rule, not a code clause: change the Trimmer bars to the engineer's. Corner bars are not added. U-bars round a slab's free edges and at a wall's ends have legs one lap length long. Bar marks and the bending schedule Identical bars share one mark: footings keep F marks (5F1), beams get B, columns C, slabs S and walls W (7B1, 8C1, 4S2). The Bending schedule tab and its CSV now list every bar of the structure, footings first. Output › More › Bending downloads it. Shop drawings Choose Output › More › Reinforcement drawings, Reinforcement drawings in the Concrete tab, or Shop drawing beside one element. Each element gets a sheet with an elevation (a plan for a slab) showing every bar and its mark, the stirrup or tie zones and the laps dimensioned, and a section at the middle; then bar-bending sheets list every mark with a sketch of its shape, its dimensions, the number, the cut length and the weight. The sheets are made from the model each time, and again after every change while the window is open: the title block carries the model ref. Save This sheet as PDF or All sheets as PDF. Bars from an IFC file File › Import IFC now reads IfcReinforcingBar: the swept disk along its centre line comes back as straight legs and bends, with its diameter, its mark and the element that holds it. The bars are kept as they were read (they are not turned into a beam's or a slab's rules), shown in the view, listed in the bending schedule and written out again with the IFC export. The import report says how many bars were read, of which sizes, in how many elements, and what was left out and why. A beam, column, slab or wall of that file that holds bars or whose material is concrete is not taken for steel; the report says so. DetailCraft's own IFC export carries each concrete element's definition, so it comes back as itself, and its bars are made again from it. Reading a structural design program's own files (not IFC) is not built: export IFC from it. The IFC export Concrete beams, columns, slabs (with openings) and walls go out as IfcBeam, IfcColumn, IfcSlab and IfcWall, each with its bars as IfcReinforcingBar (main bars, ligatures for stirrups and ties, edge bars) aggregated to it, each bar a swept disk along its centre line with true arcs at the bends, its mark and its cut length.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"concrete-column-reinforcement","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Reinforce a rectangular concrete column","summary":"A worked example of detailing a rectangular concrete column in DetailCraft: place it, give it longitudinal bars and ties, read the checks, the lap above it and the bar marks, and draw its shop sheet.","section":"tasks","order":92,"keywords":["concrete column","rectangular column","column reinforcement","longitudinal bars","vertical bars","ties","hoops","cross-ties","lap splice","splice","starter bars","column steel ratio","1 percent","8 percent","tie spacing","ACI 318-19 25.7.2","EN 1992-1-1 9.5","shop drawing","bar marks","8C1","3C2"],"headings":[{"id":"what-a-column-is-here","text":"What a column is here","level":2},{"id":"the-worked-column","text":"The worked column","level":2},{"id":"what-you-get","text":"What you get","level":3},{"id":"cross-ties","text":"Cross-ties","level":2},{"id":"the-lap-the-footing-and-the-column-below","text":"The lap, the footing and the column below","level":2},{"id":"marks-schedule-and-the-shop-sheet","text":"Marks, schedule and the shop sheet","level":2},{"id":"what-this-does-not-do","text":"What this does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis topic takes one square column from nothing to a checked, drawn and scheduled detail. The general rules for beams, slabs and walls are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/reinforced-concrete\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/reinforced-concrete\"\u003EDetail reinforced concrete\u003C/a\u003E; this is the column&#039;s side of it, with numbers you can reproduce.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft details the bars you give it. It does not design them. The bars below are starting values for the example, not a recommendation: the engineer of record chooses the size, the number and the spacing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-column-is-here\"\u003EWhat a column is here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA rectangular column has a width (along its local x) and a depth across (along its local y), a clear cover to the ties, longitudinal bars on each face and closed ties. Rotating it turns the whole thing; the faces stay &quot;width face&quot; and &quot;depth face&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe longitudinal bars are given as the number on a width face and the number on a depth face. The corner bars count on both, so 3 and 3 make 8 bars; 4 and 4 make 12.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe ties are closed rectangles with two hooks at one corner, 135° or 90°. The first tie is a set distance above the floor (and below the next), the end zones have a closer spacing, the middle has a wider one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat the bars do at the top: run on and lap with the bars above, turn in with 90° hooks into the floor, or stop under the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-worked-column\"\u003EThe worked column\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 400 × 400 mm column, 3600 mm between the floor above and the floor below, #8 longitudinal bars, #3 ties, 40 mm clear cover, f&#039;c 28 MPa, fy 420 MPa, ACI 318-19, interior.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete\u003C/span\u003E tab and set up the grid lines and levels the column stands on (left panel).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConcrete column\u003C/span\u003E, pick the grid intersection and the two levels, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth (mm)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth across (mm)\u003C/span\u003E to 400 and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel set:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear cover (mm)\u003C/span\u003E 40;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELongitudinal bars\u003C/span\u003E #8, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBars on a width face\u003C/span\u003E 3, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBars on a depth face\u003C/span\u003E 3;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETies\u003C/span\u003E #3, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHooks\u003C/span\u003E 135°, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpacing between (mm)\u003C/span\u003E 300, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd zone length (mm)\u003C/span\u003E 600, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpacing in the end zones (mm)\u003C/span\u003E 150, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst one from the face (mm)\u003C/span\u003E 50;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBars at the top\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun on and lap with the bars above\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-you-get\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELongitudinal bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 bars #8 (a 3 × 3 ring), each 4375 mm long: the 3600 mm column plus a 775 mm lap above it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E757 mm needed for a compression lap (ACI 318-19 25.5.5.1, fy 420), 775 mm provided: the need rounded up to the next 25 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETies\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E17 ties #3, each with a cut length of about 1359 mm, spaced 150 mm in the two 600 mm end zones and 300 mm between\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESteel ratio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5 % of the gross section (limits 1 % to 8 %, ACI 318-19 10.6.1.1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear spacing of the bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E112 mm (needs at least 40 mm, ACI 318-19 25.2.3)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETie spacing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E300 mm (allowed 400 mm: the least of 16 bar diameters, 48 tie diameters and the smaller side, ACI 318-19 25.7.2.1)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETie size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#3 (allowed down to 9.5 mm, ACI 318-19 25.7.2.2)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll of these are in the element&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E list; the lap is also in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELaps and anchorage\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cross-ties\"\u003ECross-ties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith one perimeter tie, only the corner bars are held. ACI 318-19 25.7.2.3 lets a middle bar on a face go without support if it is within 150 mm clear of a corner bar. With 3 bars on a face, as above, that is met. With 4 or more bars on a face it is not, and DetailCraft says so as a warning (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChecks\u003C/span\u003E and Problems): &quot;cross-ties&quot; are needed. \u003Cstrong\u003ECross-ties are not drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E: add them in the drawing by hand, or choose fewer bars on the face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor EN 1992-1-1, DetailCraft checks the bar size (at least 8 mm), the number of bars (at least 4), the steel area (0.2 % to 4 % of the section), the clear spacing and the link spacing (9.5.2 and 9.5.3).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-lap-the-footing-and-the-column-below\"\u003EThe lap, the footing and the column below\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere \u003Cstrong\u003Ea footing of the model stands under the column\u003C/strong\u003E, the column laps with the footing&#039;s starter bars, and their lap is checked too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere \u003Cstrong\u003Eanother column of the model stands under it\u003C/strong\u003E, it laps with that column&#039;s bars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive your own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELap length (mm, 0 = as needed)\u003C/span\u003E to override; one shorter than needed is a problem and is marked * in the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECompression laps are used by default (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompression lap\u003C/span\u003E in the settings); choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETension lap\u003C/span\u003E when the column can be in tension.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"marks-schedule-and-the-shop-sheet\"\u003EMarks, schedule and the shop sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIdentical bars share one mark: the longitudinal bars of the column above are 8C1, its ties 17C2 (the number first, then the kind letter C for column and the running number). They appear in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending schedule\u003C/span\u003E tab, with the cut length and the weight, and in the CSV from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/span\u003E beside the column for a sheet with the column in elevation (every bar, its mark, the tie zones and the lap dimensioned) and a section at the middle, followed by the bar-bending sheet. Save \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis sheet as PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-this-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat this does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERound columns and spirals, and columns that are not rectangular: not built.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECorner and cross-ties inside the section, and seismic detailing (ACI 318-19 chapter 18): not built.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESplices at positions you choose, or staggered: all bars lap at one section.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhether the bars are enough for the load: that is the engineer&#039;s design.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"This topic takes one square column from nothing to a checked, drawn and scheduled detail. The general rules for beams, slabs and walls are in Detail reinforced concrete; this is the column's side of it, with numbers you can reproduce. DetailCraft details the bars you give it. It does not design them. The bars below are starting values for the example, not a recommendation: the engineer of record chooses the size, the number and the spacing. What a column is here A rectangular column has a width (along its local x) and a depth across (along its local y), a clear cover to the ties, longitudinal bars on each face and closed ties. Rotating it turns the whole thing; the faces stay \"width face\" and \"depth face\". The longitudinal bars are given as the number on a width face and the number on a depth face. The corner bars count on both, so 3 and 3 make 8 bars; 4 and 4 make 12. The ties are closed rectangles with two hooks at one corner, 135° or 90°. The first tie is a set distance above the floor (and below the next), the end zones have a closer spacing, the middle has a wider one. What the bars do at the top: run on and lap with the bars above, turn in with 90° hooks into the floor, or stop under the top. The worked column A 400 × 400 mm column, 3600 mm between the floor above and the floor below, #8 longitudinal bars, #3 ties, 40 mm clear cover, f'c 28 MPa, fy 420 MPa, ACI 318-19, interior. Open the Concrete tab and set up the grid lines and levels the column stands on (left panel). In Add by grid, choose Concrete column, pick the grid intersection and the two levels, set Width (mm) and Depth across (mm) to 400 and choose Add. In the Properties panel set: Clear cover (mm) 40; Longitudinal bars #8, Bars on a width face 3, Bars on a depth face 3; Ties #3, Hooks 135°, Spacing between (mm) 300, End zone length (mm) 600, Spacing in the end zones (mm) 150, First one from the face (mm) 50; Bars at the top Run on and lap with the bars above. Each change is one undo step. What you get Part Result Longitudinal bars 8 bars #8 (a 3 × 3 ring), each 4375 mm long: the 3600 mm column plus a 775 mm lap above it Lap 757 mm needed for a compression lap (ACI 318-19 25.5.5.1, fy 420), 775 mm provided: the need rounded up to the next 25 mm Ties 17 ties #3, each with a cut length of about 1359 mm, spaced 150 mm in the two 600 mm end zones and 300 mm between Steel ratio 2.5 % of the gross section (limits 1 % to 8 %, ACI 318-19 10.6.1.1) Clear spacing of the bars 112 mm (needs at least 40 mm, ACI 318-19 25.2.3) Tie spacing 300 mm (allowed 400 mm: the least of 16 bar diameters, 48 tie diameters and the smaller side, ACI 318-19 25.7.2.1) Tie size #3 (allowed down to 9.5 mm, ACI 318-19 25.7.2.2) All of these are in the element's Checks list; the lap is also in Laps and anchorage. Cross-ties With one perimeter tie, only the corner bars are held. ACI 318-19 25.7.2.3 lets a middle bar on a face go without support if it is within 150 mm clear of a corner bar. With 3 bars on a face, as above, that is met. With 4 or more bars on a face it is not, and DetailCraft says so as a warning (Checks and Problems): \"cross-ties\" are needed. Cross-ties are not drawn yet: add them in the drawing by hand, or choose fewer bars on the face. For EN 1992-1-1, DetailCraft checks the bar size (at least 8 mm), the number of bars (at least 4), the steel area (0.2 % to 4 % of the section), the clear spacing and the link spacing (9.5.2 and 9.5.3). The lap, the footing and the column below Where a footing of the model stands under the column, the column laps with the footing's starter bars, and their lap is checked too. Where another column of the model stands under it, it laps with that column's bars. Give your own Lap length (mm, 0 = as needed) to override; one shorter than needed is a problem and is marked * in the table. Compression laps are used by default (Compression lap in the settings); choose Tension lap when the column can be in tension. Marks, schedule and the shop sheet Identical bars share one mark: the longitudinal bars of the column above are 8C1, its ties 17C2 (the number first, then the kind letter C for column and the running number). They appear in the Bending schedule tab, with the cut length and the weight, and in the CSV from Output › More › Bending. Choose Shop drawing beside the column for a sheet with the column in elevation (every bar, its mark, the tie zones and the lap dimensioned) and a section at the middle, followed by the bar-bending sheet. Save This sheet as PDF. What this does not do Round columns and spirals, and columns that are not rectangular: not built. Corner and cross-ties inside the section, and seismic detailing (ACI 318-19 chapter 18): not built. Splices at positions you choose, or staggered: all bars lap at one section. Whether the bars are enough for the load: that is the engineer's design.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"marks-and-bom","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Check marks and the bill of materials","summary":"Read the part and assembly marks DetailCraft gives every piece, check the bill of materials, and download it as CSV.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["marks","numbering","part mark","assembly mark","piece mark","bom","bill of materials","material list","weights","bolt list","csv","numbering"],"headings":[{"id":"where-marks-show","text":"Where marks show","level":2},{"id":"how-parts-are-marked","text":"How parts are marked","level":2},{"id":"read-the-bill-of-materials","text":"Read the bill of materials","level":2},{"id":"download-it","text":"Download it","level":2},{"id":"check-before-you-send","text":"Check before you send","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft marks the model by itself. There is no numbering command and nothing to run: after every change it works out which parts are the same piece of steel and gives them one mark.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-marks-show\"\u003EWhere marks show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart marks\u003C/span\u003E list in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E panel: each mark with its profile, length and count. Click one to select the first part with that mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel of a selected member: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPart mark\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssembly\u003C/span\u003E. The panel heading also carries the mark, for example &quot;Beam b1&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/span\u003E tab, the CSV, the NC file names, the shop drawings and the IFC export (as each element&#039;s tag).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-parts-are-marked\"\u003EHow parts are marked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPart marks:\u003C/strong\u003E c for columns, b for beams, br for braces, p for plates (including end plates and base plates). Then a number: c1, c2, b1, p1 …\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssembly marks:\u003C/strong\u003E C, B, BR or P. An assembly is a main member and the plates welded to it, such as a beam with its two end plates, or a column with its base plate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo parts share a mark when they have the same profile, material and cut length, and the same holes and copes, all within 1 mm, even if one is turned end for end or upside down relative to the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbering runs columns first, then beams, braces and plates; within each, by profile name, then longest first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full rules, including which turns count for channels and angles: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/mark-rules\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/mark-rules\"\u003EMark rules\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarks are worked out fresh after every change, so they can renumber. Adding a part, changing a profile or adding a hole can move b3 to b4. Treat marks as final only when the model is finished, and make the BOM, NC files and shop drawings from the same saved model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-bill-of-materials\"\u003ERead the bill of materials\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/span\u003E tab at the bottom. It has three tables.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts\u003C/strong\u003E, with the total steel weight in the heading:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart mark\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick it to select the first part with this mark\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ebeam, column, brace or plate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor example W16x26 or PL20x180\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe grade you set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength (mm)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut length, after connections\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit weight (kg)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile mass per meter × length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETotal weight (kg)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnit weight × count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe last row gives the total count and weight.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAssemblies:\u003C/strong\u003E each assembly mark, its main part, all its parts (for example &quot;b1 + p1 + p1&quot;), count, unit weight and total weight.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBolts and anchor rods:\u003C/strong\u003E grouped by kind, diameter, length and grade, with a count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWeights are gross: holes and copes are not taken off. Bolts, nuts, washers and welds are not in the steel weight.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"download-it\"\u003EDownload it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E in the tab, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBOM\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group. The file \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; BOM.csv\u003C/code\u003E holds the three tables one after another, each under its title, with a blank line between. It opens in any spreadsheet program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-before-you-send\"\u003ECheck before you send\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab and clear every line. A connection that could not be built adds no plate, holes or bolts, so the BOM would be short.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for marks you did not expect. Two beams you think are the same but with different marks usually differ by a hole, a cope or a cut length; select each and compare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave the model, then make every export from that saved state.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E","text":"DetailCraft marks the model by itself. There is no numbering command and nothing to run: after every change it works out which parts are the same piece of steel and gives them one mark. Where marks show The Part marks list in the Model panel: each mark with its profile, length and count. Click one to select the first part with that mark. The Properties panel of a selected member: Part mark and Assembly. The panel heading also carries the mark, for example \"Beam b1\". The Bill of materials tab, the CSV, the NC file names, the shop drawings and the IFC export (as each element's tag). How parts are marked Part marks: c for columns, b for beams, br for braces, p for plates (including end plates and base plates). Then a number: c1, c2, b1, p1 … Assembly marks: C, B, BR or P. An assembly is a main member and the plates welded to it, such as a beam with its two end plates, or a column with its base plate. Two parts share a mark when they have the same profile, material and cut length, and the same holes and copes, all within 1 mm, even if one is turned end for end or upside down relative to the other. Numbering runs columns first, then beams, braces and plates; within each, by profile name, then longest first. The full rules, including which turns count for channels and angles: Mark rules. Marks are worked out fresh after every change, so they can renumber. Adding a part, changing a profile or adding a hole can move b3 to b4. Treat marks as final only when the model is finished, and make the BOM, NC files and shop drawings from the same saved model. Read the bill of materials Open the Bill of materials tab at the bottom. It has three tables. Parts, with the total steel weight in the heading: Column Meaning Part mark Click it to select the first part with this mark Type beam, column, brace or plate Profile For example W16x26 or PL20x180 Material The grade you set Length (mm) Cut length, after connections Count How many Unit weight (kg) Profile mass per meter × length Total weight (kg) Unit weight × count The last row gives the total count and weight. Assemblies: each assembly mark, its main part, all its parts (for example \"b1 + p1 + p1\"), count, unit weight and total weight. Bolts and anchor rods: grouped by kind, diameter, length and grade, with a count. Weights are gross: holes and copes are not taken off. Bolts, nuts, washers and welds are not in the steel weight. Download it Choose Download CSV in the tab, or BOM in the Output group. The file \u003Cname\u003E BOM.csv holds the three tables one after another, each under its title, with a blank line between. It opens in any spreadsheet program. Check before you send Open the Problems tab and clear every line. A connection that could not be built adds no plate, holes or bolts, so the BOM would be short. Look for marks you did not expect. Two beams you think are the same but with different marks usually differ by a hole, a cope or a cut length; select each and compare. Save the model, then make every export from that saved state.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"shop-drawings","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Make shop drawings","summary":"One sheet per part mark, made all at once: preview any of them and download one sheet or the whole set as a PDF on A4 to A1 or ANSI B to D, with holes, copes, dimensions and title block.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["shop drawing","shop drawings","all parts","batch","whole set","single part drawing","fabrication drawing","pdf","detail drawing","piece drawing","print"],"headings":[{"id":"make-the-whole-set","text":"Make the whole set","level":2},{"id":"what-is-on-the-sheet","text":"What is on the sheet","level":2},{"id":"scales","text":"Scales","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2},{"id":"print-it","text":"Print it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft makes one sheet per part mark, for every member and plate in the model, all at once: you do not need to select them one by one. Identical pieces share a mark, so they share a sheet. The sheets are made on the spot from the model; they are not stored or edited.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-whole-set\"\u003EMake the whole set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group. Nothing needs to be selected. With a part selected, the window opens on that part&#039;s sheet. In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel the same button is called \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/span\u003E for members and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate drawing\u003C/span\u003E for connections.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA preview opens, titled with the part mark, for example &quot;Shop drawing b1&quot;. The first list next to the title holds every part mark with its profile, length and quantity: choose one to see its sheet. The second list changes the sheet size; the whole set is made again on it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/drawings#choose-the-sheet-size\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/drawings\" data-hash=\"choose-the-sheet-size\"\u003EGeneral-arrangement and assembly drawings\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload all\u003C/span\u003E for one PDF with a page per part mark, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; Shop drawings.pdf\u003C/code\u003E. Each sheet says &quot;Sheet 3 of 42&quot; in its title block. Or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload this sheet\u003C/span\u003E for the one you are looking at, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; &lt;mark&gt;.pdf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose the preview with the × or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/span\u003E is grayed out, the model has no parts yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-on-the-sheet\"\u003EWhat is on the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor an I-shape:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E, looking at the web: the part with its copes notched, web holes as circles and flange holes as hidden (dashed) lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETop flange plan\u003C/strong\u003E, seen from above, when any flange has holes. Bottom-flange holes show dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESection\u003C/strong\u003E through the profile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimensions:\u003C/strong\u003E chained hole positions along the part plus the overall length, web hole heights, the gauge, and cope sizes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHole list:\u003C/strong\u003E each group of holes, for example &quot;8 x Ø22 in the top flange&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWeld\u003C/strong\u003E, when the part has plates welded to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETitle block:\u003C/strong\u003E part mark, assembly mark, profile, material, length, quantity, weight each and total, model name and date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a channel, angle or hollow section the sheet is the same, looking at the web or the side: a tube&#039;s walls are hidden (dashed) lines, the section gives the wall thickness.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a plate: the plate face with its holes, and its edge as the section.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales\"\u003EScales\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe scale is picked from a list (1:1 up to 1:200) so the part fits the sheet. A long, shallow part would be too thin to read at one scale, so when the depth would come out under 30 mm on paper the depth is drawn at a larger scale than the length. The view title then says so, for example &quot;length 1:20, depth 1:5 (not to one scale; dimensions govern)&quot;. Always read sizes from the dimensions, not by measuring the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe quantity is the number of parts with that mark in the model. Marks can renumber after a change; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/marks-and-bom\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/marks-and-bom\"\u003ECheck marks and the bill of materials\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach sheet is one part on its own, without weld symbols; assembly drawings (with weld symbols) and general arrangement drawings are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/drawings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/drawings\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/a\u003E. The sheet cannot be edited or annotated in DetailCraft. To mark it up, open the PDF in a PDF markup app such as MarkupCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe title block says &quot;DetailCraft lab build — millimeters&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EText uses the standard PDF Helvetica font. Characters outside the Western European set print as &quot;?&quot;, and the status bar counts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"print-it\"\u003EPrint it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the downloaded PDF in your PDF viewer and print it at 100% (no &quot;fit to page&quot;) on paper of the size you chose if you want the stated scales to hold. On a smaller sheet, scale it and rely on the dimensions.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft makes one sheet per part mark, for every member and plate in the model, all at once: you do not need to select them one by one. Identical pieces share a mark, so they share a sheet. The sheets are made on the spot from the model; they are not stored or edited. Make the whole set Choose Shop drawing in the Output group. Nothing needs to be selected. With a part selected, the window opens on that part's sheet. In the Properties panel the same button is called Shop drawing for members and Plate drawing for connections. A preview opens, titled with the part mark, for example \"Shop drawing b1\". The first list next to the title holds every part mark with its profile, length and quantity: choose one to see its sheet. The second list changes the sheet size; the whole set is made again on it (see General-arrangement and assembly drawings). Choose Download all for one PDF with a page per part mark, \u003Cname\u003E Shop drawings.pdf. Each sheet says \"Sheet 3 of 42\" in its title block. Or choose Download this sheet for the one you are looking at, \u003Cname\u003E \u003Cmark\u003E.pdf. Close the preview with the × or Esc. If Shop drawing is grayed out, the model has no parts yet. What is on the sheet For an I-shape: Elevation, looking at the web: the part with its copes notched, web holes as circles and flange holes as hidden (dashed) lines. Top flange plan, seen from above, when any flange has holes. Bottom-flange holes show dashed. Section through the profile. Dimensions: chained hole positions along the part plus the overall length, web hole heights, the gauge, and cope sizes. Hole list: each group of holes, for example \"8 x Ø22 in the top flange\". Weld, when the part has plates welded to it. Title block: part mark, assembly mark, profile, material, length, quantity, weight each and total, model name and date. For a channel, angle or hollow section the sheet is the same, looking at the web or the side: a tube's walls are hidden (dashed) lines, the section gives the wall thickness. For a plate: the plate face with its holes, and its edge as the section. Scales The scale is picked from a list (1:1 up to 1:200) so the part fits the sheet. A long, shallow part would be too thin to read at one scale, so when the depth would come out under 30 mm on paper the depth is drawn at a larger scale than the length. The view title then says so, for example \"length 1:20, depth 1:5 (not to one scale; dimensions govern)\". Always read sizes from the dimensions, not by measuring the sheet. Things to know The quantity is the number of parts with that mark in the model. Marks can renumber after a change; see Check marks and the bill of materials. Each sheet is one part on its own, without weld symbols; assembly drawings (with weld symbols) and general arrangement drawings are in Drawings. The sheet cannot be edited or annotated in DetailCraft. To mark it up, open the PDF in a PDF markup app such as MarkupCraft. The title block says \"DetailCraft lab build — millimeters\". Text uses the standard PDF Helvetica font. Characters outside the Western European set print as \"?\", and the status bar counts them. Print it Open the downloaded PDF in your PDF viewer and print it at 100% (no \"fit to page\") on paper of the size you chose if you want the stated scales to hold. On a smaller sheet, scale it and rely on the dimensions.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Use DetailCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script read the model, its parts, problems and clashes, add members and connections, save and export (USD too): permissions, connecting through CadCraft Studio, and the activity log.","section":"tasks","order":118,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","script","automation","parts","clashes","connections","permissions","disconnect","activity","undo"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with DetailCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). DetailCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use DetailCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, DetailCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery change is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled as the assistant&#039;s (for example &quot;Assistant: add beam&quot;, &quot;Assistant: add gussets&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the assistant&#039;s last change as a whole. The assistant&#039;s own \u003Ccode\u003Eundo\u003C/code\u003E only takes back its own steps, never yours, unless it asks for that explicitly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt waits for you.\u003C/strong\u003E While you are placing a member, moving parts or pasting, DetailCraft refuses the assistant&#039;s changes and tells it to try again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll lengths are millimetres, points \u003Ccode\u003E[x, y, z]\u003C/code\u003E with z up, as in the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emodel_describe\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model open: grids, levels, phases, members by type and profile, connections, footings, stairs, parts, steel weight, problems\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eparts_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart marks with profile, material, cut length, count, weight, phase and assembly marks; by type, profile, mark or phase\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eproblems_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab lists, each with what it is about\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclash_check\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/clash-check\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/clash-check\"\u003Eclash check\u003C/a\u003E, with its tolerance and phases\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eselection_get\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you have selected\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eselection_set\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelects members, connections, footings or stairs (by id or part mark) to show them to you; changes nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emember_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA beam, column, brace or plate between two points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Econnection_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd plates, shear tabs, base plates, splices or gussets where the given members meet, as the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect\u003C/span\u003E buttons do\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eundo\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakes back the assistant&#039;s last steps\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emodel_save\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetailCraft&#039;s own Save\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emodel_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC, NC files, BOM, bending schedule, KISS file or cut list, as the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E buttons make them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eusd_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe steel model as USD (.usda, .usdc or .usdz), as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD\u003C/span\u003E writes it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/usd\"\u003EExport the steel model as USD\u003C/a\u003E); in Studio it can write the file to a folder you allowed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no DetailCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the edit permission&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E (or save, export): tick it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The person is placing a member&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E finish what you are doing (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), then let the assistant try again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with DetailCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. DetailCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). DetailCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the DetailCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use DetailCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do: Read is ticked to start with; tick Edit, Save or Export only when you need them. Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, DetailCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose Connect an assistant and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every change is one undo step, labelled as the assistant's (for example \"Assistant: add beam\", \"Assistant: add gussets\"). Ctrl+Z takes back the assistant's last change as a whole. The assistant's own undo only takes back its own steps, never yours, unless it asks for that explicitly. It waits for you. While you are placing a member, moving parts or pasting, DetailCraft refuses the assistant's changes and tells it to try again. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › DetailCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for All lengths are millimetres, points [x, y, z] with z up, as in the model. Tool Permission What it does app_info, model_describe Read The model open: grids, levels, phases, members by type and profile, connections, footings, stairs, parts, steel weight, problems parts_list Read Part marks with profile, material, cut length, count, weight, phase and assembly marks; by type, profile, mark or phase problems_list Read What the Problems tab lists, each with what it is about clash_check Read The clash check, with its tolerance and phases selection_get Read What you have selected selection_set Edit Selects members, connections, footings or stairs (by id or part mark) to show them to you; changes nothing member_add Edit A beam, column, brace or plate between two points connection_add Edit End plates, shear tabs, base plates, splices or gussets where the given members meet, as the Connect buttons do undo Edit Takes back the assistant's last steps model_save Save DetailCraft's own Save model_export Export IFC, NC files, BOM, bending schedule, KISS file or cut list, as the Output buttons make them usd_export Export The steel model as USD (.usda, .usdc or .usdz), as Output › USD writes it (see Export the steel model as USD); in Studio it can write the file to a folder you allowed If something doesn't work The assistant sees no DetailCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the edit permission\" (or save, export): tick it under Assistants, then ask again. \"The person is placing a member\": finish what you are doing (or press Esc), then let the assistant try again. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings. See also Recipes for AI assistants: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"nc-export","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Send NC files to the shop","summary":"Export DSTV NC files for every part mark (I-shapes, channels, angles, HSS, plates), zipped, and check them before they reach a machine.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["nc","nc1","dstv","cnc","machine","beam line","drill line","plate cutting","export","fabrication","zip"],"headings":[{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"what-each-file-contains","text":"What each file contains","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-written","text":"What is not written","level":2},{"id":"check-before-machining","text":"Check before machining","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft writes one DSTV NC file (\u003Ccode\u003E.nc1\u003C/code\u003E) per part mark, for I-shapes, channels, angles, rectangular and round HSS, and plates, and zips them together. Beam lines and plate machines read these files to saw, drill and cut.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClear the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab first: a connection that could not be built adds no holes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave the model (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), so the NC files match a saved model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENC files\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt; NC.zip\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says how many files it holds, and lists any marks it did not write (an angle or a round tube with a cope).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInside the zip each file is named by its part mark: \u003Ccode\u003Eb1.nc1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ec1.nc1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ep1.nc1\u003C/code\u003E … The zip is uncompressed and opens with any zip tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the model has no parts, the status bar says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-file-contains\"\u003EWhat each file contains\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHeader (ST):\u003C/strong\u003E the model name as the order, the part mark as drawing and piece, phase 1, the material grade, the quantity, the profile and its dimensions, mass per meter and paint surface per meter. Saw cuts are square.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOuter contours (AK):\u003C/strong\u003E the web outline with any copes notched out, and each flange outline shortened where a cope removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHoles (BO):\u003C/strong\u003E every hole with its face, position and diameter, including the holes made by end plates and base plates.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EField by field, with the face and coordinate rules: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/nc1-fields\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/nc1-fields\"\u003ENC file fields\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-written\"\u003EWhat is not written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAngles and round tubes with a cope: no NC file. Their marks are listed in the status bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHoles in round tubes (DetailCraft does not make any).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBevels, inner contours, part markings (scribing) and saw angles other than square.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESlotted holes (DetailCraft has round holes only).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-before-machining\"\u003ECheck before machining\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft&#039;s NC files are read back by its own test suite, but they have not been run on your shop&#039;s machines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the files in your shop&#039;s NC viewer or the machine software and compare them with the shop drawings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck one part of each kind on the machine before running a batch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck how your software reads the flange y-coordinates and the faces of channels: DetailCraft measures flange y from the behind edge, and lays channels on their back as the standard draws them (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/nc1-fields#faces-and-coordinates\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/nc1-fields\" data-hash=\"faces-and-coordinates\"\u003ENC file fields\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E","text":"DetailCraft writes one DSTV NC file (.nc1) per part mark, for I-shapes, channels, angles, rectangular and round HSS, and plates, and zips them together. Beam lines and plate machines read these files to saw, drill and cut. Export Clear the Problems tab first: a connection that could not be built adds no holes. Save the model (Ctrl+S), so the NC files match a saved model. Choose NC files in the Output group. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E NC.zip. The status bar says how many files it holds, and lists any marks it did not write (an angle or a round tube with a cope). Inside the zip each file is named by its part mark: b1.nc1, c1.nc1, p1.nc1 … The zip is uncompressed and opens with any zip tool. If the model has no parts, the status bar says so. What each file contains Header (ST): the model name as the order, the part mark as drawing and piece, phase 1, the material grade, the quantity, the profile and its dimensions, mass per meter and paint surface per meter. Saw cuts are square. Outer contours (AK): the web outline with any copes notched out, and each flange outline shortened where a cope removes it. Holes (BO): every hole with its face, position and diameter, including the holes made by end plates and base plates. Field by field, with the face and coordinate rules: NC file fields. What is not written Angles and round tubes with a cope: no NC file. Their marks are listed in the status bar. Holes in round tubes (DetailCraft does not make any). Bevels, inner contours, part markings (scribing) and saw angles other than square. Slotted holes (DetailCraft has round holes only). Check before machining DetailCraft's NC files are read back by its own test suite, but they have not been run on your shop's machines. Open the files in your shop's NC viewer or the machine software and compare them with the shop drawings. Check one part of each kind on the machine before running a batch. Check how your software reads the flange y-coordinates and the faces of channels: DetailCraft measures flange y from the behind edge, and lays channels on their back as the standard draws them (see NC file fields).","lang":"en"},{"slug":"ifc-export","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Export IFC for coordination","summary":"Write an IFC4 file of the whole model, steel, bolts, footings and rebar, to share with architects, engineers and other software.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["ifc","ifc4","ifc2x3","bim","export","import","import ifc","holes","bolts","loose bolts","openings","fasteners","coordination","model exchange","step","openbim","clash","viewer"],"headings":[{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-the-file","text":"What is in the file","level":2},{"id":"stable-ids-for-re-exports","text":"Stable ids for re-exports","level":2},{"id":"check-it","text":"Check it","level":2},{"id":"not-yet","text":"Not yet","level":2},{"id":"import","text":"Import","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe IFC export writes the whole model as one IFC4 file that BIM software and IFC viewers can open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.ifc\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says what went in, for example &quot;IFC4 exported: 25 parts, 88 fasteners, 7 footings, 132 bars.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the model has no parts and no footings, the status bar says &quot;Nothing to export yet.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-the-file\"\u003EWhat is in the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn DetailCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the IFC file\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach level\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA building storey (IfcBuildingStorey), under one project, site and building\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrids\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn IfcGrid with its axes and labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColumns, beams, braces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcColumn, IfcBeam, IfcMember (brace), each an extrusion of a standard profile shape with its real dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlates, end plates, base plates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcPlate\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHoles and copes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpenings (IfcOpeningElement) cut from the part\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA member and its welded plates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn assembly (IfcElementAssembly) tagged with the assembly mark\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolts and anchor rods\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcMechanicalFastener with diameter and length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFootings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcFooting (pad footing), with the concrete text as its material\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcReinforcingBar with its bar mark, diameter, area and length, swept along its true centerline with real arcs at the bends\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach part&#039;s tag is its part mark. Each element also carries a &quot;DetailCraft&quot; property set with its marks, profile and weight. Each element sits in the highest storey at or below its lowest point. Materials are grouped by grade.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stable-ids-for-re-exports\"\u003EStable ids for re-exports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery element&#039;s IFC id (GlobalId) is worked out from the model name and the element&#039;s own id in the model. Export the same model twice and the ids match, so software that compares versions can tell what changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERenaming the model changes every id. Keep the model name the same between issues of a project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-it\"\u003ECheck it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft&#039;s IFC files are read back by an independent IFC reader in its test suite. Programs differ in how they read IFC, so open the file in the software your team uses before you rely on it. CadCraft&#039;s BimCraft app opens IFC files too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-yet\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBringing connections, footings and rebar in from an IFC file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport IFC\u003C/span\u003E brings in beams, columns, members, plates, grids, levels, holes, copes and bolts, and lists what it left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnalytical or structural-analysis data.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnything other than the IFC4 reference view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import\"\u003EImport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport IFC\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group (or dropping an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file on the view) makes a new model from an IFC2X3 or IFC4 file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBeams, columns, members (braces) and plates\u003C/strong\u003E whose body is one extruded profile DetailCraft knows, matched to the catalog or kept as a custom profile; \u003Cstrong\u003Egrids\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Elevels\u003C/strong\u003E from the file&#039;s grid and storeys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHoles:\u003C/strong\u003E every round opening (IfcOpeningElement voiding a part that came in) square to one of the part&#039;s faces: an I-shape&#039;s or channel&#039;s web or flanges, a tube&#039;s walls, a plate, an angle&#039;s legs. A hole through both walls of a tube comes in once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopes:\u003C/strong\u003E a rectangular opening that takes the full width of an I-shape or channel from one end, down from the top or up from the bottom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBolts:\u003C/strong\u003E every IfcMechanicalFastener or IfcFastener, with its diameter, length, grade and kind (bolt or anchor rod), as a \u003Cstrong\u003Eloose bolt\u003C/strong\u003E. Loose bolts show in the view, the bill of materials and the IFC export, and stay where they are when you move members. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, with nothing selected, counts them and has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove loose bolts\u003C/span\u003E for when you add connections instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe report lists everything else by class with the reason it was left out: walls and slabs, footings and bars, slotted holes, holes in round tubes, connections (an IFC file carries their plates and bolts, not the rule that made them).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The IFC export writes the whole model as one IFC4 file that BIM software and IFC viewers can open. Export Choose IFC in the Output group. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.ifc. The status bar says what went in, for example \"IFC4 exported: 25 parts, 88 fasteners, 7 footings, 132 bars.\" If the model has no parts and no footings, the status bar says \"Nothing to export yet.\" What is in the file In DetailCraft In the IFC file Each level A building storey (IfcBuildingStorey), under one project, site and building Grids An IfcGrid with its axes and labels Columns, beams, braces IfcColumn, IfcBeam, IfcMember (brace), each an extrusion of a standard profile shape with its real dimensions Plates, end plates, base plates IfcPlate Holes and copes Openings (IfcOpeningElement) cut from the part A member and its welded plates An assembly (IfcElementAssembly) tagged with the assembly mark Bolts and anchor rods IfcMechanicalFastener with diameter and length Footings IfcFooting (pad footing), with the concrete text as its material Each bar IfcReinforcingBar with its bar mark, diameter, area and length, swept along its true centerline with real arcs at the bends Each part's tag is its part mark. Each element also carries a \"DetailCraft\" property set with its marks, profile and weight. Each element sits in the highest storey at or below its lowest point. Materials are grouped by grade. Stable ids for re-exports Every element's IFC id (GlobalId) is worked out from the model name and the element's own id in the model. Export the same model twice and the ids match, so software that compares versions can tell what changed. Renaming the model changes every id. Keep the model name the same between issues of a project. Check it DetailCraft's IFC files are read back by an independent IFC reader in its test suite. Programs differ in how they read IFC, so open the file in the software your team uses before you rely on it. CadCraft's BimCraft app opens IFC files too. Not yet Bringing connections, footings and rebar in from an IFC file. Import IFC brings in beams, columns, members, plates, grids, levels, holes, copes and bolts, and lists what it left out. Analytical or structural-analysis data. Anything other than the IFC4 reference view. Import Import IFC in the File group (or dropping an .ifc file on the view) makes a new model from an IFC2X3 or IFC4 file: Beams, columns, members (braces) and plates whose body is one extruded profile DetailCraft knows, matched to the catalog or kept as a custom profile; grids and levels from the file's grid and storeys. Holes: every round opening (IfcOpeningElement voiding a part that came in) square to one of the part's faces: an I-shape's or channel's web or flanges, a tube's walls, a plate, an angle's legs. A hole through both walls of a tube comes in once. Copes: a rectangular opening that takes the full width of an I-shape or channel from one end, down from the top or up from the bottom. Bolts: every IfcMechanicalFastener or IfcFastener, with its diameter, length, grade and kind (bolt or anchor rod), as a loose bolt. Loose bolts show in the view, the bill of materials and the IFC export, and stay where they are when you move members. The Properties panel, with nothing selected, counts them and has Remove loose bolts for when you add connections instead. The report lists everything else by class with the reason it was left out: walls and slabs, footings and bars, slotted holes, holes in round tubes, connections (an IFC file carries their plates and bolts, not the rule that made them).","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Detail one structure together with Live TeamWork","summary":"What changes in DetailCraft while you are in a Live TeamWork session from CadCraft Studio: everyone's members, connections and footings in one model, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["live","live teamwork","studio live","together","collaborate","session","share live","join","members","connections","cursor","pointer","see each other","names","color","selection","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-works-at-once","text":"Everyone works at once","level":2},{"id":"see-each-other","text":"See each other","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to a model while several people detail it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share, Join); this page is about the model itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-works-at-once\"\u003EEveryone works at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd and edit members, connections, footings, stairs and bolts as usual. Your own edits show at once; other people&#039;s appear a moment after they make them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo people change one member:\u003C/strong\u003E different settings (one the profile, the other the end point) are both kept; the same setting goes to the later change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA connection on a member someone just deleted\u003C/strong\u003E is taken back, and the status bar says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGrids, levels and phases go to whoever changed them last.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBuilt geometry, piece marks and the schedule are worked out in every window from the same model, so they agree.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-each-other\"\u003ESee each other\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEveryone in the session has a color of their own. You see each person&#039;s pointer as an arrow in their color with their name beside it, on the member or plate they are pointing at, and a dashed box in their color round the members, connections, footings and stairs they have selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPointers, names and selections are only for the session: they are never saved in the file and never go into anyone&#039;s undo. They glide to where each person is, so they stay smooth on a slow connection too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. DetailCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host&#039;s model once the host lets you in. An invite link lets someone join from another network: the host needs a Team Cloud Plan seat for the app, everyone else joins free, and the link works only while the host&#039;s session is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the model. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to a model while several people detail it at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share, Join); this page is about the model itself. Everyone works at once Add and edit members, connections, footings, stairs and bolts as usual. Your own edits show at once; other people's appear a moment after they make them. Two people change one member: different settings (one the profile, the other the end point) are both kept; the same setting goes to the later change. A connection on a member someone just deleted is taken back, and the status bar says why. Grids, levels and phases go to whoever changed them last. Built geometry, piece marks and the schedule are worked out in every window from the same model, so they agree. See each other Everyone in the session has a color of their own. You see each person's pointer as an arrow in their color with their name beside it, on the member or plate they are pointing at, and a dashed box in their color round the members, connections, footings and stairs they have selected. Pointers, names and selections are only for the session: they are never saved in the file and never go into anyone's undo. They glide to where each person is, so they stay smooth on a slow connection too. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z undoes your own last edit and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. DetailCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host's model once the host lets you in. An invite link lets someone join from another network: the host needs a Team Cloud Plan seat for the app, everyone else joins free, and the link works only while the host's session is open. When the session ends Only the host saves the model. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"usd","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Export the steel model as USD","summary":"Write every part and assembly as USD for rendering programs, scene assembly and other 3D software, with marks, profiles and weights.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["usd","usda","usdc","usdz","openusd","export","steel","assembly","marks","profiles","rendering"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUSD\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText (.usda)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBinary (.usdc)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPackage (.usdz)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach assembly is one object holding its parts; each part is its section run along its length, with copes cut. Every part carries its part mark, profile, grade, length and weight. Everything is in metres with Z up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBolt holes, bolts, welds and footings are not in the USD meshes. They are in the IFC, NC and BOM outputs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Choose USD in the Output group. Pick Text (.usda), Binary (.usdc) or Package (.usdz). Each assembly is one object holding its parts; each part is its section run along its length, with copes cut. Every part carries its part mark, profile, grade, length and weight. Everything is in metres with Z up. Bolt holes, bolts, welds and footings are not in the USD meshes. They are in the IFC, NC and BOM outputs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Coming from other detailing software","summary":"How the habits of a full detailing package map to DetailCraft, and the differences that catch experienced detailers out.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","familiar","workflow","comparison","differences","numbering","components","catalog","drawings","habits"],"headings":[{"id":"quick-map","text":"Quick map","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2},{"id":"marks-change-on-their-own","text":"Marks change on their own","level":3},{"id":"middle-mouse-button-zooms-it-does-not-pan","text":"Middle mouse button zooms; it does not pan","level":3},{"id":"columns-face-one-way-by-default","text":"Columns face one way by default","level":3},{"id":"elevations-only-on-lettered-lines","text":"Elevations only on lettered lines","level":3},{"id":"selection-copy-and-move","text":"Selection, copy and move","level":3},{"id":"grids-do-not-drag-members-along","text":"Grids do not drag members along","level":3},{"id":"connections-are-not-designed","text":"Connections are not designed","level":3},{"id":"holes-are-not-cut-in-the-3d-view","text":"Holes are not cut in the 3D view","level":3},{"id":"weights-are-gross","text":"Weights are gross","level":3},{"id":"millimeters-only","text":"Millimeters only","level":3},{"id":"no-drawings-to-manage","text":"No drawings to manage","level":3},{"id":"it-runs-in-a-browser","text":"It runs in a browser","level":3},{"id":"what-to-keep-doing-in-your-current-software","text":"What to keep doing in your current software","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you detail in Tekla Structures, most of DetailCraft will look familiar: grids, levels, profiles from a catalog, parts and assemblies, connections, numbering, NC files, IFC. The scale is very different: Tekla Structures is a full production system, and DetailCraft is a small, early browser app that does a few jobs end to end. This page tells you where your habits carry over and where they do not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your hands know the ribbon of Tekla Structures, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESteel ribbon\u003C/span\u003E menu style in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E: Modeling, Detailing, Edit, Drawings, Fabrication and View tabs, and the middle button pans. If you come from AutoCAD and like to type commands, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E adds a command line (BEAM, COLUMN, ENDPLATE, ZOOM …). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"quick-map\"\u003EQuick map\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn DetailCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECreating grids with coordinates in a grid dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrids\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel: labels and the spacings between lines, not absolute coordinates. Numbered lines run one way, lettered lines the other\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevels as grid Z coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA separate \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels\u003C/span\u003E list, name and elevation per row, applied with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply levels\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicking points anywhere with snaps\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClicks snap to grid intersections only. Off-grid points are typed into a member&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnds (x, y, z mm)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe column, beam and plate commands\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrace\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFooting\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart properties dialog (double-click a part)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick once: the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right is always live; there is no dialog and no Modify or Apply button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPosition on plane, rotation and depth settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosition\u003C/span\u003E (top of steel, centered or bottom on the line) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E. No separate horizontal offsets\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe profile catalog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA fixed list of 2,311 published sizes (AISC W, S, C, L, HSS, Pipe; European IPE, HEA, HEB, UPN; UK UB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS, CHS and cold-formed hollow sections), plus parametric plates and your own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom profile\u003C/span\u003E sections\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterial catalog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeven grades in a list: A992, A36, A572 Gr 50, A500 Gr C, S235, S275, S355\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplying a connection component and its dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect the beam, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd plates\u003C/span\u003E. The settings appear in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel. Only two connections exist (end plate to column flange, base plate)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAuto-connection, or copying a joint to similar joints\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a connection, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to similar (n)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumbering with series and start numbers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo command: marks update by themselves after every change, with fixed prefixes c, b, br, p and C, B, BR, P\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt and hole dialogs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHoles are typed in by face and position. Bolts come only from connections\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut, fit and polygon cut tools\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopes (top or bottom, at either end) on I-shapes only. Connection cut-backs are automatic\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReports and templates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThree fixed reports in tabs at the bottom, and CSV downloads\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing creation and the drawing list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne shop drawing per part mark, all made at once, previewed then downloaded as one PDF or sheet by sheet. Nothing is kept or edited\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENC file export\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENC files\u003C/span\u003E: one DSTV file per part mark, I-shapes and plates only, zipped\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC export\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC\u003C/span\u003E: IFC4 of the whole model, with rebar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERebar in any concrete part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERebar only in rectangular pad footings, US bar sizes, with a bottom mat and starter bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModel saved in a project folder, multi-user\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne \u003Ccode\u003E.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file you download. Single user, on your computer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo history\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, up to 200 steps, kept until you open or start another model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"marks-change-on-their-own\"\u003EMarks change on their own\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no numbering step. Marks are recalculated after every change, so they are always current, but they can also renumber: add a column and yesterday&#039;s c3 may now be c4. There is no &quot;numbering history&quot; to keep old marks. Finish the model, then issue the BOM, NC files and shop drawings together from one saved file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"middle-mouse-button-zooms-it-does-not-pan\"\u003EMiddle mouse button zooms; it does not pan\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the 3D view: left-drag turns, right-drag pans, the wheel zooms, and dragging with the middle button also zooms. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while left-dragging pans too. The wheel zooms toward the middle of the view, not toward the pointer. There is no rotation-center pick: the model turns around the middle of the view. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/navigate-3d\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/navigate-3d\"\u003EMove around the 3D view\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"columns-face-one-way-by-default\"\u003EColumns face one way by default\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt rotation 0 a column&#039;s flanges face along the numbered-grid direction. Beams that run between lettered grid lines frame into the web, and end plates are refused there (&quot;Beams framing into a column web are not built yet&quot;). Rotate those columns 90° before you add connections.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"elevations-only-on-lettered-lines\"\u003EElevations only on lettered lines\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E looks along lettered grid lines only. There are no views along numbered lines, no saved views and no work planes. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E for frames on numbered lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"selection-copy-and-move\"\u003ESelection, copy and move\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect several parts with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then copy, cut, paste (on a grid intersection, at a typed offset, or in place) and duplicate them, connections included (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E). There is no window selection, no select filter, no move, mirror or array, and properties are changed one member at a time in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"grids-do-not-drag-members-along\"\u003EGrids do not drag members along\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMembers keep their coordinates when you change the grid spacing; nothing moves with the grid lines. Change the grid before you place steel, or edit each member&#039;s end coordinates afterwards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"connections-are-not-designed\"\u003EConnections are not designed\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe two connections place plates, holes, bolts and welds from the numbers you give. There is no design check of any kind, only edge and spacing warnings (from EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"holes-are-not-cut-in-the-3d-view\"\u003EHoles are not cut in the 3D view\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHoles show as dark plugs. They are true holes in the NC files, the shop drawings and the IFC file. Copes are cut in the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"weights-are-gross\"\u003EWeights are gross\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe steel weight does not deduct holes or copes, and the bolt weight is not included.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"millimeters-only\"\u003EMillimeters only\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything is typed and shown in millimeters, even for AISC shapes. Profile names keep their published form (W12x50, HSS6x6x1/4).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"no-drawings-to-manage\"\u003ENo drawings to manage\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no drawing list, no drawing editor, no general arrangement or assembly drawings. A shop drawing is made when you ask for it and downloaded; to change it, change the model and make it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"it-runs-in-a-browser\"\u003EIt runs in a browser\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving downloads a file. Keyboard shortcuts that the browser keeps for itself cannot be used. Your models never leave your computer: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-to-keep-doing-in-your-current-software\"\u003EWhat to keep doing in your current software\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EToday DetailCraft suits small frames, checking an idea, teaching, or producing NC files and shop drawings for simple beam and column work. Keep complex connections, general arrangement drawings, erection drawings, multi-user projects and anything listed in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E in the software you use now.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you detail in Tekla Structures, most of DetailCraft will look familiar: grids, levels, profiles from a catalog, parts and assemblies, connections, numbering, NC files, IFC. The scale is very different: Tekla Structures is a full production system, and DetailCraft is a small, early browser app that does a few jobs end to end. This page tells you where your habits carry over and where they do not. If your hands know the ribbon of Tekla Structures, pick the Steel ribbon menu style in Settings › DetailCraft › Menus, keys and mouse: Modeling, Detailing, Edit, Drawings, Fabrication and View tabs, and the middle button pans. If you come from AutoCAD and like to type commands, Drafting classic adds a command line (BEAM, COLUMN, ENDPLATE, ZOOM …). See Menus, keys and mouse. Quick map You are used to In DetailCraft Creating grids with coordinates in a grid dialog Grids in the left panel: labels and the spacings between lines, not absolute coordinates. Numbered lines run one way, lettered lines the other Levels as grid Z coordinates A separate Levels list, name and elevation per row, applied with Apply levels Picking points anywhere with snaps Clicks snap to grid intersections only. Off-grid points are typed into a member's Ends (x, y, z mm) The column, beam and plate commands Column (C), Beam (B), Brace (R), Plate (P), Footing (F) Part properties dialog (double-click a part) Click once: the Properties panel on the right is always live; there is no dialog and no Modify or Apply button Position on plane, rotation and depth settings Position (top of steel, centered or bottom on the line) and Rotation (°). No separate horizontal offsets The profile catalog A fixed list of 2,311 published sizes (AISC W, S, C, L, HSS, Pipe; European IPE, HEA, HEB, UPN; UK UB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS, CHS and cold-formed hollow sections), plus parametric plates and your own Custom profile sections Material catalog Seven grades in a list: A992, A36, A572 Gr 50, A500 Gr C, S235, S275, S355 Applying a connection component and its dialog Select the beam, choose End plates. The settings appear in the Properties panel. Only two connections exist (end plate to column flange, base plate) Auto-connection, or copying a joint to similar joints Select a connection, choose Apply to similar (n) Numbering with series and start numbers No command: marks update by themselves after every change, with fixed prefixes c, b, br, p and C, B, BR, P Bolt and hole dialogs Holes are typed in by face and position. Bolts come only from connections Cut, fit and polygon cut tools Copes (top or bottom, at either end) on I-shapes only. Connection cut-backs are automatic Reports and templates Three fixed reports in tabs at the bottom, and CSV downloads Drawing creation and the drawing list One shop drawing per part mark, all made at once, previewed then downloaded as one PDF or sheet by sheet. Nothing is kept or edited NC file export NC files: one DSTV file per part mark, I-shapes and plates only, zipped IFC export IFC: IFC4 of the whole model, with rebar Rebar in any concrete part Rebar only in rectangular pad footings, US bar sizes, with a bottom mat and starter bars Model saved in a project folder, multi-user One .detailcraft.json file you download. Single user, on your computer Undo history Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y, up to 200 steps, kept until you open or start another model Differences that trip people up Marks change on their own There is no numbering step. Marks are recalculated after every change, so they are always current, but they can also renumber: add a column and yesterday's c3 may now be c4. There is no \"numbering history\" to keep old marks. Finish the model, then issue the BOM, NC files and shop drawings together from one saved file. Middle mouse button zooms; it does not pan In the 3D view: left-drag turns, right-drag pans, the wheel zooms, and dragging with the middle button also zooms. Holding Shift while left-dragging pans too. The wheel zooms toward the middle of the view, not toward the pointer. There is no rotation-center pick: the model turns around the middle of the view. See Move around the 3D view. Columns face one way by default At rotation 0 a column's flanges face along the numbered-grid direction. Beams that run between lettered grid lines frame into the web, and end plates are refused there (\"Beams framing into a column web are not built yet\"). Rotate those columns 90° before you add connections. Elevations only on lettered lines Elevation looks along lettered grid lines only. There are no views along numbered lines, no saved views and no work planes. Use 3D or Add by grid for frames on numbered lines. Selection, copy and move Select several parts with Shift+click or Ctrl+A, then copy, cut, paste (on a grid intersection, at a typed offset, or in place) and duplicate them, connections included (Copy, paste and duplicate). There is no window selection, no select filter, no move, mirror or array, and properties are changed one member at a time in the Properties panel. Grids do not drag members along Members keep their coordinates when you change the grid spacing; nothing moves with the grid lines. Change the grid before you place steel, or edit each member's end coordinates afterwards. Connections are not designed The two connections place plates, holes, bolts and welds from the numbers you give. There is no design check of any kind, only edge and spacing warnings (from EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3). Holes are not cut in the 3D view Holes show as dark plugs. They are true holes in the NC files, the shop drawings and the IFC file. Copes are cut in the view. Weights are gross The steel weight does not deduct holes or copes, and the bolt weight is not included. Millimeters only Everything is typed and shown in millimeters, even for AISC shapes. Profile names keep their published form (W12x50, HSS6x6x1/4). No drawings to manage There is no drawing list, no drawing editor, no general arrangement or assembly drawings. A shop drawing is made when you ask for it and downloaded; to change it, change the model and make it again. It runs in a browser Saving downloads a file. Keyboard shortcuts that the browser keeps for itself cannot be used. Your models never leave your computer: see Where your files go. What to keep doing in your current software Today DetailCraft suits small frames, checking an idea, teaching, or producing NC files and shop drawings for simple beam and column work. Keep complex connections, general arrangement drawings, erection drawings, multi-user projects and anything listed in What is not built yet in the software you use now.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"settings","scope":"detailcraft","title":"DetailCraft settings","summary":"Start new members as US or European shapes, set how far Duplicate moves a copy, choose whether copies bring their connections, and show or hide bolts in the 3D view.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","sheet size","paper size","drawings","options","steel","aisc","en","european","us shapes","metric shapes","default profile","grade","s355","a992","duplicate offset","paste offset","copy connections","bolts","anchor rods","hide bolts","reset"],"headings":[{"id":"new-members","text":"New members","level":2},{"id":"duplicate-and-paste","text":"Duplicate and paste","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"reset","text":"Reset","level":2},{"id":"drawings","text":"Drawings","level":2},{"id":"rebar","text":"Rebar","level":2},{"id":"dragging-to-select","text":"Dragging to select","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). DetailCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, they take effect at once, and this browser remembers them. They are not saved in your model file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new-members\"\u003ENew members\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhich profiles and grades the placing tools start with:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUS shapes (AISC), A992\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEuropean shapes (EN), S355\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EW16x26, A992\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIPE300, S355\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EW12x50, A992\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHEB200, S355\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHSS6x6x1/4, A500 Gr C\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHEA160, S355\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA36\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ES355\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUS shapes is the start. The European choice uses an HEA160 for braces because DetailCraft&#039;s EN catalog has no hollow sections yet (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/profile-catalog\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/profile-catalog\"\u003EProfile catalog\u003C/a\u003E). You can still pick any profile and grade for the next piece in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel while a placing tool is on. Members already in the model keep their own profile and grade.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate-and-paste\"\u003EDuplicate and paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffset (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E: x, y and z. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) puts the copy this far from the original, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste by offset (mm, from the original)\u003C/span\u003E form starts with it. It starts at 6000, 0, 0: one 6 m bay along the numbered grids. Set it to your bay spacing, or to a level height in z to copy a floor up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopy end plates and base plates with their members\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start): a connection comes along with a copy only when every member it joins is copied. Clear it to copy members and footings without their connections, for example to try a different connection on the copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow bolts and anchor rods\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start). Clear it to hide them in the view and see the holes and plates of a busy joint. Hidden bolts cannot be clicked; click the plate to select the connection. The bill of materials, drawings and every export still include every bolt and rod.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat shows in the corner of the 3D view: \u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), \u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E. Only in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E show neither. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reset\"\u003EReset\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset DetailCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of the dialog puts these back to the start (US shapes, offset 6000, 0, 0, connections copied, bolts shown), along with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E ones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheet size\u003C/strong\u003E: the sheet drawings and shop drawings are made on, landscape: A4, A3 (the start), A2, A1, ANSI B, ANSI C or ANSI D. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E and shop drawing windows have the same list. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/drawings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/drawings\"\u003EGeneral-arrangement and assembly drawings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rebar\"\u003ERebar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EASTM bar names\u003C/strong\u003E: #3 to #11, or the soft-metric #10 to #36 for the same bars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBending schedule shape codes\u003C/strong\u003E: DetailCraft&#039;s S, L, U; ACI 315 types; or BS 8666 shape codes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/rebar-reference\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/rebar-reference\"\u003ERebar reference\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dragging-to-select\"\u003EDragging to select\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangle\u003C/strong\u003E (as before) or \u003Cstrong\u003ELasso\u003C/strong\u003E: a freehand line, with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switching between inside, touching and fence while you drag. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/select-and-change#select-with-a-window\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/select-and-change\" data-hash=\"select-with-a-window\"\u003ESelect with a window\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, DetailCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). DetailCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, they take effect at once, and this browser remembers them. They are not saved in your model file. New members Which profiles and grades the placing tools start with: Tool US shapes (AISC), A992 European shapes (EN), S355 Beam W16x26, A992 IPE300, S355 Column W12x50, A992 HEB200, S355 Brace HSS6x6x1/4, A500 Gr C HEA160, S355 Plate A36 S355 US shapes is the start. The European choice uses an HEA160 for braces because DetailCraft's EN catalog has no hollow sections yet (Profile catalog). You can still pick any profile and grade for the next piece in the Properties panel while a placing tool is on. Members already in the model keep their own profile and grade. Duplicate and paste Offset (mm): x, y and z. Duplicate (Ctrl+D) puts the copy this far from the original, and the Paste by offset (mm, from the original) form starts with it. It starts at 6000, 0, 0: one 6 m bay along the numbered grids. Set it to your bay spacing, or to a level height in z to copy a floor up. Copy end plates and base plates with their members (on at the start): a connection comes along with a copy only when every member it joins is copied. Clear it to copy members and footings without their connections, for example to try a different connection on the copy. See Copy, paste and duplicate. 3D view Show bolts and anchor rods (on at the start). Clear it to hide them in the view and see the holes and plates of a busy joint. Hidden bolts cannot be clicked; click the plate to select the connection. The bill of materials, drawings and every export still include every bolt and rod. Corner indicator What shows in the corner of the 3D view: View cube (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. Only in 3D; Plan and Elevation show neither. See The view cube. Reset Reset DetailCraft settings at the bottom of the dialog puts these back to the start (US shapes, offset 6000, 0, 0, connections copied, bolts shown), along with the General ones. Drawings Sheet size: the sheet drawings and shop drawings are made on, landscape: A4, A3 (the start), A2, A1, ANSI B, ANSI C or ANSI D. The Drawings and shop drawing windows have the same list. See General-arrangement and assembly drawings. Rebar ASTM bar names: #3 to #11, or the soft-metric #10 to #36 for the same bars. Bending schedule shape codes: DetailCraft's S, L, U; ACI 315 types; or BS 8666 shape codes. See Rebar reference. Dragging to select Rectangle (as before) or Lasso: a freehand line, with Space switching between inside, touching and fence while you drag. See Select with a window.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick a menu style that matches the way you already work, give any command your own key, move commands between ribbon tabs, and set what the wheel and the mouse buttons do.","section":"reference","order":6,"keywords":["menu style","menus","ribbon","toolbar","command line","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","right button","pan","orbit","export","import","switching","familiar","drafting classic","steel ribbon"],"headings":[{"id":"the-three-styles","text":"The three styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft&#039;s commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-three-styles\"\u003EThe three styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetailCraft&#039;s own toolbar (File, Place, Connect, Edit, Output) and its single-letter keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E beam, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E column, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brace, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E plate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E footing, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stair. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrafting classic\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor people who type commands: a small ribbon (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutput\u003C/span\u003E) and a command line. Type BEAM, COLUMN, BRACE, PLATE, FOOTING, STAIR, ENDPLATE, BASEPLATE, SHEARTAB, SPLICE, GUSSET, U (undo), ZOOM, PLAN, 3D, DRAWINGS, NC or HELP and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on nothing repeats the last command, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears it. Start typing anywhere and the letters go to the command line. The middle button pans; in 3D, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the middle button orbit.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESteel ribbon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabs the way steel detailing programs arrange them: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModeling\u003C/span\u003E (members, concrete, stairs), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDetailing\u003C/span\u003E (connections), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/span\u003E (drawings and reports), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFabrication\u003C/span\u003E (NC files, IFC) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu and quick buttons on the right. The single-letter keys stay. The middle button pans; in 3D the left button orbits.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery style keeps \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The ribbons also hold the view bar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to DetailCraft&#039;s own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (&quot;In My Steel ribbon, Column is Ctrl+Alt+C&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning, and while a member is half placed or a copy follows the pointer, letters belong to DetailCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a command (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the command to another tab and panel, to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel, the buttons that pan the plan and elevations, and what each button does in the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My Steel ribbon&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the plan and elevations with\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle button, the right button, or both. With the Select tool the left button always draws a selection window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag in the 3D view\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEach view&#039;s own\u003C/span\u003E keeps DetailCraft&#039;s buttons (left orbits, middle zooms, right pans). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a button that orbits or pans swaps the two.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at DetailCraft&#039;s own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a ribbon or Drafting classic), with the changes you made in each app. DetailCraft has no menu-bar style: when another app&#039;s menu bar is chosen for every app, DetailCraft keeps its own toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other detailing software\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft's commands are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys run them. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › DetailCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The three styles Style What you get DetailCraft DetailCraft's own toolbar (File, Place, Connect, Edit, Output) and its single-letter keys: V select, B beam, C column, R brace, P plate, F footing, S stair. The start. Drafting classic For people who type commands: a small ribbon (Home, View, Output) and a command line. Type BEAM, COLUMN, BRACE, PLATE, FOOTING, STAIR, ENDPLATE, BASEPLATE, SHEARTAB, SPLICE, GUSSET, U (undo), ZOOM, PLAN, 3D, DRAWINGS, NC or HELP and press Enter; Enter on nothing repeats the last command, Esc clears it. Start typing anywhere and the letters go to the command line. The middle button pans; in 3D, Shift and the middle button orbit. Steel ribbon Tabs the way steel detailing programs arrange them: Modeling (members, concrete, stairs), Detailing (connections), Edit, Drawings (drawings and reports), Fabrication (NC files, IFC) and View, with a File menu and quick buttons on the right. The single-letter keys stay. The middle button pans; in 3D the left button orbits. Every style keeps Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+Shift+V, Ctrl+D and Ctrl+A. The ribbons also hold the view bar's 3D, Plan, Elevation and Fit. A key that belongs to DetailCraft's own layout but that your style gave another command does nothing, and the status line says where its command went (\"In My Steel ribbon, Column is Ctrl+Alt+C\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning, and while a member is half placed or a copy follows the pointer, letters belong to DetailCraft. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a command (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off. With a ribbon, Where it sits moves the command to another tab and panel, to the File menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel, the buttons that pan the plan and elevations, and what each button does in the 3D view. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My Steel ribbon\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two commands is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the plan and elevations with: the middle button, the right button, or both. With the Select tool the left button always draws a selection window. Drag in the 3D view: Each view's own keeps DetailCraft's buttons (left orbits, middle zooms, right pans). Or set each button to orbit, pan, zoom or nothing; Shift with a button that orbits or pans swaps the two. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at DetailCraft's own commands: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status line. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style (its own toolbar, a ribbon or Drafting classic), with the changes you made in each app. DetailCraft has no menu-bar style: when another app's menu bar is chosen for every app, DetailCraft keeps its own toolbar. Related: Keyboard and mouse · DetailCraft settings · Coming from other detailing software","lang":"en"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Keys and mouse actions","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut and mouse, trackpad and touch action in DetailCraft, from the code.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","mouse","trackpad","touch","ctrl","command","cheat sheet"],"headings":[{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"file-and-edit","text":"File and edit","level":2},{"id":"copy-and-paste","text":"Copy and paste","level":2},{"id":"fields-and-panels","text":"Fields and panels","level":2},{"id":"screen-and-help","text":"Screen and help","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"touch","text":"Touch","level":2},{"id":"keys-the-browser-keeps","text":"Keys the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"where-move-lives","text":"Where Move lives","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EHow keys are written in this help: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E. On a Mac, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the Command key; elsewhere it is Ctrl.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese work when the cursor is not in a text or number field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeam\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBrace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFooting\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStair\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the selection by picking two grid intersections (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/select-and-change\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/select-and-change\"\u003ESelect by window, move, rotate, mirror, change many\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop a selection window being dragged, or put dragged parts back; cancel a move, the first point of a beam, brace or plate, or a paste; press again to go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E; in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, clear the selection. With the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu open, close it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selection (everything selected, in one undo step)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"file-and-edit\"\u003EFile and edit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the model (download). Works even while typing in a field\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a model. Works even while typing in a field\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo (outside text fields; inside one it undoes your typing)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-and-paste\"\u003ECopy and paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese work when the cursor is not in a text or number field (there they copy, paste and select your text as usual). Each paste, cut, duplicate or delete is one undo step. More: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E the selected members and footings (with their holes, copes and connections)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E: copy the selection, then delete it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E: the copy follows the pointer; click a grid intersection for it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E: at the coordinates it was copied from\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E: a copy at once, moved by the offset in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E: every member, connection and footing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you have selected text on the page (in a report table, say) and nothing in the model, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies the text, as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESample\u003C/span\u003E and the exports have no keys; use the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENone of these keys do anything while a dialog (the shop drawing preview or a question) is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fields-and-panels\"\u003EFields and panels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApply a typed value (each change is one undo step)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrids\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom profile\u003C/span\u003E, hole or cope field\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same as choosing that form&#039;s button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove between the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending schedule\u003C/span\u003E tabs when a tab has focus\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the shop drawing preview or a question dialog (the same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen-and-help\"\u003EScreen and help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Control on every computer, a Mac included\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave full screen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen this help\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn 3D\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn Plan and Elevation\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a part, or place at the nearest intersection when a placing tool is on (or paste there while pasting)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd the part to the selection, or take it out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress on a selected part and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the selection across the level; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held, up and down\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the selection (in an elevation: along it and up and down)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: a selection window (to the right: wholly inside; to the left: touching). While placing: pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: a selection window, added to the selection. While placing: pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: a selection window, added to the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the middle of the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: pan. Otherwise zoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop a \u003Ccode\u003E.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E file on the app\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA click only counts if the pointer moves less than about 5 pixels between press and release; a longer movement is a drag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe up or down, or pinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn (3D) or pan (Plan and Elevation)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and press and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click (there is no menu in the view)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch\"\u003ETouch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a tablet or a touch screen:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a part, or place at the nearest grid intersection when a placing tool is on (a tap within about a finger&#039;s width of it counts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELong press on a part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd it to the selection, or take it out (what \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click does with a mouse)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger on a selected part, then drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the selection, snapping onto grid intersections\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger elsewhere\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the model (3D) or pan (Plan and Elevation)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch to zoom, move together to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EButtons, lists and fields are larger when the screen is touch only. Selection windows need a mouse; with fingers, tap and long-press.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EKeys the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome shortcuts belong to the browser and no page can use them, for example \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (close tab), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (new tab) and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (new window). If \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens your browser&#039;s history instead of redoing, use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts reach DetailCraft only when the page has the keyboard. If a key does nothing, or the browser acts on it instead, click an empty part of the DetailCraft window first (for example after typing in the address bar).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-move-lives\"\u003EWhere Move lives\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Move command: select a part and drag it across the level (hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to drag up and down), or edit its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How keys are written in this help: Keys, mice and trackpads. On a Mac, Ctrl is the Command key; elsewhere it is Ctrl. Tools These work when the cursor is not in a text or number field. Key Does V Select B Beam C Column R Brace P Plate F Footing S Stair M Move the selection by picking two grid intersections (Select by window, move, rotate, mirror, change many) Esc Drop a selection window being dragged, or put dragged parts back; cancel a move, the first point of a beam, brace or plate, or a paste; press again to go back to Select; in Select, clear the selection. With the More menu open, close it Delete or Backspace Delete the selection (everything selected, in one undo step) File and edit Key Does Ctrl+S Save the model (download). Works even while typing in a field Ctrl+O Open a model. Works even while typing in a field Ctrl+Z Undo (outside text fields; inside one it undoes your typing) Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo Copy and paste These work when the cursor is not in a text or number field (there they copy, paste and select your text as usual). Each paste, cut, duplicate or delete is one undo step. More: Copy, paste and duplicate. Key Does Ctrl+C Copy the selected members and footings (with their holes, copes and connections) Ctrl+X Cut: copy the selection, then delete it Ctrl+V Paste: the copy follows the pointer; click a grid intersection for it Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: at the coordinates it was copied from Ctrl+D Duplicate: a copy at once, moved by the offset in DetailCraft settings Ctrl+A Select all: every member, connection and footing Esc Select none If you have selected text on the page (in a report table, say) and nothing in the model, Ctrl+C copies the text, as usual. New, Sample and the exports have no keys; use the toolbar. None of these keys do anything while a dialog (the shop drawing preview or a question) is open. Fields and panels Key Does Enter or Tab Apply a typed value (each change is one undo step) Enter in a Grids, Levels, Custom profile, hole or cope field The same as choosing that form's button ← → Move between the Problems, Bill of materials and Bending schedule tabs when a tab has focus Esc Close the shop drawing preview or a question dialog (the same as Cancel) Screen and help Key Does Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Control on every computer, a Mac included Ctrl+, Open Settings (DetailCraft settings) Esc Leave full screen F1 Open this help Mouse Action In 3D In Plan and Elevation Click Select a part, or place at the nearest intersection when a placing tool is on (or paste there while pasting) The same Shift, Ctrl or Ctrl and click Add the part to the selection, or take it out The same Press on a selected part and drag Move the selection across the level; with Ctrl held, up and down Move the selection (in an elevation: along it and up and down) Drag with the left button Turn the model With Select: a selection window (to the right: wholly inside; to the left: touching). While placing: pan Shift and drag with the left button With Select: a selection window, added to the selection. While placing: pan With Select: a selection window, added to the selection Ctrl or Ctrl and drag with the left button Pan Nothing Drag with the right button Pan Pan Wheel Zoom toward the middle of the view Zoom Drag with the middle button Zoom With Select: pan. Otherwise zoom Drop a .detailcraft.json file on the app Open it Open it A click only counts if the pointer moves less than about 5 pixels between press and release; a longer movement is a drag. Trackpad Action Does Two-finger swipe up or down, or pinch Zoom Press and drag Turn (3D) or pan (Plan and Elevation) Shift and press and drag Pan in 3D Two-finger click Right-click (there is no menu in the view) Touch On a tablet or a touch screen: Action Does Tap Select a part, or place at the nearest grid intersection when a placing tool is on (a tap within about a finger's width of it counts) Long press on a part Add it to the selection, or take it out (what Shift+click does with a mouse) One finger on a selected part, then drag Move the selection, snapping onto grid intersections One finger elsewhere Turn the model (3D) or pan (Plan and Elevation) Two fingers Pinch to zoom, move together to pan Buttons, lists and fields are larger when the screen is touch only. Selection windows need a mouse; with fingers, tap and long-press. Keys the browser keeps Some shortcuts belong to the browser and no page can use them, for example Ctrl+W (close tab), Ctrl+T (new tab) and Ctrl+N (new window). If Ctrl+Y opens your browser's history instead of redoing, use Ctrl+Shift+Z. Shortcuts reach DetailCraft only when the page has the keyboard. If a key does nothing, or the browser acts on it instead, click an empty part of the DetailCraft window first (for example after typing in the address bar). Where Move lives There is no Move command: select a part and drag it across the level (hold Ctrl to drag up and down), or edit its Start and End in Properties.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"profile-catalog","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Profile catalog","summary":"Every built-in steel size, how profile names are written, where the dimensions come from, and how plates and custom sections are named.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["profiles","sections","catalog","sizes","w shapes","wide flange","s shapes","channel","angle","hss","tube","pipe","ipe","hea","heb","upn","uk","british","ub","uc","pfc","ea","uea","unequal angle","shs","rhs","chs","cold formed","hot finished","blue book","plate","aisc","en 10365","naming"],"headings":[{"id":"naming","text":"Naming","level":2},{"id":"sources","text":"Sources","level":2},{"id":"the-sizes","text":"The sizes","level":2},{"id":"what-each-shape-can-do","text":"What each shape can do","level":2},{"id":"need-another-size","text":"Need another size?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft has 2,311 built-in sizes, each copied from a published table and checked by the app&#039;s tests: the area worked out from the dimensions must match the published area, and the published weight must match that area times the density of steel. Plates and custom profiles are made from their dimensions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"naming\"\u003ENaming\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUS shapes\u003C/strong\u003E are written the way the AISC Manual prints them, with a lowercase x: W12x50, C10x15.3, L4x4x1/2, HSS6x6x1/4, HSS6.625x0.280. Typing is not case-sensitive.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPipe\u003C/strong\u003E is written as in the AISC database: Pipe4STD, Pipe4XS, Pipe4XXS (standard, extra strong, double extra strong).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEuropean shapes\u003C/strong\u003E are written as in EN 10365: IPE200, HEA240, HEB300, UPN200 (no space).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUK shapes\u003C/strong\u003E are written with the family first, then the Blue Book&#039;s size: UB305x165x40, UC203x203x46, PFC200x90x30, EA100x100x10.0, UEA150x90x10.0, SHS100x100x5.0, RHS200x100x8.0, CHS88.9x5.0. Cold-formed hollow sections start with CF: CFSHS100x100x5.0. Typing the way drawings often say it also works: 305x165x40 UB, L100x100x10, SHS100x5.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlates\u003C/strong\u003E are \u003Ccode\u003EPL&lt;thickness&gt;x&lt;width&gt;\u003C/code\u003E in millimeters: PL20x200 is 20 mm thick and 200 mm wide. Any size from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E tool works.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECustom profiles\u003C/strong\u003E have whatever name you give them, as long as it is not a catalog name and does not look like a plate name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E lists, each group&#039;s heading names its source, for example &quot;W shapes — AISC Shapes Database v16.0&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sources\"\u003ESources\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESource\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EW shapes, S shapes, Channels (C), Angles (L), HSS, Pipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAISC Shapes Database v16.0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions converted from inches to millimeters exactly; mass per meter from the published lb/ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIPE, HEA, HEB, UPN\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEN 10365:2017\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMillimeters and kg/m as published\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS, CHS, CFSHS, CFRHS, CFCHS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESteel for Life Blue Book (UK), table v18 (BS EN 10365, BS EN 10056-1, BS EN 10210-2, BS EN 10219-2)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMillimeters and kg/m as published\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParametric\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMass from area × 7850 kg/m³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECustom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMass from area × 7850 kg/m³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow shapes are drawn:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EW shapes:\u003C/strong\u003E the AISC database gives no fillet radius, so DetailCraft draws the fillet as r = kdes − tf.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ES shapes and channels (C, UPN):\u003C/strong\u003E parallel flanges of the published average thickness, square corners (the real flange is sloped).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngles:\u003C/strong\u003E square corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangular HSS:\u003C/strong\u003E the design wall thickness (0.93 × nominal, as AISC 360-22 B4.2 allows) with an outer corner radius of twice that.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERound HSS and Pipe:\u003C/strong\u003E design wall thickness (0.93 × nominal).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIPE, HEA, HEB, UB, UC:\u003C/strong\u003E the published root radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPFC, EA, UEA:\u003C/strong\u003E square corners (the published mass and area still include the radii).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESHS, RHS, CHS:\u003C/strong\u003E the nominal wall. Hot finished corners: outer 1.5 t, inner t. Cold formed: outer 2 t, inner t up to 6 mm; 2.5 t and 1.5 t up to 10 mm; 3 t and 2 t above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-sizes\"\u003EThe sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery size the tables list in these groups, smallest first:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESizes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EW shapes (289)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EW4x13 to W44x408\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ES shapes (28)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ES3x5.7 to S24x121\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChannels (32)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC3x3.5 to C15x50\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngles, equal legs (61)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL2x2x1/8 to L12x12x1-3/8\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHSS, rectangular and square (525)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHSS1-1/2x1-1/2x1/8 to HSS34x10x1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHSS, round (189)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHSS1.660x0.140 to HSS28.000x1.000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe (51)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe1/2STD to Pipe26XS\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIPE (18)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIPE80 to IPE600\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHEA (24)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHEA100 to HEA1000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHEB (24)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHEB100 to HEB1000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUPN (18)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUPN50 to UPN400\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngles, unequal legs (76)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL2-1/2x1-1/2x3/16 to L8x6x1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUB (107)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUB127x76x13 to UB1016x305x584\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUC (46)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUC152x152x23 to UC356x406x1299\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPFC (16)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPFC100x50x10 to PFC430x100x64\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEA (42)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEA20x20x3.0 to EA200x200x24.0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUEA (39)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUEA30x20x3.0 to UEA200x150x18.0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESHS, hot finished (123)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESHS40x40x3.2 to SHS400x400x17.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERHS, hot finished (161)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERHS50x30x3.2 to RHS500x300x17.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECHS, hot finished (103)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECHS42.4x3.2 to CHS508.0x17.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECFSHS, cold formed (96)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECFSHS25x25x2.0 to CFSHS400x400x12.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECFRHS, cold formed (137)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECFRHS50x25x2.0 to CFRHS500x300x12.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECFCHS, cold formed (106)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECFCHS33.7x3.0 to CFCHS508.0x16.0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA mirrored unequal angle is the same piece turned end for end, the way a shop makes the opposite hand: its holes, copes and gusset go with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a placing tool and pick a size to see its depth, flange width, web and flange thickness, fillet radius, area (cm²), mass (kg/m) and source in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProfile\u003C/span\u003E box of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-shape-can-do\"\u003EWhat each shape can do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHoles\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECopes\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEnd plates and base plates\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENC file\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EI-shapes (W, S, IPE, HEA, HEB, UB, UC, custom I)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb, top flange, bottom flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd plates: beam and column. Base plates: yes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChannels (C, UPN, PFC)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb, top flange, bottom flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase plates only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERectangular HSS, SHS, RHS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESide wall (web), top, bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase plates only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound HSS, Pipe, CHS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase plates only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngles (L, EA, UEA)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase plates only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlates and custom flat bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a rectangular tube, a hole on the &quot;web&quot; face goes through both side walls. What each NC file holds is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/nc1-fields\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/nc1-fields\"\u003ENC file fields\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"need-another-size\"\u003ENeed another size?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd it as a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/plates-and-profiles#add-a-custom-profile\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/plates-and-profiles\" data-hash=\"add-a-custom-profile\"\u003Ecustom profile\u003C/a\u003E. The built-in list cannot be edited from the app yet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft has 2,311 built-in sizes, each copied from a published table and checked by the app's tests: the area worked out from the dimensions must match the published area, and the published weight must match that area times the density of steel. Plates and custom profiles are made from their dimensions. Naming US shapes are written the way the AISC Manual prints them, with a lowercase x: W12x50, C10x15.3, L4x4x1/2, HSS6x6x1/4, HSS6.625x0.280. Typing is not case-sensitive. Pipe is written as in the AISC database: Pipe4STD, Pipe4XS, Pipe4XXS (standard, extra strong, double extra strong). European shapes are written as in EN 10365: IPE200, HEA240, HEB300, UPN200 (no space). UK shapes are written with the family first, then the Blue Book's size: UB305x165x40, UC203x203x46, PFC200x90x30, EA100x100x10.0, UEA150x90x10.0, SHS100x100x5.0, RHS200x100x8.0, CHS88.9x5.0. Cold-formed hollow sections start with CF: CFSHS100x100x5.0. Typing the way drawings often say it also works: 305x165x40 UB, L100x100x10, SHS100x5. Plates are PL\u003Cthickness\u003Ex\u003Cwidth\u003E in millimeters: PL20x200 is 20 mm thick and 200 mm wide. Any size from the Plate tool works. Custom profiles have whatever name you give them, as long as it is not a catalog name and does not look like a plate name. In the Profile lists, each group's heading names its source, for example \"W shapes — AISC Shapes Database v16.0\". Sources Group Source Notes W shapes, S shapes, Channels (C), Angles (L), HSS, Pipe AISC Shapes Database v16.0 Dimensions converted from inches to millimeters exactly; mass per meter from the published lb/ft IPE, HEA, HEB, UPN EN 10365:2017 Millimeters and kg/m as published UB, UC, PFC, EA, UEA, SHS, RHS, CHS, CFSHS, CFRHS, CFCHS Steel for Life Blue Book (UK), table v18 (BS EN 10365, BS EN 10056-1, BS EN 10210-2, BS EN 10219-2) Millimeters and kg/m as published Plates Parametric Mass from area × 7850 kg/m³ Custom Your dimensions Mass from area × 7850 kg/m³ How shapes are drawn: W shapes: the AISC database gives no fillet radius, so DetailCraft draws the fillet as r = kdes − tf. S shapes and channels (C, UPN): parallel flanges of the published average thickness, square corners (the real flange is sloped). Angles: square corners. Rectangular HSS: the design wall thickness (0.93 × nominal, as AISC 360-22 B4.2 allows) with an outer corner radius of twice that. Round HSS and Pipe: design wall thickness (0.93 × nominal). IPE, HEA, HEB, UB, UC: the published root radius. PFC, EA, UEA: square corners (the published mass and area still include the radii). SHS, RHS, CHS: the nominal wall. Hot finished corners: outer 1.5 t, inner t. Cold formed: outer 2 t, inner t up to 6 mm; 2.5 t and 1.5 t up to 10 mm; 3 t and 2 t above. The sizes Every size the tables list in these groups, smallest first: Group Sizes W shapes (289) W4x13 to W44x408 S shapes (28) S3x5.7 to S24x121 Channels (32) C3x3.5 to C15x50 Angles, equal legs (61) L2x2x1/8 to L12x12x1-3/8 HSS, rectangular and square (525) HSS1-1/2x1-1/2x1/8 to HSS34x10x1 HSS, round (189) HSS1.660x0.140 to HSS28.000x1.000 Pipe (51) Pipe1/2STD to Pipe26XS IPE (18) IPE80 to IPE600 HEA (24) HEA100 to HEA1000 HEB (24) HEB100 to HEB1000 UPN (18) UPN50 to UPN400 Angles, unequal legs (76) L2-1/2x1-1/2x3/16 to L8x6x1 UB (107) UB127x76x13 to UB1016x305x584 UC (46) UC152x152x23 to UC356x406x1299 PFC (16) PFC100x50x10 to PFC430x100x64 EA (42) EA20x20x3.0 to EA200x200x24.0 UEA (39) UEA30x20x3.0 to UEA200x150x18.0 SHS, hot finished (123) SHS40x40x3.2 to SHS400x400x17.5 RHS, hot finished (161) RHS50x30x3.2 to RHS500x300x17.5 CHS, hot finished (103) CHS42.4x3.2 to CHS508.0x17.5 CFSHS, cold formed (96) CFSHS25x25x2.0 to CFSHS400x400x12.5 CFRHS, cold formed (137) CFRHS50x25x2.0 to CFRHS500x300x12.5 CFCHS, cold formed (106) CFCHS33.7x3.0 to CFCHS508.0x16.0 A mirrored unequal angle is the same piece turned end for end, the way a shop makes the opposite hand: its holes, copes and gusset go with it. Choose a placing tool and pick a size to see its depth, flange width, web and flange thickness, fillet radius, area (cm²), mass (kg/m) and source in the Profile box of the Properties panel. What each shape can do Shape Holes Copes End plates and base plates NC file Shop drawing I-shapes (W, S, IPE, HEA, HEB, UB, UC, custom I) Web, top flange, bottom flange Yes End plates: beam and column. Base plates: yes Yes Yes Channels (C, UPN, PFC) Web, top flange, bottom flange No Base plates only Yes Yes Rectangular HSS, SHS, RHS Side wall (web), top, bottom No Base plates only Yes Yes Round HSS, Pipe, CHS Not yet No Base plates only Yes Yes Angles (L, EA, UEA) Not yet No Base plates only Yes Yes Plates and custom flat bars Plate face No No Yes Yes For a rectangular tube, a hole on the \"web\" face goes through both side walls. What each NC file holds is in NC file fields. Need another size? Add it as a custom profile. The built-in list cannot be edited from the app yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"constructability","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Constructability checks","summary":"Problems for the things a detailer catches by eye: a bolt a wrench cannot reach, bolts too close for the socket, fillet welds outside their size limits, welds a torch cannot reach, and tubes that hold water.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["constructability","wrench","socket","bolt access","tightening","weld size","fillet weld","minimum weld","maximum weld","water","vent hole","drain hole","galvanising","hollow section","problems"],"headings":[{"id":"a-bolt-a-wrench-cannot-reach","text":"A bolt a wrench cannot reach","level":2},{"id":"bolts-too-close-for-the-socket","text":"Bolts too close for the socket","level":2},{"id":"fillet-weld-sizes","text":"Fillet weld sizes","level":2},{"id":"water-and-vents","text":"Water and vents","level":2},{"id":"can-the-welder-reach-it","text":"Can the welder reach it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft looks for four kinds of trouble that no strength check finds, and lists them in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E panel. They are warnings and notes to look at, not errors: the model still builds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-bolt-a-wrench-cannot-reach\"\u003EA bolt a wrench cannot reach\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor every bolt, DetailCraft puts a socket over the head and over the nut. The socket is a cylinder as wide as the nut across its corners plus a wall of 4 mm, 60 mm long. If a beam web, a flange, a plate or a brace stands inside that cylinder, the problem names the connection, how many bolts, which part is in the way and how far from the bolt it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe socket is a typical impact socket. A spanner needs less room and a bigger tool more. Ask your connected assistant to run \u003Ccode\u003Econstructability_check\u003C/code\u003E with another \u003Ccode\u003EsocketLength\u003C/code\u003E to see how the answer changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bolts-too-close-for-the-socket\"\u003EBolts too close for the socket\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA socket over one bolt must not touch the head of the next. The bolts of a connection that are nearer than the socket&#039;s width plus the neighbour&#039;s head are listed with the spacing found and the spacing needed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fillet-weld-sizes\"\u003EFillet weld sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAISC 360-16 Table J2.4 sets the smallest fillet weld by the thickness of the thinner part (3 mm up to 6.35 mm thick, 5 mm to 12.7, 6 mm to 19.05, 8 mm above), and J2.2b the largest along an edge of material: no bigger than the material when it is thinner than 6.35 mm, otherwise 1.6 mm less. Connections with the same size on the same thickness are listed as one problem.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thickness used is the thinnest part named by the weld, so an 8 mm weld all round a beam with a 6.35 mm web is listed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"water-and-vents\"\u003EWater and vents\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Eopen upper end\u003C/strong\u003E of a hollow section that stands upright or slopes by more than 10° lets rain in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA hollow section \u003Cstrong\u003Eclosed at both ends\u003C/strong\u003E needs a vent and a drain hole; hot-dip galvanising and the pressure of heated air both ask for one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"can-the-welder-reach-it\"\u003ECan the welder reach it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA fillet weld is made with the torch half way between the two faces it joins. For each all-round fillet weld (an end plate, a base plate, a mitre plate) the check takes the member&#039;s section outline at the plate and, every 20 mm, runs a torch path out at 45° from where its nozzle (10 mm radius to start) first clears both faces, for another 30 mm. If the path meets the beam&#039;s own flange or another part, that share of the weld is listed with the part and the distance. These numbers are typical, not a standard&#039;s: ask your fabricator for the process and nozzle. Welds along two edges (a shear tab) are not located and are not checked.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft looks for four kinds of trouble that no strength check finds, and lists them in the Problems panel. They are warnings and notes to look at, not errors: the model still builds. A bolt a wrench cannot reach For every bolt, DetailCraft puts a socket over the head and over the nut. The socket is a cylinder as wide as the nut across its corners plus a wall of 4 mm, 60 mm long. If a beam web, a flange, a plate or a brace stands inside that cylinder, the problem names the connection, how many bolts, which part is in the way and how far from the bolt it is. The socket is a typical impact socket. A spanner needs less room and a bigger tool more. Ask your connected assistant to run constructability_check with another socketLength to see how the answer changes. Bolts too close for the socket A socket over one bolt must not touch the head of the next. The bolts of a connection that are nearer than the socket's width plus the neighbour's head are listed with the spacing found and the spacing needed. Fillet weld sizes AISC 360-16 Table J2.4 sets the smallest fillet weld by the thickness of the thinner part (3 mm up to 6.35 mm thick, 5 mm to 12.7, 6 mm to 19.05, 8 mm above), and J2.2b the largest along an edge of material: no bigger than the material when it is thinner than 6.35 mm, otherwise 1.6 mm less. Connections with the same size on the same thickness are listed as one problem. The thickness used is the thinnest part named by the weld, so an 8 mm weld all round a beam with a 6.35 mm web is listed. Water and vents An open upper end of a hollow section that stands upright or slopes by more than 10° lets rain in. A hollow section closed at both ends needs a vent and a drain hole; hot-dip galvanising and the pressure of heated air both ask for one. Can the welder reach it A fillet weld is made with the torch half way between the two faces it joins. For each all-round fillet weld (an end plate, a base plate, a mitre plate) the check takes the member's section outline at the plate and, every 20 mm, runs a torch path out at 45° from where its nozzle (10 mm radius to start) first clears both faces, for another 30 mm. If the path meets the beam's own flange or another part, that share of the weld is listed with the part and the distance. These numbers are typical, not a standard's: ask your fabricator for the process and nozzle. Welds along two edges (a shear tab) are not located and are not checked.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"mark-rules","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Mark rules","summary":"Exactly when two parts or two assemblies share a mark, how marks are numbered, and how footing and bar marks work.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["marks","numbering","part mark","assembly mark","piece mark","prefix","tolerance","identical parts","bar marks","footing marks"],"headings":[{"id":"part-marks","text":"Part marks","level":2},{"id":"turned-over-parts","text":"Turned-over parts","level":3},{"id":"numbering","text":"Numbering","level":3},{"id":"assembly-marks","text":"Assembly marks","level":2},{"id":"footing-marks","text":"Footing marks","level":2},{"id":"bar-marks","text":"Bar marks","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft recalculates every mark after every change. Nothing is stored, so the marks always describe the model as it is now. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/marks-and-bom\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/marks-and-bom\"\u003ECheck marks and the bill of materials\u003C/a\u003E for how to use them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"part-marks\"\u003EPart marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo parts share a part mark when all of these match:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe part type (beam, column, brace or plate).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe profile name (not case-sensitive).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe material (not case-sensitive).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe cut length, within 1 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery hole: face, position along and across, within 1 mm, and diameter within 0.1 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery cope: end, side, length and depth, within 1 mm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe member&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E does not count. Neither do its position in the model, its rotation, nor which way it was drawn, as long as the part itself is the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"turned-over-parts\"\u003ETurned-over parts\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part is compared in every way it could be turned over that maps its section onto itself. If any of those makes it match another part, they share a mark.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETurns that count\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EI-shapes, rectangular HSS, plates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd for end (about the vertical axis), end for end (about the horizontal axis), and upside down about its own length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChannels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnd for end about the horizontal axis only, so the web stays on the same side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngles, round HSS\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone: compared as placed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESo a beam with a hole near its left end matches a beam with the same hole near its right end, and a column with holes in one flange matches a column with the same holes in the other flange.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"numbering\"\u003ENumbering\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart prefix\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAssembly prefix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColumn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ec\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EC\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBeam\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eb\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBrace\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ebr\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBR\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate (placed, end plate or base plate)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ep\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EP\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarks are numbered from 1 within each prefix. The order is: columns, then beams, braces and plates; within a type, by profile name in natural order (W8x10 before W12x26); then longer parts first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBecause numbering follows that order, adding or changing one part can shift the numbers of others.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assembly-marks\"\u003EAssembly marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery part that is not welded to another part is the main part of its own assembly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEnd plates are welded to their beam, and base plates to their column, so they belong to that member&#039;s assembly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo assemblies share a mark when their main parts share a part mark and their attached parts have the same marks at the same places (within 1 mm), allowing the same turns as the main part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe assembly prefix follows the main part: a beam with two end plates is a B assembly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlates you place with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlate\u003C/span\u003E tool are not welded to anything, so each is its own P assembly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"footing-marks\"\u003EFooting marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFootings with the same length, width, depth, concrete text, bottom mat and starter bars share a mark: F1, F2 … in the order they were placed. Position and rotation do not count.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bar-marks\"\u003EBar marks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBars with the same size, shape and dimensions A, B and C (rounded to the millimeter) share a mark across all footings. The mark is the bar number, F, and a counter per bar size: 5F1, 5F2, 6F1. A long-way bar and a short-way bar of a square footing are the same bar and share a mark; the schedule&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUsed for\u003C/span\u003E column lists both uses.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft recalculates every mark after every change. Nothing is stored, so the marks always describe the model as it is now. See Check marks and the bill of materials for how to use them. Part marks Two parts share a part mark when all of these match: The part type (beam, column, brace or plate). The profile name (not case-sensitive). The material (not case-sensitive). The cut length, within 1 mm. Every hole: face, position along and across, within 1 mm, and diameter within 0.1 mm. Every cope: end, side, length and depth, within 1 mm. The member's Name does not count. Neither do its position in the model, its rotation, nor which way it was drawn, as long as the part itself is the same. Turned-over parts A part is compared in every way it could be turned over that maps its section onto itself. If any of those makes it match another part, they share a mark. Shape Turns that count I-shapes, rectangular HSS, plates End for end (about the vertical axis), end for end (about the horizontal axis), and upside down about its own length Channels End for end about the horizontal axis only, so the web stays on the same side Angles, round HSS None: compared as placed So a beam with a hole near its left end matches a beam with the same hole near its right end, and a column with holes in one flange matches a column with the same holes in the other flange. Numbering Type Part prefix Assembly prefix Column c C Beam b B Brace br BR Plate (placed, end plate or base plate) p P Marks are numbered from 1 within each prefix. The order is: columns, then beams, braces and plates; within a type, by profile name in natural order (W8x10 before W12x26); then longer parts first. Because numbering follows that order, adding or changing one part can shift the numbers of others. Assembly marks Every part that is not welded to another part is the main part of its own assembly. End plates are welded to their beam, and base plates to their column, so they belong to that member's assembly. Two assemblies share a mark when their main parts share a part mark and their attached parts have the same marks at the same places (within 1 mm), allowing the same turns as the main part. The assembly prefix follows the main part: a beam with two end plates is a B assembly. Plates you place with the Plate tool are not welded to anything, so each is its own P assembly. Footing marks Footings with the same length, width, depth, concrete text, bottom mat and starter bars share a mark: F1, F2 … in the order they were placed. Position and rotation do not count. Bar marks Bars with the same size, shape and dimensions A, B and C (rounded to the millimeter) share a mark across all footings. The mark is the bar number, F, and a counter per bar size: 5F1, 5F2, 6F1. A long-way bar and a short-way bar of a square footing are the same bar and share a mark; the schedule's Used for column lists both uses.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"nc1-fields","scope":"detailcraft","title":"NC file fields","summary":"What DetailCraft writes in each DSTV NC file, field by field, and how faces and coordinates are measured.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["nc1","dstv","nc file","st block","ak","bo","header","faces","coordinates","cnc","format"],"headings":[{"id":"header-st","text":"Header (ST)","level":2},{"id":"outer-contours-ak","text":"Outer contours (AK)","level":2},{"id":"holes-bo","text":"Holes (BO)","level":2},{"id":"faces-and-coordinates","text":"Faces and coordinates","level":2},{"id":"not-written","text":"Not written","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEach \u003Ccode\u003E.nc1\u003C/code\u003E file describes one part mark. Lines end with CR LF. Text fields carry plain ASCII; other characters become &quot;?&quot;. The second line is a comment, \u003Ccode\u003E** DetailCraft &lt;mark&gt;.nc1\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"header-st\"\u003EHeader (ST)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EI-shape, channel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAngle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERectangular HSS\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERound HSS\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPlate\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing, piece\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPart mark\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPhase\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe part&#039;s phase, or 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESteel quality\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe material grade\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantity\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber of parts with this mark\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe profile name, for example W16x26\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPL&lt;t&gt;*&lt;width&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProfile code\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EI or U (channel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EM\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERO\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut length\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHeight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth d\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeg\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHeight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate width\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlange width\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ebf\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeg\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDiameter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlange thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etf\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Et\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall t\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall t\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etw\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Et\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall t\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall t\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlate thickness\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet radius (0 for AISC channels)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOutside corner radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeight per meter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ekg/m\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ekg/m\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ekg/m\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ekg/m\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ekg/m²\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaint surface per meter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOutside perimeter, m²/m\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 (both faces, m²/m²)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaw angles (4 fields)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0 (square)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText fields (4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlates follow the standard&#039;s column &quot;for the sheets&quot;. The standard has no column for round tubes: DetailCraft writes the diameter as both height and flange width and the wall as both thicknesses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"outer-contours-ak\"\u003EOuter contours (AK)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI-shapes and channels:\u003C/strong\u003E the web (v), with a rectangular notch for each cope; each flange (o and u), shortened at an end where a cope is at least as deep as the flange.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangular HSS:\u003C/strong\u003E both side walls (v and h), notched for copes, and the top and bottom walls (o and u).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngles:\u003C/strong\u003E one rectangle per leg (v and u). Copes on angles are not written: such a part is listed as not written. An angle brace bolted to a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/brace-gussets\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/brace-gussets\"\u003Egusset\u003C/a\u003E has its holes on the leg that is bolted: v for the leg along the part&#039;s u, u for the leg along its v.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERound HSS:\u003C/strong\u003E one rectangle on v, the tube seen from the front (length × diameter).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlates:\u003C/strong\u003E one rectangle on face v.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach contour is closed (the first point is repeated at the end) and runs counter-clockwise.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"holes-bo\"\u003EHoles (BO)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne line per hole: face, x, y, diameter, depth 0 (through). All holes are included, the ones you typed and the ones connections made. A hole through the side walls of a rectangular HSS is written twice, once on v and once on h.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"faces-and-coordinates\"\u003EFaces and coordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach profile is placed as the standard&#039;s figure of profile positions draws it (DSTV 1998, p. 7): x along the part, Y across, Z up from the behind face, with the zero point at the outside corner. DetailCraft never mirrors a part to fit the figure.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFace\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EI-shape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChannel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAngle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERectangular HSS\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPlate\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ev\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb, front\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb, seen from the toes&#039; side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe leg along the part&#039;s u\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESide wall at −u\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe plate face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eh\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESide wall at +u\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETop flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBottom\u003C/strong\u003E flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETop wall\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eu\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBottom flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETop\u003C/strong\u003E flange\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe leg along the part&#039;s v\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBottom wall\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ex\u003C/strong\u003E runs along the part from its cut start.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI-shapes and rectangular HSS:\u003C/strong\u003E y on the web or side walls from the underside of the profile; y on a flange or the top and bottom walls from its edge on the behind side (the part&#039;s +u side).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChannels\u003C/strong\u003E lie on their back with the toes up, as in the standard&#039;s figure. So y on the web runs from the top flange&#039;s outside down, the top flange is face u and the bottom flange face o, and y on a flange runs from the back of the web toward the toe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAngles:\u003C/strong\u003E y on each leg from the heel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlates:\u003C/strong\u003E y from the plate&#039;s −u edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck how your machine software reads channel faces before cutting: some programs turn channels the other way up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-written\"\u003ENot written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBevels, inner contours, markings (SI), saw angles other than square, slotted holes, holes in round tubes (DetailCraft has none), and copes on angles and round tubes.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Each .nc1 file describes one part mark. Lines end with CR LF. Text fields carry plain ASCII; other characters become \"?\". The second line is a comment, ** DetailCraft \u003Cmark\u003E.nc1. Header (ST) Field I-shape, channel Angle Rectangular HSS Round HSS Plate Order The model name ← ← ← ← Drawing, piece Part mark ← ← ← ← Phase The part's phase, or 1 ← ← ← ← Steel quality The material grade ← ← ← ← Quantity Number of parts with this mark ← ← ← ← Profile The profile name, for example W16x26 ← ← ← PL\u003Ct\u003E*\u003Cwidth\u003E Profile code I or U (channel) L M RO B Length Cut length ← ← ← Length Height Depth d Leg Height Diameter Plate width Flange width bf Leg Width Diameter 0 Flange thickness tf t Wall t Wall t 0 Web thickness tw t Wall t Wall t Plate thickness Radius Fillet radius (0 for AISC channels) 0 Outside corner radius 0 0 Weight per meter kg/m kg/m kg/m kg/m kg/m² Paint surface per meter Outside perimeter, m²/m ← ← ← 2 (both faces, m²/m²) Saw angles (4 fields) 0 (square) ← ← ← ← Text fields (4) - ← ← ← ← Plates follow the standard's column \"for the sheets\". The standard has no column for round tubes: DetailCraft writes the diameter as both height and flange width and the wall as both thicknesses. Outer contours (AK) I-shapes and channels: the web (v), with a rectangular notch for each cope; each flange (o and u), shortened at an end where a cope is at least as deep as the flange. Rectangular HSS: both side walls (v and h), notched for copes, and the top and bottom walls (o and u). Angles: one rectangle per leg (v and u). Copes on angles are not written: such a part is listed as not written. An angle brace bolted to a gusset has its holes on the leg that is bolted: v for the leg along the part's u, u for the leg along its v. Round HSS: one rectangle on v, the tube seen from the front (length × diameter). Plates: one rectangle on face v. Each contour is closed (the first point is repeated at the end) and runs counter-clockwise. Holes (BO) One line per hole: face, x, y, diameter, depth 0 (through). All holes are included, the ones you typed and the ones connections made. A hole through the side walls of a rectangular HSS is written twice, once on v and once on h. Faces and coordinates Each profile is placed as the standard's figure of profile positions draws it (DSTV 1998, p. 7): x along the part, Y across, Z up from the behind face, with the zero point at the outside corner. DetailCraft never mirrors a part to fit the figure. Face I-shape Channel Angle Rectangular HSS Plate v Web, front Web, seen from the toes' side The leg along the part's u Side wall at −u The plate face h Side wall at +u o Top flange Bottom flange Top wall u Bottom flange Top flange The leg along the part's v Bottom wall x runs along the part from its cut start. I-shapes and rectangular HSS: y on the web or side walls from the underside of the profile; y on a flange or the top and bottom walls from its edge on the behind side (the part's +u side). Channels lie on their back with the toes up, as in the standard's figure. So y on the web runs from the top flange's outside down, the top flange is face u and the bottom flange face o, and y on a flange runs from the back of the web toward the toe. Angles: y on each leg from the heel. Plates: y from the plate's −u edge. Check how your machine software reads channel faces before cutting: some programs turn channels the other way up. Not written Bevels, inner contours, markings (SI), saw angles other than square, slotted holes, holes in round tubes (DetailCraft has none), and copes on angles and round tubes.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"rebar-reference","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Rebar reference","summary":"Bar sizes, hook dimensions, shape codes, cut lengths and the bending schedule columns DetailCraft uses.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["rebar","bar sizes","hooks","bend diameter","shape codes","cut length","bending schedule","bar list","astm a615","aci 318"],"headings":[{"id":"bar-sizes","text":"Bar sizes","level":2},{"id":"metric-bars-bs-4449","text":"Metric bars (BS 4449)","level":3},{"id":"hooks","text":"Hooks","level":2},{"id":"shapes","text":"Shapes","level":2},{"id":"cut-length","text":"Cut length","level":2},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout","level":2},{"id":"checks","text":"Checks","level":2},{"id":"bending-schedule-columns","text":"Bending schedule columns","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"bar-sizes\"\u003EBar sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom ASTM A615/A615M Table 1 (inch-pound designations). Millimeters and kg/m are exact conversions of the published inch and lb/ft values. The lists show each size as, for example, &quot;#5 (15.9 mm)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBar\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMass (kg/m)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EArea (mm²)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInside bend diameter\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E90° hook leg, out to out (mm)\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.560\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E71\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E152\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12.7\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.994\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E129\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E203\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E15.9\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.552\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E200\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E254\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#6\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E19.1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.235\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E284\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E305\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#7\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E22.2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.042\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E387\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E356\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E25.4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.973\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E510\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E406\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#9\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E28.7\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E5.060\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E645\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E487\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#10\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E32.3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6.404\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E819\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E548\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E#11\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E35.8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E7.907\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1006\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8 db\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E609\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebar\u003C/span\u003E), \u003Cstrong\u003EASTM bar names\u003C/strong\u003E can show these bars by their soft-metric A615M names instead: #3 = #10, #4 = #13, #5 = #16, #6 = #19, #7 = #22, #8 = #25, #9 = #29, #10 = #32, #11 = #36 (the same bars; ACI 315-99 Table 1 lists both).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"metric-bars-bs-4449\"\u003EMetric bars (BS 4449)\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBar\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDiameter (mm)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003Ekg/m\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMin. bend radius r (mm)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHook leg = end projection P (mm)\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.395\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E115\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH10\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E10\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.617\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E120\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.888\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E125\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH16\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.578\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E32\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E130\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.466\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E70\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E190\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH25\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E25\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.853\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E87\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E240\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH32\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E32\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6.313\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E112\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E305\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EH40\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E40\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E9.865\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E140\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E380\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe mass is the nominal section times 7850 kg/m³. The radius and end projection are the minimums of BS 8666:2020 (its table of minimum dimensions; the former diameter is 2r). Footings in BS bars get hook legs of P and cut lengths by the BS 8666 formulas below. Choosing European steel in settings starts new footings with H16 mats and H20 starter bars in C30/37 concrete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hooks\"\u003EHooks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EASTM bars: standard 90° hooks from ACI 318-19 Table 25.3.1: inside bend diameter 6 db for #3 to #8 and 8 db for #9 to #11, with a straight extension of 12 db past the bend. The hook leg, measured out to out, is half the bend diameter + db + 12 db.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a mat bar&#039;s hook does not fit in the footing depth inside the cover, DetailCraft shortens the leg to fit and lists a warning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shapes\"\u003EShapes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft stores three shapes. The bending schedule prints them as DetailCraft&#039;s codes, as ACI 315 types or as BS 8666 shape codes: choose \u003Cstrong\u003EBending schedule shape codes\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/settings\"\u003EDetailCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERebar\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECode\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShape\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ES\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStraight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA = length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne 90° bend\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA = main leg, B = hook leg, both out to out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EU\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo 90° bends on the same side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA = middle, B and C = the two legs, out to out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMat bars with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd hooks\u003C/span\u003E on are U; without, S. Starter bars with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHook at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E on are L; without, S.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetailCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBS 8666:2020\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EACI 315-99 (Fig. 10)\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ES\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E00, L = A\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno type number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E11, L = A + B − 0.5r − d\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno type number: the figure has no bar with one hook\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EU\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E21, A and C the legs, B the middle: L = A + B + C − r − 2d\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType 2, hooks A and G, straight B\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith BS 8666 or ACI 315 codes the schedule&#039;s A, B and C columns follow that standard&#039;s lettering (the middle of a U is B).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-length\"\u003ECut length\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor BS 4449 bars the cut length is the BS 8666 formula of the bar&#039;s shape code (above). For ASTM bars it is the exact length of the bar&#039;s centerline: the straight parts plus the bends as arcs at half the bend diameter plus half the bar diameter. It is not worked out from a table of bend deductions, so it can differ by a few millimeters from a schedule that uses one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"layout\"\u003ELayout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBottom mat: bars along L form the lower layer, bars along B sit on them. Each set is spread evenly across the footing inside the cover, at the spacing you give or closer, with at least two bars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStarter bars: a grid of bars along L and along B at the spacings you give, centered on the footing, standing on the mat and projecting above the top by the projection you give. Each hook points outward.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"checks\"\u003EChecks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECheck\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERule\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoom for bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECover on both sides leaves space for the bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERefused if not\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth for two layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDepth minus two covers holds both mat layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERefused if not\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarters fit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe starter grid fits inside the cover\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERefused if not\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear spacing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt least the larger of 25.4 mm and db (ACI 318-19 25.2.1; aggregate size not checked)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHook depth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHook leg fits in the depth inside the cover\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWarning, leg shortened\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarter hooks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHooks stay out of the cover\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bending-schedule-columns\"\u003EBending schedule columns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending schedule\u003C/span\u003E tab and its CSV have these columns: Bar mark, Size, Shape, A (mm), B (mm), C (mm), Count, Cut length (mm), Total length (m), kg/m, Weight each (kg), Total weight (kg), Used for. The last row totals the count, length and weight.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Bar sizes From ASTM A615/A615M Table 1 (inch-pound designations). Millimeters and kg/m are exact conversions of the published inch and lb/ft values. The lists show each size as, for example, \"#5 (15.9 mm)\". Bar Diameter (mm) Mass (kg/m) Area (mm²) Inside bend diameter 90° hook leg, out to out (mm) #3 9.5 0.560 71 6 db 152 #4 12.7 0.994 129 6 db 203 #5 15.9 1.552 200 6 db 254 #6 19.1 2.235 284 6 db 305 #7 22.2 3.042 387 6 db 356 #8 25.4 3.973 510 6 db 406 #9 28.7 5.060 645 8 db 487 #10 32.3 6.404 819 8 db 548 #11 35.8 7.907 1006 8 db 609 In DetailCraft settings (Rebar), ASTM bar names can show these bars by their soft-metric A615M names instead: #3 = #10, #4 = #13, #5 = #16, #6 = #19, #7 = #22, #8 = #25, #9 = #29, #10 = #32, #11 = #36 (the same bars; ACI 315-99 Table 1 lists both). Metric bars (BS 4449) Bar Diameter (mm) kg/m Min. bend radius r (mm) Hook leg = end projection P (mm) H8 8 0.395 16 115 H10 10 0.617 20 120 H12 12 0.888 24 125 H16 16 1.578 32 130 H20 20 2.466 70 190 H25 25 3.853 87 240 H32 32 6.313 112 305 H40 40 9.865 140 380 The mass is the nominal section times 7850 kg/m³. The radius and end projection are the minimums of BS 8666:2020 (its table of minimum dimensions; the former diameter is 2r). Footings in BS bars get hook legs of P and cut lengths by the BS 8666 formulas below. Choosing European steel in settings starts new footings with H16 mats and H20 starter bars in C30/37 concrete. Hooks ASTM bars: standard 90° hooks from ACI 318-19 Table 25.3.1: inside bend diameter 6 db for #3 to #8 and 8 db for #9 to #11, with a straight extension of 12 db past the bend. The hook leg, measured out to out, is half the bend diameter + db + 12 db. If a mat bar's hook does not fit in the footing depth inside the cover, DetailCraft shortens the leg to fit and lists a warning. Shapes DetailCraft stores three shapes. The bending schedule prints them as DetailCraft's codes, as ACI 315 types or as BS 8666 shape codes: choose Bending schedule shape codes in DetailCraft settings (Rebar). Code Shape Dimensions S Straight A = length L One 90° bend A = main leg, B = hook leg, both out to out U Two 90° bends on the same side A = middle, B and C = the two legs, out to out Mat bars with End hooks on are U; without, S. Starter bars with Hook at the bottom on are L; without, S. DetailCraft BS 8666:2020 ACI 315-99 (Fig. 10) S 00, L = A no type number L 11, L = A + B − 0.5r − d no type number: the figure has no bar with one hook U 21, A and C the legs, B the middle: L = A + B + C − r − 2d Type 2, hooks A and G, straight B With BS 8666 or ACI 315 codes the schedule's A, B and C columns follow that standard's lettering (the middle of a U is B). Cut length For BS 4449 bars the cut length is the BS 8666 formula of the bar's shape code (above). For ASTM bars it is the exact length of the bar's centerline: the straight parts plus the bends as arcs at half the bend diameter plus half the bar diameter. It is not worked out from a table of bend deductions, so it can differ by a few millimeters from a schedule that uses one. Layout Bottom mat: bars along L form the lower layer, bars along B sit on them. Each set is spread evenly across the footing inside the cover, at the spacing you give or closer, with at least two bars. Starter bars: a grid of bars along L and along B at the spacings you give, centered on the footing, standing on the mat and projecting above the top by the projection you give. Each hook points outward. Checks Check Rule Result Room for bars Cover on both sides leaves space for the bars Refused if not Depth for two layers Depth minus two covers holds both mat layers Refused if not Starters fit The starter grid fits inside the cover Refused if not Clear spacing At least the larger of 25.4 mm and db (ACI 318-19 25.2.1; aggregate size not checked) Warning Hook depth Hook leg fits in the depth inside the cover Warning, leg shortened Starter hooks Hooks stay out of the cover Warning Bending schedule columns The Bending schedule tab and its CSV have these columns: Bar mark, Size, Shape, A (mm), B (mm), C (mm), Count, Cut length (mm), Total length (m), kg/m, Weight each (kg), Total weight (kg), Used for. The last row totals the count, length and weight.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"detailcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"What DetailCraft's messages mean and what to do, from a missing 3D view to connections that will not build.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","error","not working","help","fix","message","refused","webgl","blank","warning","cannot","issue"],"headings":[{"id":"the-3d-view-is-missing","text":"The 3D view is missing","level":2},{"id":"clicking-in-the-view-does-nothing","text":"Clicking in the view does nothing","level":2},{"id":"placing-messages","text":"Placing messages","level":2},{"id":"connections-will-not-build","text":"Connections will not build","level":2},{"id":"this-beam-does-not-end-on-a-column-line","text":"\"This beam does not end on a column line.\"","level":3},{"id":"this-beam-meets-a-column-web-or-at-an-angle","text":"\"This beam meets a column web or at an angle\"","level":3},{"id":"end-plates-are-built-for-level-beams-only-this-beam-slopes","text":"\"End plates are built for level beams only (this beam slopes).\"","level":3},{"id":"end-plates-need-the-beam-upright-rotation-0","text":"\"End plates need the beam upright (rotation 0).\"","level":3},{"id":"end-plates-need-an-i-shape-beam-end-plates-need-an-i-shape-column","text":"\"End plates need an I-shape beam.\" / \"End plates need an I-shape column.\"","level":3},{"id":"the-beam-end-is-not-on-the-column-line-any-more","text":"\"The beam end is not on the column line any more.\"","level":3},{"id":"connections-cut-this-member-to-nothing","text":"\"Connections cut this member to nothing.\"","level":3},{"id":"no-other-joints-like-this-one-are-without-a-connection","text":"\"No other joints like this one … are without a connection.\"","level":3},{"id":"both-ends-already-have-connections-this-column-already-has-a-base-plate","text":"\"Both ends already have connections.\" / \"This column already has a base plate.\"","level":3},{"id":"warnings-in-the-problems-tab","text":"Warnings in the Problems tab","level":2},{"id":"footing-changes-are-refused","text":"Footing changes are refused","level":2},{"id":"grids-and-levels-are-refused","text":"Grids and levels are refused","level":2},{"id":"opening-a-file-fails","text":"Opening a file fails","level":2},{"id":"exports","text":"Exports","level":2},{"id":"downloads-do-not-appear","text":"Downloads do not appear","level":2},{"id":"keys-do-nothing","text":"Keys do nothing","level":2},{"id":"offline-problems","text":"Offline problems","level":2},{"id":"unsaved-work","text":"Unsaved work","level":2},{"id":"still-stuck","text":"Still stuck","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMost refusals are explained in the status bar at the bottom of the screen, or in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E tab. Read those first. The messages below are quoted as DetailCraft shows them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-3d-view-is-missing\"\u003EThe 3D view is missing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This browser could not start 3D graphics (WebGL). The model, reports and exports still work.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe browser could not start WebGL, the 3D graphics DetailCraft draws with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUpdate the browser to its latest version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on hardware acceleration and restart the browser (see below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUpdate your graphics driver, or try another browser. Remote desktops and virtual machines often have no WebGL.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMeanwhile you can still work: place members with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E form, read the reports, and export. Only clicking in the view needs WebGL.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo turn on hardware acceleration, open \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings, System\u003C/strong\u003E and turn on \u003Cstrong\u003EUse graphics acceleration when available\u003C/strong\u003E, then relaunch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo turn on hardware acceleration, open \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings, General, Performance\u003C/strong\u003E, clear \u003Cstrong\u003EUse recommended performance settings\u003C/strong\u003E, and check \u003Cstrong\u003EUse hardware acceleration when available\u003C/strong\u003E. Restart Firefox.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari has WebGL on by default. Update macOS if the view still does not start, and check that no extension blocks it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMore in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clicking-in-the-view-does-nothing\"\u003EClicking in the view does nothing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo intersection near the click.\u003C/strong\u003E Placing tools snap to grid intersections only. A click away from every dot does nothing. Move closer until the dot lights up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe tool is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E and you clicked empty space.\u003C/strong\u003E That clears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou dragged.\u003C/strong\u003E A press and release more than a few pixels apart is a drag: it turns or pans the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe part is cut away.\u003C/strong\u003E In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E, steel outside the cut is hidden and cannot be clicked. Switch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe intersection is at the wrong level.\u003C/strong\u003E In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, intersections at several levels can overlap on screen; the click takes the nearest one. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElevation\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-messages\"\u003EPlacing messages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is no level above Level 2 for the column to reach. Add a level first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd a level in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELevels\u003C/span\u003E, or click a lower level\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick a different intersection for the other end.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou clicked the same point twice\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe two ends are the same point.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E, the &quot;From&quot; and &quot;To&quot; picks are the same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd grid lines on the left to place members.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model has no grid lines in one direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnknown profile &quot;…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe profile is not in the catalog or the model&#039;s custom profiles. Pick one from the list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA number field was left empty or has text in it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connections-will-not-build\"\u003EConnections will not build\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"this-beam-does-not-end-on-a-column-line\"\u003E&quot;This beam does not end on a column line.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEnd plates need the beam&#039;s end exactly on a vertical column&#039;s line, between its bottom and top (within 1 mm). Check the beam&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnds (x, y, z mm)\u003C/span\u003E and the column&#039;s position.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"this-beam-meets-a-column-web-or-at-an-angle\"\u003E&quot;This beam meets a column web or at an angle&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E: &quot;Beams framing into a column web are not built yet: turn the column 90° or use another connection.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe beam frames into the column web. Select the column and set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E to 90, then try again. Skewed joints are not built.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"end-plates-are-built-for-level-beams-only-this-beam-slopes\"\u003E&quot;End plates are built for level beams only (this beam slopes).&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth ends of the beam must be at the same height.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"end-plates-need-the-beam-upright-rotation-0\"\u003E&quot;End plates need the beam upright (rotation 0).&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet the beam&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation (°)\u003C/span\u003E back to 0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"end-plates-need-an-i-shape-beam-end-plates-need-an-i-shape-column\"\u003E&quot;End plates need an I-shape beam.&quot; / &quot;End plates need an I-shape column.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly I-shapes (W, IPE, HEA, HEB or a custom I) take end plates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-beam-end-is-not-on-the-column-line-any-more\"\u003E&quot;The beam end is not on the column line any more.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou moved the beam or the column after adding the connection. Move it back, or delete the connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"connections-cut-this-member-to-nothing\"\u003E&quot;Connections cut this member to nothing.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe connections at both ends cut the member back further than its length, for example a very short beam between two deep columns. Check the member&#039;s ends and the plate thickness.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"no-other-joints-like-this-one-are-without-a-connection\"\u003E&quot;No other joints like this one … are without a connection.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESimilar\u003C/span\u003E copies only to joints with the same beam profile, column profile and kind of connection, beam into a flange, that do not have a connection yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"both-ends-already-have-connections-this-column-already-has-a-base-plate\"\u003E&quot;Both ends already have connections.&quot; / &quot;This column already has a base plate.&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the existing connection to change it, or delete it first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"warnings-in-the-problems-tab\"\u003EWarnings in the Problems tab\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWarnings do not stop a connection or footing from building; they ask you to check it. The edge and spacing ones use the EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3 minimums (edge 1.2 × hole, pitch 2.2 × hole, gauge 2.4 × hole). If your project uses other rules, the warning may not apply; DetailCraft does not design connections.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;a hole is off the end of the part&quot; means a hole&#039;s x is less than 0 or more than the cut length. Remember x is from the cut start, after connections cut the part back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"footing-changes-are-refused\"\u003EFooting changes are refused\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe cover leaves no room for bars in this footing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake the footing bigger or the cover smaller\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe footing is too shallow for two layers of bars inside the cover.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake it deeper or reduce the cover\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe starter bars do not fit inside the footing with 75 mm cover.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFewer starters, closer spacing, or a bigger footing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBar spacing must be 50–1000 mm.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a spacing in that range\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength must be 300–50000 mm.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFooting sizes have limits: length and width 300 to 50000, depth 150 to 10000\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grids-and-levels-are-refused\"\u003EGrids and levels are refused\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumbered grids: give one spacing between each pair of lines (4).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount your gaps: five lines need four spacings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELettered grids: spacings must be positive millimeters.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo zeros, negatives or text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumbered grids: grid labels must be different.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lines have the same label\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt most 60 grid lines each way.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplit the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep at least one level.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou removed every level row\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery level needs a name. / Two levels are called &quot;…&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive each level a different name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-a-file-fails\"\u003EOpening a file fails\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetailCraft opens its own models (.detailcraft.json). IFC import is not built yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not a \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis is not a DetailCraft model (not JSON).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or is some other JSON\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis is not a DetailCraft model.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA JSON file from another program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis model was saved by a newer DetailCraft (format 2).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in the newer DetailCraft that saved it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat file is too big to be a DetailCraft model.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOver 50 MB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a file opens with warnings such as &quot;2 members were not valid and left out.&quot;, the rest of the model is fine. Do not save over the original until you have checked what was left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"exports\"\u003EExports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing to export yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC\u003C/span\u003E with an empty model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo I-shape or plate parts to write NC files for.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly channels, angles or tubes in the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot written (only I-shapes and plates so far): …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThose marks are not I-shapes or plates; the rest were written\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo footings, so no bars to schedule.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending\u003C/span\u003E with no footings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E… character(s) outside the PDF font printed as &quot;?&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model name or a grade has characters the PDF font lacks\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShop drawing\u003C/span\u003E grayed out: the model has no parts yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"downloads-do-not-appear\"\u003EDownloads do not appear\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery save and export is a browser download. Check your browser&#039;s downloads list. If the browser asks before downloading several files, or blocks downloads from this site, allow them. In Safari, check the downloads button in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-do-nothing\"\u003EKeys do nothing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe cursor is in a text or number field: tool keys and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E are ignored there so you can type. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dialog is open: close it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser has the keyboard (for example the address bar): click inside DetailCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E uses Control even on a Mac.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offline-problems\"\u003EOffline problems\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E button in the app bar shows what state the offline copy is in. How saving works: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe button reads\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo offline copy in this browser yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose it while online\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving 5 of 20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving is under way\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E; keep the tab open\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetailCraft, its 3D view, every export and this help work with no connection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing. Click it for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome files did not download\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck your connection and click it to try again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis window cannot keep an offline copy: private windows and some managed computers block it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a normal window, or work online\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you are offline and the button does not read \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, do not reload or close the tab: what has loaded keeps working. Save your model with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and wait until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unsaved-work\"\u003EUnsaved work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the browser asks whether to leave the page, DetailCraft has unsaved changes. Choose to stay, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. DetailCraft cannot recover a model after the tab is closed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-stuck\"\u003EStill stuck\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENote what you did and the exact message, and tell us through the \u003Ca href=\"https://getcadcraft.com/support\" class=\"hx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003Esupport page\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Most refusals are explained in the status bar at the bottom of the screen, or in the Problems tab. Read those first. The messages below are quoted as DetailCraft shows them. The 3D view is missing \"This browser could not start 3D graphics (WebGL). The model, reports and exports still work.\" The browser could not start WebGL, the 3D graphics DetailCraft draws with. Update the browser to its latest version. Turn on hardware acceleration and restart the browser (see below). Update your graphics driver, or try another browser. Remote desktops and virtual machines often have no WebGL. Meanwhile you can still work: place members with the Add by grid form, read the reports, and export. Only clicking in the view needs WebGL. To turn on hardware acceleration, open Settings, System and turn on Use graphics acceleration when available, then relaunch. To turn on hardware acceleration, open Settings, General, Performance, clear Use recommended performance settings, and check Use hardware acceleration when available. Restart Firefox. Safari has WebGL on by default. Update macOS if the view still does not start, and check that no extension blocks it. More in Browsers and computers. Clicking in the view does nothing No intersection near the click. Placing tools snap to grid intersections only. A click away from every dot does nothing. Move closer until the dot lights up. The tool is Select and you clicked empty space. That clears the selection. You dragged. A press and release more than a few pixels apart is a drag: it turns or pans the view. The part is cut away. In Plan and Elevation, steel outside the cut is hidden and cannot be clicked. Switch to 3D. The intersection is at the wrong level. In 3D, intersections at several levels can overlap on screen; the click takes the nearest one. Use Plan or Elevation. Placing messages Message What to do There is no level above Level 2 for the column to reach. Add a level first. Add a level in Levels, or click a lower level Pick a different intersection for the other end. You clicked the same point twice The two ends are the same point. In Add by grid, the \"From\" and \"To\" picks are the same Add grid lines on the left to place members. The model has no grid lines in one direction Unknown profile \"…\" The profile is not in the catalog or the model's custom profiles. Pick one from the list Type a number. A number field was left empty or has text in it Connections will not build \"This beam does not end on a column line.\" End plates need the beam's end exactly on a vertical column's line, between its bottom and top (within 1 mm). Check the beam's Ends (x, y, z mm) and the column's position. \"This beam meets a column web or at an angle\" Or in Problems: \"Beams framing into a column web are not built yet: turn the column 90° or use another connection.\" The beam frames into the column web. Select the column and set Rotation (°) to 90, then try again. Skewed joints are not built. \"End plates are built for level beams only (this beam slopes).\" Both ends of the beam must be at the same height. \"End plates need the beam upright (rotation 0).\" Set the beam's Rotation (°) back to 0. \"End plates need an I-shape beam.\" / \"End plates need an I-shape column.\" Only I-shapes (W, IPE, HEA, HEB or a custom I) take end plates. \"The beam end is not on the column line any more.\" You moved the beam or the column after adding the connection. Move it back, or delete the connection. \"Connections cut this member to nothing.\" The connections at both ends cut the member back further than its length, for example a very short beam between two deep columns. Check the member's ends and the plate thickness. \"No other joints like this one … are without a connection.\" Similar copies only to joints with the same beam profile, column profile and kind of connection, beam into a flange, that do not have a connection yet. \"Both ends already have connections.\" / \"This column already has a base plate.\" Select the existing connection to change it, or delete it first. Warnings in the Problems tab Warnings do not stop a connection or footing from building; they ask you to check it. The edge and spacing ones use the EN 1993-1-8 Table 3.3 minimums (edge 1.2 × hole, pitch 2.2 × hole, gauge 2.4 × hole). If your project uses other rules, the warning may not apply; DetailCraft does not design connections. \"a hole is off the end of the part\" means a hole's x is less than 0 or more than the cut length. Remember x is from the cut start, after connections cut the part back. Footing changes are refused Message What to do The cover leaves no room for bars in this footing. Make the footing bigger or the cover smaller The footing is too shallow for two layers of bars inside the cover. Make it deeper or reduce the cover The starter bars do not fit inside the footing with 75 mm cover. Fewer starters, closer spacing, or a bigger footing Bar spacing must be 50–1000 mm. Type a spacing in that range Length must be 300–50000 mm. Footing sizes have limits: length and width 300 to 50000, depth 150 to 10000 Grids and levels are refused Message What to do Numbered grids: give one spacing between each pair of lines (4). Count your gaps: five lines need four spacings Lettered grids: spacings must be positive millimeters. No zeros, negatives or text Numbered grids: grid labels must be different. Two lines have the same label At most 60 grid lines each way. Split the model Keep at least one level. You removed every level row Every level needs a name. / Two levels are called \"…\". Give each level a different name Opening a file fails Message Cause DetailCraft opens its own models (.detailcraft.json). IFC import is not built yet. The file is not a .json file This is not a DetailCraft model (not JSON). The file is damaged or is some other JSON This is not a DetailCraft model. A JSON file from another program This model was saved by a newer DetailCraft (format 2). Open it in the newer DetailCraft that saved it That file is too big to be a DetailCraft model. Over 50 MB When a file opens with warnings such as \"2 members were not valid and left out.\", the rest of the model is fine. Do not save over the original until you have checked what was left out. Exports Message Cause Nothing to export yet. IFC with an empty model No I-shape or plate parts to write NC files for. Only channels, angles or tubes in the model Not written (only I-shapes and plates so far): … Those marks are not I-shapes or plates; the rest were written No footings, so no bars to schedule. Bending with no footings … character(s) outside the PDF font printed as \"?\". The model name or a grade has characters the PDF font lacks Shop drawing grayed out: the model has no parts yet. Downloads do not appear Every save and export is a browser download. Check your browser's downloads list. If the browser asks before downloading several files, or blocks downloads from this site, allow them. In Safari, check the downloads button in the toolbar. Keys do nothing The cursor is in a text or number field: tool keys and Delete are ignored there so you can type. Press Tab or click the view. A dialog is open: close it first. The browser has the keyboard (for example the address bar): click inside DetailCraft. Ctrl+Shift+F uses Control even on a Mac. Offline problems The Save for offline button in the app bar shows what state the offline copy is in. How saving works: Working offline. The button reads What it means What to do Save for offline No offline copy in this browser yet Choose it while online Saving 5 of 20 Saving is under way Wait for Offline ready; keep the tab open Offline ready DetailCraft, its 3D view, every export and this help work with no connection Nothing. Click it for Save again (get the newest version) or Remove the offline copy Could not save Some files did not download Check your connection and click it to try again Offline not available This window cannot keep an offline copy: private windows and some managed computers block it Use a normal window, or work online If you are offline and the button does not read Offline ready, do not reload or close the tab: what has loaded keeps working. Save your model with Ctrl+S and wait until you are online again. Unsaved work If the browser asks whether to leave the page, DetailCraft has unsaved changes. Choose to stay, then press Ctrl+S. DetailCraft cannot recover a model after the tab is closed. Still stuck Note what you did and the exact message, and tell us through the support page.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"detailcraft","title":"What is not built yet","summary":"What this early DetailCraft build cannot do, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["limits","limitations","missing","roadmap","not supported","coming","planned","workaround","todo"],"headings":[{"id":"modeling","text":"Modeling","level":2},{"id":"stairs","text":"Stairs","level":2},{"id":"connections","text":"Connections","level":2},{"id":"analysis","text":"Analysis","level":2},{"id":"rebar","text":"Rebar","level":2},{"id":"drawings-and-exports","text":"Drawings and exports","level":2},{"id":"panels","text":"Panels","level":2},{"id":"working-together-and-saving","text":"Working together and saving","level":2},{"id":"platforms","text":"Platforms","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you can plan around it. Nothing here is a promise of a date.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modeling\"\u003EModeling\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDragging one end of a member (stretching it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnds (x, y, z mm)\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA select filter (only beams, only one profile)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a window, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click to take parts out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArray a selection; rotating about a sloped axis; mirroring in a sloped plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste it at an offset, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E it; rotate and mirror work in plan (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/select-and-change\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/select-and-change\"\u003ESelect by window, move, rotate, mirror, change many\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints off the grid while clicking\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace on the grid, then type the end coordinates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElevations along numbered grid lines; saved views; work planes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd by grid\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHoles drawn as real cuts in the 3D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHoles show as dark plugs; they are real in every export\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERounded or angled copes, other cuts and fittings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare copes on I-shapes only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHoles you add to angles and round tubes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngle holes come from \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/brace-gussets\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/brace-gussets\"\u003Ebrace gussets\u003C/a\u003E only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImperial input\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType millimeters (25.4 per inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing or removing a custom profile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd a corrected profile under a new name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stairs\"\u003EStairs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHandrails, landings, toe plates, grating treads, spiral and dog-leg stairs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStraight flights only; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/stairs\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/stairs\"\u003EStairs\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStringer end cuts against the floors\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare-cut stringers; cut them in your shop software\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connections\"\u003EConnections\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDetailCraft builds six connections: a brace gusset (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/brace-gussets\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/brace-gussets\"\u003EBrace gussets\u003C/a\u003E); a bolted splice between two pieces of one I-shape (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/splices\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/splices\"\u003ESplices\u003C/a\u003E); a mitre between two I-shapes meeting at an angle, cut only or with bolted end plates (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/mitres\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/mitres\"\u003EMitre joints\u003C/a\u003E); a flush or extended bolted end plate from a level, upright I-shape beam to an I-shape column flange or web; a shear tab from a beam (square, skewed or sloped) to a column flange or web; and a column base plate with four anchor rods.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESkewed or sloped end plates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/shear-tabs\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/shear-tabs\"\u003Eshear tabs\u003C/a\u003E, which take skewed and sloped beams\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClip angles; splice fill plates and splices between different sizes; corner gussets welded to beam and column, chamfered gussets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDetail them in other software, or add plates and holes by hand as loose parts; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/splices\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/splices\"\u003ESplices\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/brace-gussets\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/brace-gussets\"\u003EBrace gussets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChamfered or tapered base stiffeners; grout holes; the footing pocket for a shear lug\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERectangular stiffeners; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/base-plates\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/base-plates\"\u003EBase plates\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHaunches; welded mitres with a butt weld; stiffeners in line with the other member&#039;s flanges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/mitres\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/mitres\"\u003Emitre\u003C/a\u003E with end plates and web stiffeners, or a plain mitre cut; add other plates by hand as loose parts\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConnection design checks (strength)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck connections with your engineer&#039;s calculations; DetailCraft only warns about edge distance and spacing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlotted holes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound holes only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"analysis\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign checks beyond steel I-shapes: tubes, angles, channels, welded shapes, slender or class 4 parts, torsion, connections; concrete: sway slenderness, torsion, seismic detailing, slabs and walls\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnalysis\u003C/span\u003E tab checks steel I-shapes to AISC 360 or EN 1993-1-1 (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/design-checks\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/design-checks\"\u003ECheck steel members to a design code\u003C/a\u003E); the rest it leaves marked &quot;not checked&quot;; an engineer reviews it all\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuckling analysis, vibration\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESecond order is P-Delta only (elastic); no buckling analysis (modal, response spectrum, time history and pushover are in the Analysis tab)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign checks that an engineer has reviewed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are an aid until then: check two or three members by hand or in your engineer&#039;s own software before you rely on them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFloor, wind and snow loads made for you from areas\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut loads on members by hand, in kN/m or kN\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMember offsets, eccentric joints, tension-only braces, plates and footings in the frame\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMembers are analysed on their work lines; braces carry tension and compression\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rebar\"\u003ERebar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERound columns, spirals, sloped or curved beams, slabs other than rectangles, walls that are not straight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERectangular beams, columns, slabs and walls (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/reinforced-concrete\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/reinforced-concrete\"\u003EDetail reinforced concrete\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBars cranked into a lap, staggered laps, laps placed where you choose\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELaps at equal steps, every bar lapped at one section (ACI Class B, EN α6 = 1.5)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECorner bars at openings, punching shear reinforcement, seismic detailing (ACI 318-19 chapter 18)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrimmers each side of an opening; check the rest yourself\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReading a structural design program&#039;s own files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport IFC from it: its IfcReinforcingBar come in as bars (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/reinforced-concrete#bars-from-an-ifc-file\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/reinforced-concrete\" data-hash=\"bars-from-an-ifc-file\"\u003EBars from an IFC file\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShape codes beyond straight, one bend and a U; ACI 315 types for straight and one-hook bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe three shapes print as BS 8666 00, 11, 21 or ACI Type 2; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/rebar-reference\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/rebar-reference\"\u003ERebar reference\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMetric bars other than BS 4449 H8–H40 (e.g. CSA 10M–35M)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EASTM soft-metric names #10–#36, or BS bars\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-and-exports\"\u003EDrawings and exports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPortrait sheets, A0, and sheets of your own size; general-arrangement views on skewed grids\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELandscape A4 to A1 and ANSI B to D (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/drawings\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/drawings\"\u003EGeneral-arrangement and assembly drawings\u003C/a\u003E); scale the PDF to print on another size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeld symbols on single-part shop drawings; symbols other than fillets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssembly drawings carry fillet weld symbols; the shop drawing gives the weld as text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing a drawing in the app\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the model and make the drawing again; mark up the PDF in a PDF app such as MarkupCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawings as DWG with dimension objects, or the model at full scale in model space with the sheets as layouts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach sheet is a full-size DWG or DXF of the paper, dimensions as lines and figures (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/drawings#download\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/drawings\" data-hash=\"download\"\u003EGeneral-arrangement and assembly drawings\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENC holes in round tubes, copes on angles and round tubes in NC files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot written; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/nc1-fields\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/nc1-fields\"\u003ENC file fields\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBevels, markings and inner contours in NC files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENone today\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBolt holes, bolts, welds and footings in the USD export\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are in the IFC, NC and BOM outputs; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/usd\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/usd\"\u003EExport the steel model as USD\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBringing connections, footings, slotted or rectangular holes and holes in round tubes in from an IFC file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport IFC\u003C/span\u003E brings in beams, columns, members, plates, grids, levels, round holes, copes, bolts and reinforcing bars, and its report lists what it left out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECustom report layouts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe three CSV files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panels\"\u003EPanels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving the reports at the bottom (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProblems\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBending schedule\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panels move. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"working-together-and-saving\"\u003EWorking together and saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave \u003Ccode\u003E.detailcraft.json\u003C/code\u003E files and share them like any file. Cloud saving is not available yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther people&#039;s cursors and &quot;is editing&quot; labels in a Live TeamWork session; grids, levels and phases merged item by item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdits still merge (grids, levels and phases go whole, the later change wins); see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/detailcraft/work-live\" data-topic=\"detailcraft/work-live\"\u003EDetail one structure together, live\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInvite links to a Live TeamWork session over the internet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoin with a short code on the same network\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"platforms\"\u003EPlatforms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDetailCraft is made for a desktop, laptop or tablet screen. It works on a phone too: the model keeps the screen and the panels and reports open as sheets from a bar at the bottom, but there is little room for the panels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOffline use needs DetailCraft saved for offline use first, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar; once it reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, everything in this build works with no connection. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf one of these gaps blocks your work, tell us through the \u003Ca href=\"https://getcadcraft.com/support\" class=\"hx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\u003Esupport page\u003C/a\u003E; it helps us decide what to build next.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"DetailCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you can plan around it. Nothing here is a promise of a date. Modeling Not yet Instead, today Dragging one end of a member (stretching it) Edit Start or End under Ends (x, y, z mm) in the Properties panel A select filter (only beams, only one profile) Drag a window, then Shift+click to take parts out Array a selection; rotating about a sloped axis; mirroring in a sloped plane Copy and paste it at an offset, or Duplicate it; rotate and mirror work in plan (Select by window, move, rotate, mirror, change many) Points off the grid while clicking Place on the grid, then type the end coordinates Elevations along numbered grid lines; saved views; work planes Use 3D, or Add by grid Holes drawn as real cuts in the 3D view Holes show as dark plugs; they are real in every export Rounded or angled copes, other cuts and fittings Square copes on I-shapes only Holes you add to angles and round tubes Angle holes come from brace gussets only Imperial input Type millimeters (25.4 per inch) Editing or removing a custom profile Add a corrected profile under a new name Stairs Not yet Instead, today Handrails, landings, toe plates, grating treads, spiral and dog-leg stairs Straight flights only; see Stairs Stringer end cuts against the floors Square-cut stringers; cut them in your shop software Connections DetailCraft builds six connections: a brace gusset (see Brace gussets); a bolted splice between two pieces of one I-shape (see Splices); a mitre between two I-shapes meeting at an angle, cut only or with bolted end plates (see Mitre joints); a flush or extended bolted end plate from a level, upright I-shape beam to an I-shape column flange or web; a shear tab from a beam (square, skewed or sloped) to a column flange or web; and a column base plate with four anchor rods. Not yet Instead, today Skewed or sloped end plates Use shear tabs, which take skewed and sloped beams Clip angles; splice fill plates and splices between different sizes; corner gussets welded to beam and column, chamfered gussets Detail them in other software, or add plates and holes by hand as loose parts; see Splices, Brace gussets Chamfered or tapered base stiffeners; grout holes; the footing pocket for a shear lug Rectangular stiffeners; see Base plates Haunches; welded mitres with a butt weld; stiffeners in line with the other member's flanges A mitre with end plates and web stiffeners, or a plain mitre cut; add other plates by hand as loose parts Connection design checks (strength) Check connections with your engineer's calculations; DetailCraft only warns about edge distance and spacing Slotted holes Round holes only Analysis Not yet Instead, today Design checks beyond steel I-shapes: tubes, angles, channels, welded shapes, slender or class 4 parts, torsion, connections; concrete: sway slenderness, torsion, seismic detailing, slabs and walls The Analysis tab checks steel I-shapes to AISC 360 or EN 1993-1-1 (Check steel members to a design code); the rest it leaves marked \"not checked\"; an engineer reviews it all Buckling analysis, vibration Second order is P-Delta only (elastic); no buckling analysis (modal, response spectrum, time history and pushover are in the Analysis tab) Design checks that an engineer has reviewed They are an aid until then: check two or three members by hand or in your engineer's own software before you rely on them Floor, wind and snow loads made for you from areas Put loads on members by hand, in kN/m or kN Member offsets, eccentric joints, tension-only braces, plates and footings in the frame Members are analysed on their work lines; braces carry tension and compression Rebar Not yet Instead, today Round columns, spirals, sloped or curved beams, slabs other than rectangles, walls that are not straight Rectangular beams, columns, slabs and walls (Detail reinforced concrete) Bars cranked into a lap, staggered laps, laps placed where you choose Laps at equal steps, every bar lapped at one section (ACI Class B, EN α6 = 1.5) Corner bars at openings, punching shear reinforcement, seismic detailing (ACI 318-19 chapter 18) Trimmers each side of an opening; check the rest yourself Reading a structural design program's own files Export IFC from it: its IfcReinforcingBar come in as bars (Bars from an IFC file) Shape codes beyond straight, one bend and a U; ACI 315 types for straight and one-hook bars The three shapes print as BS 8666 00, 11, 21 or ACI Type 2; see Rebar reference Metric bars other than BS 4449 H8–H40 (e.g. CSA 10M–35M) ASTM soft-metric names #10–#36, or BS bars Drawings and exports Not yet Instead, today Portrait sheets, A0, and sheets of your own size; general-arrangement views on skewed grids Landscape A4 to A1 and ANSI B to D (General-arrangement and assembly drawings); scale the PDF to print on another size Weld symbols on single-part shop drawings; symbols other than fillets Assembly drawings carry fillet weld symbols; the shop drawing gives the weld as text Editing a drawing in the app Change the model and make the drawing again; mark up the PDF in a PDF app such as MarkupCraft Drawings as DWG with dimension objects, or the model at full scale in model space with the sheets as layouts Each sheet is a full-size DWG or DXF of the paper, dimensions as lines and figures (General-arrangement and assembly drawings) NC holes in round tubes, copes on angles and round tubes in NC files Not written; see NC file fields Bevels, markings and inner contours in NC files None today Bolt holes, bolts, welds and footings in the USD export They are in the IFC, NC and BOM outputs; see Export the steel model as USD Bringing connections, footings, slotted or rectangular holes and holes in round tubes in from an IFC file Import IFC brings in beams, columns, members, plates, grids, levels, round holes, copes, bolts and reinforcing bars, and its report lists what it left out Custom report layouts The three CSV files Panels Not yet Instead, today Moving the reports at the bottom (Problems, Bill of materials, Bending schedule) Only the Model and Properties panels move. See Arrange the panels Working together and saving Not yet Instead, today Cloud saving and sync between computers Save .detailcraft.json files and share them like any file. Cloud saving is not available yet Other people's cursors and \"is editing\" labels in a Live TeamWork session; grids, levels and phases merged item by item Edits still merge (grids, levels and phases go whole, the later change wins); see Detail one structure together, live Invite links to a Live TeamWork session over the internet Join with a short code on the same network Platforms DetailCraft is made for a desktop, laptop or tablet screen. It works on a phone too: the model keeps the screen and the panels and reports open as sheets from a bar at the bottom, but there is little room for the panels. Offline use needs DetailCraft saved for offline use first, with Save for offline in the app bar; once it reads Offline ready, everything in this build works with no connection. See Working offline. If one of these gaps blocks your work, tell us through the support page; it helps us decide what to build next.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Diese Hilfe benutzen","summary":"Die Hilfe öffnen, durchsuchen und die Schritte für Ihren Rechner sehen.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["Hilfe","f1","Suche","Tutorials","Handbuch"],"headings":[{"id":"die-hilfe-ffnen","text":"Die Hilfe öffnen","level":2},{"id":"ein-thema-finden","text":"Ein Thema finden","level":2},{"id":"schritte-f-r-ihren-rechner","text":"Schritte für Ihren Rechner","level":2},{"id":"dieselbe-hilfe-auf-der-website","text":"Dieselbe Hilfe auf der Website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EJede App bringt ihre eigene Hilfe mit. Sie ist Teil der App, öffnet sich also ohne Verbindung und beschreibt immer die Version, die Sie gerade benutzen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-hilfe-ffnen\"\u003EDie Hilfe öffnen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drücken oder oben in der App-Leiste \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHilfe\u003C/span\u003E wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drücken oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchließen\u003C/span\u003E wählen, um sie wegzulegen. Ihre Zeichnung bleibt, wie sie war.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAuf den meisten Mac-Tastaturen steuert die obere Reihe Helligkeit und Lautstärke. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zusammen mit der Taste \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (oder der Globus-Taste) drücken oder die Schaltfläche \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHilfe\u003C/span\u003E benutzen. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003ETasten auf einem Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ein-thema-finden\"\u003EEin Thema finden\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIm Suchfeld oben in der Hilfe tippen. Die Ergebnisse aktualisieren sich beim Tippen und enthalten Befehlsnamen anderer Programme. Wer also tippt, was er woanders getippt hätte, findet meist die richtige Seite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Themen sind in jeder App gleich gruppiert: Erste Schritte, Schritt-für-Schritt-Anleitungen, Umstieg von anderer Software, Referenz und Fehlerbehebung.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks in einem Thema öffnen das nächste Thema im selben Bereich. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZurück\u003C/span\u003E bringt Sie dorthin zurück, wo Sie waren.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"schritte-f-r-ihren-rechner\"\u003ESchritte für Ihren Rechner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EManche Schritte unterscheiden sich zwischen Mac, Windows und Linux, zwischen Browsern und zwischen online und offline. Die Hilfe ermittelt, was für Sie gilt, und zeigt diese Schritte. Die Zeile oben in einem Thema sagt, was sie gewählt hat, zum Beispiel „Schritte für Mac, Chrome oder Edge, online“.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÄndern\u003C/span\u003E in dieser Zeile wählen, um einen anderen Rechner oder Browser zu wählen, zum Beispiel um einer Kollegin oder einem Kollegen zu helfen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlle anzeigen\u003C/span\u003E wählen, um jede Version jedes Schritts zu sehen, jeweils beschriftet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETastenkürzel stehen so, wie Ihre Tastatur sie beschriftet: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E steht auf einem Mac als ⌘S und unter Windows und Linux als Ctrl+S.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dieselbe-hilfe-auf-der-website\"\u003EDieselbe Hilfe auf der Website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Website zeigt dieselben Themen unter getcadcraft.com/tutorials, mit den Schritten jeder Plattform. In der App öffnet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuf der Website öffnen\u003C/span\u003E unten in einem Thema diese Seite, wenn Sie online sind.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Jede App bringt ihre eigene Hilfe mit. Sie ist Teil der App, öffnet sich also ohne Verbindung und beschreibt immer die Version, die Sie gerade benutzen. Die Hilfe öffnen F1 drücken oder oben in der App-Leiste Hilfe wählen. Esc drücken oder Schließen wählen, um sie wegzulegen. Ihre Zeichnung bleibt, wie sie war. Auf den meisten Mac-Tastaturen steuert die obere Reihe Helligkeit und Lautstärke. F1 zusammen mit der Taste fn (oder der Globus-Taste) drücken oder die Schaltfläche Hilfe benutzen. Siehe Tasten auf einem Mac. Ein Thema finden Im Suchfeld oben in der Hilfe tippen. Die Ergebnisse aktualisieren sich beim Tippen und enthalten Befehlsnamen anderer Programme. Wer also tippt, was er woanders getippt hätte, findet meist die richtige Seite. Die Themen sind in jeder App gleich gruppiert: Erste Schritte, Schritt-für-Schritt-Anleitungen, Umstieg von anderer Software, Referenz und Fehlerbehebung. Links in einem Thema öffnen das nächste Thema im selben Bereich. Zurück bringt Sie dorthin zurück, wo Sie waren. Schritte für Ihren Rechner Manche Schritte unterscheiden sich zwischen Mac, Windows und Linux, zwischen Browsern und zwischen online und offline. Die Hilfe ermittelt, was für Sie gilt, und zeigt diese Schritte. Die Zeile oben in einem Thema sagt, was sie gewählt hat, zum Beispiel „Schritte für Mac, Chrome oder Edge, online“. Ändern in dieser Zeile wählen, um einen anderen Rechner oder Browser zu wählen, zum Beispiel um einer Kollegin oder einem Kollegen zu helfen. Alle anzeigen wählen, um jede Version jedes Schritts zu sehen, jeweils beschriftet. Tastenkürzel stehen so, wie Ihre Tastatur sie beschriftet: Ctrl+S steht auf einem Mac als ⌘S und unter Windows und Linux als Ctrl+S. Dieselbe Hilfe auf der Website Die Website zeigt dieselben Themen unter getcadcraft.com/tutorials, mit den Schritten jeder Plattform. In der App öffnet Auf der Website öffnen unten in einem Thema diese Seite, wenn Sie online sind.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browser und Rechner","summary":"In welchen Browsern die Apps laufen, was sich zwischen ihnen unterscheidet und was Ihr Rechner braucht.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","Browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","Tablet","webgl","Anforderungen","Arbeitsspeicher"],"headings":[{"id":"welcher-browser","text":"Welcher Browser","level":2},{"id":"was-sich-zwischen-browsern-unterscheidet","text":"Was sich zwischen Browsern unterscheidet","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-und-linux","text":"Mac, Windows und Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-und-touchscreens","text":"Tablets und Touchscreens","level":2},{"id":"der-lteste-browser-und-das-lteste-system","text":"Der älteste Browser und das älteste System","level":2},{"id":"was-ihr-rechner-braucht","text":"Was Ihr Rechner braucht","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDie Apps laufen in einem Webbrowser. Sie erledigen die Arbeit auf Ihrem eigenen Rechner: Ihre Dateien werden dort geöffnet und nie an uns gesendet (siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWohin Ihre Dateien gehen\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"welcher-browser\"\u003EWelcher Browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBenutzen Sie eine aktuelle Version von einem dieser Browser:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHinweise\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDer vollständigste: Ordnerauswahl, Speichern für die Offline-Nutzung, Dateiverknüpfung.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunktioniert. Einen ganzen Ordner zu öffnen läuft über eine einfachere Auswahl. Das Offline-Speichern geht in privaten Fenstern unter Umständen nicht.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunktioniert auf Mac und iPad. Einen ganzen Ordner zu öffnen läuft über eine einfachere Auswahl. Safari löscht unter Umständen eine Offline-Kopie, die Sie einige Wochen nicht benutzt haben.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlle drei Engines sind für die tägliche Arbeit in Ordnung. Wo eine App in einer von ihnen etwas anders macht, zeigt ihre Hilfe die Schritte für Ihren Browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-sich-zwischen-browsern-unterscheidet\"\u003EWas sich zwischen Browsern unterscheidet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEinen Ordner öffnen.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium-Browser zeigen eine echte Ordnerauswahl und können den Ordner direkt lesen. Firefox und Safari lassen Sie einen Ordner wählen und lesen dann jede Datei darin, was bei großen Projektordnern länger dauert. Den Ordner auf die App zu ziehen funktioniert in allen dreien.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpeichern.\u003C/strong\u003E Die Apps speichern durch das Herunterladen einer Datei. Ihr Browser entscheidet, wohin Downloads gehen und ob er vorher fragt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome oder Edge schalten Sie „Vor dem Herunterladen immer nach dem Speicherort für jede Datei fragen“ ein (unter Einstellungen, Downloads), um jedes Mal einen Ordner zu wählen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox öffnen Sie Einstellungen, dann Allgemein, dann Downloads und wählen „Immer nachfragen, wo Dateien gespeichert werden sollen“, um jedes Mal einen Ordner zu wählen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari öffnen Sie Einstellungen, dann Allgemein, und stellen „Speicherort für geladene Dateien“ auf „Für jeden Download nachfragen“, um jedes Mal einen Ordner zu wählen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-und-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows und Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Apps sind auf jedem Rechner gleich. Was sich ändert, ist die Tastatur: Tastenkürzel werden automatisch für Ihren Rechner geschrieben. Siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003ETasten, Mäuse und Trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-und-touchscreens\"\u003ETablets und Touchscreens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EManche Apps nehmen Touch an (ein Finger zeichnet oder wählt, zwei Finger zoomen durch Zusammenziehen und verschieben). Die Hilfe jeder App sagt, ob sie das tut. Benutzen Sie auf einem iPad Safari oder Chrome. Eine Tastatur hilft bei Apps, die getippte Befehle benutzen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"der-lteste-browser-und-das-lteste-system\"\u003EDer älteste Browser und das älteste System\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWir lesen diese Angaben aus dem Code der Apps der Version 0.7, nicht aus einer Schätzung: Jede Datei wurde geparst, und jedes darin benutzte Sprachfeature wurde in den veröffentlichten Kompatibilitätsdaten der Browser nachgeschlagen. Unterhalb dieser Versionen können die Apps nicht einmal starten, weil der Browser den Code nicht versteht.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EÄlteste Version, die die Apps starten kann\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium 94\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E93\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari (Mac, iPad, iPhone)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS und iOS 16.4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft Studio bringt unter Windows seine eigene Web-Engine mit und braucht daher Windows 10 oder 11. Auf einem Mac benutzt es die des Systems und braucht daher macOS 13.3 oder neuer. Einstellungen › Updates in Studio zeigt, worauf Ihre Kopie läuft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDas ist die älteste Version, die \u003Cem\u003Estartet\u003C/em\u003E, aus dem Code gelesen. Es ist keine Liste alter Browser, in denen wir die Apps ausgeführt haben. Ein älterer Browser, der startet, kann deshalb trotzdem ein Feature vermissen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-ihr-rechner-braucht\"\u003EWas Ihr Rechner braucht\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDas sind geplante Mindestwerte für die Apps, keine gemessenen Anforderungen:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB Arbeitsspeicher (16 GB werden für große föderierte Modelle empfohlen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEin Grafikchip mit WebGL 2 für die 3D-Apps. Wenn eine 3D-Ansicht leer bleibt, prüfen Sie, ob die Hardwarebeschleunigung in den Einstellungen Ihres Browsers eingeschaltet ist.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEin Bildschirm von mindestens 1280 Pixel Breite für die vollständigen Symbolleisten. Auf kleineren Bildschirmen brechen sie um.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESehr große Dateien (große Plansätze, große Modelle) brauchen in einem Browsertab viel Arbeitsspeicher. Schließen Sie andere aufwendige Tabs, wenn eine App langsamer wird.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Die Apps laufen in einem Webbrowser. Sie erledigen die Arbeit auf Ihrem eigenen Rechner: Ihre Dateien werden dort geöffnet und nie an uns gesendet (siehe Wohin Ihre Dateien gehen). Welcher Browser Benutzen Sie eine aktuelle Version von einem dieser Browser: Browser Engine Hinweise Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium Der vollständigste: Ordnerauswahl, Speichern für die Offline-Nutzung, Dateiverknüpfung. Firefox Gecko Funktioniert. Einen ganzen Ordner zu öffnen läuft über eine einfachere Auswahl. Das Offline-Speichern geht in privaten Fenstern unter Umständen nicht. Safari WebKit Funktioniert auf Mac und iPad. Einen ganzen Ordner zu öffnen läuft über eine einfachere Auswahl. Safari löscht unter Umständen eine Offline-Kopie, die Sie einige Wochen nicht benutzt haben. Alle drei Engines sind für die tägliche Arbeit in Ordnung. Wo eine App in einer von ihnen etwas anders macht, zeigt ihre Hilfe die Schritte für Ihren Browser. Was sich zwischen Browsern unterscheidet Einen Ordner öffnen. Chromium-Browser zeigen eine echte Ordnerauswahl und können den Ordner direkt lesen. Firefox und Safari lassen Sie einen Ordner wählen und lesen dann jede Datei darin, was bei großen Projektordnern länger dauert. Den Ordner auf die App zu ziehen funktioniert in allen dreien. Speichern. Die Apps speichern durch das Herunterladen einer Datei. Ihr Browser entscheidet, wohin Downloads gehen und ob er vorher fragt. In Chrome oder Edge schalten Sie „Vor dem Herunterladen immer nach dem Speicherort für jede Datei fragen“ ein (unter Einstellungen, Downloads), um jedes Mal einen Ordner zu wählen. In Firefox öffnen Sie Einstellungen, dann Allgemein, dann Downloads und wählen „Immer nachfragen, wo Dateien gespeichert werden sollen“, um jedes Mal einen Ordner zu wählen. In Safari öffnen Sie Einstellungen, dann Allgemein, und stellen „Speicherort für geladene Dateien“ auf „Für jeden Download nachfragen“, um jedes Mal einen Ordner zu wählen. Mac, Windows und Linux Die Apps sind auf jedem Rechner gleich. Was sich ändert, ist die Tastatur: Tastenkürzel werden automatisch für Ihren Rechner geschrieben. Siehe Tasten, Mäuse und Trackpads. Tablets und Touchscreens Manche Apps nehmen Touch an (ein Finger zeichnet oder wählt, zwei Finger zoomen durch Zusammenziehen und verschieben). Die Hilfe jeder App sagt, ob sie das tut. Benutzen Sie auf einem iPad Safari oder Chrome. Eine Tastatur hilft bei Apps, die getippte Befehle benutzen. Der älteste Browser und das älteste System Wir lesen diese Angaben aus dem Code der Apps der Version 0.7, nicht aus einer Schätzung: Jede Datei wurde geparst, und jedes darin benutzte Sprachfeature wurde in den veröffentlichten Kompatibilitätsdaten der Browser nachgeschlagen. Unterhalb dieser Versionen können die Apps nicht einmal starten, weil der Browser den Code nicht versteht. Älteste Version, die die Apps starten kann Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium 94 Firefox 93 Safari (Mac, iPad, iPhone) 16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS und iOS 16.4) CadCraft Studio bringt unter Windows seine eigene Web-Engine mit und braucht daher Windows 10 oder 11. Auf einem Mac benutzt es die des Systems und braucht daher macOS 13.3 oder neuer. Einstellungen › Updates in Studio zeigt, worauf Ihre Kopie läuft. Das ist die älteste Version, die startet, aus dem Code gelesen. Es ist keine Liste alter Browser, in denen wir die Apps ausgeführt haben. Ein älterer Browser, der startet, kann deshalb trotzdem ein Feature vermissen. Was Ihr Rechner braucht Das sind geplante Mindestwerte für die Apps, keine gemessenen Anforderungen: 8 GB Arbeitsspeicher (16 GB werden für große föderierte Modelle empfohlen). Ein Grafikchip mit WebGL 2 für die 3D-Apps. Wenn eine 3D-Ansicht leer bleibt, prüfen Sie, ob die Hardwarebeschleunigung in den Einstellungen Ihres Browsers eingeschaltet ist. Ein Bildschirm von mindestens 1280 Pixel Breite für die vollständigen Symbolleisten. Auf kleineren Bildschirmen brechen sie um. Sehr große Dateien (große Plansätze, große Modelle) brauchen in einem Browsertab viel Arbeitsspeicher. Schließen Sie andere aufwendige Tabs, wenn eine App langsamer wird.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Tasten, Mäuse und Trackpads","summary":"Wie Tastenkürzel geschrieben werden, Funktionstasten auf einem Mac, Rechtsklick und Verschieben ohne Maus.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["Tastatur","Tastenkürzel","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","Funktionstasten","Trackpad","Rechtsklick","mittlere Taste","Magic Mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"wie-tastenk-rzel-geschrieben-werden","text":"Wie Tastenkürzel geschrieben werden","level":2},{"id":"funktionstasten-auf-einem-mac","text":"Funktionstasten auf einem Mac","level":2},{"id":"tastenk-rzel-die-der-browser-beh-lt","text":"Tastenkürzel, die der Browser behält","level":2},{"id":"maustasten-auf-einem-trackpad","text":"Maustasten auf einem Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"in-ein-textfeld-tippen","text":"In ein Textfeld tippen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"wie-tastenk-rzel-geschrieben-werden\"\u003EWie Tastenkürzel geschrieben werden\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Hilfe schreibt jedes Tastenkürzel so, wie Ihre Tastatur es beschriftet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGeschrieben\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAuf einem Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnter Windows und Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBefehlstaste (⌘) und S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl und S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) und F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift und F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) und ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt und ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edie Taste Delete\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn und Delete (Entf-Funktion)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEin Pluszeichen bedeutet: die Tasten zusammen halten. „Dann“ bedeutet: eine nach der anderen drücken.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie meisten Tastenkürzel der Apps akzeptieren auf einem Mac entweder Befehl oder Control, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E funktioniert also in beiden Fällen. Einige, etwa der Vollbildmodus (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), benutzen auf jedem Rechner die Control-Taste. Die Hilfe sagt es dort, wo es gilt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"funktionstasten-auf-einem-mac\"\u003EFunktionstasten auf einem Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAuf den meisten Mac-Tastaturen steuert die obere Reihe Helligkeit, Lautstärke und Medien. So senden Sie F1 bis F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (oder die Globus-Taste) halten, während Sie die Taste drücken, oder\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESystemeinstellungen\u003C/strong\u003E, dann \u003Cstrong\u003ETastatur\u003C/strong\u003E, dann \u003Cstrong\u003ETastaturkurzbefehle\u003C/strong\u003E, dann \u003Cstrong\u003EFunktionstasten\u003C/strong\u003E öffnen und „F1, F2 usw. als Standardfunktionstasten verwenden“ einschalten.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EManche F-Tasten belegt auch macOS selbst (zum Beispiel Mission Control). Wenn eine Taste in der App nichts bewirkt, probieren Sie sie mit fn oder benutzen Sie stattdessen die Schaltfläche in der Symbolleiste.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tastenk-rzel-die-der-browser-beh-lt\"\u003ETastenkürzel, die der Browser behält\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEine Webseite kann dem Browser manche Tastenkürzel nicht abnehmen, egal was sie tut. In jeder App gilt:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E öffnen oder schließen immer Browserfenster und Tabs. Die Apps benutzen stattdessen Schaltflächen für Neu und Schließen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E auf einem Mac und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E unter Windows beenden den Browser. Bei nicht gespeicherter Arbeit fragt der Browser zuerst nach („Website verlassen?“). Wählen Sie, zu bleiben, und speichern Sie dann.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDer Vollbildmodus des Browsers (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F oder die grüne Schaltfläche am Fenster\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) funktioniert weiterhin. Die Apps haben außerdem eigene Bildschirmmodi auf \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, die auch die Leisten der App ausblenden können.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"maustasten-auf-einem-trackpad\"\u003EMaustasten auf einem Trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Apps benutzen die rechte und die mittlere Maustaste. Ohne Maus mit drei Tasten:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERechtsklick:\u003C/strong\u003E mit zwei Fingern klicken. „Sekundärklick“ in den \u003Cstrong\u003ESystemeinstellungen\u003C/strong\u003E unter \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E einschalten, falls der Zwei-Finger-Klick nichts bewirkt. Mit gehaltener \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zu klicken öffnet das Menü des Browsers, aber innerhalb einer Zeichnung lesen manche Apps es als gewöhnlichen Klick (mit gehaltener Control-Taste). Nehmen Sie deshalb besser den Zwei-Finger-Klick.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZiehen mit der mittleren Taste (Pan):\u003C/strong\u003E Jede App bietet eine andere Möglichkeit zum Verschieben, etwa \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E beim Ziehen zu halten oder mit der rechten Taste zu ziehen. Die Tastaturseite der App sagt, welche.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom mit dem Rad:\u003C/strong\u003E Das Scrollen mit zwei Fingern auf dem Trackpad zoomt in den meisten Apps. Auch Zusammenziehen zoomt, wo die App es unterstützt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERechtsklick:\u003C/strong\u003E mit zwei Fingern klicken oder auf die untere rechte Ecke des Touchpads klicken. Das hängt von den Einstellungen Ihres Touchpads ab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZiehen mit der mittleren Taste (Pan):\u003C/strong\u003E Jede App bietet eine andere Möglichkeit zum Verschieben, etwa \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E beim Ziehen zu halten oder mit der rechten Taste zu ziehen. Die Tastaturseite der App sagt, welche.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom mit dem Rad:\u003C/strong\u003E Das Scrollen mit zwei Fingern auf dem Touchpad zoomt in den meisten Apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-ein-textfeld-tippen\"\u003EIn ein Textfeld tippen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolange Sie in ein Feld tippen (einen Namen, eine Notiz, eine Suche), sind die Ein-Buchstaben-Tastenkürzel der App aus, „L“ tippt also ein L. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drücken oder auf die Zeichnung klicken, um die Tasten wieder der App zu geben.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Wie Tastenkürzel geschrieben werden Die Hilfe schreibt jedes Tastenkürzel so, wie Ihre Tastatur es beschriftet. Geschrieben Auf einem Mac Unter Windows und Linux Ctrl+S Befehlstaste (⌘) und S Ctrl und S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) und F Ctrl, Shift und F Alt+← Option (⌥) und ← Alt und ← Backspace die Taste Delete Backspace Delete fn und Delete (Entf-Funktion) Delete Enter Return Enter Ein Pluszeichen bedeutet: die Tasten zusammen halten. „Dann“ bedeutet: eine nach der anderen drücken. Die meisten Tastenkürzel der Apps akzeptieren auf einem Mac entweder Befehl oder Control, Ctrl+Z funktioniert also in beiden Fällen. Einige, etwa der Vollbildmodus (Ctrl+Shift+F), benutzen auf jedem Rechner die Control-Taste. Die Hilfe sagt es dort, wo es gilt. Funktionstasten auf einem Mac Auf den meisten Mac-Tastaturen steuert die obere Reihe Helligkeit, Lautstärke und Medien. So senden Sie F1 bis F12: fn (oder die Globus-Taste) halten, während Sie die Taste drücken, oder Systemeinstellungen, dann Tastatur, dann Tastaturkurzbefehle, dann Funktionstasten öffnen und „F1, F2 usw. als Standardfunktionstasten verwenden“ einschalten. Manche F-Tasten belegt auch macOS selbst (zum Beispiel Mission Control). Wenn eine Taste in der App nichts bewirkt, probieren Sie sie mit fn oder benutzen Sie stattdessen die Schaltfläche in der Symbolleiste. Tastenkürzel, die der Browser behält Eine Webseite kann dem Browser manche Tastenkürzel nicht abnehmen, egal was sie tut. In jeder App gilt: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T und Ctrl+W öffnen oder schließen immer Browserfenster und Tabs. Die Apps benutzen stattdessen Schaltflächen für Neu und Schließen. Ctrl+Q auf einem Mac und Alt+F4 unter Windows beenden den Browser. Bei nicht gespeicherter Arbeit fragt der Browser zuerst nach („Website verlassen?“). Wählen Sie, zu bleiben, und speichern Sie dann. Der Vollbildmodus des Browsers (Control-Command-F oder die grüne Schaltfläche am Fenster / F11) funktioniert weiterhin. Die Apps haben außerdem eigene Bildschirmmodi auf Ctrl+Shift+F, die auch die Leisten der App ausblenden können. Maustasten auf einem Trackpad Die Apps benutzen die rechte und die mittlere Maustaste. Ohne Maus mit drei Tasten: Rechtsklick: mit zwei Fingern klicken. „Sekundärklick“ in den Systemeinstellungen unter Trackpad einschalten, falls der Zwei-Finger-Klick nichts bewirkt. Mit gehaltener Ctrl zu klicken öffnet das Menü des Browsers, aber innerhalb einer Zeichnung lesen manche Apps es als gewöhnlichen Klick (mit gehaltener Control-Taste). Nehmen Sie deshalb besser den Zwei-Finger-Klick. Ziehen mit der mittleren Taste (Pan): Jede App bietet eine andere Möglichkeit zum Verschieben, etwa Space beim Ziehen zu halten oder mit der rechten Taste zu ziehen. Die Tastaturseite der App sagt, welche. Zoom mit dem Rad: Das Scrollen mit zwei Fingern auf dem Trackpad zoomt in den meisten Apps. Auch Zusammenziehen zoomt, wo die App es unterstützt. Rechtsklick: mit zwei Fingern klicken oder auf die untere rechte Ecke des Touchpads klicken. Das hängt von den Einstellungen Ihres Touchpads ab. Ziehen mit der mittleren Taste (Pan): Jede App bietet eine andere Möglichkeit zum Verschieben, etwa Space beim Ziehen zu halten oder mit der rechten Taste zu ziehen. Die Tastaturseite der App sagt, welche. Zoom mit dem Rad: Das Scrollen mit zwei Fingern auf dem Touchpad zoomt in den meisten Apps. In ein Textfeld tippen Solange Sie in ein Feld tippen (einen Namen, eine Notiz, eine Suche), sind die Ein-Buchstaben-Tastenkürzel der App aus, „L“ tippt also ein L. Esc drücken oder auf die Zeichnung klicken, um die Tasten wieder der App zu geben.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Ein Werkzeug oder eine Einstellung finden","summary":"Ctrl+K drücken und ein paar Buchstaben tippen, um ein Werkzeug auszuführen, eine Einstellung zu ändern oder ein Hilfethema zu öffnen.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["finden","suchen","Befehlspalette","Schnellöffnen","Befehl ausführen","ctrl k","cmd k","Werkzeug","Einstellung","Tastenkürzel"],"headings":[{"id":"finden-und-ausf-hren","text":"Finden und ausführen","level":2},{"id":"gut-zu-wissen","text":"Gut zu wissen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EJede App hat ein Suchfeld für ihre Werkzeuge, ihre Einstellungen und diese Hilfe. Sie müssen nicht wissen, wo ein Werkzeug in der Symbolleiste sitzt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"finden-und-ausf-hren\"\u003EFinden und ausführen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drücken oder in der Leiste oben \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESuchen\u003C/span\u003E wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEin paar Buchstaben dessen tippen, was Sie suchen: „Abrunden“, „Bereichstext“, „Einheiten“. Jedes getippte Wort muss passen, mit einem zweiten Wort wird die Liste also kürzer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drücken, um den ersten Treffer auszuführen, oder vorher mit den Pfeiltasten wählen. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E schließt das Feld.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEin Werkzeug läuft so, als hätten Sie es in der Symbolleiste angeklickt. Eine Einstellung öffnet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E an dieser Einstellung. Ein Hilfethema öffnet sich hier im Hilfebereich.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gut-zu-wissen\"\u003EGut zu wissen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWas Sie zuletzt benutzt haben, steht beim nächsten Mal oben, ein oft benutztes Werkzeug ist also nur einen Tastendruck entfernt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWerkzeuge, die gerade nicht laufen können, zum Beispiel solange keine Datei geöffnet ist, stehen grau in der Liste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Werkzeuge von Add-ons stehen ebenfalls in der Liste, sobald das Add-on eingeschaltet ist.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Jede App hat ein Suchfeld für ihre Werkzeuge, ihre Einstellungen und diese Hilfe. Sie müssen nicht wissen, wo ein Werkzeug in der Symbolleiste sitzt. Finden und ausführen Ctrl+K drücken oder in der Leiste oben Suchen wählen. Ein paar Buchstaben dessen tippen, was Sie suchen: „Abrunden“, „Bereichstext“, „Einheiten“. Jedes getippte Wort muss passen, mit einem zweiten Wort wird die Liste also kürzer. Enter drücken, um den ersten Treffer auszuführen, oder vorher mit den Pfeiltasten wählen. Esc schließt das Feld. Ein Werkzeug läuft so, als hätten Sie es in der Symbolleiste angeklickt. Eine Einstellung öffnet Einstellungen an dieser Einstellung. Ein Hilfethema öffnet sich hier im Hilfebereich. Gut zu wissen Was Sie zuletzt benutzt haben, steht beim nächsten Mal oben, ein oft benutztes Werkzeug ist also nur einen Tastendruck entfernt. Werkzeuge, die gerade nicht laufen können, zum Beispiel solange keine Datei geöffnet ist, stehen grau in der Liste. Die Werkzeuge von Add-ons stehen ebenfalls in der Liste, sobald das Add-on eingeschaltet ist.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Bereiche anordnen und einen zweiten Monitor verwenden","summary":"Seitliche Bereiche links, rechts oder unten andocken, stapeln, als Reiter gruppieren, anheften oder ausblenden, über der Zeichnung schweben lassen, das Layout verdichten, die Anzahl der Zeilen der Symbolleiste wählen oder sie in ein eigenes Fenster auf einem anderen Bildschirm verschieben.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["Bereiche","andocken","schweben","Bereich verschieben","ziehen","Layout","Reiter","Registerkarte gruppieren","stapeln","anheften","automatisch ausblenden","Bereich ausblenden","Bereich schließen","kompakt","Layoutdichte","dicht","Symbolleistenzeilen","umbrechen","1 Zeile","2 Zeilen","Symbolleistenreihen","zweiter Monitor","zwei Bildschirme","Dualmonitor","mehrere Monitore","Fenster","Pop-up","Layout zurücksetzen"],"headings":[{"id":"einen-bereich-im-fenster-verschieben","text":"Einen Bereich im Fenster verschieben","level":2},{"id":"bereiche-als-reiter-gruppieren","text":"Bereiche als Reiter gruppieren","level":2},{"id":"anheften-ausblenden-und-zur-ckholen","text":"Anheften, ausblenden und zurückholen","level":2},{"id":"das-layout-verdichten","text":"Das Layout verdichten","level":2},{"id":"die-anzahl-der-zeilen-der-symbolleiste-w-hlen","text":"Die Anzahl der Zeilen der Symbolleiste wählen","level":2},{"id":"die-tastatur-verwenden","text":"Die Tastatur verwenden","level":2},{"id":"die-bereiche-auf-einem-zweiten-monitor-anzeigen","text":"Die Bereiche auf einem zweiten Monitor anzeigen","level":2},{"id":"mehr-als-ein-bereichsfenster","text":"Mehr als ein Bereichsfenster","level":2},{"id":"einen-dialog-verschieben","text":"Einen Dialog verschieben","level":2},{"id":"die-bereiche-zur-cksetzen","text":"Die Bereiche zurücksetzen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDie Bereiche neben der Zeichnung lassen sich verschieben. Platzieren Sie sie dort, wo sie Ihnen passen, oder geben Sie ihnen ein eigenes Fenster auf einem zweiten Monitor, damit die Zeichnung den gesamten ersten Monitor nutzen kann.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJeder Bereich hat eine Titelleiste: einen schmalen Balken mit dem Namen des Bereichs, einem \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-Menü, einer Anheftnadel und einer Schließen-Schaltfläche oben am Bereich. Versionen vor 0.7 haben nur den Namen und das \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-Menü.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"einen-bereich-im-fenster-verschieben\"\u003EEinen Bereich im Fenster verschieben\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZiehen Sie den Bereich an seinem Namen oben.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWährend Sie ziehen, erscheinen umrandete Flächen links, rechts und unten an der Zeichnung. Lassen Sie den Bereich auf einer davon los, um ihn dort anzudocken. Die Fläche, über der Sie sich befinden, wird dunkel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELassen Sie ihn an einer anderen Stelle über der Zeichnung los, damit er schwebt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBereiche können sich eine Seite teilen: Sie werden gestapelt, und jede Linie zwischen zwei von ihnen lässt sich ziehen, um den Platz zu teilen (bei fokussierter Linie verschieben die Pfeiltasten sie, und ein Doppelklick darauf teilt den Platz gleichmäßig auf). Ziehen Sie den inneren Rand einer angedockten Seite, um sie breiter oder schmaler zu machen; ein Doppelklick auf den Rand stellt sie wieder her.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEin schwebender Bereich bleibt über der Zeichnung. Verschieben Sie ihn an seinem Namen, und ändern Sie seine Größe, indem Sie an seiner unteren rechten Ecke ziehen (oder mit \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zur Ecke gehen und die Pfeiltasten verwenden). Doppelklicken Sie auf seinen Namen, um ihn an der vorherigen Stelle wieder anzudocken.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EZiehen Sie mit einem Finger am Namen des Bereichs. Ziehen Sie die untere rechte Ecke eines schwebenden Bereichs mit einem Finger, um ihn zu vergrößern, und die Ränder zwischen angedockten Bereichen auf die gleiche Weise. Um ohne Ziehen zu schweben oder anzudocken, verwenden Sie das \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-Menü.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bereiche-als-reiter-gruppieren\"\u003EBereiche als Reiter gruppieren\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EZwei Bereiche können sich einen Rahmen teilen und zeigen jeweils nur einen. Ziehen Sie einen Bereich an seinem Namen auf die Titelleiste eines anderen Bereichs (die Leiste ist umrandet, solange Sie darüber sind) und lassen Sie los: Der Rahmen hat nun für jeden Bereich einen Reiter in seiner Titelleiste. Klicken Sie auf einen Reiter, um diesen Bereich anzuzeigen. Wenn ein Reiter fokussiert ist, wechseln \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E die Reiter, und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E springen zum ersten bzw. zum letzten.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm einen Bereich aus der Gruppe zu nehmen, wählen Sie im \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-Menü des Bereichs \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAus der Reitergruppe herausnehmen\u003C/span\u003E oder ziehen Sie ihn an eine andere Stelle. Wählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAls Reiter mit\u003C/span\u003E und den Namen eines Bereichs, um sie ohne Ziehen zu gruppieren.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"anheften-ausblenden-und-zur-ckholen\"\u003EAnheften, ausblenden und zurückholen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Anheftnadel in der Titelleiste eines Bereichs macht ihn \u003Cstrong\u003Eautomatisch ausblenden\u003C/strong\u003E: Der Bereich verlässt seine Andockstelle und wird zu einem kleinen Reiter an dieser Kante. Zeigen Sie auf den Reiter, fokussieren oder klicken Sie ihn, und der Bereich gleitet über die Zeichnung heraus. Entfernen Sie den Zeiger (oder drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), und er gleitet zurück. Drücken Sie die Anheftnadel erneut oder wählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnheften aufheben\u003C/span\u003E, um ihn dauerhaft anzudocken.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Schließen-Schaltfläche oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiesen Bereich ausblenden\u003C/span\u003E im Menü blendet den Bereich aus und schließt seine Andockstelle. Ein gestrichelter Reiter am Rand holt ihn zurück: Klicken Sie darauf, oder fokussieren Sie ihn und drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"das-layout-verdichten\"\u003EDas Layout verdichten\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllgemein\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayoutdichte\u003C/span\u003E hat zwei Auswahlmöglichkeiten. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKomfortabel\u003C/span\u003E ist die Voreinstellung. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKompakt\u003C/span\u003E verringert die Zeilenhöhen, die Innenabstände der Bereiche, die Symbolleistenschaltflächen und den Text der Bereiche in dieser App, damit mehr auf den Bildschirm passt. Die Einstellung wird für jede App gespeichert. Ein Touchscreen behält in beiden Fällen seine großen Schaltflächen, und ein Telefon behält sein eigenes Layout. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout zurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E lässt die Dichte so, wie Sie sie eingestellt haben.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-anzahl-der-zeilen-der-symbolleiste-w-hlen\"\u003EDie Anzahl der Zeilen der Symbolleiste wählen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllgemein\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbolleistenzeilen\u003C/span\u003E hat drei Auswahlmöglichkeiten. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUmbrechen\u003C/span\u003E ist die Voreinstellung: Jede Werkzeuggruppe wird angezeigt, auf so vielen Zeilen, wie sie braucht. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 Zeilen\u003C/span\u003E hält die Symbolleiste auf zwei Zeilen und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E1 Zeile\u003C/span\u003E auf einer, damit die Zeichnung mehr Höhe bekommt. Die Gruppen, die nicht hineinpassen, lassen sich innerhalb der Symbolleiste seitlich scrollen (mit dem Mausrad, mit einer Zwei-Finger-Wischbewegung oder mit \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E bis zu einem Werkzeug). Eine Symbolleiste, die schon in zwei Zeilen passt, bleibt bei \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 Zeilen\u003C/span\u003E unverändert, weil ein Raster sie nur höher machen würde. Die Auswahl wird für jede App gespeichert. Ein Touchscreen und ein Telefon behalten ihr eigenes Layout. CadCraft hat eine eigene Einstellung für seine Symbolleiste unter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-tastatur-verwenden\"\u003EDie Tastatur verwenden\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-Schaltfläche jedes Bereichs öffnet ein Menü: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinks andocken\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERechts andocken\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnten andocken\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÜber der Zeichnung schweben\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAm Rand anheften\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiesen Bereich ausblenden\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAls Reiter mit\u003C/span\u003E einem anderen Bereich, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E. Gehen Sie mit \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zur Schaltfläche, drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, bewegen Sie sich mit den Pfeiltasten und drücken Sie erneut \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E schließt das Menü.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-bereiche-auf-einem-zweiten-monitor-anzeigen\"\u003EDie Bereiche auf einem zweiten Monitor anzeigen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E an einem Bereich und dann \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlle Bereiche in ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E. Sie können auch \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBereiche in ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEs öffnet sich ein kleines Fenster mit den Bereichen darin. Ziehen Sie es auf Ihren anderen Monitor und machen Sie es so groß, wie Sie möchten.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArbeiten Sie weiter: Die Bereiche wirken wie zuvor auf die Zeichnung, und die Zeichnung füllt das Hauptfenster aus.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETastenkürzel funktionieren weiterhin, während das Bereichsfenster vorne ist: Tasten, die Sie dort drücken, gehen an die Zeichnung, außer wenn Sie in einem Feld tippen. Fragen und Dialoge, die die App stellt, öffnen sich weiterhin im Hauptfenster. Schauen Sie dort nach, wenn einer erscheint. Das Design und die Textgröße der Bereiche folgen dem Hauptfenster.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome und Edge können das Fenster für Sie auf den anderen Bildschirm setzen. Wenn ein zweiter Bildschirm angeschlossen ist, wählen Sie im Bereichsfenster \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuf meinen anderen Bildschirm legen\u003C/span\u003E (oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBereiche auf meinen anderen Bildschirm legen\u003C/span\u003E im \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-Menü eines Bereichs). Der Browser fragt einmal, ob diese Website Ihre Bildschirme sehen darf. Nachdem Sie es erlaubt haben, öffnet sich das Bereichsfenster jedes Mal auf diesem Bildschirm.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EZiehen Sie das Bereichsfenster selbst auf den anderen Monitor. Beim nächsten Mal öffnet es sich an derselben Stelle und in derselben Größe.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mehr-als-ein-bereichsfenster\"\u003EMehr als ein Bereichsfenster\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJeder Bereich kann ein eigenes Fenster haben: bis zu vier Bereichsfenster, zum Beispiel eines pro Monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIm \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E-Menü eines Bereichs öffnet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E ein weiteres Fenster für diesen Bereich. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn Bereichsfenster 2 verschieben\u003C/span\u003E (und so weiter) verschiebt ihn in ein bereits geöffnetes Fenster. Das zweite Fenster heißt „Bereiche 2“.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZiehen Sie einen Bereich an seinem Namen von einem Fenster in ein anderes: Lassen Sie ihn über dem Hauptfenster los, um ihn dort anzudocken (die umrandeten Flächen erscheinen, solange Sie darüber sind) oder um ihn schweben zu lassen. Über einem anderen Bereichsfenster lassen Sie ihn los, um ihn dorthin zu verschieben. Lassen Sie ihn an einer anderen Stelle los, bleibt er, wo er war.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEin Bereichsfenster ohne verbleibende Bereiche schließt sich von selbst.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENach einem Neuladen bietet die App Ihnen Ihre Bereichsfenster wieder an. Browser öffnen pro Klick ein Fenster. Wählen Sie daher \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDas nächste öffnen\u003C/span\u003E für jedes Fenster einmal.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"einen-dialog-verschieben\"\u003EEinen Dialog verschieben\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJeder Dialog lässt sich aus dem Weg der Zeichnung verschieben: Drücken Sie auf seine Titelleiste und ziehen Sie. Der Dialog merkt sich, wo Sie ihn gelassen haben, jeder Dialog für sich, und kehrt beim nächsten Öffnen an diese Stelle zurück. Doppelklicken Sie auf die Titelleiste, um ihn wieder zu zentrieren.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EÜber die Tastatur drücken Sie bei Fokus im Dialog \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+eine Pfeiltaste, um ihn jeweils 16 Pixel zu verschieben. Mit \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sind es 64 Pixel. Ein Dialog, der nach einem kleineren Fenster oder Bildschirm außerhalb des sichtbaren Bereichs liegt, wird zurückgeholt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAuf einem Telefon bleiben Dialogfelder vollbreite Ansichten und werden nicht verschoben.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio hat der Dialog \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAls PDF plotten\u003C/span\u003E von CadCraft, den Sie beim Arbeiten geöffnet lassen können, in seiner Titelleiste eine Schaltfläche \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn eigenem Fenster öffnen\u003C/span\u003E. Der Dialog wechselt in ein eigenes Fenster, das Sie auf einen anderen Monitor legen können. Wenn Sie den Dialog schließen, schließt sich das Fenster, und wenn Sie das Fenster schließen, schließt sich der Dialog.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"die-bereiche-zur-cksetzen\"\u003EDie Bereiche zurücksetzen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWählen Sie im Bereichsfenster \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBereiche zurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E, oder schließen Sie es einfach. Die Bereiche kehren an ihre vorherige Stelle zurück.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWenn Sie das Hauptfenster schließen oder neu laden, schließt sich auch das Bereichsfenster. Wenn die App wieder startet, teilt sie mit, dass Ihre Bereiche in einem eigenen Fenster waren. Wählen Sie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EÖffnen\u003C/span\u003E, um das Fenster wiederherzustellen, oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHier behalten\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllgemein\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout zurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E stellt jeden Bereich an seinen ursprünglichen Platz zurück.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWenn beim Verschieben von Bereichen in ein neues Fenster nichts geöffnet wird, hat der Browser das Pop-up blockiert. Die App weist darauf hin und lässt die Bereiche, wo sie waren. Erlauben Sie Pop-ups für getcadcraft.com und versuchen Sie es erneut.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDas Layout wird für jede App in diesem Browser zusammen mit Ihren anderen Einstellungen gespeichert.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Die Bereiche neben der Zeichnung lassen sich verschieben. Platzieren Sie sie dort, wo sie Ihnen passen, oder geben Sie ihnen ein eigenes Fenster auf einem zweiten Monitor, damit die Zeichnung den gesamten ersten Monitor nutzen kann. Jeder Bereich hat eine Titelleiste: einen schmalen Balken mit dem Namen des Bereichs, einem ⋯-Menü, einer Anheftnadel und einer Schließen-Schaltfläche oben am Bereich. Versionen vor 0.7 haben nur den Namen und das ⋯-Menü. Einen Bereich im Fenster verschieben Ziehen Sie den Bereich an seinem Namen oben. Während Sie ziehen, erscheinen umrandete Flächen links, rechts und unten an der Zeichnung. Lassen Sie den Bereich auf einer davon los, um ihn dort anzudocken. Die Fläche, über der Sie sich befinden, wird dunkel. Lassen Sie ihn an einer anderen Stelle über der Zeichnung los, damit er schwebt. Bereiche können sich eine Seite teilen: Sie werden gestapelt, und jede Linie zwischen zwei von ihnen lässt sich ziehen, um den Platz zu teilen (bei fokussierter Linie verschieben die Pfeiltasten sie, und ein Doppelklick darauf teilt den Platz gleichmäßig auf). Ziehen Sie den inneren Rand einer angedockten Seite, um sie breiter oder schmaler zu machen; ein Doppelklick auf den Rand stellt sie wieder her. Ein schwebender Bereich bleibt über der Zeichnung. Verschieben Sie ihn an seinem Namen, und ändern Sie seine Größe, indem Sie an seiner unteren rechten Ecke ziehen (oder mit Tab zur Ecke gehen und die Pfeiltasten verwenden). Doppelklicken Sie auf seinen Namen, um ihn an der vorherigen Stelle wieder anzudocken. Ziehen Sie mit einem Finger am Namen des Bereichs. Ziehen Sie die untere rechte Ecke eines schwebenden Bereichs mit einem Finger, um ihn zu vergrößern, und die Ränder zwischen angedockten Bereichen auf die gleiche Weise. Um ohne Ziehen zu schweben oder anzudocken, verwenden Sie das ⋯-Menü. Bereiche als Reiter gruppieren Zwei Bereiche können sich einen Rahmen teilen und zeigen jeweils nur einen. Ziehen Sie einen Bereich an seinem Namen auf die Titelleiste eines anderen Bereichs (die Leiste ist umrandet, solange Sie darüber sind) und lassen Sie los: Der Rahmen hat nun für jeden Bereich einen Reiter in seiner Titelleiste. Klicken Sie auf einen Reiter, um diesen Bereich anzuzeigen. Wenn ein Reiter fokussiert ist, wechseln ← und → die Reiter, und Home und End springen zum ersten bzw. zum letzten. Um einen Bereich aus der Gruppe zu nehmen, wählen Sie im ⋯-Menü des Bereichs Aus der Reitergruppe herausnehmen oder ziehen Sie ihn an eine andere Stelle. Wählen Sie Als Reiter mit und den Namen eines Bereichs, um sie ohne Ziehen zu gruppieren. Anheften, ausblenden und zurückholen Die Anheftnadel in der Titelleiste eines Bereichs macht ihn automatisch ausblenden: Der Bereich verlässt seine Andockstelle und wird zu einem kleinen Reiter an dieser Kante. Zeigen Sie auf den Reiter, fokussieren oder klicken Sie ihn, und der Bereich gleitet über die Zeichnung heraus. Entfernen Sie den Zeiger (oder drücken Sie Esc), und er gleitet zurück. Drücken Sie die Anheftnadel erneut oder wählen Sie Anheften aufheben, um ihn dauerhaft anzudocken. Die Schließen-Schaltfläche oder Diesen Bereich ausblenden im Menü blendet den Bereich aus und schließt seine Andockstelle. Ein gestrichelter Reiter am Rand holt ihn zurück: Klicken Sie darauf, oder fokussieren Sie ihn und drücken Sie Enter. Das Layout verdichten Einstellungen › Allgemein › Layoutdichte hat zwei Auswahlmöglichkeiten. Komfortabel ist die Voreinstellung. Kompakt verringert die Zeilenhöhen, die Innenabstände der Bereiche, die Symbolleistenschaltflächen und den Text der Bereiche in dieser App, damit mehr auf den Bildschirm passt. Die Einstellung wird für jede App gespeichert. Ein Touchscreen behält in beiden Fällen seine großen Schaltflächen, und ein Telefon behält sein eigenes Layout. Layout zurücksetzen lässt die Dichte so, wie Sie sie eingestellt haben. Die Anzahl der Zeilen der Symbolleiste wählen Einstellungen › Allgemein › Symbolleistenzeilen hat drei Auswahlmöglichkeiten. Umbrechen ist die Voreinstellung: Jede Werkzeuggruppe wird angezeigt, auf so vielen Zeilen, wie sie braucht. 2 Zeilen hält die Symbolleiste auf zwei Zeilen und 1 Zeile auf einer, damit die Zeichnung mehr Höhe bekommt. Die Gruppen, die nicht hineinpassen, lassen sich innerhalb der Symbolleiste seitlich scrollen (mit dem Mausrad, mit einer Zwei-Finger-Wischbewegung oder mit Tab bis zu einem Werkzeug). Eine Symbolleiste, die schon in zwei Zeilen passt, bleibt bei 2 Zeilen unverändert, weil ein Raster sie nur höher machen würde. Die Auswahl wird für jede App gespeichert. Ein Touchscreen und ein Telefon behalten ihr eigenes Layout. CadCraft hat eine eigene Einstellung für seine Symbolleiste unter Einstellungen › CadCraft. Die Tastatur verwenden Die ⋯-Schaltfläche jedes Bereichs öffnet ein Menü: Links andocken, Rechts andocken, Unten andocken, Über der Zeichnung schweben, Am Rand anheften, Diesen Bereich ausblenden, Als Reiter mit einem anderen Bereich, In ein neues Fenster verschieben und Zurücksetzen. Gehen Sie mit Tab zur Schaltfläche, drücken Sie Enter, bewegen Sie sich mit den Pfeiltasten und drücken Sie erneut Enter. Esc schließt das Menü. Die Bereiche auf einem zweiten Monitor anzeigen Wählen Sie ⋯ an einem Bereich und dann Alle Bereiche in ein neues Fenster verschieben. Sie können auch Bereiche in ein neues Fenster verschieben in Einstellungen wählen. Es öffnet sich ein kleines Fenster mit den Bereichen darin. Ziehen Sie es auf Ihren anderen Monitor und machen Sie es so groß, wie Sie möchten. Arbeiten Sie weiter: Die Bereiche wirken wie zuvor auf die Zeichnung, und die Zeichnung füllt das Hauptfenster aus. Tastenkürzel funktionieren weiterhin, während das Bereichsfenster vorne ist: Tasten, die Sie dort drücken, gehen an die Zeichnung, außer wenn Sie in einem Feld tippen. Fragen und Dialoge, die die App stellt, öffnen sich weiterhin im Hauptfenster. Schauen Sie dort nach, wenn einer erscheint. Das Design und die Textgröße der Bereiche folgen dem Hauptfenster. Chrome und Edge können das Fenster für Sie auf den anderen Bildschirm setzen. Wenn ein zweiter Bildschirm angeschlossen ist, wählen Sie im Bereichsfenster Auf meinen anderen Bildschirm legen (oder Bereiche auf meinen anderen Bildschirm legen im ⋯-Menü eines Bereichs). Der Browser fragt einmal, ob diese Website Ihre Bildschirme sehen darf. Nachdem Sie es erlaubt haben, öffnet sich das Bereichsfenster jedes Mal auf diesem Bildschirm. Ziehen Sie das Bereichsfenster selbst auf den anderen Monitor. Beim nächsten Mal öffnet es sich an derselben Stelle und in derselben Größe. Mehr als ein Bereichsfenster Jeder Bereich kann ein eigenes Fenster haben: bis zu vier Bereichsfenster, zum Beispiel eines pro Monitor. Im ⋯-Menü eines Bereichs öffnet In ein neues Fenster verschieben ein weiteres Fenster für diesen Bereich. In Bereichsfenster 2 verschieben (und so weiter) verschiebt ihn in ein bereits geöffnetes Fenster. Das zweite Fenster heißt „Bereiche 2“. Ziehen Sie einen Bereich an seinem Namen von einem Fenster in ein anderes: Lassen Sie ihn über dem Hauptfenster los, um ihn dort anzudocken (die umrandeten Flächen erscheinen, solange Sie darüber sind) oder um ihn schweben zu lassen. Über einem anderen Bereichsfenster lassen Sie ihn los, um ihn dorthin zu verschieben. Lassen Sie ihn an einer anderen Stelle los, bleibt er, wo er war. Ein Bereichsfenster ohne verbleibende Bereiche schließt sich von selbst. Nach einem Neuladen bietet die App Ihnen Ihre Bereichsfenster wieder an. Browser öffnen pro Klick ein Fenster. Wählen Sie daher Das nächste öffnen für jedes Fenster einmal. Einen Dialog verschieben Jeder Dialog lässt sich aus dem Weg der Zeichnung verschieben: Drücken Sie auf seine Titelleiste und ziehen Sie. Der Dialog merkt sich, wo Sie ihn gelassen haben, jeder Dialog für sich, und kehrt beim nächsten Öffnen an diese Stelle zurück. Doppelklicken Sie auf die Titelleiste, um ihn wieder zu zentrieren. Über die Tastatur drücken Sie bei Fokus im Dialog Alt+Shift+eine Pfeiltaste, um ihn jeweils 16 Pixel zu verschieben. Mit Ctrl sind es 64 Pixel. Ein Dialog, der nach einem kleineren Fenster oder Bildschirm außerhalb des sichtbaren Bereichs liegt, wird zurückgeholt. Auf einem Telefon bleiben Dialogfelder vollbreite Ansichten und werden nicht verschoben. In CadCraft Studio hat der Dialog Als PDF plotten von CadCraft, den Sie beim Arbeiten geöffnet lassen können, in seiner Titelleiste eine Schaltfläche In eigenem Fenster öffnen. Der Dialog wechselt in ein eigenes Fenster, das Sie auf einen anderen Monitor legen können. Wenn Sie den Dialog schließen, schließt sich das Fenster, und wenn Sie das Fenster schließen, schließt sich der Dialog. Die Bereiche zurücksetzen Wählen Sie im Bereichsfenster Bereiche zurücksetzen, oder schließen Sie es einfach. Die Bereiche kehren an ihre vorherige Stelle zurück. Wenn Sie das Hauptfenster schließen oder neu laden, schließt sich auch das Bereichsfenster. Wenn die App wieder startet, teilt sie mit, dass Ihre Bereiche in einem eigenen Fenster waren. Wählen Sie Öffnen, um das Fenster wiederherzustellen, oder Hier behalten. Einstellungen › Allgemein › Layout zurücksetzen stellt jeden Bereich an seinen ursprünglichen Platz zurück. Wenn beim Verschieben von Bereichen in ein neues Fenster nichts geöffnet wird, hat der Browser das Pop-up blockiert. Die App weist darauf hin und lässt die Bereiche, wo sie waren. Erlauben Sie Pop-ups für getcadcraft.com und versuchen Sie es erneut. Das Layout wird für jede App in diesem Browser zusammen mit Ihren anderen Einstellungen gespeichert.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"layout-ribbon","scope":"basics","title":"Im Layout mit Menüband und Projektbrowser arbeiten","summary":"Die App auf ein Menüband aus Registerkarten über der Zeichnung umstellen, mit Eigenschaften und einem Projektbrowser links und einem Reiter für jede geöffnete Ansicht, und jederzeit zum klassischen Layout zurückwechseln.","section":"start","order":36,"keywords":["Layout","Arbeitsbereich","Menüband","Registerkarten des Menübands","Projektbrowser","Browser","Paletten","Eigenschaftenpalette","Ansichtsreiter","Statusleiste","Schnellzugriff","Menüband einklappen","Menüband minimieren","Menüband-Fenster","klassisches Layout","Layout wechseln","Layout umschalten","angedockt"],"headings":[{"id":"ausw-hlen","text":"Auswählen","level":2},{"id":"was-wo-ist","text":"Was wo ist","level":2},{"id":"das-men-band-mit-der-tastatur-benutzen","text":"Das Menüband mit der Tastatur benutzen","level":2},{"id":"das-men-band-einklappen-oder-in-ein-eigenes-fenster-setzen","text":"Das Menüband einklappen oder in ein eigenes Fenster setzen","level":2},{"id":"ihre-bereiche-bleiben-ihre","text":"Ihre Bereiche bleiben Ihre","level":2},{"id":"zum-klassischen-layout-zur-ckkehren","text":"Zum klassischen Layout zurückkehren","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenüband und Projektbrowser\u003C/strong\u003E ist eines der drei Arbeitsbereichslayouts der App. Ein Menüband aus Registerkarten läuft oben quer, Eigenschaften und ein Projektbrowser teilen sich den linken Bereich, jede geöffnete Ansicht bekommt einen Reiter über der Zeichnung, und unten sitzt eine Statusleiste. Die Werkzeuge sind die der App: Alles, was Sie im klassischen Layout tun können, geht auch hier, und Ihre Zeichnung, Ihr Menüstil und Ihre Tasten bleiben, wie sie sind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ausw-hlen\"\u003EAuswählen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E öffnen (das Zahnrad oder \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E wählen und dann zu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeitsbereich\u003C/span\u003E gehen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenüband und Projektbrowser\u003C/span\u003E wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDas Layout ändert sich sofort. Die Wahl gilt für jede App in diesem Browser: Jede App öffnet sich von da an darin, und es steht bereit, bevor die Seite zeichnet, Sie sehen also nie zuerst das klassische Layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-wo-ist\"\u003EWas wo ist\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETitelleiste:\u003C/strong\u003E der Name der App, Schnellzugriffsschaltflächen (Öffnen, Speichern, Rückgängig, Wiederholen), der Name der geöffneten Datei, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWas jedes Werkzeug hier tut\u003C/span\u003E (eine Liste aller Werkzeuge mit Suchfeld, die sagt, welche jetzt bereit sind und warum die anderen es nicht sind), Vollbild, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHilfe\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E. In diesem Layout ersetzt die Titelleiste die App-Leiste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenüband:\u003C/strong\u003E die Werkzeuge der App in Registerkarten, jede Registerkarte in Gruppen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDatei\u003C/span\u003E ganz links öffnet, speichert und so weiter. Manche Apps fügen eine eigene Registerkarte hinzu, solange Sie in einem bestimmten Modus oder Arbeitsbereich arbeiten. Sie leuchtet, solange sie gilt, und verschwindet danach.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOptionsleiste:\u003C/strong\u003E unter dem Menüband, was das laufende Werkzeug von Ihnen braucht oder was gewählt ist.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinker Bereich:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenschaften\u003C/span\u003E oben und darunter die Paletten (der Projektbrowser und der eigene Inhalt des Bereichs) als Reiter. Die Linie dazwischen ziehen, um den Platz aufzuteilen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAnsichtsreiter:\u003C/strong\u003E ein Reiter pro geöffneter Ansicht (ein Blatt, ein Grundriss, eine Szene …). Mit seiner Schaltfläche zum Schließen schließen Sie einen. Die Datei bleibt geöffnet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAnsichtsleiste und Statusleiste:\u003C/strong\u003E unter der Zeichnung die eigenen Bedienelemente der Ansicht (Maßstab, Zoom), unten die Meldungen der App.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEin Werkzeug, das gerade nicht laufen kann, ist grau, und sein Tooltip sagt warum (zum Beispiel „Zuerst eine Datei öffnen“ oder dass die App dieses Werkzeug noch nicht hat). Klicken Sie es an oder drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E darauf, um den Grund in einem Feld zu lesen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"das-men-band-mit-der-tastatur-benutzen\"\u003EDas Menüband mit der Tastatur benutzen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBei fokussierter Registerkarte des Menübands wechseln \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zwischen den Registerkarten, und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E gehen zur ersten und zur letzten. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E beendet zuerst das laufende Werkzeug, wie im klassischen Layout, und jedes Tastenkürzel sowie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Bildschirmmodi) funktionieren wie zuvor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"das-men-band-einklappen-oder-in-ein-eigenes-fenster-setzen\"\u003EDas Menüband einklappen oder in ein eigenes Fenster setzen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEine Registerkarte des Menübands doppelklicken, um das Menüband auf seine Registerkarten einzuklappen. Ein Klick auf eine Registerkarte zeigt dann ihre Werkzeuge, bis Sie weitergehen. Erneut doppelklicken, um es geöffnet zu halten. Die Schaltfläche \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenüband verschieben\u003C/span\u003E am rechten Ende des Menübands hat auch \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenüband einklappen\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenüband geöffnet halten\u003C/span\u003E. Jede App merkt es sich.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenüband verschieben\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E setzt das Menüband in ein eigenes Fenster, zum Beispiel auf einen zweiten Monitor. Dort gedrückte Tasten erreichen die App. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenüband zurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E oder das Schließen dieses Fensters bringt es zurück.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Einstellungen der Symbolleiste gelten für das Menüband: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbolleiste\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbole und Beschriftungen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENur Symbole\u003C/span\u003E (das Menüband zeigt dann zwei Reihen kleiner Symbole pro Gruppe) oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENur Beschriftungen\u003C/span\u003E, und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGruppennamen anzeigen (Datei, Zeichnen, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ihre-bereiche-bleiben-ihre\"\u003EIhre Bereiche bleiben Ihre\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDer linke und der rechte Bereich behalten alles, was unter \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EBereiche anordnen\u003C/a\u003E beschrieben ist: andocken, schweben lassen, anheften, ausblenden, Reiter, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout zurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"zum-klassischen-layout-zur-ckkehren\"\u003EZum klassischen Layout zurückkehren\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeitsbereich\u003C/span\u003E öffnen und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft klassisch\u003C/span\u003E wählen. Jede Symbolleiste, jede Registerkarte und jeder Bereich geht dorthin zurück, wo er war.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAuf einem Telefon oder in einem Fenster von 760 Pixel Breite oder schmaler benutzt die App ihr Telefonlayout, egal was Sie gewählt haben. Ein breiteres Fenster bringt Ihr Layout zurück.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm eine andere Anordnung auszuprobieren, siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Edas Layout mit Werkzeugpalette und Infofeld\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Menüband und Projektbrowser ist eines der drei Arbeitsbereichslayouts der App. Ein Menüband aus Registerkarten läuft oben quer, Eigenschaften und ein Projektbrowser teilen sich den linken Bereich, jede geöffnete Ansicht bekommt einen Reiter über der Zeichnung, und unten sitzt eine Statusleiste. Die Werkzeuge sind die der App: Alles, was Sie im klassischen Layout tun können, geht auch hier, und Ihre Zeichnung, Ihr Menüstil und Ihre Tasten bleiben, wie sie sind. Auswählen Einstellungen öffnen (das Zahnrad oder Ctrl+,). Die Registerkarte Global wählen und dann zu Layout › Arbeitsbereich gehen. Menüband und Projektbrowser wählen. Das Layout ändert sich sofort. Die Wahl gilt für jede App in diesem Browser: Jede App öffnet sich von da an darin, und es steht bereit, bevor die Seite zeichnet, Sie sehen also nie zuerst das klassische Layout. Was wo ist Titelleiste: der Name der App, Schnellzugriffsschaltflächen (Öffnen, Speichern, Rückgängig, Wiederholen), der Name der geöffneten Datei, Was jedes Werkzeug hier tut (eine Liste aller Werkzeuge mit Suchfeld, die sagt, welche jetzt bereit sind und warum die anderen es nicht sind), Vollbild, Hilfe und Einstellungen. In diesem Layout ersetzt die Titelleiste die App-Leiste. Menüband: die Werkzeuge der App in Registerkarten, jede Registerkarte in Gruppen. Datei ganz links öffnet, speichert und so weiter. Manche Apps fügen eine eigene Registerkarte hinzu, solange Sie in einem bestimmten Modus oder Arbeitsbereich arbeiten. Sie leuchtet, solange sie gilt, und verschwindet danach. Optionsleiste: unter dem Menüband, was das laufende Werkzeug von Ihnen braucht oder was gewählt ist. Linker Bereich: Eigenschaften oben und darunter die Paletten (der Projektbrowser und der eigene Inhalt des Bereichs) als Reiter. Die Linie dazwischen ziehen, um den Platz aufzuteilen. Ansichtsreiter: ein Reiter pro geöffneter Ansicht (ein Blatt, ein Grundriss, eine Szene …). Mit seiner Schaltfläche zum Schließen schließen Sie einen. Die Datei bleibt geöffnet. Ansichtsleiste und Statusleiste: unter der Zeichnung die eigenen Bedienelemente der Ansicht (Maßstab, Zoom), unten die Meldungen der App. Ein Werkzeug, das gerade nicht laufen kann, ist grau, und sein Tooltip sagt warum (zum Beispiel „Zuerst eine Datei öffnen“ oder dass die App dieses Werkzeug noch nicht hat). Klicken Sie es an oder drücken Sie Enter darauf, um den Grund in einem Feld zu lesen. Das Menüband mit der Tastatur benutzen Bei fokussierter Registerkarte des Menübands wechseln ← und → zwischen den Registerkarten, und Home und End gehen zur ersten und zur letzten. Esc beendet zuerst das laufende Werkzeug, wie im klassischen Layout, und jedes Tastenkürzel sowie Ctrl+Shift+F (Bildschirmmodi) funktionieren wie zuvor. Das Menüband einklappen oder in ein eigenes Fenster setzen Eine Registerkarte des Menübands doppelklicken, um das Menüband auf seine Registerkarten einzuklappen. Ein Klick auf eine Registerkarte zeigt dann ihre Werkzeuge, bis Sie weitergehen. Erneut doppelklicken, um es geöffnet zu halten. Die Schaltfläche Menüband verschieben am rechten Ende des Menübands hat auch Menüband einklappen und Menüband geöffnet halten. Jede App merkt es sich. Menüband verschieben › In ein neues Fenster verschieben setzt das Menüband in ein eigenes Fenster, zum Beispiel auf einen zweiten Monitor. Dort gedrückte Tasten erreichen die App. Menüband zurücksetzen oder das Schließen dieses Fensters bringt es zurück. Die Einstellungen der Symbolleiste gelten für das Menüband: Einstellungen › Global › Symbolleiste › Symbole und Beschriftungen, Nur Symbole (das Menüband zeigt dann zwei Reihen kleiner Symbole pro Gruppe) oder Nur Beschriftungen, und Gruppennamen anzeigen (Datei, Zeichnen, …). Ihre Bereiche bleiben Ihre Der linke und der rechte Bereich behalten alles, was unter Bereiche anordnen beschrieben ist: andocken, schweben lassen, anheften, ausblenden, Reiter, In ein neues Fenster verschieben und Layout zurücksetzen. Zum klassischen Layout zurückkehren Einstellungen › Global › Layout › Arbeitsbereich öffnen und CadCraft klassisch wählen. Jede Symbolleiste, jede Registerkarte und jeder Bereich geht dorthin zurück, wo er war. Auf einem Telefon oder in einem Fenster von 760 Pixel Breite oder schmaler benutzt die App ihr Telefonlayout, egal was Sie gewählt haben. Ein breiteres Fenster bringt Ihr Layout zurück. Um eine andere Anordnung auszuprobieren, siehe das Layout mit Werkzeugpalette und Infofeld.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"layout-toolbox","scope":"basics","title":"Im Layout mit Werkzeugpalette und Infofeld arbeiten","summary":"Die App auf eine Werkzeugspalte links mit Plätzen für eigene Werkzeuge umstellen, mit einem Infofeld quer oben, das dem laufenden Werkzeug oder Ihrer Auswahl folgt, einem Navigator rechts, einem Reiter für jede Ansicht, Schnelloptionen und einer Bearbeitungspalette, und jederzeit zum klassischen Layout zurückwechseln.","section":"start","order":37,"keywords":["Layout","Arbeitsbereich","Werkzeugpalette","Werkzeugspalte","Infofeld","Infoleiste","Navigator","Projektübersicht","Schnellleiste","Schnelloptionen","Bearbeitungspalette","Eigene Werkzeuge","Lieblingswerkzeuge","Ansichtsreiter","Werkzeugpalette-Fenster","klassisches Layout","Layout wechseln","Layout umschalten"],"headings":[{"id":"ausw-hlen","text":"Auswählen","level":2},{"id":"was-wo-ist","text":"Was wo ist","level":2},{"id":"der-werkzeugpalette-ein-eigenes-fenster-geben","text":"Der Werkzeugpalette ein eigenes Fenster geben","level":2},{"id":"ihre-bereiche-bleiben-ihre","text":"Ihre Bereiche bleiben Ihre","level":2},{"id":"zum-klassischen-layout-zur-ckkehren","text":"Zum klassischen Layout zurückkehren","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWerkzeugpalette und Infofeld\u003C/strong\u003E ist eines der drei Arbeitsbereichslayouts der App. Die Zeichenwerkzeuge stehen in einer Spalte links, ein Infofeld quer oben zeigt, was das laufende Werkzeug braucht oder was Sie gewählt haben, jede Ansicht hat einen Reiter, und eine Schnellleiste unter der Zeichnung enthält ein paar alltägliche Optionen. Die Werkzeuge sind die der App: Alles, was Sie im klassischen Layout tun können, geht auch hier, und Ihre Zeichnung, Ihr Menüstil und Ihre Tasten bleiben, wie sie sind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ausw-hlen\"\u003EAuswählen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E öffnen (das Zahnrad oder \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie Registerkarte \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E wählen und dann zu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeitsbereich\u003C/span\u003E gehen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWerkzeugpalette und Infofeld\u003C/span\u003E wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDas Layout ändert sich sofort. Die Wahl gilt für jede App in diesem Browser: Jede App öffnet sich von da an darin, und es steht bereit, bevor die Seite zeichnet, Sie sehen also nie zuerst das klassische Layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-wo-ist\"\u003EWas wo ist\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymbolleiste:\u003C/strong\u003E Die eigene Symbolleiste der App bleibt oben im Zeichenbereich, mit ihren Menüs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInfofeld:\u003C/strong\u003E unter der Symbolleiste. Während ein Werkzeug läuft, zeigt es das Werkzeug und was es verlangt. Wenn Sie etwas wählen, zeigt es dessen wichtigste Einstellungen (bei einer Wand: Typ, Dicke, Länge, Ortungslinie und Höhe), und Sie können sie direkt dort ändern: Eine Änderung dort ist dasselbe wie in den Eigenschaften, und \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E nimmt sie zurück. Ist nichts gewählt, steht dort „Nichts ausgewählt“. Jede App fügt hier eigene Felder hinzu, etwa den aktuellen Layer oder das Geschoss oder das geöffnete Dokument.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWerkzeugpalette:\u003C/strong\u003E die Spalte links, die Werkzeuge der App in Gruppen. Auf den Namen einer Gruppe klicken, um sie ein- oder wieder auszuklappen. Das laufende Werkzeug leuchtet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEigene Werkzeuge:\u003C/strong\u003E die erste Gruppe der Werkzeugpalette, acht leere Plätze für die Werkzeuge, die Sie am meisten benutzen. Auf einen leeren Platz klicken, um ein beliebiges Werkzeug oder einen beliebigen Befehl der App zu wählen; oder in der Werkzeugpalette ein Werkzeug rechtsklicken und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZu Eigene Werkzeuge hinzufügen\u003C/span\u003E wählen; oder ein Werkzeug auf einen Platz ziehen. Einen Platz rechtsklicken, um ihn zu ändern oder zu leeren. Ihre Plätze werden pro App in diesem Browser gespeichert.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENavigator:\u003C/strong\u003E der rechte Bereich. Oben steht der Projektbrowser der App (Grundrisse, Schnitte, Ansichten, 3D-Ansichten, Listen, Blätter, je nachdem, was die App hat): Einen anklicken, um ihn zu öffnen. Darunter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenschaften\u003C/span\u003E und ein Reiter mit dem übrigen Inhalt des rechten Bereichs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAnsichtsreiter:\u003C/strong\u003E ein Reiter für jede Ansicht der geöffneten Datei (ein Blatt, ein Grundriss, eine Szene …), über der Zeichnung.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESchnellleiste:\u003C/strong\u003E unter der Zeichnung die Ansichtseinstellungen und alltäglichen Optionen, die die App hat, etwa Maßstab, Detailgrad, Ansichtsbereich, Einpassen, Grundriss, Geteilt oder 3D, Fänge und Einheiten.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBearbeitungspalette:\u003C/strong\u003E Wenn Sie etwas in der Zeichnung anklicken, erscheint eine kleine Palette am Zeiger mit den Bearbeitungsbefehlen der App dafür, etwa Verschieben, Kopieren, Drehen, Spiegeln, Duplizieren oder Löschen, und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenschaften\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, das Mausrad, ein weiterer Klick oder ihre Schaltfläche zum Schließen legen sie weg.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEin Werkzeug, das gerade nicht laufen kann, ist grau, und sein Tooltip sagt warum. Klicken Sie es an oder drücken Sie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E darauf, um den Grund in einem Feld zu lesen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E beendet zuerst das laufende Werkzeug, wie im klassischen Layout, und jedes Tastenkürzel sowie \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Bildschirmmodi) funktionieren wie zuvor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"der-werkzeugpalette-ein-eigenes-fenster-geben\"\u003EDer Werkzeugpalette ein eigenes Fenster geben\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Schaltfläche \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWerkzeugpalette verschieben\u003C/span\u003E oben in der Werkzeugpalette hat \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E: Die Werkzeugpalette öffnet sich in einem eigenen schmalen Fenster, das Sie neben die App oder auf einen zweiten Monitor setzen können. Dort gedrückte Tasten erreichen die App. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWerkzeugpalette zurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E oder das Schließen dieses Fensters bringt sie zurück.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDie Einstellungen der Symbolleiste gelten auch für die Werkzeugpalette: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbolleiste\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbole und Beschriftungen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENur Symbole\u003C/span\u003E oder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENur Beschriftungen\u003C/span\u003E, und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGruppennamen anzeigen (Datei, Zeichnen, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ihre-bereiche-bleiben-ihre\"\u003EIhre Bereiche bleiben Ihre\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDer linke und der rechte Bereich behalten alles, was unter \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EBereiche anordnen\u003C/a\u003E beschrieben ist: andocken, schweben lassen, anheften, ausblenden, Reiter, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn ein neues Fenster verschieben\u003C/span\u003E und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout zurücksetzen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"zum-klassischen-layout-zur-ckkehren\"\u003EZum klassischen Layout zurückkehren\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeitsbereich\u003C/span\u003E öffnen und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft klassisch\u003C/span\u003E wählen. Jede Symbolleiste, jede Registerkarte und jeder Bereich geht dorthin zurück, wo er war.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAuf einem Telefon oder in einem Fenster von 760 Pixel Breite oder schmaler benutzt die App ihr Telefonlayout, egal was Sie gewählt haben. Ein breiteres Fenster bringt Ihr Layout zurück.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm eine andere Anordnung auszuprobieren, siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Edas Layout mit Menüband und Projektbrowser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Werkzeugpalette und Infofeld ist eines der drei Arbeitsbereichslayouts der App. Die Zeichenwerkzeuge stehen in einer Spalte links, ein Infofeld quer oben zeigt, was das laufende Werkzeug braucht oder was Sie gewählt haben, jede Ansicht hat einen Reiter, und eine Schnellleiste unter der Zeichnung enthält ein paar alltägliche Optionen. Die Werkzeuge sind die der App: Alles, was Sie im klassischen Layout tun können, geht auch hier, und Ihre Zeichnung, Ihr Menüstil und Ihre Tasten bleiben, wie sie sind. Auswählen Einstellungen öffnen (das Zahnrad oder Ctrl+,). Die Registerkarte Global wählen und dann zu Layout › Arbeitsbereich gehen. Werkzeugpalette und Infofeld wählen. Das Layout ändert sich sofort. Die Wahl gilt für jede App in diesem Browser: Jede App öffnet sich von da an darin, und es steht bereit, bevor die Seite zeichnet, Sie sehen also nie zuerst das klassische Layout. Was wo ist Symbolleiste: Die eigene Symbolleiste der App bleibt oben im Zeichenbereich, mit ihren Menüs. Infofeld: unter der Symbolleiste. Während ein Werkzeug läuft, zeigt es das Werkzeug und was es verlangt. Wenn Sie etwas wählen, zeigt es dessen wichtigste Einstellungen (bei einer Wand: Typ, Dicke, Länge, Ortungslinie und Höhe), und Sie können sie direkt dort ändern: Eine Änderung dort ist dasselbe wie in den Eigenschaften, und Ctrl+Z nimmt sie zurück. Ist nichts gewählt, steht dort „Nichts ausgewählt“. Jede App fügt hier eigene Felder hinzu, etwa den aktuellen Layer oder das Geschoss oder das geöffnete Dokument. Werkzeugpalette: die Spalte links, die Werkzeuge der App in Gruppen. Auf den Namen einer Gruppe klicken, um sie ein- oder wieder auszuklappen. Das laufende Werkzeug leuchtet. Eigene Werkzeuge: die erste Gruppe der Werkzeugpalette, acht leere Plätze für die Werkzeuge, die Sie am meisten benutzen. Auf einen leeren Platz klicken, um ein beliebiges Werkzeug oder einen beliebigen Befehl der App zu wählen; oder in der Werkzeugpalette ein Werkzeug rechtsklicken und Zu Eigene Werkzeuge hinzufügen wählen; oder ein Werkzeug auf einen Platz ziehen. Einen Platz rechtsklicken, um ihn zu ändern oder zu leeren. Ihre Plätze werden pro App in diesem Browser gespeichert. Navigator: der rechte Bereich. Oben steht der Projektbrowser der App (Grundrisse, Schnitte, Ansichten, 3D-Ansichten, Listen, Blätter, je nachdem, was die App hat): Einen anklicken, um ihn zu öffnen. Darunter Eigenschaften und ein Reiter mit dem übrigen Inhalt des rechten Bereichs. Ansichtsreiter: ein Reiter für jede Ansicht der geöffneten Datei (ein Blatt, ein Grundriss, eine Szene …), über der Zeichnung. Schnellleiste: unter der Zeichnung die Ansichtseinstellungen und alltäglichen Optionen, die die App hat, etwa Maßstab, Detailgrad, Ansichtsbereich, Einpassen, Grundriss, Geteilt oder 3D, Fänge und Einheiten. Bearbeitungspalette: Wenn Sie etwas in der Zeichnung anklicken, erscheint eine kleine Palette am Zeiger mit den Bearbeitungsbefehlen der App dafür, etwa Verschieben, Kopieren, Drehen, Spiegeln, Duplizieren oder Löschen, und Eigenschaften. Esc, das Mausrad, ein weiterer Klick oder ihre Schaltfläche zum Schließen legen sie weg. Ein Werkzeug, das gerade nicht laufen kann, ist grau, und sein Tooltip sagt warum. Klicken Sie es an oder drücken Sie Enter darauf, um den Grund in einem Feld zu lesen. Esc beendet zuerst das laufende Werkzeug, wie im klassischen Layout, und jedes Tastenkürzel sowie Ctrl+Shift+F (Bildschirmmodi) funktionieren wie zuvor. Der Werkzeugpalette ein eigenes Fenster geben Die Schaltfläche Werkzeugpalette verschieben oben in der Werkzeugpalette hat In ein neues Fenster verschieben: Die Werkzeugpalette öffnet sich in einem eigenen schmalen Fenster, das Sie neben die App oder auf einen zweiten Monitor setzen können. Dort gedrückte Tasten erreichen die App. Werkzeugpalette zurücksetzen oder das Schließen dieses Fensters bringt sie zurück. Die Einstellungen der Symbolleiste gelten auch für die Werkzeugpalette: Einstellungen › Global › Symbolleiste › Symbole und Beschriftungen, Nur Symbole oder Nur Beschriftungen, und Gruppennamen anzeigen (Datei, Zeichnen, …). Ihre Bereiche bleiben Ihre Der linke und der rechte Bereich behalten alles, was unter Bereiche anordnen beschrieben ist: andocken, schweben lassen, anheften, ausblenden, Reiter, In ein neues Fenster verschieben und Layout zurücksetzen. Zum klassischen Layout zurückkehren Einstellungen › Global › Layout › Arbeitsbereich öffnen und CadCraft klassisch wählen. Jede Symbolleiste, jede Registerkarte und jeder Bereich geht dorthin zurück, wo er war. Auf einem Telefon oder in einem Fenster von 760 Pixel Breite oder schmaler benutzt die App ihr Telefonlayout, egal was Sie gewählt haben. Ein breiteres Fenster bringt Ihr Layout zurück. Um eine andere Anordnung auszuprobieren, siehe das Layout mit Menüband und Projektbrowser.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"layout-own","scope":"basics","title":"Eigenes Layout anordnen und Setups speichern","summary":"Wählen, welcher Bereich den Projektbrowser, die Eigenschaften oder seinen eigenen Inhalt zeigt, wohin die Werkzeugpalette kommt und was die Leisten enthalten, als eigenes Layout speichern und ganze Setups unter einem Namen aufbewahren, um zwischen ihnen zu wechseln.","section":"start","order":39,"keywords":["Layout","Arbeitsbereich","anordnen","anpassen","mein Layout","eigenes Layout","Bereiche","Editoren","Projektbrowser","Eigenschaften","Werkzeugpalette","Schnellleiste","Infofeld","Arbeitsumgebung","Setup","Profil","Setup speichern","Setup wechseln","Setup exportieren","Setup importieren","ccenv"],"headings":[{"id":"eigenes-layout-anordnen","text":"Eigenes Layout anordnen","level":2},{"id":"ihr-setup-als-arbeitsumgebung-speichern","text":"Ihr Setup als Arbeitsumgebung speichern","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENeben den drei eingebauten Layouts können Sie ein eigenes anordnen und Ihr ganzes Setup unter einem Namen speichern, um mit einem Schritt dorthin zurückzuwechseln.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"eigenes-layout-anordnen\"\u003EEigenes Layout anordnen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEinstellungen\u003C/span\u003E öffnen (das Zahnrad oder \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E wählen, dann \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigenes Layout anordnen…\u003C/span\u003E wählen. Das Dialogfeld beginnt mit dem Layout, das Sie gerade benutzen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEinen Namen vergeben und dann wählen:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EÜber der Zeichnung:\u003C/strong\u003E die Symbolleiste der App, ein Menüband oder keines von beiden; eine Titelleiste; ein Infofeld und welche Bedienelemente es enthält.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENeben der Zeichnung:\u003C/strong\u003E die Werkzeugpalette links, rechts oder gar nicht, mit oder ohne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigene Werkzeuge\u003C/span\u003E; welcher Bereich die Paletten enthält, welche oben liegt (zum Beispiel der Projektbrowser oder die Eigenschaften) und welche als Reiter darunter stehen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDer Zeichenbereich:\u003C/strong\u003E Ansichtsreiter, eine Schnellleiste und eine Ansichtsleiste mit den Bedienelementen, die Sie ankreuzen, die Bearbeitungspalette, eine Statusleiste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAussehen:\u003C/strong\u003E Größe, Schaltflächenumrisse und Reiterstil.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDieses Layout verwenden\u003C/span\u003E wählen. Die App wechselt sofort dazu, und jede App mit Layouts benutzt es ebenfalls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAngeboten wird nur, was diese App hat. Ihr Layout steht unter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAus Add-ons und Dateien\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDieses Layout exportieren\u003C/span\u003E speichert es als Datei, die Sie an Kolleginnen und Kollegen weitergeben können, und die Schaltfläche zum Entfernen nimmt es weg. Die Bereiche, die es füllt, lassen sich weiterhin andocken, schweben lassen und in eigene Fenster verschieben, wie unter \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EBereiche anordnen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ihr-setup-als-arbeitsumgebung-speichern\"\u003EIhr Setup als Arbeitsumgebung speichern\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEine Arbeitsumgebung ist Ihr ganzes Setup für jede App in diesem Browser: das Layout, der Menüstil und Ihre Tasten, der Symbolleistenstil, die Bereiche und ihre Größen sowie \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEigene Werkzeuge\u003C/span\u003E. Das Design und die Sprache gehören nicht dazu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpeichern:\u003C/strong\u003E Unter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArbeitsumgebungen\u003C/span\u003E einen Namen tippen und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMein Setup speichern\u003C/span\u003E wählen. Wer unter einem vorhandenen Namen speichert, ersetzt dieses Setup.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWechseln:\u003C/strong\u003E Ein Setup in der Liste wählen und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDorthin wechseln\u003C/span\u003E wählen. Die App lädt in diesem Setup neu. Bei nicht gespeicherter Arbeit fragt die App zuerst nach, wie bei jedem Neuladen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETeilen:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExportieren\u003C/span\u003E speichert das gewählte Setup als Datei \u003Ccode\u003E.ccenv.json\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESetup importieren…\u003C/span\u003E fügt eines aus einer Datei hinzu. Eine Datei setzt immer nur diese Auswahlen, nichts sonst.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEntfernen:\u003C/strong\u003E Ein Setup wählen und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntfernen\u003C/span\u003E wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESie können bis zu zwölf Setups behalten.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUm zuerst die eingebauten Anordnungen auszuprobieren, siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Edas Layout mit Werkzeugpalette und Infofeld\u003C/a\u003E und \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Edas Layout mit Menüband und Projektbrowser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Neben den drei eingebauten Layouts können Sie ein eigenes anordnen und Ihr ganzes Setup unter einem Namen speichern, um mit einem Schritt dorthin zurückzuwechseln. Eigenes Layout anordnen Einstellungen öffnen (das Zahnrad oder Ctrl+,), Global wählen, dann Layout. Eigenes Layout anordnen… wählen. Das Dialogfeld beginnt mit dem Layout, das Sie gerade benutzen. Einen Namen vergeben und dann wählen: Über der Zeichnung: die Symbolleiste der App, ein Menüband oder keines von beiden; eine Titelleiste; ein Infofeld und welche Bedienelemente es enthält. Neben der Zeichnung: die Werkzeugpalette links, rechts oder gar nicht, mit oder ohne Eigene Werkzeuge; welcher Bereich die Paletten enthält, welche oben liegt (zum Beispiel der Projektbrowser oder die Eigenschaften) und welche als Reiter darunter stehen. Der Zeichenbereich: Ansichtsreiter, eine Schnellleiste und eine Ansichtsleiste mit den Bedienelementen, die Sie ankreuzen, die Bearbeitungspalette, eine Statusleiste. Aussehen: Größe, Schaltflächenumrisse und Reiterstil. Dieses Layout verwenden wählen. Die App wechselt sofort dazu, und jede App mit Layouts benutzt es ebenfalls. Angeboten wird nur, was diese App hat. Ihr Layout steht unter Aus Add-ons und Dateien. Dieses Layout exportieren speichert es als Datei, die Sie an Kolleginnen und Kollegen weitergeben können, und die Schaltfläche zum Entfernen nimmt es weg. Die Bereiche, die es füllt, lassen sich weiterhin andocken, schweben lassen und in eigene Fenster verschieben, wie unter Bereiche anordnen. Ihr Setup als Arbeitsumgebung speichern Eine Arbeitsumgebung ist Ihr ganzes Setup für jede App in diesem Browser: das Layout, der Menüstil und Ihre Tasten, der Symbolleistenstil, die Bereiche und ihre Größen sowie Eigene Werkzeuge. Das Design und die Sprache gehören nicht dazu. Speichern: Unter Arbeitsumgebungen einen Namen tippen und Mein Setup speichern wählen. Wer unter einem vorhandenen Namen speichert, ersetzt dieses Setup. Wechseln: Ein Setup in der Liste wählen und Dorthin wechseln wählen. Die App lädt in diesem Setup neu. Bei nicht gespeicherter Arbeit fragt die App zuerst nach, wie bei jedem Neuladen. Teilen: Exportieren speichert das gewählte Setup als Datei .ccenv.json. Setup importieren… fügt eines aus einer Datei hinzu. Eine Datei setzt immer nur diese Auswahlen, nichts sonst. Entfernen: Ein Setup wählen und Entfernen wählen. Sie können bis zu zwölf Setups behalten. Um zuerst die eingebauten Anordnungen auszuprobieren, siehe das Layout mit Werkzeugpalette und Infofeld und das Layout mit Menüband und Projektbrowser.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"addons-basics","scope":"basics","title":"Add-ons and your account list","summary":"How add-ons work in every app and edition. Add one once in the Library and it follows your account, loads the first time an app needs it, and updates inside its major version. Sign in on a computer, pin a version, add a package from a file, or keep working offline.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["add-ons","library","add","remove","update","pin","lab channel","your add-ons","sign in","offline","offline on this device","use offline","add from a file","download as a file","drag and drop","ccaddon","package","paid","licence","entitlement","this file uses","engine","needs","orphan","team add-ons"],"headings":[{"id":"what-an-add-on-is","text":"What an add-on is","level":2},{"id":"your-list","text":"Your list","level":2},{"id":"needs","text":"Needs","level":2},{"id":"files-that-use-add-ons","text":"Files that use add-ons","level":2},{"id":"skill-packs-and-agents","text":"Skill packs and agents","level":2},{"id":"offline","text":"Offline","level":2},{"id":"add-from-a-file","text":"Add from a file","level":2},{"id":"paid-add-ons-offline","text":"Paid add-ons offline","level":2},{"id":"ipad-and-iphone","text":"iPad and iPhone","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-add-on-is\"\u003EWhat an add-on is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-list\"\u003EYour list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour add-ons\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESign in\u003C/strong\u003E connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/strong\u003E lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep a version\u003C/strong\u003E pins it. \u003Cstrong\u003EFollow newest\u003C/strong\u003E unpins it. \u003Cstrong\u003ETry lab versions\u003C/strong\u003E moves one add-on to the lab channel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoll back\u003C/strong\u003E returns to the version you had.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"needs\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-use-add-ons\"\u003EFiles that use add-ons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says &quot;This file uses ...&quot; and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skill-packs-and-agents\"\u003ESkill packs and agents\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe add-on directory at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E on getcadcraft.com also lists skill packs and agents for your own AI assistant. They are text only: a skill pack is instructions your assistant reads, an agent is a listing that describes a program someone wrote that uses the apps&#039; tools. We host, run and vouch for none of an outside maker&#039;s code, and they have no price here. Open them with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESkill packs\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgents\u003C/span\u003E filters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo offer one, a verified member or lab tester uses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend a skill pack\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend an agent listing\u003C/span\u003E in the directory once submissions are open. A person on our team reads every submission before it is listed, and the same review decides it as for any add-on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload everything for offline\u003C/span\u003E fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a browser, the website does the same for your whole list in one go: open \u003Cstrong\u003EYour add-ons\u003C/strong\u003E on getcadcraft.com and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EMake my add-ons available offline\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EOffline on this device\u003C/strong\u003E, in the browser you will take with you. The check below it says how many are ready, how much is stored and when, and names anything missing or out of date. Add-ons that come inside an app are ready once that app is saved for offline (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar). Browsers can clear stored files when space runs low, so look at that check before you go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith no account, the Library keeps a list for this browser (&quot;Your add-ons in this browser&quot;) with the same button; \u003Cstrong\u003EUse offline\u003C/strong\u003E on an add-on&#039;s page adds it there and fetches it at once. Free add-ons only. Sign up and the list moves onto your account.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-from-a-file\"\u003EAdd from a file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn an add-on&#039;s page, \u003Cstrong\u003EDownload as a file\u003C/strong\u003E saves its signed package (a \u003Ccode\u003E.ccaddon.json\u003C/code\u003E file). In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd from a file\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour add-ons\u003C/span\u003E takes that file with no connection needed, or drop the file onto the app&#039;s window. The app checks the signature first and refuses a file that was changed, is not signed by getcadcraft.com, or is made for another app. Reload the app to use it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paid-add-ons-offline\"\u003EPaid add-ons offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA paid add-on keeps working offline through its licence on the device: a signed entitlement that lasts up to 30 days from the last time the app (or the Your add-ons page) was online, and never past the end of what you bought. Going online again renews it. A paid package file only installs on a device that holds a licence for it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ipad-and-iphone\"\u003EiPad and iPhone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What an add-on is An add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the Library and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet. Your list Open Settings › Add-ons › Your add-ons. Sign in connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser. Remove takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which. Check for updates lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes. Keep a version pins it. Follow newest unpins it. Try lab versions moves one add-on to the lab channel. Roll back returns to the version you had. Needs An add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days. Files that use add-ons A drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says \"This file uses ...\" and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched. Skill packs and agents The add-on directory at Add-ons on getcadcraft.com also lists skill packs and agents for your own AI assistant. They are text only: a skill pack is instructions your assistant reads, an agent is a listing that describes a program someone wrote that uses the apps' tools. We host, run and vouch for none of an outside maker's code, and they have no price here. Open them with the Skill packs and Agents filters. To offer one, a verified member or lab tester uses Send a skill pack or Send an agent listing in the directory once submissions are open. A person on our team reads every submission before it is listed, and the same review decides it as for any add-on. Offline Download everything for offline fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used. In a browser, the website does the same for your whole list in one go: open Your add-ons on getcadcraft.com and choose Make my add-ons available offline under Offline on this device, in the browser you will take with you. The check below it says how many are ready, how much is stored and when, and names anything missing or out of date. Add-ons that come inside an app are ready once that app is saved for offline (Save for offline in the app bar). Browsers can clear stored files when space runs low, so look at that check before you go. With no account, the Library keeps a list for this browser (\"Your add-ons in this browser\") with the same button; Use offline on an add-on's page adds it there and fetches it at once. Free add-ons only. Sign up and the list moves onto your account. Add from a file On an add-on's page, Download as a file saves its signed package (a .ccaddon.json file). In the app, Add from a file under Your add-ons takes that file with no connection needed, or drop the file onto the app's window. The app checks the signature first and refuses a file that was changed, is not signed by getcadcraft.com, or is made for another app. Reload the app to use it. Paid add-ons offline A paid add-on keeps working offline through its licence on the device: a signed entitlement that lasts up to 30 days from the last time the app (or the Your add-ons page) was online, and never past the end of what you bought. Going online again renews it. A paid package file only installs on a device that holds a licence for it. iPad and iPhone Add-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Offline arbeiten","summary":"Eine App für die Offline-Nutzung speichern, was ohne Verbindung geht und was eine Verbindung braucht.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","kein Internet","Flugmodus","Baustellenbesuch","Cache","Service Worker","installieren","herunterladen","Verbindung"],"headings":[{"id":"eine-app-f-r-die-offline-nutzung-speichern","text":"Eine App für die Offline-Nutzung speichern","level":2},{"id":"was-offline-geht","text":"Was offline geht","level":2},{"id":"was-eine-verbindung-braucht","text":"Was eine Verbindung braucht","level":2},{"id":"gut-zu-wissen","text":"Gut zu wissen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EDie Apps erledigen ihre Arbeit auf Ihrem Rechner, das meiste geht also ohne Verbindung. Der Browser muss nur vorher die Dateien der App gespeichert haben.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"eine-app-f-r-die-offline-nutzung-speichern\"\u003EEine App für die Offline-Nutzung speichern\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie App öffnen, solange Sie online sind.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn der Leiste oben \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFür Offline speichern\u003C/span\u003E wählen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWarten, bis „Offline bereit“ erscheint. Die Dateien der App, ihre Dateileser und diese Hilfe sind jetzt in Ihrem Browser gespeichert. Apps, die DWG- oder PDF-Dateien lesen, speichern auch ihre Leser, beim ersten Mal kann das also eine Minute dauern.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETun Sie das einmal pro App, in jedem Browser, den Sie benutzen. Um später ohne Verbindung zur App zurückzukehren, öffnen Sie dieselbe Adresse wie zuvor oder ein Lesezeichen darauf.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESie sind jetzt offline. Wenn diese App für die Offline-Nutzung gespeichert wurde, geht alles Folgende. Wenn nicht, können Sie weiter benutzen, was in diesem Tab schon geladen ist, laden ihn aber nicht neu, bevor Sie wieder online sind.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-offline-geht\"\u003EWas offline geht\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDateien von Ihrem Rechner öffnen, zeichnen, bearbeiten, messen und prüfen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESpeichern und Exportieren: Jedes Speichern ist ein Download auf Ihren Rechner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDiese Hilfe, einschließlich der Suche.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-eine-verbindung-braucht\"\u003EWas eine Verbindung braucht\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDie App zum ersten Mal öffnen und für die Offline-Nutzung speichern.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEine neuere Version der App holen. Sind Sie online, übernimmt die App beim nächsten Öffnen die neueste Version, und \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFür Offline speichern\u003C/span\u003E aktualisiert die gespeicherte Kopie.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks zur Website, etwa \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuf der Website öffnen\u003C/span\u003E, und Links zu anderen Seiten.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud-Speicherung und Sync zwischen Rechnern, die noch nicht verfügbar sind.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gut-zu-wissen\"\u003EGut zu wissen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEine gespeicherte Kopie gehört zu einem Browser auf einem Rechner. Das Löschen der Websitedaten Ihres Browsers entfernt sie.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate Fenster mancher Browser und manche stark abgesicherte Arbeitsrechner erlauben kein Offline-Speichern. Die Schaltfläche sagt Ihnen, wenn sie nicht speichern kann.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari entfernt gespeicherte Websitedaten von Websites, die Sie mehrere Wochen nicht besucht haben. Öffnen Sie die App ab und zu online oder speichern Sie sie vor einer Reise erneut.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, die Desktop-App mit allen acht Apps in einer Installation, gibt es unter getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Die Apps erledigen ihre Arbeit auf Ihrem Rechner, das meiste geht also ohne Verbindung. Der Browser muss nur vorher die Dateien der App gespeichert haben. Eine App für die Offline-Nutzung speichern Die App öffnen, solange Sie online sind. In der Leiste oben Für Offline speichern wählen. Warten, bis „Offline bereit“ erscheint. Die Dateien der App, ihre Dateileser und diese Hilfe sind jetzt in Ihrem Browser gespeichert. Apps, die DWG- oder PDF-Dateien lesen, speichern auch ihre Leser, beim ersten Mal kann das also eine Minute dauern. Tun Sie das einmal pro App, in jedem Browser, den Sie benutzen. Um später ohne Verbindung zur App zurückzukehren, öffnen Sie dieselbe Adresse wie zuvor oder ein Lesezeichen darauf. Sie sind jetzt offline. Wenn diese App für die Offline-Nutzung gespeichert wurde, geht alles Folgende. Wenn nicht, können Sie weiter benutzen, was in diesem Tab schon geladen ist, laden ihn aber nicht neu, bevor Sie wieder online sind. Was offline geht Dateien von Ihrem Rechner öffnen, zeichnen, bearbeiten, messen und prüfen. Speichern und Exportieren: Jedes Speichern ist ein Download auf Ihren Rechner. Diese Hilfe, einschließlich der Suche. Was eine Verbindung braucht Die App zum ersten Mal öffnen und für die Offline-Nutzung speichern. Eine neuere Version der App holen. Sind Sie online, übernimmt die App beim nächsten Öffnen die neueste Version, und Für Offline speichern aktualisiert die gespeicherte Kopie. Links zur Website, etwa Auf der Website öffnen, und Links zu anderen Seiten. Cloud-Speicherung und Sync zwischen Rechnern, die noch nicht verfügbar sind. Gut zu wissen Eine gespeicherte Kopie gehört zu einem Browser auf einem Rechner. Das Löschen der Websitedaten Ihres Browsers entfernt sie. Private Fenster mancher Browser und manche stark abgesicherte Arbeitsrechner erlauben kein Offline-Speichern. Die Schaltfläche sagt Ihnen, wenn sie nicht speichern kann. Safari entfernt gespeicherte Websitedaten von Websites, die Sie mehrere Wochen nicht besucht haben. Öffnen Sie die App ab und zu online oder speichern Sie sie vor einer Reise erneut. CadCraft Studio, die Desktop-App mit allen acht Apps in einer Installation, gibt es unter getcadcraft.com/download.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Wohin Ihre Dateien gehen","summary":"Dateien bleiben auf Ihrem Rechner, wie das Speichern funktioniert und was der Browser sich merkt.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["Datenschutz","Upload","Cloud","speichern","herunterladen","Speicher","lokal","Sicherheit","vertraulich"],"headings":[{"id":"ihre-dateien-bleiben-auf-ihrem-rechner","text":"Ihre Dateien bleiben auf Ihrem Rechner","level":2},{"id":"wie-das-speichern-funktioniert","text":"Wie das Speichern funktioniert","level":2},{"id":"was-der-browser-sich-merkt","text":"Was der Browser sich merkt","level":2},{"id":"speichern-in-der-cloud","text":"Speichern in der Cloud","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"ihre-dateien-bleiben-auf-ihrem-rechner\"\u003EIhre Dateien bleiben auf Ihrem Rechner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWenn Sie eine Zeichnung, ein Modell oder eine PDF-Datei öffnen, liest die App sie in Ihrem Browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EDie Datei wird nicht hochgeladen.\u003C/strong\u003E Wir sehen sie nie, und es funktioniert genauso ohne Verbindung.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wie-das-speichern-funktioniert\"\u003EWie das Speichern funktioniert\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEine Browserseite kann Dateien auf Ihrem Rechner nicht von sich aus überschreiben. Die Apps speichern deshalb per \u003Cstrong\u003EDownload\u003C/strong\u003E einer neuen Datei:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDer Download landet dort, wo Ihr Browser Downloads ablegt, meist im Ordner „Downloads“, es sei denn, der Browser fragt jedes Mal nach (siehe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowser und Rechner\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps, die eine Datei ändern, speichern eine Kopie mit neuem Namen, etwa mit „(edited)“ oder „(marked up)“, damit Ihr Original unberührt bleibt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWenn Sie den Tab mit nicht gespeicherter Arbeit schließen, fragt der Browser zuerst nach („Website verlassen?“).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"was-der-browser-sich-merkt\"\u003EWas der Browser sich merkt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EManche Apps merken sich kleine Einstellungen in Ihrem Browser, etwa Ihren Autorennamen oder Werkzeugstile. Sie bleiben in diesem Browser auf diesem Rechner, und das Löschen der Websitedaten entfernt sie. Die Hilfe jeder App sagt, was sie speichert.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"speichern-in-der-cloud\"\u003ESpeichern in der Cloud\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud-Speicherung und Sync zwischen Rechnern gehören zur kommerziellen Lizenz. Sie sind noch nicht verfügbar. Bis dahin legen Sie Ihre Dateien dort ab, wo Sie Ihre anderen Projektdateien aufbewahren, und sichern sie auf dem üblichen Weg.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Ihre Dateien bleiben auf Ihrem Rechner Wenn Sie eine Zeichnung, ein Modell oder eine PDF-Datei öffnen, liest die App sie in Ihrem Browser. Die Datei wird nicht hochgeladen. Wir sehen sie nie, und es funktioniert genauso ohne Verbindung. Wie das Speichern funktioniert Eine Browserseite kann Dateien auf Ihrem Rechner nicht von sich aus überschreiben. Die Apps speichern deshalb per Download einer neuen Datei: Der Download landet dort, wo Ihr Browser Downloads ablegt, meist im Ordner „Downloads“, es sei denn, der Browser fragt jedes Mal nach (siehe Browser und Rechner). Apps, die eine Datei ändern, speichern eine Kopie mit neuem Namen, etwa mit „(edited)“ oder „(marked up)“, damit Ihr Original unberührt bleibt. Wenn Sie den Tab mit nicht gespeicherter Arbeit schließen, fragt der Browser zuerst nach („Website verlassen?“). Was der Browser sich merkt Manche Apps merken sich kleine Einstellungen in Ihrem Browser, etwa Ihren Autorennamen oder Werkzeugstile. Sie bleiben in diesem Browser auf diesem Rechner, und das Löschen der Websitedaten entfernt sie. Die Hilfe jeder App sagt, was sie speichert. Speichern in der Cloud Cloud-Speicherung und Sync zwischen Rechnern gehören zur kommerziellen Lizenz. Sie sind noch nicht verfügbar. Bis dahin legen Sie Ihre Dateien dort ab, wo Sie Ihre anderen Projektdateien aufbewahren, und sichern sie auf dem üblichen Weg.","lang":"de"},{"slug":"connect-ai-windows","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Windows","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":63,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","windows"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-macos","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a Mac","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":64,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","macos"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-linux","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Linux","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":65,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","linux"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-mobile","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a phone or tablet","summary":"How your own AI app reaches the CadCraft apps from a phone or tablet: through the hosted connector, with CadCraft Studio open on a computer. Includes the one-line test.","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","android","ipad","phone","tablet","hosted connector","test"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"what-it-can-and-cannot-do","text":"What it can and cannot do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the computer, open \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EHosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn your AI app, add a connector (a &quot;remote&quot; or &quot;custom&quot; connector) with that address and sign in when asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-can-and-cannot-do\"\u003EWhat it can and cannot do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn An AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a hosted connector instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer. How On the computer, open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI and choose Hosted connector. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in. In your AI app, add a connector (a \"remote\" or \"custom\" connector) with that address and sign in when asked. Keep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. What it can and cannot do The hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"local-models","scope":"basics","title":"Use an AI that runs on your own computer","summary":"Work with a local AI model and no network: through an AI app that can use tools, .","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","local","offline","ollama","lm studio","llama.cpp","jan","air-gapped","private","model","ask box"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp&#039;s server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (&quot;MCP&quot;). Add the single \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E entry shown in \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProgram\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECan use tools through MCP\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELM Studio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ellama.cpp (llama-server)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, start it with \u003Ccode\u003E--jinja\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, turn on tool calling for the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOllama\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis list is taken from each program&#039;s own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERaise the model&#039;s context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESmall models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything else stays the same: the permission levels, \u003Cstrong\u003ESuggest\u003C/strong\u003E mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. How Use an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp's server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\"). Add the single cadcraft entry shown in Connect your AI, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app. Program Can use tools through MCP LM Studio Yes llama.cpp (llama-server) Yes, start it with --jinja Jan Yes, turn on tool calling for the model Ollama Not on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front This list is taken from each program's own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system. Good to know Raise the model's context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit. Small models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools. Everything else stays the same: the permission levels, Suggest mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"engines","scope":"basics","title":"Run an engine on a model","summary":"The Engines panel runs the shared calculation engines (schematics, pipe and power networks, motion, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems, PLC) on a model you paste.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["engines","schematic","network","power","load flow","robot","kinematics","queue","simulation","schedule","critical path","requirements","risk","fault tree","modbus","circuit","spice","board","pcb","modelica","plc"],"headings":[{"id":"run-one","text":"Run one","level":2},{"id":"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene","text":"Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open \u003Cstrong\u003EEngines\u003C/strong\u003E from the button in the bar at the top, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and type &quot;engines&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-one\"\u003ERun one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The results come back as tables, with the engine&#039;s own problems above them and the limits of its method below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E puts the example text back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene\"\u003EControls on a model, or on the 3D scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to a flow model\u003C/span\u003E is the queues engine&#039;s model. In ModelCraft, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to the 3D scene\u003C/span\u003E ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads \u003Ccode\u003EName.x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.z\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.rotZ\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EName.hidden\u003C/code\u003E, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a \u003Ccode\u003Escale\u003C/code\u003E (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and \u003Ccode\u003Evalues\u003C/code\u003E says what off and on mean (\u003Ccode\u003E[0, 2]\u003C/code\u003E puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;keep&quot;: true\u003C/code\u003E to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProblems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Some calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open Engines from the button in the bar at the top, or press Ctrl+K and type \"engines\". Run one Choose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls. Choose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text). Press Run. The results come back as tables, with the engine's own problems above them and the limits of its method below. Reset puts the example text back. Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene The controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. The program bound to a flow model is the queues engine's model. In ModelCraft, The program bound to the 3D scene ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads Name.x, Name.y, Name.z, Name.rotZ or Name.hidden, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a scale (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and values says what off and on mean ([0, 2] puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \"keep\": true to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name. Things to know Everything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file. Problems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source. The examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design. The results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assistants-recipes","scope":"basics","title":"Recipes for AI assistants","summary":"Worked examples for using your own AI assistant with the CadCraft apps: what to ask, the tools it calls, and the permissions each needs. Every recipe here is run by our tests with the same calls.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","recipes","examples","prompts","automation","batch","convert","plot","check","takeoff","cut","fill","parameters","script"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes","level":2},{"id":"tidy-a-drawing","text":"Tidy a drawing","level":3},{"id":"plot-a-set-of-sheets","text":"Plot a set of sheets","level":3},{"id":"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant","text":"Convert drawings for a consultant","level":3},{"id":"check-drawings-before-sending-them","text":"Check drawings before sending them","level":3},{"id":"ask-a-building-model-questions","text":"Ask a building model questions","level":3},{"id":"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft","text":"Draw a room in BimCraft","level":3},{"id":"total-a-takeoff","text":"Total a takeoff","level":3},{"id":"site-numbers","text":"Site numbers","level":3},{"id":"try-a-part-variant","text":"Try a part variant","level":3},{"id":"fill-in-a-title-block","text":"Fill in a title block","level":3},{"id":"your-files-and-your-assistant","text":"Your files and your assistant","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReady-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps&#039; tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in. In \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). \u003Cstrong\u003EWithout a window\u003C/strong\u003E, the CadCraft file server for assistants (\u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root &lt;folder&gt;\u003C/code\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recipes\"\u003ERecipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tidy-a-drawing\"\u003ETidy a drawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open {path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg&quot;}            → d1\ndrawing_describe {doc: &quot;d1&quot;}                                    layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses\ncommand_run {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, input: &quot;PURGE&quot;}                         one undo step: &quot;Assistant: PURGE&quot;\ndrawing_save {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot-a-set-of-sheets\"\u003EPlot a set of sheets\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_open {path: …}  ·  drawing_describe {doc}                the sheets of each drawing\ndrawing_plot {doc, layout: &quot;&lt;sheet&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Plots/&lt;drawing&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.pdf&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant\"\u003EConvert drawings for a consultant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_convert {path: &quot;Kitchen/&lt;file&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Out/&lt;file&gt;.dwg&quot;, version: &quot;2013&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"check-drawings-before-sending-them\"\u003ECheck drawings before sending them\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [&quot;0&quot;]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. Keep your office&#039;s rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json\u003C/code\u003E (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ask-a-building-model-questions\"\u003EAsk a building model questions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eifc_open {path: &quot;house.ifc&quot;}                                    → d1\nifc_query {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, class: &quot;IfcDoor&quot;}                         each door with its level\nifc_properties {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, id: &lt;door&gt;}                          Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. In Studio, BimCraft&#039;s own tools answer the same about the model you have open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft\"\u003EDraw a room in BimCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ewalls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true}     one undo step\nopening_add {wall: &quot;&lt;the first wall&#039;s GlobalId&gt;&quot;, kind: &quot;door&quot;}                    one undo step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_new\u003C/code\u003E starts a building first and \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_save\u003C/code\u003E writes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"total-a-takeoff\"\u003ETotal a takeoff\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open {path: &quot;job.takeoff.json&quot;}  ·  takeoff_estimate {doc}  ·  takeoff_export {doc, out: &quot;job.xlsx&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"site-numbers\"\u003ESite numbers\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How much cut and fill does the pad need?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esite_open {path: &quot;site.civilcraft.json&quot;}  ·  surface_volume {doc}       cut, fill and net, per pad\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. With a level instead of a design: \u003Ccode\u003Esurface_volume {doc, datum: 100}\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"try-a-part-variant\"\u003ETry a part variant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epart_open {path: &quot;screw.partcraft.json&quot;}  ·  params_list {doc}\nparam_set {doc, name: &quot;l&quot;, value: 40}                           one undo step, the part rebuilt\nmass_properties {doc}                                            volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit; Save (\u003Ccode\u003Epart_save\u003C/code\u003E) to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fill-in-a-title-block\"\u003EFill in a title block\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eblock_insert {doc, block: &quot;TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD&quot;, at: [0, 0], attributes: {&quot;SHEET-NO&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;SHEET-TITLE&quot;: &quot;Kitchen plan&quot;}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-and-your-assistant\"\u003EYour files and your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps never send your files anywhere. \u003Cstrong\u003EBut what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant:\u003C/strong\u003E a cloud assistant&#039;s maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job&#039;s folders out of the ones you allow.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn Ready-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps' tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working. There are two ways in. In CadCraft Studio, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). Without a window, the CadCraft file server for assistants (npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root \u003Cfolder\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the cadcraft command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways. Start read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with. Recipes Tidy a drawing Ask: \"List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.\" The assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE: drawing_open {path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg\"} → d1 drawing_describe {doc: \"d1\"} layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses command_run {doc: \"d1\", input: \"PURGE\"} one undo step: \"Assistant: PURGE\" drawing_save {doc: \"d1\", path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg\"} Needs Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay. Plot a set of sheets Ask: \"Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_open {path: …} · drawing_describe {doc} the sheets of each drawing drawing_plot {doc, layout: \"\u003Csheet\u003E\", out: \"Plots/\u003Cdrawing\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.pdf\"} Needs Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: cadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all. Convert drawings for a consultant Ask: \"Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_convert {path: \"Kitchen/\u003Cfile\u003E\", out: \"Out/\u003Cfile\u003E.dwg\", version: \"2013\"} Needs Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: cadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013. Check drawings before sending them Ask: \"Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.\" drawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [\"0\"]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}} Needs Read. Keep your office's rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: cadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script). Ask a building model questions Ask: \"How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?\" ifc_open {path: \"house.ifc\"} → d1 ifc_query {doc: \"d1\", class: \"IfcDoor\"} each door with its level ifc_properties {doc: \"d1\", id: \u003Cdoor\u003E} Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal Needs Read. In Studio, BimCraft's own tools answer the same about the model you have open. Draw a room in BimCraft Ask: \"Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.\" walls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true} one undo step opening_add {wall: \"\u003Cthe first wall's GlobalId\u003E\", kind: \"door\"} one undo step Needs Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, ifc_new starts a building first and ifc_save writes it. Total a takeoff Ask: \"Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.\" takeoff_open {path: \"job.takeoff.json\"} · takeoff_estimate {doc} · takeoff_export {doc, out: \"job.xlsx\"} Needs Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so. Site numbers Ask: \"How much cut and fill does the pad need?\" site_open {path: \"site.civilcraft.json\"} · surface_volume {doc} cut, fill and net, per pad Needs Read. With a level instead of a design: surface_volume {doc, datum: 100}. Try a part variant Ask: \"Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.\" part_open {path: \"screw.partcraft.json\"} · params_list {doc} param_set {doc, name: \"l\", value: 40} one undo step, the part rebuilt mass_properties {doc} volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia Needs Read and Edit; Save (part_save) to keep it. Fill in a title block Ask: \"Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.\" block_insert {doc, block: \"TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD\", at: [0, 0], attributes: {\"SHEET-NO\": \"A-101\", \"SHEET-TITLE\": \"Kitchen plan\"}} Needs Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults. Your files and your assistant The apps never send your files anywhere. But what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant: a cloud assistant's maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job's folders out of the ones you allow.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"community-testing","scope":"basics","title":"Community testing","summary":"How to try a test on the Community testing page and send your result.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["community","testing","test","results","report","feedback","files","passed","failing"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-result","text":"Send a result","level":2},{"id":"sending-files","text":"Sending files","level":2},{"id":"your-privacy","text":"Your privacy","level":2},{"id":"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work","text":"What counts as \"known not to work\"","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-result\"\u003ESend a result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ECommunity testing\u003C/strong\u003E page on the website and choose a test.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse the public build named in \u003Cstrong\u003EBefore you start\u003C/strong\u003E, never a lab build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFollow the \u003Cstrong\u003ESteps\u003C/strong\u003E and compare with the \u003Cstrong\u003EExpected result\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in \u003Cstrong\u003ESend a result\u003C/strong\u003E: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sending-files\"\u003ESending files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-privacy\"\u003EYour privacy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour first name is shown next to a result only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EShow my first name\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENotes are shown to others only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EAnyone may read my notes\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFiles are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work\"\u003EWhat counts as &quot;known not to work&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Some checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help. Send a result Open the Community testing page on the website and choose a test. Use the public build named in Before you start, never a lab build. Follow the Steps and compare with the Expected result. Fill in Send a result: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened. You do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files. Sending files When a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages. Your privacy A result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page. Your first name is shown next to a result only if you tick Show my first name. Notes are shown to others only if you tick Anyone may read my notes. Files are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first. What counts as \"known not to work\" Each test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"input-devices","scope":"basics","title":"Use a trackpad, 3D mouse or controller","summary":"Set what a trackpad, your mouse's side buttons, a pen, finger taps, a 3D mouse, a game controller or a MIDI controller does, for every app or for one app.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","two fingers","pinch","scroll","mouse","side button","back button","forward button","tilt wheel","pen","stylus","eraser","tap","3D mouse","space mouse","6 axis","game controller","gamepad","joystick","MIDI","knob","fader","pad","input device","customize","assign"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"every-app-or-only-this-app","text":"Every app, or only this app","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons","text":"Mouse buttons","level":2},{"id":"pen-and-finger-taps","text":"Pen and finger taps","level":2},{"id":"3d-mouse","text":"3D mouse","level":2},{"id":"game-controller","text":"Game controller","level":2},{"id":"midi-controller","text":"MIDI controller","level":2},{"id":"share-your-settings","text":"Share your settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInput devices\u003C/span\u003E, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-app-or-only-this-app\"\u003EEvery app, or only this app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThese settings are for\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly\u003C/span\u003E this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E again to drop this app&#039;s own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app&#039;s apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger scroll\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan the view\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit (3D views)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPinch\u003C/span\u003E: zoom, as the app does, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse the direction\u003C/span\u003E apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons\"\u003EMouse buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack side button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForward side button\u003C/span\u003E: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit everything in view\u003C/span\u003E. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWheel tilted sideways\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe wheel&#039;s zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app&#039;s own tab, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pen-and-finger-taps\"\u003EPen and finger taps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser end\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, or any command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThree-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPalm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-mouse\"\u003E3D mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect a 3D mouse\u003C/span\u003E and pick it in the browser&#039;s list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach movement (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELift and press\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwist\u003C/span\u003E) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E turns it round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly the strongest movement at a time\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach button runs a command or fits the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"game-controller\"\u003EGame controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse up and down on the sticks\u003C/span\u003E are there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"midi-controller\"\u003EMIDI controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect MIDI\u003C/span\u003E. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a control for\u003C/span\u003E (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearn it\u003C/span\u003E, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-settings\"\u003EShare your settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the settings shown as a file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E reads one back (settings this version doesn&#039;t know are left out, and you&#039;re told how many). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the defaults\u003C/span\u003E undoes every change for the settings shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app&#039;s own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in Settings › Global › Input devices, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before. Every app, or only this app At the top, These settings are for: Every app (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own. Only this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps. Choose Every app again to drop this app's own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app's apps. Trackpad Two-finger scroll: What the app does, Pan the view, Zoom or Orbit (3D views). Pinch: zoom, as the app does, or Nothing. Speed and Reverse the direction apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit. The app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does. Mouse buttons Back side button and Forward side button: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or Fit everything in view. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page. Wheel tilted sideways: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing. The wheel's zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app's own tab, under Menus, keys and mouse. Pen and finger taps Side button and Eraser end: what the app does, or any command. Two-finger tap and Three-finger tap (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or Nothing. Palm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under Pen and touch. 3D mouse Choose Connect a 3D mouse and pick it in the browser's list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer: Each movement (Push left and right, Push forward and back, Lift and press, Tilt forward and back, Tilt left and right, Twist) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and Reverse turns it round. Speed, Dead zone (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and Only the strongest movement at a time. Each button runs a command or fits the view. A 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Game controller Plug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. Speed, Dead zone and Reverse up and down on the sticks are there too. MIDI controller Choose Connect MIDI. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under Add a control for (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose Learn it, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Share your settings Export saves the settings shown as a file; Import reads one back (settings this version doesn't know are left out, and you're told how many). Back to the defaults undoes every change for the settings shown. Not built yet A wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app's own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"undo-across-windows","scope":"basics","title":"Undo the last step across windows","summary":"In CadCraft Studio, take back the last thing you did in any app window from the Studio window, in the order you worked, without changing windows first.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["undo","undo across apps","undo last step","last step","project undo","studio undo","order I worked","undo in another app","windows","journal"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-do","text":"What you will do","level":2},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How it works","level":2},{"id":"what-it-will-not-do","text":"What it will not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-do\"\u003EWhat you will do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou drew a wall in BimCraft, then moved a mark in TakeoffCraft, and you want the last one back without hunting for the right window. In the Studio window, under \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen windows\u003C/strong\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo last step\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-it-works\"\u003EHow it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach app window tells Studio when it finishes a step, with the name you see in its own Undo. Studio keeps the newest step of every window and the order between them. The line beside the button names the newest one, for example &quot;Rename the takeoff in TakeoffCraft&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo last step\u003C/span\u003E in the Studio window. Studio asks &quot;Undo this step in that app?&quot;; nothing happens until you say \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo it\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio asks that window for its own next undo and the window runs it, exactly as if you had pressed Undo there. Choose the button again for the step before.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-will-not-do\"\u003EWhat it will not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt takes back \u003Cstrong\u003Eone window&#039;s own last step\u003C/strong\u003E only. It does not undo the effects of that step in another app: a change in BimCraft that fed a takeoff is not undone in TakeoffCraft by this.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you have changed that window since (typed in it, opened another file, or undone by hand there), Studio does not force anything. It skips that window and says nothing was undone; use Undo inside the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA closed window&#039;s steps are gone. Steps older than half an hour are not offered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt does not undo a save, and it is not offered in a browser tab on its own: it needs the apps open as windows of one Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach app&#039;s own Undo (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) works as before, and is told to Studio too, so the two never disagree.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will do You drew a wall in BimCraft, then moved a mark in TakeoffCraft, and you want the last one back without hunting for the right window. In the Studio window, under Open windows, choose Undo last step. How it works Each app window tells Studio when it finishes a step, with the name you see in its own Undo. Studio keeps the newest step of every window and the order between them. The line beside the button names the newest one, for example \"Rename the takeoff in TakeoffCraft\". Choose Undo last step in the Studio window. Studio asks \"Undo this step in that app?\"; nothing happens until you say Undo it. Studio asks that window for its own next undo and the window runs it, exactly as if you had pressed Undo there. Choose the button again for the step before. What it will not do It takes back one window's own last step only. It does not undo the effects of that step in another app: a change in BimCraft that fed a takeoff is not undone in TakeoffCraft by this. If you have changed that window since (typed in it, opened another file, or undone by hand there), Studio does not force anything. It skips that window and says nothing was undone; use Undo inside the app. A closed window's steps are gone. Steps older than half an hour are not offered. It does not undo a save, and it is not offered in a browser tab on its own: it needs the apps open as windows of one Studio. Each app's own Undo (Ctrl+Z) works as before, and is told to Studio too, so the two never disagree.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"full-screen-crosshair","scope":"basics","title":"Full-screen crosshair","summary":"Two thin lines through the pointer, edge to edge across the drawing or view, to check by eye that things line up. One switch turns it on in every app.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["full screen crosshair","full-screen cursor","crosshair","cursor","cursorsize","cursorsize 100","edge to edge","line up","alignment","align","guide lines","cross hairs"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does","text":"What it does","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD&#039;s \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E 100.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), go to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E and tick \u003Cstrong\u003ECrosshair from edge to edge in every app\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EFull-screen crosshair\u003C/strong\u003E. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EToolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003ECursor\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EFull screen\u003C/strong\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E and 100.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD's CURSORSIZE 100. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,), go to General and tick Crosshair from edge to edge in every app under Full-screen crosshair. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open. What it does The lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running. Toolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them. With a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen. In CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: Settings › CadCraft › Cursor › Full screen, or type CURSORSIZE and 100.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"unit-lists","scope":"basics","title":"Units, typed values and the unit lists","summary":"Type a length in any unit with its mark, choose which units the unit lists show, and star the unit new work starts in. A file always keeps its own unit.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["units","unit list","metric","imperial","feet","inches","feet and inches","meters","millimeters","US survey foot","survey feet","international foot","yards","miles","nautical mile","chain","rod","link","furlong","fathom","point","pica","acre","hectare","square","roofing square","acre-foot","board foot","gallon","gon","grad","radian","degrees minutes seconds","slope","percent","H:V","rise run","default unit","star","INSUNITS"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-a-length-in-any-unit","text":"Type a length in any unit","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-units-the-lists-show","text":"Choose the units the lists show","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used","text":"Where the lists and the star are used","level":2},{"id":"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit","text":"A file keeps its own unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-length-in-any-unit\"\u003EType a length in any unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit&#039;s mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet and inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5 km\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2 dm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E400 nm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emetric lengths (\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is the nanometer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5 mi\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E250 mil\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyards, miles, thousandths of an inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 US mi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe US survey foot and mile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2 rd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100 li\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 fur\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erods, links, chains, furlongs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 nmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1 NM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6 ftm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enautical miles, fathoms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12 pt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 pc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoints and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type \u003Ccode\u003Enmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ENM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ENm\u003C/code\u003E is refused, because it could be either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eft\u003C/code\u003E is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type \u003Ccode\u003EUS ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eusft\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Esurvey ft\u003C/code\u003E. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: \u003Ccode\u003E2*3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3 ch\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so \u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 and \u003Ccode\u003E10 ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 project feet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-units-the-lists-show\"\u003EChoose the units the lists show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear on the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, and find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E. There is one list for each kind: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVolume\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file&#039;s unit, or the one for your region).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the unit lists\u003C/span\u003E puts the starting lists back, with no stars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used\"\u003EWhere the lists and the star are used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EApp\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat follows the switches\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the star sets\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s units list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list beside the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit of a new model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list on the plan toolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring and position units in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, and the unit in the calibrate dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring unit, until MarkupCraft&#039;s own settings have been saved\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit lists of an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength and area: the unit of a new item\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe angle and slope lists are saved, but no app&#039;s box follows them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit\"\u003EA file keeps its own unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give. Type a length in any unit In a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit's mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in. Type Means 12'6\", 12' 6 1/2\", 12'-6\", 3 ft 6 in feet and inches 450 mm, 3.2 m, 1.5 km, 2 dm, 400 nm metric lengths (nm is the nanometer) 3 yd, 2.5 mi, 250 mil yards, miles, thousandths of an inch 10 US ft, 1 US mi the US survey foot and mile 2 rd, 100 li, 3 ch, 1 fur rods, links, chains, furlongs 1 nmi or 1 NM, 6 ftm nautical miles, fathoms 12 pt, 3 pc points and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch) nm is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type nmi or NM. Nm is refused, because it could be either. ft is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type US ft, usft or survey ft. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones. Sums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: 2*3 yd, 12'6\" + 3 ch. In CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch. In CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so 10 US ft is 10 and 10 ft is 10 project feet. Choose the units the lists show Open Settings (the gear on the app bar, or Ctrl+,), choose General, and find Units. There is one list for each kind: Length, Area, Volume, Angle and Slope. Each unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps. Click the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file's unit, or the one for your region). Reset the unit lists puts the starting lists back, with no stars. The choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them. Where the lists and the star are used App What follows the switches What the star sets CadCraft The units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry CivilCraft The project's units list Length: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in ModelCraft The units list beside the value box The unit of a new model BimCraft The units list on the plan toolbar The display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept) MarkupCraft The measuring and position units in Settings, and the unit in the calibrate dialog The measuring unit, until MarkupCraft's own settings have been saved TakeoffCraft The unit lists of an item Length and area: the unit of a new item The angle and slope lists are saved, but no app's box follows them yet. A file keeps its own unit The settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in Settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does.","lang":"en"}],"locale":"de","chain":["de","en"]};
